1 NOTE {geni:about_me} '''Beatrice d’Este''' (Ferrara, 1268 - Milan, 15 September 1334) was an Italian noblewoman, now primarily known for Dante Alighieri's allusion to her in Purgatorio, the second canticle of the [https://en.wikipedia.
2 CONC org/wiki/Divine_Comedy Divine Comedy]. Through her first marriage to Nino Visconti, she was judge (giudichessa) of Gallura, and through her second marriage to Galeazzo I Visconti, following Nino’s death, lady of Milan.
2 CONT
2 CONT == Family Relationships ==
2 CONT
2 CONT === Parents ===
2 CONT * '''Father:''' Marquis Opizzo II d’Este (Lord of Ferrara, Modena, and Reggio Emilia)
2 CONT * '''Mother:''' Jacopina Fieschi
2 CONT
2 CONT === Siblings ===
2 CONT * '''Brother:''' Azzo VIII d'Este (Lord of Ferrara)
2 CONT
2 CONT === Marriages and Spouses ===
2 CONT * '''First Husband:''' Nino Visconti (Judge of Gallura in Sardinia; grandson of Count Ugolino della Gherardesca) – married c. 1291, died 1296.
2 CONT * '''Second Husband:''' Galeazzo I Visconti (Son of Matteo I Visconti, Lord of Milan) – married 24 June 1300, died 6 August 1328.
2 CONT
2 CONT === Children ===
2 CONT * '''Daughter (with Nino Visconti):''' Joanna (born 1291)
2 CONT * '''Son (with Galeazzo I Visconti):''' Azzone Visconti (proclaimed lord of Milan on 15 January 1329)
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT In ''Purgatorio'' (Canto VIII), Nino uses her swift remarriage to Galeazzo I Visconti of Milan as an example to lament the fickleness of women's love when physical presence is lost. Beyond personal betrayal, the union also crossed
2 CONC  political lines—Nino was a prominent Guelph leader, while her new husband belonged to a rival faction—adding a layer of political disloyalty to his grievance.
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT https://youtu.be/UuYyTxXVVds?si=z5Xxq1lJku7r-P8w
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT ===References 
2 CONT - https://it.wikipedia.org/wiki/Beatrice_d%27Este_(1268-1334)_
2 CONT - https://www.treccani.it/enciclopedia/beatrice-d-este_(Enciclopedia-Dantesca)
