2 CONT http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fieschi Fieschi family From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
2 CONT   (Redirected from Fieschi) Jump to: navigation, search 	This article does not cite any references or sources. Please help improve this article by adding citations to reliable sources. Unsourced material may be challenged and remo
2 CONC ved. (December 2009)
2 CONT
2 CONT The Fieschi were a noble family from Genoa, Italy, from whom descend the Fieschi Ravaschieri Princes of Belmonte.
2 CONT
2 CONT They held the fief of Lavagna under the Holy Roman Emperors. As Counts of Lavagna the Fieschi represented the Emperor of the West in Liguria from the earliest years of the C11th. In 1010 the investiture of the Fieschi took place a
2 CONC t Genoa: the family were created Counts of Lavagna and Imperial Vicars General (ie Viceroys) of the whole of Tuscany and of the coast of Genoa. In the words of Henry the Holy, King of Italy since 1004 and Holy Roman Emperor from 1
2 CONC 014 and the last of the Ottonian dynasty, 'Ordiniamo il predominato Fieschi vicario generale di essa città con ampio potere.' ('We appoint the pre-eminent Fieschi to be Vicars General of this city-state with broad powers').
2 CONT
2 CONT The Imperial Houses of Hohenstaufen, Luxembourg and later Habsburg each in turn confirmed the Counts in the rank of Count Palatine.
2 CONT
2 CONT Males of the Fieschi— all of them styled Conte di Lavagna— played major roles as Guelph partisans in the governance and military history of medieval Genoa, ever in conflict with the Republic and always retaining their connection w
2 CONC ith their holdings here.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1138, in an agreement between the Fieschi and the commune of Genoa, the Fieschi agreed to spend part of the year in the city. They earned great riches from trading and financial activities, and later developed in numerous diffe
2 CONC rent branches. Apart from Liguria, they possessed fiefs in Piedmont, Lombardy, Umbria and in the Kingdom of Naples.
2 CONT
2 CONT Sinibaldo de' Fieschi, Count of Lavagna, became pope as Innocent IV in 1243, and his nephew Ottobuono was elected pope to succeed Adrian V on July 12, 1276, but died at Viterbo on August 18.
2 CONT
2 CONT In the Fieschi conspiracy of 1547, Giovanni Luigi Fieschi and the nobles unsuccessfully attempted to recapture the dogate from Andrea Doria, and the power of the Fieschi was broken. [edit] Famous members
2 CONT
2 CONT     * Pope Innocent IV
2 CONT     * Pope Adrian V
2 CONT     * Niccolò Fieschi
2 CONT     * St Catherine (Fieschi) (1447-1510)
2 CONT     * Giovanni Luigi Fieschi (1522-1547)
2 CONT     * Don Orazio Giovan Battista Ravaschieri Fieschi, 1st Prince of Belmonte (?-1645)
2 CONT     * Giuseppe Fieschi (1790-1836)
2 CONT
2 CONT [edit] See also
2 CONT
2 CONT     * Republic of Genoa
2 CONT     * Guelphs and Ghibellines
2 CONT     * The Fieschi Letter
2 CONT     * the character Fiescho in Verdi's opera Simone Boccanegra
