2 CONT
2 CONT Obizzo II d'Este (b. ca. 1247 – d. 13 February 1293) was Marquis of Ferrara and the March of Ancona.
2 CONT
2 CONT Contents
2 CONT
2 CONT [show]
2 CONT
2 CONT     * 1 Early years
2 CONT
2 CONT     * 2 Ruler of Ferrara
2 CONT
2 CONT     * 3 Marriages and Issue
2 CONT
2 CONT     * 4 References
2 CONT
2 CONT [edit] Early years
2 CONT
2 CONT He was a bastard,[1] the fruit of an illegitimate relation of Rinaldo I d'Este -the only son and heir of the Margrave Azzo VII d'Este- with a napolitan laundress. Soon after his birth, Obizzo was expelled from Ferrara with his mot
2 CONC her and settled in Ravenna.
2 CONT
2 CONT For his condition, Obizzo was destined to an obscure future; nevertheless, this situation changed in 1251: his father Rinaldo, a hostage of the Emperor Frederick I since 1238, was poisoned with his barren wife, Adelaida da Romano.
2 CONC  Without any other sons to continue his line, the Margrave Azzo VII saw in Obizzo the only chance of survival of the House of Este and fought for his recognition as his heir. Obizzo was legitimated by the Pope in 1252, shortly aft
2 CONC er his mother was drowned in the Adriatic.[2]
2 CONT
2 CONT [edit] Ruler of Ferrara
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1264 he was proclaimed life-long ruler of Ferrara, Lord of Modena in 1288 and of Reggio in 1289. His rule marked the end of the communal period in Ferrara and the beginning of the Lordship, which lasted until the 17th century.
2 CONT
2 CONT He was probably killed by his son Azzo VIII d'Este without having nominated an heir. Azzo VIII claimed the government as the eldest son (primogeniture) but his brothers Aldobrandino and Francesco made a violent dispute for his rig
2 CONC hts. Finally, they made a divisionary treaty over the lands: Azzo retained Ferrara, Aldobrandino received Modena and Francesco obtained Reggio Emilia.
2 CONT
2 CONT Obizzo d'Este is cited in Dante's Inferno.
2 CONT
2 CONT [edit] Marriages and Issue
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1263 Obizzo married firstly with Giacomina (d. December 1287), daughter of Niccolo Fieschi di Lavagna and niece of Pope Innocent IV. They had five children:
2 CONT
2 CONT    1. Azzo VIII (b. after 1263 - d. Este, 31 January 1308).
2 CONT
2 CONT    2. Beatrice (d. 15 September 1334), married firstly with Ugolino Visconti, Giudice of Gallura and secondly on 24 June 1300 to Galeazzo I Visconti, Lord of Milan.
2 CONT
2 CONT    3. Maddalena, married firstly with Aldobrandino Turchi, from Ferrara, and secondly with Raniero di Canossa.
2 CONT
2 CONT    4. Aldobrandino II (d. 1326).
2 CONT
2 CONT    5. Francesco (killed in action, Ferrara, 23 August 1312).
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1289 Obizzo married secondly with Constanza (d. Mantua, 1306), daughter of Alberto I della Scala, Lord of Verona. This union was childless.
2 CONT
