2 CONT
2 CONT *https://fmg.ac/Projects/MedLands/MONFERRATO,%20SALUZZO,%20SAVONA.htm#TomasoISaluzzodied1296B 
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT '''TOMMASO di Saluzzo''', son of MANFREDO III Marchese di Saluzzo & his wife Béatrix de Savoie (-3 Dec 1296, bur Staffarda Santa Maria).  A continuation of the Chronica Jacobi de Aquis names "Thomaso" as the son of "Manfredo" and 
2 CONC his wife "Biatrix figlola dil Conte Amedeo di Savoya"[563].  "Mainfredus III marchio Salutiarum" appointed "Beatricem de Sabaudia suam uxorem" as co-tutor of "Thomæ et Alasinæ filiorum suorum" in his testament dated 1244[564].  He
2 CONC  succeeded his father in 1244 as TOMMASO I Marchese di Saluzzo.  The men of "marchionatus Salutiarum" swore homage to "Mainfredi marchionis, Thomæ…filio" by charter dated 1244[565].  The testament of "Bonefacius Montisferrati marc
2 CONC hio", dated 12 Jun 1253, appoints "Guilelminum filium meum inpuberem" as his heir, substituting in turn "Alaxinam…filia mea" and "Tomam de Saluciis", if his son died childless[566].  "Thomas marchio Salutiarum atque…Aluysia de Cev
2 CONC a eius uxor et Salutiarum marchionissa" donated property to "monasterii beati Marie de Revello" by charter dated 25 Jun 1291[567].  The testament of "domini Thomæ Marchionis Salutiarum", dated 17 Oct 1294, chooses his burial "in m
2 CONC onasterio S. Mariæ de Staffarda" with his heart buried "in monumento dominæ Alasiæ quondam marchionissæ Salutiarum consortis suæ in monasterio S. Mariæ de Revello", bequeathes property to "filium suum Philippum…filium suum Joannem
2 CONC …Bonifacium…Georgium… Elianoram filiam suam…filios dominæ Alasiæ filiæ suæ…filiam suam Beatricem…filiam suam sororem Constantiam…filiam suam sororem Margaritam…filiam suam sororem Alianam…filiam suam Catherinam…filiam suam Alvisia
2 CONC m…filiam suam Jolam…Manfredum suum primogenitum"[568]. 
2 CONT
2 CONT '''m''' ALOISIA di Ceva, daughter of GUGLIELMO [II] Marchese di Ceva & his wife --- di Saluzzo (-[22 Aug 1291/1293], bur Revello Santa Maria).  A continuation of the Chronica Jacobi de Aquis names "Loysa figlola dil Marchese di Ce
2 CONC va" as the wife of "Thomaso", son of "Manfredo"[569].  A charter dated 31 Mar 1282 names "Thome marchionis Salutiarum et Aluyisia eius uxoris"[570].  "Aloisia comitissa Salutiarum uxor Thomæ I marchionis Salutiarum" consented to d
2 CONC onations by "eundem marchionem eorum filio Mainfredo" on the occasion of the latter´s marriage to "Beatrice filia quondam Mainfredi regis Siciliæ" by charter dated 1286[571].  "Marchese Tomaso di Saluzzo e Marchesa Alosia padre e 
2 CONC madre di detto Manfredo" consented to the marriage between "Manfredo figlio primogenito del Marchese Tomaso di Saluzzo" and "Beatrice figlia del fù Re Manfredo, sorella della Regina Costanza d'Aragona e Sicilia" by contract dated 
2 CONC 3 Jul 1286[572].  "Thomas marchio Salutiarum atque…Aluysia de Ceva eius uxor et Salutiarum marchionissa" donated property to "monasterii beati Marie de Revello" by charter dated 25 Jun 1291[573]. 
2 CONT
2 CONT   Marchese Tomaso I & his wife had fifteen children: 
2 CONT
2 CONT ------------
2 CONT Thomas I (died 1296) was the fourth margrave of Saluzzo from 1244 to his death. He succeeded his father Manfred III.
2 CONT
2 CONT Under the reign of Thomas, Saluzzo blossomed, achieving a greatness which had eluded his ancestors. He crafted a state whose borders remained unchanged for over two centuries. He extended the march to include Carmagnola. He was of
2 CONC ten at odds with Asti and he was a prime enemy of the Charles of Anjou and his Italian pretensions. During his tenure, he made Saluzzo a free city, giving it a podestà to govern in his name. He defended his castles and roccaforti 
2 CONC (strongholds) vigorously and built many new ones in the cities. He was succeeded by his son Manfred.
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT Fourth margrave of Saluzzo Made Saluzzo a free city Extended borders of state - to where it remained for two centuries
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT Thomas I (died 1296) was the fourth margrave of Saluzzo from 1244 to his death. He succeeded his father Manfred III.
2 CONT
2 CONT Under the reign of Thomas, Saluzzo blossomed, achieving a greatness which had eluded his ancestors. He crafted a state whose borders remained unchanged for over two centuries. He extended the march to include Carmagnola. He was of
2 CONC ten at odds with Asti and he was a prime enemy of the Charles of Anjou and his Italian pretensions. During his tenure, he made Saluzzo a free city, giving it a podestà to govern in his name. He defended his castles and roccaforti 
2 CONC (strongholds) vigorously and built many new ones in the cities. He was succeeded by his son Manfred.
2 CONT
2 CONT https://ancestors.familysearch.org/en/LRSM-MTQ/thomas-di-saluzzo-1239-1296
2 CONT https://www.wikitree.com/wiki/Saluzzo-22
2 CONT
2 CONT https://www.findagrave.com/memorial/150859017/tommaso-di_saluzzo
2 CONT
2 CONT Marquess (Marchese) of Saluzzo from 1244 until 1296. Under his reign Saluzzo prospered and achieved a level of success which had eluded his ancestors. He extended its border to include Carmagnola and was often at odds with Asti, m
2 CONC ade Saluzzo a free city and instituted a podestà to govern in his name, and constructed new fortresses to defend his possessions. Tomasso was the chief nemesis of Charles d'Anjou (1226-1285) in his Italian pretensions. He died wit
2 CONC h a testament dated October 17, 1294, choosing his place of burial and requesting that his heart be placed in his wife's sepulchral monument.
2 CONT
2 CONT Tomasso was the son and heir of Manfredo III, Marchese di Saluzzo (d. 1244), by his wife Béatrice de Savoie, daughter of Amédée IV, Comte de Savoie (d. 1253) by his first wife Marguerite de Bourgogne, daughter of Hugues III, Duc d
2 CONC e Bourgogne (d. 1192) by his second wife Béatrice, Dauphine de Viennois (d. 1228).
2 CONT
2 CONT By his wife Aloisia di Ceva (d. 1293), Tomasso was the father of fifteen children, Manfredo, Eleonora, Alasia, Violante, Filippo, Giovanni, Bonifazio, Giorgio, Beatrice, Constanza, Margherita, Aliana, Caterina, Aloisia, and Anna. 
2 CONC He was also the father of three illegitimate children.
2 CONT
2 CONT Descendant of Karolus Magnus (d. 814), Pater Europæ.
