1 NOTE {geni:about_me} [poorly translated from the Italian} source: http://www.treccani.it/enciclopedia/gigliola-gonzaga_(Dizionario-Biografico)_ which suggests she died after 1375.
2 CONT
2 CONT The Milanese historiographic tradition posits Gigliola married to Matteo (II), son of Stefano Visconti already at the end of the thirties (even if 1338, mentioned in the Annales Mediolanenses , seems to be the result of an error, 
2 CONC since it refers to Matteo's exile, which actually took place a decade later) or at least in 1340, when Matteo came to Mantua to represent the Visconti family at the Magna curia for the fourfold wedding of Luigi (I), his son Corrad
2 CONC o and two of his grandchildren.
2 CONT
2 CONT As Matteo's wife, Gigliola  shared with him in 1347 the exile in Morano in Monferrato to which Luchino Visconti forced his nephew into  an exile mitigated by the intercession of her father, Filippino . The couple returned to Milan
2 CONC  after the death of Luchino, when Archbishop Giovanni summoned his nephews Matteo, Galeazzo and Bernabò.  In the absence of brothers (she had only one sister, Elizabeth, married in March 1354 with Count Rodolfo of Habsburg) Giglio
2 CONC la., who was widowed in 1355 by Matteo and having borne only one daughter, Caterina, found herself at the death of her father in to be his only heir, as evidenced by the inventory of Filippino's assets, drawn up a few days after h
2 CONC is death, on 20 April. 1356.
2 CONT
2 CONT She  therefore found herself controlling the part due to Filippino of the assets and prerogatives on the territories controlled by the Gonzagas and the complex of the dotal assets of her  mother Anna, and in turn the only heir to 
2 CONC a huge portion of the Cremonese lands of the da Dovara ( Pomponesco, Correggioverde, Fossa Caprara, Vescovato, Cicognara, Sabbioneta, Commessaggio, Rivarolo, Viadana, Isola Dovarese). It is evident that the destination of this imp
2 CONC ortant complex of prerogatives and lands, which came into the hands of two female heirs, soon became a fact of capital importance for the Gonzaga family.
2 CONT
2 CONT Gigiola who returned to Mantua on the death of her husband, was subjected to heavy pressure from her uncles Guido and Feltrino, so much so that she ended up giving them all her possessions on July 21, 1357.   In 1359, however, Gig
2 CONC iola, emancipated according to Daino (the sixteenth-century archivist of the Gonzaga) by Luigi (I).  In February, she appealed to the emperor Charles IV to do her justice.  Then in May she wrote to the imperial court that Guido an
2 CONC d Feltrino had forced her into presence of his father Luigi "non propterea abilis ad dictis suis filiis resistendum" (Arch. of State of Mantua, Gonzaga Archive [ inde Arch. Gonzaga], b. 337, May 17 1357). Daino also traces the veh
2 CONC ement reaction of Gigliola  to the fact that she was now supported by her cousin Ugolino di Guido, who had a son, Bernabò. The trial was favorable and the sentence of 23 oct. 1359 returned the disputed assets. Shortly thereafter, 
2 CONC Gigliola. made a full donation of what was due to Ugolino, as guardian of Bernabò ( ibid ., 1st-17th April 1360).
2 CONT
2 CONT The strenuous defense of her rights was not over for  Gigiola.  In 1360,  a further long cause began between her and another of Luigi's children (I), Federico (born from Luigi's second marriage to Caterina Malatesta , who along wi
2 CONC th Mantuan blood relatives, had occupied between the end of 1358 and the beginning of the following year some Cremonese lands belonging to her, claiming to be entitled on the basis of a donation made by Anna da Dovara in case of d
2 CONC eath of she and her   husband without male children. The matter dragged on until 1375, with Federico's appeal to Bernabò Visconti and Gigiola  to the emperor.    The imperial arbitral tribunal, presided over by the patriarch of Aq
2 CONC uileia, Marquardo, in 1368 sustained Gigiola's suit., backed by her  uncle Guido and her cousins ​​Ludovico and Francesco. In the meantime both Ugolino (assassinated by the brothers in 1362), and probably the little Bernabò, whom 
2 CONC Daino says died precisely in 1368, had disappeared.  Gigiola  had returned to the blood of blood relatives interested only in ensuring control of the share of land and prerogatives that belonged to her and her descendants: 27 ag. 
2 CONC 1364 her sister Elizabeth complained from Innsbruck with Guido, Ludovico and Francesco that "soror mea Ziliola esset in captivitate vestra et reclusam et custoditam" ( she had returned to the blood of blood relatives interested on
2 CONC ly in ensuring control of the share of land and prerogatives that were due to her and her descendants: on 27 August. 1364 her sister Elizabeth complained from Innsbruck with Guido, Ludovico and Francesco that "soror mea Ziliola es
2 CONC set in captivitate vestra et reclusam et custoditam" ( she had returned to the blood of blood relatives interested only in ensuring control of the share of land and prerogatives that were due to her and her descendants: on 27 Augu
2 CONC st. 1364 her sister Elizabeth complained from Innsbruck with Guido, Ludovico and Francesco that "soror mea Ziliola esset in captivitate vestra et reclusam et custoditam" (Arch. Gonzaga , b. 544). G. regained possession of the land
2 CONC s occupied by Federico in September 1375 and in October made a complete donation to Francesco, son of Ludovico (II) Gonzaga, now the only lord of Mantua.
2 CONT
2 CONT After almost twenty years - during which the first two generations of the Gonzaga family had convulsively defined the balance of power between the different branches of the family and the legitimacy of the new lords had gone throu
2 CONC gh complex foreign and matrimonial policy choices in a continuous cross-comparison with the growing Visconti power  Hohop;s therefore had to resign herself to seeing, after the disappearance of each direct heir of Filippino, the l
2 CONC ands and the prerogatives of the father definitively become part of the prerogative of the main branch of the dynasty.
2 CONT
2 CONT After 1375 there is no further news of her. 
2 CONT
2 CONT See also:  https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/House_of_Gonzaga
2 CONT
