1 NOTE {geni:about_me} OF THE TORRE, Erecco (Errecco, Arrecco, Henry). - Son of Ermanno of Pagano, the date of birth is unknown. 
2 CONT
2 CONT In the years in which the rule of his uncle Napoleon was established, called Napo Della Torre in Milan the D., like many other relatives, participated in the municipal government and also held the podestainate in several cities, a
2 CONC ccording to a wise policy aimed at ensuring the family control of centers considered important and the support of the Ruling parties in power in other major cities. He was granted power in Novara in 1266, in Bologna in 1267, in Cr
2 CONC emona in 1268 and in Orvieto in 1269. In 1266, following the killing of his uncle Pagano, Paganino, his father's brother, in Vercelli in January, he was at the side of his uncles Napo and Francis in the action taken against politi
2 CONC cal opponents and those responsible for the crime.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1277 he participated in the clash between Torriani and Visconti, which ended unhappily with the defeat suffered by his family in Desio on 21 January. Captured with Napo, Caverna, Corrado said Moscow, Guido and Lombardo, he was 
2 CONC locked up with them in the castle of Baradello, a place not far from Como. After the death of Caverna, Lombardo, Napo, and Guido's escape, organized by the enemies of the Visconti in October 1284, only D. and Corrado remained clos
2 CONC ed in the fortress. In the meantime, however, thanks to the intervention of his father's uncle, Raymond Della Torre patriarch of Aquileia, the alliance between William VII Marquis of Monferrato, Loterio Rusca, lord of Como and the
2 CONC  Torriani was consolidated; In December 1284, Loterio Rusca freed the last two Towers still in captivity. D. and Corrado, finally freed after seven years of captivity, publicly expressed their gratitude and swore perpetual hostili
2 CONC ty to the Archbishop of Milan and the Bishop of Como.
2 CONT
2 CONT In the following years, although the clashes between Torriani and Visconti had ended, with the peace of Lomazzo in 1286, which however had not allowed Della Torre to return to Milan, the D., joining Corrado and other Milanese exil
2 CONC es, continued hostilities in the Visconti. In 1290, they were attested to their presence in Pavia, where they joined the Marquis of Monferrato who intended to wage war on Asti, opposed by the city's allies, including the Milanese.
2 CONC  In September, a real threat seemed to be looming for the Visconti, when the Marquis entered Milanese, together with Torriani. The danger was short-lived, however: when aid from Como, Cremona, Crema and Brescia arrived, the enemie
2 CONC s abandoned the enterprise and preferred to retire again to Pavia. The Marquis of Monferrato, turning his weapons again against Asti, was defeated and captured.
2 CONT
2 CONT With his imprisonment, which followed after a year and a half his death, the Della Torre lost the most powerful ally, and were forced to temporarily abandon any attempt to collect anti-viscounty that would allow them to return to 
2 CONC Milan. The D., like many other members of the family, had to take refuge with his uncle Raymond, patriarch of Aquileia, who in those years represented a reference point of fundamental importance for the Torrian fuors. With the hel
2 CONC p of the powerful patriarch, the D. was able to exercise the podestaal office again, which was entrusted to him for Trieste in 1296 and reconfirmed in 1299.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1302, the long-awaited opportunity to return home seemed to be presented. The enemies of the Visconti, including the Marquis of Monferrato and the lord of Piacenza Alberto Scotti, were ready to fight for the resurgence of the t
2 CONC owering power. The young Galeazzo Visconti attempted two unfortunate military feats, turning his weapons first against Novara, then against Pavia. The unfortunate outcome of the two expeditions only tired the army and sow disconte
2 CONC nt among the Milanese, at the very moment when the danger of a torrid rescue loomed.
2 CONT
2 CONT In March Della Torre, including D., Corrado and Martino, arrived in Cremona; from there they advanced to Lodi and prepared to attack the Milanese army. In Milan, however, while within the same Visconti family a rift opened and Pie
2 CONC tro Visconti sided with Matteo's opponents, an uproar broke out that prevented the army from receiving supplies from the city; Monza, meanwhile, had also abandoned Matthew and declared herself pro-Torrian. In this situation, Matte
2 CONC o Visconti was forced to negotiate peace, after which the Visconti had to leave Milan.
2 CONT
2 CONT The D. and his family were able to return to the city, and here he had to die a short time later.
2 CONT
2 CONT The D.'s brother, Gotifredo, was above all a valuable man of arms. Hermajesty of Novara in 1265, vicar Angioino in Florence in 1267, was armed miles in 1273 on the occasion of the visit to Milan of Edward I of England. In 1274 he 
2 CONC was the president of Padua and in this capacity attended the negotiations between his uncle, the patriarch of Aquileia Raymond Della Torre, and the king of Bohemia. In January 1277, at the time of the Battle of Desio, he was in ch
2 CONC arge of Cantu. Upon learning of the Torrian defeat, he moved with his troops to Milan in the hope of rescuing his family. Penetrated into the city by force, he found it already abandoned by his own and tried unnecessarily to lift 
2 CONC the people in defense of the Torrian government. He then took refuge in Parma, from where the fight against the Visconti continued. In January 1279 he participated in the negotiations between the Della Torre and the Visconti, whic
2 CONC h took place in Melegnano with the arbitration of the Marquis of Monferrato. The failure to implement the agreement - which called for the return to the family of the goods that had been expropriated to it - led Gotifredo to take 
2 CONC up arms against the Milanese. In 1281 he suffered a defeat at Vaprio, in 1285, at the head of the militias of the patriarch of Aquileia, occupied and destroyed Castelseprio and forced the Visconti to peace (concluded on 3 Apr. 128
2 CONC 6). In the following years Gotifredo was with his uncle Raymond: in 1287 he was appointed Marquess of Istria and fought against the Venetians. There is little further news about him: it is certainly only known that in 1291 he was 
2 CONC the authority of Alexandria.
2 CONT
2 CONT -by Anna Caso http://www.treccani.it/enciclopedia/erecco-della-torre_(Dizionario-Biografico)/ 
2 CONT
2 CONT Fonti e Bibl.: F. Pippini Chronicon, in L. A. Muratori, Rer. Ital. Script., IX,Mediolani 1726, col. 729; G. Flamma, Manipulus florum, ibid., XI, ibid. 1727, col. 704; Annales Mediolanenses, ibid., XVI, ibid. 1730, coll. 675 s.; Ch
2 CONC ronicon Parmense, in Rer. Ital. Script., 2 ed., IX, 9, a cura di G. Bonazzi, pp. 32, 47; Stephanardi de Vicomercato Liber de gestis in civitate Mediolani, ibid., IX, 1, a cura di G. Calligaris, p. 81; Mutius de Modoetia, Annales P
2 CONC lacentim gibellini a. 1154-1284, in Mon. Germ. Hist., Script., XVIII,Hannoverae 1863, pp. 564, 566, 569 (per Gotifredo); G. Bianchi, Indice dei docc. per la storia del Friuli...,Udine 1877, p. 20 (per Gotifredo); T. Calco, Mediola
2 CONC nensis historiae patriae libri viginti, Mediolani 1627, p. 366; G. Ronchetti, Memorie istor. della città e della Chiesa di Bergamo, II,Bergamo 1807, p. 341; C. de' Rosmini, Dell'istoria di Milano, I,Milano 1820, pp. 328 (per il D.
2 CONC ), 341 (per Gotifredo); G. Arrigoni, Memorie stor. della Valsassina..., Milano 1840, pp. 115, 123, 132 s., 135 (per Gotifredo); P. Giovio Vite dei dodici Visconti, Milano 1853, pp. 67, 83, 85; G. Giulini, Memorie spettanti alla st
2 CONC oria... di Milano ne' secoli bassi, IV,Milano 1855, pp. 551, 571, 589 s., 635, 679 s., 737, 739 ss., 912, 816 (per il D.), 656, 658, 666 (per Gotifredo); F. Cusani, Storia di Milano, a cura di E. De Magni, I, Milano 1861, p. 167; 
2 CONC B. Corio, Storia di Milano, I,Milano 1855, pp. 531 (per il D.), 595 ss., 606, 609, 626 (per Gotifredo); V. Franchini, Saggio di ricerche su l'istituto del podestà nei Comuni medioevali, Bologna 1912, p. 213; P. Paschini, Raimondo 
2 CONC della Torre patriarca d'Aquileia, in Mem. storiche forogiuliesi, XVIII (1922), pp. 92 (per il D.), 53, 71, 92 (per Gotifredo); Id., Storia del Friuli, II,Udine 1935, pp. 194 (per il D.), 184 (per Gotifredo); G. Franceschini, La vi
2 CONC ta sociale e politica nel Duecento, in Storia di Milano, IV, 2, Milano 1954, pp. 306, 330, 342, 363 (per il D.), 321, 330, 337, 342 (per Gotifredo); O. Cornaggia Castiglioni, Sigillo inedito di Guido da Castiglione podestà di Como
2 CONC , in Arch. stor. lomb.,LXXXI-LXXXII (1954-55), p. 372; E. Cazzani, Vescovi ed arcivescovi di Milano, Milano 1955, p. 185; F. Cognasso, I Visconti, Varese 1966, pp. 68, 79, 91; P. Litta, Le famiglie celebri italiane, sub voce Torri
2 CONC ani di Valsassina.
