2 CONC  date and place of birth. The Chronique de Saint-Denis records that Isabeau gave birth 24 August 1392 from the context (which must be incorrect given the births of her other children), “dans l’hôtel royal de Saint-Paul” to “une fi
2 CONC lle...Marie”, whose mother “promit de vouer cette enfant à Dieu” if the King recovered his senses.[92] It is assumed that 1392 should be corrected to 1393. A missal dated 2 Jul 1416 records the birth 22 August “au bois de Vincenne
2 CONC s” of Marie daughter of King Charles VI.[93] She entered the convent of Poissy on 8 September 1397, taking her formal vows as a nun on 26 May 1408 and later became Prioress.[80] Pierre Cochon’s Chronique Normande records that the 
2 CONC Duke of Burgundy wanted to take “une fille du roy, qui estoit nonnain o Poissy” and marry her to “i seigneur estrangier”, and that Charles VI tried to persuade her, but that she refused, dated to 1405 from the context.[94] She die
2 CONC d of the Plague. Michelle	11/12 January 1395	8 July 1422	A missal dated 2 July 1416 records the birth 11 January 1395 “en l’hôtel de Saint-Pol à Paris” of Michelle daughter of King Charles VI.[95] The Chronique de Saint-Denis reco
2 CONC rds that Isabeau gave birth 12 January 1395 from the context, “en l’hôtel royal de Saint-Pol” to “une fille...Michelle”.[96] Married Philip the Good, Duke of Burgundy, in 1409. Had no surviving issue. Louis, Dauphin of Viennois	22
2 CONC  January 1397	18 December 1415	The Chronique Rouennaise records the birth “le jour Saint-Vinchent” 1396 of “le tiers enfant maale du roy Kalles”, adding that he later married “la fille de monsr. de Bourguongne”.[97] Leader of the 
2 CONC Council of the King on 31 December 1409 and Count of Mortain since March 1412. The Chronique Rouennaise records that King Charles VI had three (surviving) sons of whom “l’ainsné” died “merquedi xviii jour decembre” 1415.[98] The G
2 CONC este des Nobles records the death “ou chastel du Louvre” of “le duc Loys de Guienne sans lignie de son corps” and his burial “en l’église de Paris”, dated to end December 1415 from the context.[99] Married Margaret of Burgundy in 
2 CONC 1409. No issue. John, Dauphin of Viennois	31 August 1398	5 April 1417	A missal dated 2 Jul 1416 records the birth 31 Aug 1398 “à Paris en l’hôtel de Saint-Pol” of Jean son of King Charles VI.[100] Duc of Touraine on 12 July 1401, 
2 CONC confirmed 24 May 1414. Baron de Mortagne in 1403. Duc of Berry –the Geste des Nobles records that, after the death in May 1416 of “le duc Jehan de Berry et d’Auvergne conte de Poitou”, the King succeeded to “ses duchié de Berry et
2 CONC  conté de Poitou” and gave Berry to “monseigneur Jehan son filz...qui par avant tenoit Touraine avecques de Dauphiniat”.[101] He lived with his father-in-law, returning to France January 1417. The Geste des Nobles records the deat
2 CONC h “de maladie...le dimenche avant Pasques fleuries” 1416 of “Messire Jehan” and his burial “en l’abbaye saint Cornille”.[102] Married Jacqueline, Countess of Hainaut in 1415. No issue. Catherine	27 October 1401	3 January 1438	A mi
2 CONC ssal dated 2 July 1416 records the birth 27 October 1401 “en l’hôtel de Saint-Paul à Paris” of Catherine daughter of King Charles VI.[103] The Chronicle of Adam de Usk records that “dominus rex” requested “in Franciam...regem ecia
2 CONC m et reginam ac eorum filiam Katerinam” as his wife and “regnum” after her father´s death.[104] Pierre Cochon’s Chronique Normande records that King Henry V entered Troyes 21 May 1420 and “le dimence ensuiant” there married “la fi
2 CONC lle du roy...Catherine”.[105] Crowned Queen of England 24 February 1421 at Westminster Abbey. The Historie of England by Polydore Vergil records that King Henry V´s widow married, after he died, “Owen Tyder a gentleman of Wales...
2 CONC who derived his pedigree from Cadwallider the last king of Brittons”.[106] Her second marriage is confirmed by the will of [her son] "Jasper Duke of Bedford and Earl of Pembroke", dated 15 December 1495, proved 2 July 1496, which 
2 CONC ordered masses for the souls of “Katherine sometime Queen of England my mother, Edmund late Earl of Richmond my brother”.[107] She died in childbirth. Married firstly Henry V, King of England, in 1420. Had issue. Married (?) secon
2 CONC dly Owen Tudor. Had issue.[80] Charles, Dauphin of Viennois	22 February 1403	21 July 1461	A missal dated 2 July 1416 records the birth 22 February 1403 (N.S.) “à Paris en l’hôtel de Saint-Paul” of Charles son of King Charles VI.[1
2 CONC 03] Count of Ponthieu, Duke of Touraine (4/5 April 1417). Member of the Royal Council from 3 September 1416. Duc de Berry, Count of Poitou 17 May 1417. He was named President of the Royal Council 14 Jun 1417 during the periods of 
2 CONC insanity of his father. Invested as Lieutenant General of the King in the whole kingdom on 6 November 1417. He escaped from Paris 29 May 1418 after it was invaded by the Burgundians, and established his parliament at Poitou 21 Sep
2 CONC tember 1418. Regent of France 26 December 1418. After the Treaty of Troyes (21 May 1420), naming King Henry V of England as regent and heir to the throne of France, he withdrew to Anjou, Poitou and Berry. Deprived of the regency 3
2 CONC  January 1421. Married Marie of Anjou in 1422. Had issue.[80] Philip	10 November 1407	10/11 November 1407	The Chronique de Saint-Denis records that Isabeau gave birth “la veille de la Saint-Martin d’hiver...en son hôtel à Paris pr
2 CONC ès la porte Barbette” to “un fils...Philippe” whose body was taken the next day to Saint-Denis for burial.[108] According to modern historians Isabeau stayed in close proximity to the children during their childhood, had them trav
2 CONC el with her, bought them gifts, wrote letters, bought devotional texts, and arranged for her daughters to be educated. She resisted separation and reacted against having her sons sent to other households to live (as was the custom
2 CONC  at the time). Pintoin records she was dismayed at the marriage contract that stipulated her third surviving son, John, be sent to live in Hainaut. She maintained relationships with her daughters after their marriages, writing let
2 CONC ters to them frequently.[80] She sent them out of Paris during an outbreak of plague, staying behind herself with the youngest infant, John, too young to travel. The Celestines allowed "whenever and as often as she liked, she and 
2 CONC her could enter the monastery and church ... their vineyards and gardens, both for devotion and for entertainment and pleasure of herself and her children."[109]
2 CONT
