2 CONT *[http://thepeerage.com/p3921.htm#i39203 The Peerage] 
2 CONT *[http://www.geneall.net/D/per_page.php?id=7828 Geneall]
2 CONT *'''Duke of Bavaria-Munich:''' Reign 1438–1460
2 CONT >'''Precessor:''' [http://www.geni.com/people/Ernst-von-Bayern-M%C3%BCnchen/6000000003858683694 Ernst] '''Successor:''' [http://www.geni.com/people/Johann-IV-von-Bayern-M%C3%BCnchen/6000000006727805931  Johan IV]
2 CONT *'''Wikipedia:''' [http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Albert_III,_Duke_of_Bavaria English ] [http://de.wikipedia.org/wiki/Albrecht_III._(Bayern)_ Deutsch]
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT Albert III of Bavaria-Munich, (27 March 1401 – 29 February 1460), since 1438 Duke of Bavaria-Munich.
2 CONT
2 CONT He had earlier in 1429 been engaged to Elisabeth, the daughter of Eberhard III, Count of Württemberg, but she had eloped and married a Count of Werdenberg, who had been a page at her fathers court.
2 CONT
2 CONT As administrator of his father, Duke Ernest, in the former duchy of Bavaria-Straubing he secretly married Agnes Bernauer, a maid from Augsburg, in 1432, but his father was against this marriage. In 1435, when Agnes lived in Straub
2 CONC ing, Ernest ordered to murder Agnes Bernauer. She was accused of witchcraft, thrown into the River Danube and drowned while Albert was absent hunting. After her death, Albert took himself to Duke Louis at Ingolstadt, but he reconc
2 CONC iled with his father that November.
2 CONT
2 CONT After reconciliation with his father Albert married princess Anna of Brunswick-Grubenhagen-Einbeck and had ten children with her.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1438 he followed his father as duke of Bavaria-Munich. In 1440 he abstained the offered Bohemian crown and 1444 and 1445 he initiated two campaigns against the Robber barons. After the extinction of the dukes of Bavaria-Ingolst
2 CONC adt he also released this duchy to his father's cousin Henry XVI of Bavaria-Landshut in 1447.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1455 Albert founded the Benedictine monastery in Andechs. He died in Munich in 1460 and is buried in Andechs.
2 CONT
2 CONT On 22 January 1437, in Munich, he married Anna of Brunswick-Grubenhagen-Einbeck, daughter of Duke Erich I of Brunswick-Salzderhelden and Elisabeth of Brunswick-Göttingen and they had the following children:
2 CONT
2 CONT *1) John IV, Duke of Bavaria (4 October 1437, Munich–18 November 1463, Haidhausen). 
2 CONT *2) Ernst (26 August 1438, Munich–29 February 1460, Straubing). 
2 CONT *3) Sigismund of Bavaria (1439, Straubing–1 February 1501, Blutenburg Castle). 
2 CONT *4) Albrecht (24 December 1440–1445, Straubing). 
2 CONT *5) Margarete (1 January 1442, Munich–14 October 1479, Mantua), married in Mantua 10 May 1463 to Federico I Gonzaga. 
2 CONT *6) Elisabeth (2 February 1443–5 March 1486, Leipzig), married in Leipzig 19 November 1460 to Elector Ernst of Saxony. 
2 CONT *7) Albert IV, Duke of Bavaria (15 December 1447, Munich–10 March 1508, Munich). 
2 CONT *8) Christoph, Duke of Bavaria (6 January 1449–8 August 1493, Rhodos). 
2 CONT *9) Wolfgang (1 November 1451–24 May 1514, Landsberg am Lech), a canon in Passau, Augsburg and Köln. 
2 CONT *10) Barbara (9 June 1454, Munich–24 June 1472, Munich), a nun in Munich. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Also he had at least three illegitimate children.
