2 CONC  consanguineo vestro”[78]. ''' The most likely explanation is that the family relationship was through the bishop’s mother, the wife of Ebles [III] Seigneur de Grandson.'''  If that is correct, she may have been Ebles’s wife who w
2 CONC as referred to, but not named, in a charter dated 1174 (vidimus dated 1285) which records confirmation of donations to Théla [Montheron] made by “domnus Yblo de Belmont et Iordanus frater eius”, with the support of “uxor ipsius Yb
2 CONC lonis”[79].  If that is right, the marriage probably too place not long before 1174 if Ebles’s birth is correctly estimated to [1144 or after?] as suggested in the document BURGUNDY KINGDOM-NEUCHÂTEL, VAUD, VALAIS, GRUYERE, althou
2 CONC gh the possibility cannot be excluded that Ebles’s 1174 wife died soon afterwards and was replaced by another wife who was the mother of all or some of his children.  '''Charrière names “Béatrice” as “la mère de cette nombreuse fa
2 CONC mille”[80], presumably because Ebles named “...uxoris mee Beatricis...” in his 1235  charter.  However, as pointed out by Williams, if the unnamed wife in 1174 was the same person as Béatrix named in 1235, the couple’s marriage wo
2 CONC uld have lasted more than 60 years which seems “highly improbable”'''[81].  Ebles’s wife was mentioned, but not named, in his 1200 charter, although there is no guarantee that the 1200 wife was the same person as his 1174 wife, no
2 CONC r that she was the mother of all of his six children who are named in the 1200 document.  The problem then arises in identifying the parentage of this unnamed first wife of Ebles [III].  '''Citing the 6 Nov 1252 charter, Charrière
2 CONC  suggests that she “aurait été alors la fille du comte Amédée I”[82].  If this suggestion is correct, she would have been born from the second marriage of [https://www.geni.com/people/Amadeo-Count-of-Geneva/6000000009573554456 Amé
2 CONC dée] [I] Comte de Genève which is dated to [1137/40?]'''  
2 CONT In a different book, Charrière suggests that Ebles’s wife was the daughter of [https://www.geni.com/people/Humbert-comte-de-Gen%C3%A8ve/6000000017090768575 Humbert Comte de Genève], which is unlikely to be correct if Humbert’s bir
2 CONC th is correctly estimated to [1150/60?]. 
2 CONT A further possibility is that the family relationship referred to in the 6 Nov 1252 charter was through the female line, an ancestress of Ebles’s wife being a daughter of the Genevois family.  It should be borne in mind that the t
2 CONC erm “consanguineus” could by the mid-13th century have included quite remote blood family relationships, particularly if the bishop was an individual of some prestige with whom Comte Guillaume [II] may have wished to emphasise a f
2 CONC amily connection to boost his own standing or if Guillaume wished to flatter the bishop (from a seigneurial family) by emphasing his connection with a comital family.  Until more information comes to light, the name and parentage 
2 CONC of the [first] wife of Ebles [III] is shown as unknown. 
2 CONT
