1 NOTE {geni:about_me} *'''Eleanor FitzAlan1,2,3
2 CONT *'''F, #19553, b. circa 1308, d. before 30 March 1347
2 CONT *Father	Sir Edmund FitzAlan, 9th Earl Arundel, Chief Justice of North & South Wales4,5,3 b. 1 May 1285, d. 17 Nov 1326
2 CONT *Mother	Alice de Warren4,5,3 b. c 1287, d. bt 1330 - 23 May 1338
2 CONT *'''     Eleanor FitzAlan was born circa 1308 at of Arundel, Sussex, England.1 A settlement for the marriage Eleanor FitzAlan and William de St. John was made before 1323; No issue.1,2,3 Eleanor FitzAlan married Sir Gerard de L'Is
2 CONC le, 1st Lord Lisle''', son of Sir Warin de Lisle, Keeper of Windsor Castle and Alice Tyeys, '''after 3 December 1329; They had 4 sons''' (Sir Warin, 2nd Lord Lisle; Richard; Gerard; & Edmund).1,2,3 '''Eleanor FitzAlan died before 
2 CONC 30 March 1347.1,3
2 CONT *'''Family 1     William de St. John''' b. 14 Jun 1309, d. b 4 Apr 1329
2 CONT *'''Family 2     Sir Gerard de L'Isle, 1st Lord Lisle''' b. c 1304, d. 9 Jun 1360
2 CONT *'''Child	
2 CONT **Sir Warin de Lisle, 2nd Lord Lisle, 5th Lord Tyeys+1,3 b. c 4 Apr 1333, d. 28 Jun 1382
2 CONT *Citations
2 CONT *[S16] Douglas Richardson, Magna Carta Ancestry, 2nd Edition, Vol. II, p. 180-181.
2 CONT *[S4] Douglas Richardson, Royal Ancestry, Vol. II, p. 604.
2 CONT *[S4] Douglas Richardson, Royal Ancestry, Vol. III, p. 440-441.
2 CONT *[S16] Douglas Richardson, Magna Carta Ancestry, 2nd Edition, Vol. II, p. 178-179.
2 CONT *[S4] Douglas Richardson, Royal Ancestry, Vol. II, p. 602-603.
2 CONT *From: http://our-royal-titled-noble-and-commoner-ancestors.com/p651.htm#i19553 ____________________
2 CONT *'''Gerard II de Lisle, 1st Baron Lisle''' (1304 – 9 June 1360) of Kingston Lisle, (Latinised to de Insula ("from the Island"), French de L'Isle) was an English nobleman and soldier during King Edward III's campaigns in Scotland a
2 CONC nd France.
2 CONT *He was born circa 1304,[1] the son and heir of Sir Warin de Lisle, of Kingston Lisle in the parish of Sparsholt, Berkshire, Keeper of Windsor Castle, by his wife Alice le Tyeys, daughter of Henry le Tyeys, 1st Lord Tyeys. His gra
2 CONC ndfather was Gerard I de Lisle of Kingston Lisle, the younger son of Robert de Lisle of Rougemont by his wife Alice FitzGerold (granddaughter of Henry I FitzGerold (d.1173/4)), the heiress of Kingston. The arms of Barons Lisle of 
2 CONC Rougemont were: Or, a fess between two chevrons sable. In 1269 Alice granted the manor of Kingston to her younger son Gerard I de Lisle, whose family adopted the arms of FitzGerold: Gules, a lion statant guardant argent crowned or
2 CONC .[2]
2 CONT *He resided chiefly at Stowe Nine Churches in Northamptonshire and Kingston Lisle (in Sparsholt), Berkshire (now Oxfordshire).
2 CONT *He was knighted in 1327. In 1329 he proved his right to free warren in his demesne lands at Stowe and Kislingbury, Northamptonshire by grant of King Henry III to Geoffrey de Armenters. In 1332 Richard Herman was attached to answe
2 CONC r Gerard de Lisle concerning a plea why with force and arms he broke the close of the said Gerard at Alverston, Hampshire and dug in his separate soil there, and took and carried away twenty cartloads of earth extracted therefrom 
2 CONC to the value of 40 shillings, and depastured, trampled on, and consumed his grass once growing there to the value of 60 shillings. In 1339 Gerard had a dispute with his mother, Alice, regarding the presentation of the church of St
2 CONC owe, Northamptonshire, but admitted it was not his turn to present; he also complained of trespass on his park at Stowe.
2 CONT *He served on the English side in the Second War of Scottish Independence in 1333 and 1335, under Richard FitzAlan, 10th Earl of Arundel. He served with him again during the Hundred Years' War in France and fought at the Battle of
2 CONC  Crecy, 1346.
2 CONT *He was summoned to Parliament on 15 December 1357, by writs directed to Gerardo de Insula or de L'Isle, whereby he is held to have become Baron Lisle. He was summoned to a Council 20 June 1358.
2 CONT *He married twice:
2 CONT *'''Firstly (after 3 December 1329) to Eleanor de Arundel (d. before 1347), daughter''' of Edmund de Arundel (or Fitz Alan), Knight, 9th Earl of Arundel, by Alice, daughter of William de Warenne, Knight. '''She was the widow of Wi
2 CONC lliam de Saint John. In 1338 he settled the manors of Stowe and Church Brampton, Northamptonshire on himself and his wife Eleanor. She was living in 1342, in which year Gerard sued his receiver, John son of Robert de Hulle, regard
2 CONC ing various monies received from various manors, among them Abbotstone, Hampshire and Walberton, Sussex, which Eleanor held in dower of her first marriage'''. He presented to the churches of Abbotstone, Hampshire, 1349, and Mundfo
2 CONC rd, Norfolk, 1349 and 1352. '''She was dead by 1347 when he contracted to marry''' Margery, widow of Nicholas de la Beche, late Constable of the Tower of London, but who was abducted and married by Sir John Dalton. '''By Eleanor, 
2 CONC Gerard had one son:
2 CONT **Sir Warin Lisle, 2nd Baron Lisle.
2 CONT *He married secondly (before 1 July 1351 (date of settlement) and possibly as early as 29 July 1349 (date of his presentation to Abbotstone church)) to Elizabeth le Strange (d.1362), daughter of Sir John le Strange, 2nd Baron Stra
2 CONC nge of Blackmere, by Ankaret le Boteler, daughter of William le Boteler, 1st Baron Boteler of Wem. Elizabeth was the widow of Edmund de Saint John, 3rd Baron Saint John of Basing (died at Calais 18 Aug. 1347). After his death his 
2 CONC widow Elizabeth, married as her third husband Richard de Pembridge (or Pembrugge), KG, of Orwell, Cambridgeshire, Burgate and Lyndhurst, Hampshire, etc., Warden of the New Forest, Governor of Bamborough Castle, Governor of Dover C
2 CONC astle and Warden of the Cinque Ports, Chamberlain of the Household. She died 14–16 Sept. 1362. Warin (??? Surely should be Gerard de Lisle) had by his second wife Elizabeth le Strange two sons and one daughter:
2 CONT **Richard de Lisle
2 CONT **Henry de Lisle
2 CONT **Elizabeth de Lisle, who married Edmund de Stonor, Knight, of Stonor, in Pyrton, Oxfordshire.
2 CONT *In 1351 Philip Warde, formerly bailiff of Gerard de Lisle in the manor of Walberton, Sussex, owed him a debt of £10 7s.
2 CONT *From: http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gerard_de_Lisle,_1st_Baron_Lisle _______________
2 CONT *Links
2 CONT * https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Edmund_FitzAlan,_9th_Earl_of_Arundel
2 CONT * http://www.tudorplace.com.ar/FITZALAN.htm#Edmund FITZALAN (4º E. Arundel)
2 CONT * https://en.wikisource.org/wiki/Fitzalan,_Edmund_(DNB00)
2 CONT
2 CONT _______________________
