2 CONC ally located, it is not known that anyone—noble or peasant—attempted to rescue their king.  While negotiating a peace accord, he was at first held in the Savoy Palace, then at a variety of locations, including Windsor, Hertford, S
2 CONC omerton Castle in Lincolnshire, Berkhamsted Castle in Hertfordshire and briefly at King John's Lodge, formerly known as Shortridges, in East Sussex. A local tradition in St Albans is that he was held in a house in that town, at th
2 CONC e site of the 15th-century Fleur de Lys inn, before he was moved to Hertford. There is a sign on the inn to that effect, but apparently no evidence to confirm the tradition.[4] Eventually, John was taken to the Tower of London.  P
2 CONC risoner of the English: As a prisoner of the English, John was granted royal privileges, permitting him to travel about and to enjoy a regal lifestyle. At a time when law and order was breaking down in France and the government wa
2 CONC s having a hard time raising money for the defence of the realm, his account books during his captivity show that he was purchasing horses, pets, and clothes while maintaining an astrologer and a court band.  The Treaty of Brétign
2 CONC y (1360) set his ransom at 3 million crowns. Leaving his son Louis of Anjou in English-held Calais as a replacement hostage, John was allowed to return to France to raise the funds.  But all did not go according to plan. In July 1
2 CONC 363, King John was informed that Louis had escaped. Troubled by the dishonour of this, and the arrears in his ransom, John did something that shocked and dismayed his people: he announced that he would voluntarily return to captiv
2 CONC ity in England. His council tried to dissuade him, but he persisted, citing reasons of "good faith and honour." He sailed for England that winter and left the impoverished citizens of France again without a king.  John was greeted
2 CONC  in London 1364 with parades and feasts. A few months after his arrival, however, he fell ill with an unknown malady. He died at the Savoy in April 1364. His body was returned to France, where he was interred in the royal chambers
2 CONC  at Saint Denis Basilica.  Personality: John suffered from fragile health. He engaged little in physical activity, practised jousting rarely, and only occasionally hunted. Contemporaries report that he was quick to get angry and r
2 CONC esort to violence, leading to frequent political and diplomatic confrontations. He enjoyed literature, and was patron to painters and musicians. The image of a "warrior king" probably emerged from the courage in battle he showed a
2 CONC t Poitiers, and the creation of the Order of the Star. This was guided by political need as John was determined to prove the legitimacy of his crown–particularly as his reign, like that of his father, was marked by continuing disp
2 CONC utes over the Valois claim from both Charles II of Navarre and Edward III of England. From a young age, John was called to resist the de-centralising forces which impacted upon the cities and the nobility; each attracted either by
2 CONC  English economic influence or the reforming party. He grew up amongst intrigue and treason, and in consequence he governed in secrecy only with a close circle of trusted advisers.  He took as wife Bonne of Bohemia, and fathered 1
2 CONC 0 children, in eleven years. Due to his close relationship with Charles de la Cerda, rumors were spread by Charles II of Navarre of a romantic attachment between the two. La Cerda was given various honours and appointed to the hig
2 CONC h position of connetable when John became king; he accompanied the king on all his official journeys to the provinces. La Cerda's rise at court excited the jealousy of the French barons, several of whom stabbed him to death in 135
2 CONC 4. As such, La Cerda's fate paralleled that of Edward II of England's Piers Gaveston in England, and John II of Castile's Alvaro de Luna in Spain; the position of a royal favourite was a dangerous one. John's grief on La Cerda's d
2 CONC eath was overt and public.  Ancestry:
2 CONT
2 CONT Family and children:  On 28 July 1332, at the age of 13, John was married to Bonne of Bohemia (d. 1349), daughter of John I (the Blind) of Bohemia. Their children were:
2 CONT  1.Charles V (21 January 1338 – 16 September 1380)
2 CONT  2.Louis I, Duke of Anjou (23 July 1339 – 20 September 1384)
2 CONT  3.John, Duke of Berry (30 November 1340 – 15 June 1416)
2 CONT  4.Philip II, Duke of Burgundy (17 January 1342–27 April 1404)
2 CONT  5.Joan (24 June 1343 – 3 November 1373), married Charles II (the Bad) of Navarre
2 CONT  6.Marie (12 September 1344 – October 1404), married Robert I, Duke of Bar
2 CONT  7.Agnes (9 December 1345 – April 1350)
2 CONT  8.Margaret (20 September 1347 – 25 April 1352)
2 CONT  9.Isabelle of Valois (1 October 1348 – 11 September 1372), married Gian Galeazzo I, Duke of Milan  On 19 February 1350, at Nanterre, he married Joanna I of Auvergne (d. 1361), Countess of Auvergne and Boulogne. She was the widow 
2 CONC of Philip of Burgundy, the deceased heir of that duchy, and mother of the young Philip I, Duke of Burgundy (1344–61) who became John's stepson and ward. John and Joanna had three children, all of whom died young:
2 CONT  1.Blanche (b. November 1350)
2 CONT  2.Catherine (b. early 1352)
2 CONT  3.a son (b. early 1353)  He was succeeded by his son, Charles.
