2 FILE https://media.geni.com/p13/2d/59/4c/62/534448390200d5f6/johnofgaunt_original.jpg?hash=7c7bb0562f184f8bdbd80330d657310ffda2069005dc5899f26ea81b8f022d28.1795161599
2 FORM jpg
2 NOTE An anachronistic portrait of John of Gaunt, 1st Duke of Lancaster (1340–1399), Knight of the Garter and fourth-born but third surviving son of King Edward III of England. This painting is owned by his descendant, the Duke of Beauf
3 CONC ort, and currently hangs at Badminton House in Gloucestershire. It was commissioned around 1593 by Sir Edward Hoby (1560–1617) for display within Queenborough Castle, Kent. The likeness was likely based on John of Gaunt’s now-lost
3 CONC  tomb effigy formerly housed in Old St Paul’s Cathedral, London (see: Harris, Oliver D. (2010). "'Une tres riche sepulture': the tomb and chantry of John of Gaunt and Blanche of Lancaster in Old St Paul's Cathedral, London", *Jour
3 CONC nal of the Church Monuments Society*, Vol. 25, p. 16).
3 CONT
3 CONT Sir Edward Hoby, who became Constable of Queenborough Castle in 1597 and had already served as a Member of Parliament for Kent from 1592, ordered the portrait during this period. In the painting, Gaunt wears black armor, in the st
3 CONC yle favored by his elder brother Edward the Black Prince. His tabard displays the royal arms of Castile and León impaled with his differenced paternal royal arms of England. On his shield, designed in the Tudor style and encircled
3 CONC  by the Order of the Garter, the arms of Castile and León appear as an inescutcheon of pretence, symbolizing his dynastic claim to the Castilian throne through his second wife, Constance of Castile.
3 CONT
3 CONT The Latin inscription reads: *Johannes filius quartus Edwardi Tertii. Rex Castellae et Legione (sic, Legionis?) Dux Lancastriae Constabularius Caste(llae) de Queensbourg quinto Octobris Anno Regni Edwardi Tertii Anglia(e) 50o Fran
3 CONC ciae 37o*, which translates as: "John, fourth son of Edward III. King of Castile and León, Duke of Lancaster, Constable of the Castle of Queenborough, on the fifth of October in the fiftieth year of the reign of Edward III of Engl
3 CONC and (i.e. 1376), and the thirty-seventh of France."
