1 NOTE [FAVthomas.FTW]
2 CONT
2 CONT    Powerful opponent of King Richard II of England (ruled 1377/99). Theseventh son of King Edward III (ruled 1327/77), he was created Duke ofGloucester in 1385 and soon became the leader of a party opposed toRichard II, his young 
2 CONC nephew. In 1386 Gloucester and his associates,later known as the appellants, took virtual control of the king'sgovernment. Gloucester defeated one of Richard's favourites, Robert deVere, Duke of Ireland, at Radcot Bridge, Oxfordsh
2 CONC ire, in December 1387and then had a number of the king's friends executed. In 1389 Richardgained the upper hand and worked out a compromise with his enemies.Gloucester was made lieutenant of Ireland in 1392, but in 1397 Richardarr
2 CONC ested him and two other leading appellants. Committed to the charge ofThomas Mowbray, Earl of Nottingham (later Duke of Norfolk), at theEnglish port of Calais, France, Gloucester was murdered, possibly onorders from Richard. Accor
2 CONC ding to one of Mowbray's servants, who waslater executed for his part in the crime, the duke was suffocated with afeather bed.
2 CONT
2 CONT To cite this page: "Gloucester, Thomas of Woodstock, Duke of"Encyclopædia Britannica
2 CONT <http://www.britannica.com/eb/article?eu=37805&tocid=0&query=thomas%20of%20woodstock>
1 NOTE The youngest of 12 children, Thomas was named Guardian of the Kingdom at the age of 6 months old until his father returned from the French Wars. At the Age of 19, he married 8 year old, Lady Eleanor de Bohun. He served as Constabl
2 CONC e of England and was elected to Parliament. He is mostremembered for his plot to overthrow his corrupt nephew, Richard, and establish control in the reigns of government. He later opposed the marriage of King Richard to the infant
2 CONC  child, Isabella, Princess of France.Thomas was arrested and died under great suspension of suffocation. Atthe age of 42. After death, he was striped of his lands, titles, and monies.
