2 CONT
2 CONT http://www.genealogy.euweb.cz/italy/scalla2.html
2 CONT
2 CONT C1. [illegitimate] '''Guglielmo I''', Lord of Verona (17-28.4.1404), +poisoned, Verona 28.4.1404;  m.Onesta Mortone
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT -https://it.wikipedia.org/wiki/Guglielmo_della_Scala
2 CONT
2 CONT Guglielmo della Scala (English: William; died 1404) was the son of Cangrande II della Scala. He assassinated his father in 1359, but was edged out of power by his uncle Cansignorio.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1404, together with his two sons:
2 CONT
2 CONT # Brunoro and 
2 CONT # Antonio II, 
2 CONT
2 CONT he led a revolt against the Milanese. He was proclaimed Lord of Verona on 17 April, but was expelled from the city by the populace on 28 April. Francesco da Carrara, Lord of Padua, took over the city a few days later.
2 CONT
2 CONT By his marriage to Onesta Mortone, Guglielmo left the two aforementioned sons, who never regained power in Verona, and three younger sons:
2 CONT
2 CONT # Bartolomeo (died 21 March 1453), 
2 CONT # Fregnano (died 4 December 1443 at Vienna),
2 CONT #  Nicodemus, and 
2 CONT # Paul.
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT -https://www.treccani.it/enciclopedia/guglielmo-della-scala_(Dizionario-Biografico)
2 CONT
2 CONT DELLA SCALA, Guglielmo
2 CONT
2 CONT Illegitimate son of Cangrande (II), lord of Verona and Vicenza - who had no descendants from his wife Elizabeth of Bavaria -, he was probably born around 1350, a year indicatively indicated by the age of his father (born in 1332) 
2 CONC and the date of obtaining a canonical seat in the chapter of the cathedral of Verona (1356, according to Verci), to obtain which at least five-six years of age were required. With his brothers or half-brothers Tebaldo (also a cano
2 CONC n, since 1358) and Fregnano (or Fregnanino, sometimes confused with the homonymous, and better known son of Mastino [II]), D. is remembered in his father's will. Since 1355 - according to the chronicles - the latter had been conce
2 CONC rned about the fate of his three sons, making their stipends swear loyalty to them and imposing a burdensome tax in both the Verona and Vicenza districts with the aim of raising money in their favour. This money is mentioned in th
2 CONC e aforementioned will: Cangrande, while favoring Tebaldo and Fregnanino, to whom he left over 190,000 ducats in Venetian public debt securities, also allocated a substantial sum to the D., 60,000 ducats preserved in the castle of 
2 CONC Peschiera. Two very authoritative court men were appointed as guardians of D. and his brothers: Reguccio Pegolotti, a Florentine immigrant, and the factor Iacopo a Leone. A few days before his father's assassination, D. was also a
2 CONC ssigned a canonry in Vicenza (3 Dec. 1359). …
2 CONT
2 CONT From his wife, whose name and family are unknown, D. had nine children: Brunoro, Antonio, Nicodemo, Paolo, Fregnano, Bartolomeo, Oria, Chiara and Caterina (for the last six see at the bottom of the entry Della Scala, Anthony, in t
2 CONC his Dictionary).
2 CONT
2 CONT -------
