1 NOTE {geni:about_me} What is junker in german?
2 CONT Junker, (German: “country squire”), member of the landowning aristocracy of Prussia and eastern Germany, which, under the German Empire (1871–1918) and the Weimar Republic (1919–33), exercised substantial political power.
2 CONT
2 CONT See below:
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT Between Heinrich XLVI von Bünau and Janet Milburn (Schultz)
2 CONT
2 CONT Heinrich XLVI von Bünau
2 CONT Today at 5:16 AM
2 CONT Sehr geehrte Janet,
2 CONT
2 CONT Grundsätzlich: ein Adliger ohne Ritterschlag.
2 CONT Aber auch: ein Rittergutsbesitzer in ländlich geprägten Gebieten. Daher auch die abfällige Bezeichnung „Krautjunker“ oder auch Landjunker.
2 CONT
2 CONT Otto von Bismarck was an "Junker"
2 CONT
2 CONT Your translation is not quite correct - a Junker is a noble without accolade, a manor owner in a rural area. a Squire is of noble birth and serves his knight - a Junker serves nobody.
2 CONT
2 CONT Sorry for my English
2 CONT
2 CONT Sincerely
2 CONT Heinrich von Bünau
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT Janet Milburn (Schultz)
2 CONT yesterday at 7:45 PM
2 CONT Managers of Graf Ulrich "der Junker" von Württemberg,
2 CONT
2 CONT I am contacting you about this profile: https://www.geni.com/people/Graf-Ulrich-von-W%C3%BCrttemberg/600000...
2 CONT
2 CONT What is junker in german?
2 CONT Junker, (German: “country squire”), member of the landowning aristocracy of Prussia and eastern Germany, which, under the German Empire (1871–1918) and the Weimar Republic (1919–33), exercised substantial political power.
2 CONT Is this correct?
2 CONT
2 CONT Sincerely,
2 CONT
2 CONT Janet Milburn (Schultz)
2 CONT Graf Ulrich "der Junker" von Württemberg is our 18th great grandfather
2 CONT
2 CONT  Graf Ulrich "der Junker" von Württemberg
2 CONT ________________________________________________________________
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT _____________________________________________________________________
2 CONT
2 CONT - https://de.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ulrich_von_W%C3%BCrttemberg
2 CONT
2 CONT Ulrich von Württemberg (* after 1340 ; † August 23, 1388 at the Battle of Döffingen ) was the son of Count Eberhard II of Württemberg and Elisabeth von Henneberg-Schleusingen .
2 CONT
2 CONT life 
2 CONT In 1362 Ulrich married Elisabeth of Bavaria (* 1329; † 1402), daughter of Emperor Ludwig IV. From this marriage the later Count Eberhard III. from Württemberg. Various sources assume that there were other sons named Heinrich and U
2 CONC lrich and a daughter.
2 CONT
2 CONT After the death of his uncle Ulrich IV in 1366, he apparently ruled Württemberg together with his father Eberhard II. Both escaped an assassination attempt by Count Wolf von Eberstein in 1367 during a stay "in the Wildbad" (presum
2 CONC ably Wildbad or Teinach ) . In the time that followed, Eberhard oriented his policy strongly against the imperial cities that stood in the way of further expansion of Württemberg. In 1372, Württemberg fought the first battle again
2 CONC st the towns near Altheim, which was victorious. On May 14, 1377, Ulrich was at Achalm Castle with a large number of knights , as a mercenary from the Free Imperial City of Reutlingenundertook a raid on Württemberg territory. Ulri
2 CONC ch attacked them on their return march below the Achalm ( Battle of Reutlingen ). After heavy losses, Ulrich's team withdrew to the Achalm; he himself was wounded in battle. As early as 1380, shortly after its founding, Ulrich joi
2 CONC ned the Löwenbund , an association of nobility against the cities. Ulrich thus became a kind of link between the county of Württemberg and the lower nobility, who were in a position to front the cities. On August 23, 1388, the mil
2 CONC itary clashes between Württemberg and other nobles and the Swabian League of Towns , founded in 1376, broke out in the Battle of Döffingentheir peak. The vanguard of the noble army was led by Ulrich. This was almost destroyed in t
2 CONC he course of the battle and Ulrich fell. After the arrival of the main army led by his father, Württemberg was able to win the battle. In Ulrich's 500th year of death, a memorial stone was erected in his honor near Döffingen.
2 CONT
2 CONT Literature 
2 CONT Gerhard Raff : Hie good Wirtemberg all the way. Volume 1: The House of Württemberg from Count Ulrich the Founder to Duke Ludwig. 6th edition. Landhege, Schwaigern 2014, ISBN 978-3-943066-34-0 , pp. 181–188.
2 CONT Eugen Schneider :  Ulrich, Count of Württemberg . In: General German Biography (ADB). Volume 39, Duncker & Humblot, Leipzig 1895, p. 235.
2 CONT Web Links 
2 CONT Commons : Ulrich von Württemberg  - Collection of images, videos and audio files
