1 OCCU Heiress of Mompelgard Porrentruye Granges
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} also known as '''Henriette d'Orbe''' and '''Henrietta von Mömpelgard'''
2 CONT
2 CONT Henriette d'Orbe- Montfaucon ( 1385 - 1444 ) or Henriette de Montbéliard , countess of Montbéliard , brought, through her marriage to Eberhard IV of Württemberg , the county of Montbéliard to the house of Württemberg .
2 CONT
2 CONT Biography 
2 CONT She was the granddaughter of Étienne de Montfaucon , Count of Montbéliard (see the genealogy table in the article Richard III ). Étienne had married his eldest son Henri, Lord of Orbe , to Marie de Châtillon, Viscountess of Blaign
2 CONC y . Henriette d'Orbe-Montfaucon was the eldest of their four daughters.
2 CONT
2 CONT After the death (1394) of his wife (died in childbirth during the birth of the last daughter), Henri d'Orbe remarried with Béatrix von Furstemberg . From their union three children were born. Henri d'Orbe disappeared during the cr
2 CONC usade against Sultan Bazajet ( Battle of Nicopolis in September 1396). Her father, Étienne de Montfaucon , who no longer had a direct heir, betrothed Henrietta as a child to Eberhard IV of Württemberg (1397). The engagement took p
2 CONC lace at the Château de Montbéliard when her grandfather had just died. Henri, son of Jean II de la Roche-Saint-Hippolyte(fief neighboring the county of Montbéliard), was appointed guardian of the four daughters of Henri d'Orbe.
2 CONT
2 CONT Concerning Henriette's three sisters, Jeanne was engaged to Louis de Chalon-Arlay , son of the Prince of Orange, her cousin; she brought as a dowry the great barony of Montfaucon. Agnès was destined for Thiébaud VIII , lord of Neu
2 CONC fchatel-en-Bourgogne whose family held Blamont and the region of Pont de Roide. Finally, Marguerite was engaged to Humbert de Villersexel , the own son of Henri de la Roche-Saint-Hippolyte who held the four girls in guardianship. 
2 CONC All these marriages took place later, because all of them were still young.
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT Marriage of Count Eberhard IV and Henriette Countess of Montbéliard.
2 CONT Henriette d'Orbe's marriage to Eberhard IV of Württemberg took place in 1407, but the exact place and circumstances are unknown. However, it is difficult to deviate from Stuttgart. Henriette had brought her possessions in the wedd
2 CONC ing basket: the county of Montbéliard and its seigneuries; fiefs, such as Granges, Clerval, Passavant, Etobon , Porrentruy , with the fiefs of La Roche Saint-Hippolyte, as well as the lands of Franquemont (Goumois, on the edge of 
2 CONC the Swiss border). His inheritance was added to that of Württemberg which brought the seigneury of Riquewhir, Ferrette, and the county of Horbourg in Alsace.
2 CONT
2 CONT The County of Montbéliard was not a Württemberg vassalage ; he was hereditarily attached to that of Württemberg through the marriage of Henriette, while remaining his equal: he retained all his rights, his habits and customs, as w
2 CONC ell as his language (German was never imposed).
2 CONT
2 CONT Eberhard IV was, in fact, the first Württembergeois prince who became Count of Montbéliard . When he died in 1419, Henrietta continued to rule Württemberg and all its possessions alone.
2 CONT
2 CONT After being "deposed" from Württemberg by her two sons, she returned to the country of Montbéliard, and died in 1444 at the Château d' Étobon (near Héricourt, Haute-Saône) at the age of 57 . During the last years of her life, she 
2 CONC gave on more than one occasion proofs of her affection for the inhabitants of this small village, whose descendants kept an unalterable memory for a long time.
2 CONT
2 CONT Relatives  :
2 CONT
2 CONT Henri II de Montfaucon (1360-1396), son of Etienne de Montfaucon (1325-1397) and Marguerite de Chalon-Arlay (1333-1392);
2 CONT
2 CONT Marie de Châtillon (1365-1394), daughter of Gaucher de Châtillon (1330-1404), Viscount of Blaigny and Jeanne de Coucy.
2 CONT
2 CONT Descendants 
2 CONT Eberhard IV and Henriette had three children:
2 CONT
2 CONT Anne of Württemberg (1408-1471). His mother tried to assign him the county of Montbéliard in her will. His brothers violently opposed it. Anne married Count Philip von Katzenelnbogen in 1422.
2 CONT Louis IV of Württemberg (1412-1454)
2 CONT Ulrich V of Württemberg (1413-1480).
2 CONT Caption: Christmas and others 
2 CONT The legend will make her the replacement of Father Christmas and Saint Nicolas under the name of Aunt Arie or Airie in the Pays de Montbéliard. Many tales or legends have been written about him.
2 CONT
2 CONT Source: Wikipedia
2 CONT Added by Janet Milburn 1/3/23
2 CONT She is our 16th great grandmother.
2 CONT
