2 CONT      Charles VI, Roi de France was born on 3 December 1368 at Paris, France. He was the son of Charles V, Roi de France and Jeanne de Bourbon.1 He married Isabelle von Bayern, daughter of Stefan III Herzog von Bayern-Ingolstadt an
2 CONC d Thaddea Visconti, on 17 July 1385. He died on 22 October 1422 at age 53 at Paris, France. He was buried at Saint-Denis, Île-de-France, France.
2 CONT      Charles VI, Roi de France was a member of the House of Valois.1 Charles VI, Roi de France also went by the nick-name of Charles 'the Beloved'.1 He succeeded to the title of Roi Charles VI de France in 1380.1 Children of Charl
2 CONC es VI, Roi de France and Isabelle von Bayern 1.Marie de Valois 2.Michelle de France 3.Isabelle de France+ b. 9 Nov 1387, d. 13 Sep 1409 4.Jeanne de France+ b. 1391, d. 1433 5.Louis de France, Dauphin de France b. 1396, d. 1415 6.J
2 CONC ean de France, Duc de Touraine b. 1398 7.Catherine de France+ b. 27 Oct 1401, d. 3 Jan 1437 8.Charles VII, Roi de France+1 b. 22 Feb 1403, d. 21 Jul 1461 9.Philippe de Valois b. 1407 Citations 1.[S38] John Morby, Dynasties of the 
2 CONC World: a chronological and genealogical handbook (Oxford, Oxfordshire, U.K.: Oxford University Press, 1989), page 78. Hereinafter cited as Dynasties of the World.
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Charles_VI_of_France
2 CONT
2 CONT Charles VI of France From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia Charles VI the Beloved Charles VI de France - Dialogues de Pierre Salmon - Bib de Genève MsFr165f4.jpg Charles VI of France by the painter known as the Master of Boucicaut
2 CONC  (1412). King of France Reign	16 September 1380 – 21 October 1422 Coronation	4 November 1380 Predecessor	Charles V Successor	Charles VII or Henry VI (disputed) Regents	 See[show] Born	3 December 1368 Paris, France Died	21 October 
2 CONC 1422 (aged 53) Paris, France Burial	Saint Denis Basilica Spouse	Isabeau of Bavaria Issue among others...	Isabella, Queen of England Joan, Duchess of Brittany Marie, Prioress of Poissy Michelle, Duchess of Burgundy Louis, Dauphin o
2 CONC f Viennois John, Dauphin of Viennois Catherine, Queen of England Charles VII of France House	Valois Father	Charles V of France Mother	Joan of Bourbon Religion	Roman Catholicism Charles VI (3 December 1368 – 21 October 1422), calle
2 CONC d the Beloved (French: le Bien-Aimé) and the Mad (French: le Fol or le Fou), was King of France from 1380 to his death. He was a member of the House of Valois.
2 CONT
2 CONT Charles VI was only 11 when he inherited the throne in the midst of the Hundred Years' War. The government was entrusted to his four uncles: Philip the Bold, Duke of Burgundy; John, Duke of Berry; Louis I, Duke of Anjou; and Louis
2 CONC  II, Duke of Bourbon. Although the royal age of majority was fixed at 14, the dukes maintained their grip on Charles until he took power at the age of 21.
2 CONT
2 CONT During the rule of his uncles, the financial resources of the kingdom, painstakingly built up by his father, Charles V, were squandered for the personal profit of the dukes, whose interests were frequently divergent or even oppose
2 CONC d. As royal funds drained, new taxes had to be raised, which caused several revolts.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1388 Charles VI dismissed his uncles and brought back to power his father's former advisers, known as the Marmousets. Political and economic conditions in the kingdom improved significantly, and Charles earned the epithet "the 
2 CONC Beloved". But in August 1392 en route to Brittany with his army in the forest of Le Mans, Charles suddenly went mad and slew four knights and almost killed his brother, Louis of Orléans.[1]
2 CONT
2 CONT From then on, Charles' bouts of insanity became more frequent and of longer duration. During these attacks, he had delusions, believing he was made of glass or denying he had a wife and children.[1] He could also attack servants o
2 CONC r run until exhaustion, wailing that he was threatened by his enemies. Between crises, there were intervals of months during which Charles was relatively sane.[1] However, unable to concentrate or make decisions, political power w
2 CONC as taken away from him by the princes of the blood, which would cause much chaos and conflict in France.
2 CONT
2 CONT A fierce struggle for power developed between Louis of Orléans, the king's brother, and John the Fearless, Duke of Burgundy, the son of Philip the Bold. When John instigated the murder of Louis in November 1407, the conflict degen
2 CONC erated into a civil war between the Armagnacs (supporters of the House of Valois) and the Burgundians. John offered large parts of France to King Henry V of England, who was still at war with the Valois monarchy, in exchange for h
2 CONC is support. After the assassination of John the Fearless, his son Philip the Good led Charles the Mad to sign the infamous Treaty of Troyes (1420), which disinherited his offspring and recognized Henry V as his legitimate successo
2 CONC r on the throne of France.
2 CONT
2 CONT When Charles VI died, he was succeeded by his son Charles VII, who found the Valois cause in a desperate situation.
2 CONT
