1 NOTE {geni:about_me} http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Isabella_of_Valois
2 CONT
2 CONT For other people named Isabella of Valois, see Isabella of Valois (disambiguation).
2 CONT Isabella of Valois
2 CONT Duchess of Orléans
2 CONT
2 CONT Queen consort of England
2 CONT Tenure	31 October 1396 – 29 September 1399
2 CONT Coronation	8 January 1397
2 CONT Born	9 November 1389
2 CONT Paris, France
2 CONT Died	13 September 1409 (aged 19)
2 CONT Blois, Loir-et-Cher, France
2 CONT Burial	Abbey of Saint Laumer of Blois; reinterred in the Couvent des Célestins, Paris, France
2 CONT Spouse	
2 CONT Richard II of England
2 CONT ​
2 CONT ​(m. 1396; died 1400)​
2 CONT Charles, Duke of Orléans
2 CONT ​
2 CONT ​(m. 1406)​
2 CONT Issue	Joan, Duchess of Alençon
2 CONT House	Valois
2 CONT Father	Charles VI of France
2 CONT Mother	Isabeau of Bavaria
2 CONT Isabella of Valois (9 November 1389 – 13 September 1409) was Queen of England as the wife of Richard II, King of England, between 1396 and 1399, and Duchess of Orléans as the wife of Charles, Duke of Orléans, from 1406 until her d
2 CONC eath in 1409. She had been born a princess of France as the daughter of King Charles VI and Isabeau of Bavaria.
2 CONT
2 CONT Life
2 CONT Isabella was born on 9 November 1389 in Paris, France, as the third child and second daughter of Charles VI, King of France, and his wife, Isabeau of Bavaria.[1] Her eldest sibling had already died by the time of her birth, and th
2 CONC e second-eldest died the following year; however, she had nine younger siblings, seven of whom survived infancy. Five of her younger siblings were born after Isabella had already been married off to England, and one of them died w
2 CONC hile she was still there.[2]
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1396 negotiations started about marrying six-year-old Isabella to the widower Richard II, King of England (1367–1400), who was 22 years her senior, to ensure peace between their countries. Isabella told the English envoys (who 
2 CONC described her as pretty) that she was happy to be Queen of England as she had been told that this would make her a great lady.[3] She also started practicing for the role.
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT Richard and Isabella on their wedding day in 1396. He was 29 years old; she was six.
2 CONT Richard travelled to Paris for his bride, where great festivities were held. Afterwards, the court and the English guests went to Calais where the wedding ceremony was performed on 31 October 1396, but would not be consummated at 
2 CONC least until the bride's twelfth birthday.[4][5]
2 CONT
2 CONT A tearful Princess Isabelle, dressed in a blue velvet dress sewn with golden fleurs de lys and wearing a diadem of gold and pearls, was carried by the Dukes of Berry and Burgundy to Richard’s pavilion. She was taken away by a dele
2 CONC gation of English ladies led by the Duchesses of Lancaster and Gloucester. Four days later, on 4 November 1396, she was brought to the church of St. Nicholas in Calais where Richard married her. She was five days short of her seve
2 CONC nth birthday. Her dolls were included in her trousseau.[6]
2 CONT
2 CONT Queen of England
2 CONT After the wedding, Isabella went to England and was moved into Windsor Castle in Berkshire. She had her own court, supervised by a governess and chief lady-in-waiting, Madame de Coucy (later replaced by Lady Mortimer). She was mad
2 CONC e a Lady of Garter in 1396 and was crowned Queen of England in Westminster Abbey in 1397.
2 CONT
2 CONT In spite of their age difference and the marriage being politically arranged, Isabella and Richard developed a mutually respectful relationship. Due to the age of Isabella (the Canonical law for sexual consummation being twelve), 
2 CONC and due to Richard's death just three years later, the marriage was never sexually consummated. However, Isabella and Richard enjoyed a good platonic relationship, which has been compared to that between a father and his adopted d
2 CONC aughter or between a niece and a doting uncle. He was noted to have treated her not as a wife but rather as the daughter he and his first wife Anne never had. The king regularly visited her in Windsor, where she was tutored by Mar
2 CONC garet de Courcy. He treated her with respect, entertained her and her ladies-in-waiting with humorous conversation, and pampered her with gifts and toys. Isabella reportedly enjoyed and looked forward to these visits.[citation nee
2 CONC ded]
2 CONT
2 CONT By May 1399 the Queen had been moved to Portchester Castle for protection while Richard went on a military campaign in Ireland. Before he departed, he reportedly visited her, kissed her hand and promised he would let her come to I
2 CONC reland soon. In June, Isabella's uncle, Louis I, Duke of Orléans (1372–1407) took power in France from her mentally troubled father. He decided that a peaceful relationship with England was no longer important or desirable, and le
2 CONC t Henry Bolingbroke (1367–1413), Richard's cousin and rival, return to England. Henry's declared goal was to regain the lands of his father, John of Gaunt, Duke of Lancaster (1340–1399) who had died in February of that year, promp
2 CONC ting King Richard to cancel the act by which Henry would have inherited his lands automatically.[citation needed]
2 CONT
2 CONT Many of England's lords supported Henry, who started a military campaign and took the country without much resistance, taking advantage of Richard being in Ireland. Edmund of Langley, Duke of York, Keeper of the Realm and an uncle
2 CONC  of both Richard and Henry, eventually also sided with the rebels. He moved Isabella first to Wallingford Castle, then to Leeds Castle. On 19 August, Richard surrendered, and he was imprisoned in London on 1 September. On 13 Octob
2 CONC er 1399, Henry was crowned king. Isabella was confined at Sonning Bishop's Palace (residence of the Bishop of Salisbury).[citation needed] After the unsuccessful attempt to murder Henry IV and his sons during the failed Epiphany R
2 CONC ising where the conspirators had gone to Sonning to meet with Isabella, she was moved to Essex and held under heavier guard at Havering Palace.
2 CONT
2 CONT On or around 14 February 1400, Richard died under mysterious circumstances, possibly of starvation. The French court requested that his widow be returned to France, but Henry IV wanted her to marry his son and heir, Henry of Monmo
2 CONC uth (1386–1422). Isabella refused his demands and went into mourning for her late husband. In May 1401 Henry IV promised to return Isabella to France with her jewels and her property. She was finally allowed to return to France on
2 CONC  21 July 1401, but Henry IV kept her dowry, which she was supposed to get back if the marriage was never consummated.[7] The same year, marriage negotiations were started for a match between Prince Henry and Catherine of Pomerania
2 CONC  instead.
2 CONT
2 CONT Duchess of Orléans
2 CONT In 1406, when the marriage negotiations between the Prince of Wales and Catherine of Pomerania had been terminated, Henry IV repeated his suggestion that Isabella should marry his son, but was refused by the French court. In 1420,
2 CONC  Henry's son married Isabella's sister, Catherine of Valois.[8]
2 CONT
2 CONT On 29 June 1406 Isabella, aged 16, married her paternal cousin, Charles of Orléans (1394–1465), aged 11,[9] who became Duke of Orléans in 1407 following the assassination of his father. Isabella died in childbirth on 13 September 
2 CONC 1409 at the age of 19. Her daughter, Joan of Valois (1409–1432) survived and married John II, Duke of Alençon (1409–1476) in 1424.[10]
2 CONT
2 CONT Isabella was buried in Blois, in the Abbey of Saint Laumer of Blois,[11] where her body was discovered in 1624, wrapped in bands of linen plated with mercury. Her remains were then transferred to the Couvent des Célestins (Convent
2 CONC  of the Celestines) in Paris, the second most important burial site for French royalty, which was desecrated during the French Revolution.
2 CONT
2 CONT http://genealogics.org/getperson.php?personID=I00005728&tree=LEO
