2 CONT Descent from William the Conqueror with further Descents to Henry VI and Henry VII In historical fiction[edit] William Shakespeare's play Henry V depicts Catherine of Valois' marriage to Henry V of England after the Battle of Agin
2 CONC court. Catherine of Valois is the subject of Rosemary Hawley Jarman's novel Crown in Candlelight (1978) Margaret Frazer's medieval mystery "The Boy's Tale" (1995) features Catherine and her sons Edmund and Jasper. In The Queen's S
2 CONC ecret by Jean Plaidy, Catherine is the title character. In the historical novel, Fortune Made His Sword by Martha Rofheart, Catherine is one of five narrators who tell the story of Henry V. Dedwydd Jones's novel,The Lily and the D
2 CONC ragon (2002) tells the story of Owain Tudor and Catherine of Valois. Joanna Hickson's novel The Agincourt Bride (2013) tells the story of the early life of Catherine of Valois. Anne O'Brien's novel The Forbidden Queen (2013) detai
2 CONC ls the life of Catherine of Valois. Vanora Bennett's novel Blood Royal/The Queen's Lover (2009) tells the story of Catherine's early years through her secret marriage to Owen Tudor. Mari Griffith's novel "Root of the Tudor Rose"(2
2 CONC 014) is the story of Catherine's brief marriage to Henry V and her subsequent clandestine relationship with Owain ap Maredydd ap Tudur. Rosemary Anne Sisson's play "The Queen and the Welshman" (1957) tells the story of Catherine d
2 CONC e Valois and Owen Tudor. References[edit] Jump up ^ Europäisch Stammtafeln Band II tafel 63. says she died on 3 January 1438 Jump up ^ The Cambridge historical encyclopedia of Great Britain and Ireland, Ed. Christopher Haigh, (Cam
2 CONC bridge University Press, 2000) , 345. Jump up ^ Williams, Neville and Antonia Fraser, The Tudors, (University of California Press, 2000), 19. Jump up ^ Historical dictionary of late medieval England, 1272–1485, Editors Ronald H. F
2 CONC ritze, William Baxter Robison, (Greenwood Publishing Group, 1992), 94. Jump up ^ Strickland, Agnes (1840). Queens of England. London: Su Stackland. p. 264. Jump up ^ Gibbon, Rachel (December 1996). "Isabeau of Bavaria, Queen of Fr
2 CONC ance (1385-1422): The Creation of an Historical Villainess: The Alexander Prize Essay". Transactions of the Royal Historical Society. 6: 51–63. doi:10.2307/3679229. Jump up ^ Boutell, Charles (1863). "A Manual of Heraldry, Histori
2 CONC cal and Popular". London: Winsor & Newton: 276 Jump up ^ Fields, Bertram (1998). Royal Blood: Richard III and the Mystery of the Princes. New York: Regan Books. ISBN 0-06-039269-X. External links[edit]
