2 CONT
2 CONT Adelaide of Susa (also Adelheid, Adelais, or Adeline; ca. 1014/1020 – 19 December 1091[1]) was the Marchioness of Turin from 1034 to her death. She moved the seat of the march from Turin to Susa and settled the itinerant court the
2 CONC re. She was the last of the Arduinici.
2 CONT
2 CONT Born in Turin to Ulric Manfred II and Bertha, daughter of Oberto II around 1014/1020, Adelaide's early life is not well-known. Her only brother predeceased her father in 1034, though she had two younger sisters, Immilla and Bertha
2 CONC . Thus, on Ulric's death, the great margraviate was divided between his three daughters, though the greatest part by far went to Adelaide. She received the counties of Ivrea, Auriate, Aosta, and Turin. The margravial title, howeve
2 CONC r, had primarily a military purpose at the time and, thus, was not suitable for a woman.
2 CONT
2 CONT Conrad II, Holy Roman Emperor, therefore arranged a marriage between Adelaide and Herman IV, Duke of Swabia, to serve as margrave of Turin after Ulric's death (1034). The two were married in January 1037, but Herman died of the pl
2 CONC ague while fighting at Naples in July 1038.[2]
2 CONT
2 CONT Adelaide remarried in order to secure her vast march to Henry of Montferrat (1041), but he died in 1045 and left her a widow for the second time. Immediately, a third marriage was undertaken, this time to Otto of Savoy (1046). Wit
2 CONC h Otto she had three sons, Peter I, Amadeus II, and Otto. She also had two daughters, Bertha and Adelaide. Bertha, the countess of Maurienne, married the Henry IV, Holy Roman Emperor, while Adelaide married Rudolf of Rheinfeld, wh
2 CONC o opposed Henry as King of Germany.
2 CONT
2 CONT After 1060, Adelaide acted as regent for her sons. In 1068, Henry tried to divorce Bertha and consequently drove Adelaide to an intense hatred of him and his family. However, through the intervention of Bertha, Henry received Adel
2 CONC aide's support when he came to Italy to submit to Pope Gregory VII and Matilda of Tuscany at Canossa. Adelaide and Amadeus accompanied the humiliated emperor to Canossa. In gratitude for her mediation, Henry donated Bugey to Adela
2 CONC ide and her family and took back Bertha as his wife, returning to Germany.
2 CONT
2 CONT Adelaide later played the mediator between her two royal sons-in-law, Henry and the aforementioned Rudolf during the wars of the 1080s in Germany. She was an opponent of the Gregorian reform, though she honoured the papacy, and de
2 CONC fender of the autonomy of abbacies.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1091, Adelaide died, to the general mourning of her people, and was buried in the parochial church of Canischio (Canisculum), a small village on the Cuorgnè in the Valle dell'Orco, to which she had retired in her later years.[3
2 CONC ] In the cathedral of Susa, in a niche in the wall, there is a statue of walnut wood, beneath a bronze veneer, representing Adelaide, genuflecting in prayer. Above it can be read the inscription: Questa è Adelaide, cui l'istessa R
2 CONC oma Cole, e primo d'Ausonia onor la noma.
2 CONT
2 CONT Adelaide had passed her childhood amongst the retainers of her father and had even learned the martial arts when young, bearing her own arms and armour. She was reputed to be beautiful and virtuous. She was pious, putting eternal 
2 CONC things ahead of temporal. Strong in temperament, she did not hesitate to punish even the bishops and grandees of her realm. She patronised the minstrels and always received them at her court, urging them to compose songs emphasisi
2 CONC ng religious values. She was a founder of cloisters and monasteries that transmitted the history of the region. The only failure of Adelaide's career was the loss of the County of Albon. Greatly admired in her own time, she was co
2 CONC mpared to Deborah of Biblical fame and was known affectionately as the "marchioness of the Italians." Peter Damian summed up her life and career in the admiring words:
2 CONT
2 CONT “ 	Tu, senza l'aiuto di un re, sostieni il peso del regno, ed a te ricorrono quelli che alle loro decisioni desiderano aggiungere il peso di una sentenza legale. Dio onnipotente benedica te ed i tuoi figlioli d'indole regia.
2 CONT
2 CONT You, without the help of a king, sustain the weight of a kingdom, and to you return those who wish to add to their decisions the weight of legal pronouncement. Omnipotent God bless you and your regal children. 	”
2 CONT
2 CONT [edit] Children
2 CONT
2 CONT Adelaide and Herman IV, Duke of Swabia had at least three children:
2 CONT
2 CONT     * Gebhard I, Count of Sulzbach
2 CONT
2 CONT     * Adalbert I, Count of Windberg
2 CONT
2 CONT     * Adelaide, married Hermann von Peugen
2 CONT
2 CONT Adelaide and Otto of Savoy had five children:
2 CONT
2 CONT     * Peter I of Savoy
2 CONT
2 CONT     * Amedeus II of Savoy
2 CONT
2 CONT     * Otto, Bishop of Asti
2 CONT
2 CONT     * Bertha of Savoy, married Henry IV, Holy Roman Emperor
2 CONT
2 CONT     * Adelaide (died 1080), married Rudolf von Rheinfeld
2 CONT
