1 OCCU Duchess of Auvergne, Daughter of Jean I "Duke de Berry"
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} '''Marie de Berry (c. 1375 – June 1434) was suo jure Duchess of Auvergne and Countess of Montpensier in 1416–1434. She was the daughter of''' John, Duke of Berry, and Joanna of Armagnac. '''She was married three ti
2 CONC mes. She acted as administrator of the Duchy of Bourbon for her third spouse John I, Duke of Bourbon, during his imprisonment in England after he was captured following the French defeat at the Battle of Agincourt in 1415, until 1
2 CONC 434.'''
2 CONT Life
2 CONT '''Marie was born about the year 1375,[1][2] the youngest daughter''' of John "the Magnificent", Duke of Berry and Joanna of Armagnac. '''Through her father, a great collector of antiquities, art patron and bibliophile, she was a 
2 CONC granddaughter''' of King John II of France. '''She had three brothers''', Charles, Louis, and John; and one older sister, Bonne.
2 CONT '''Countess consort of Châtillon'''
2 CONT '''The first of Marie's three marriages[3] took place on 29 May 1386 in the Cathedral of Saint-Etienne at Bourges: aged about 11, Marie married Louis III de Châtillon as her first husband. Marie's father gave her a dowry of 70,000
2 CONC  francs; he gave Louis, his son-in-law, the county of Dunois.[4] The marriage and trousseau had been arranged by the two fathers in 1384: "A Duke will dress her, in bed and out of it, and a Count will put the jewels on her",[citat
2 CONC ion needed]''' John Duke of Berry and Guy Count de Blois-Châtillon agreed. '''The festivities at the wedding de ces jeunes enfants ("of these young children") are described in Jean Froissart's Chronicles'''.[5]
2 CONT '''Countess of Eu'''
2 CONT '''There were no children from this marriage''', and Louis died on 15 July 1391. '''On 27 January 1393 a marriage contract was drawn up for Marie and Philip of Artois, Count of Eu. They were married the next month at the Palais du
2 CONC  Louvre in Paris; King Charles VI of France himself paid for the festivities, while her father gave her a dowry of 70,000 francs. They had two sons and two daughters'''. The King appointed Philip Constable in 1392. Philippe went o
2 CONC n Crusade and fought alongside his friend Jean Le Maingre ("Boucicaut"), marshal of France at the disastrous Battle of Nicopolis on 25 September 1396. Both were captured, and Philip died some months later in captivity at Micalizo,
2 CONC  now called Mihaliççik, in western Turkey.[6]
2 CONT After Philippe's death inter Sarracenos ("among the Saracens"), his body was brought back to Eu, his home town, and '''Marie gave an endowment of £100 annually to the Collegial Church of Notre-Dame-et-Saint-Laurent for a mass to b
2 CONC e celebrated there on 17 June each year in his memory.[7] Their eldest son''', Philippe, died on 23 December in the same year and is also buried in Eu.[citation needed] '''Jointly with her widowed sister-in-law''', Jeanne of Thoua
2 CONC rs,''' Marie was appointed guardian of the three surviving children of her marriage with Philippe''': Charles, Bonne and Catherine. Aged about three, Charles succeeded his father as Count of Eu. His revenues were held for him unti
2 CONC l he came of age by three trustees: '''Marie herself''', her father, and her uncle Philip the Bold, Duke of Burgundy.[1]
2 CONT '''Duchess consort of Bourbon'''
2 CONT '''Marie married her third husband John of Bourbon at the "King's Palace" (the Palais de la Cité) in Paris on 21 June 1401. The contract had been signed at Paris on 27 May 1400 after complex negotiations.[8][9] They had three chil
2 CONC dren'''.[citation needed] He was appointed Grand Chamberlain of France on 18 March 1408 and succeeded his father as Duke of Bourbon on 19 August 1410. Marie's father had persuaded King Charles VI not to fight at the battle of Agin
2 CONC court on 25 October 1415, '''but Marie's husband did fight, was captured, and spent the rest of his life in English captivity.'''
2 CONT '''Marie is believed to be depicted in one or possibly two full-page miniatures in the Très Riches Heures du Duc de Berry, a lavishly-illustrated manuscript made for her father in the years after his sons' deaths. In the illustrat
2 CONC ion for the month of April, the young noblemen and women in the foreground are grouped around a couple agreeing to be married. According to Patricia Stirnemann, referencing Saint-Jean Boudin, the scene, although not painted until 
2 CONC about 1410, depicts the engagement of Marie with John of Bourbon in 1401.[10] Raymond Cazelles disputes this, arguing that the couple are Marie's niece''' Bonne of Armagnac and Charles, Duke of Orléans, who were to marry in 1411.[
2 CONC 11] A May Day celebration among nobles takes place in the foreground of the illustration for the month of May. Details appear to confirm that the house of Bourbon is represented.[12] '''Both Cazelles and Stirnemann believe that th
2 CONC e woman seen in the foreground, riding on a white horse and wearing a large white headdress, is Marie on the occasion of her marriage on 21 June 1401. These scholars do not agree as to which of the accompanying men is John of Bour
2 CONC bon.[10][11] The buildings in the background have been variously identified, but G. Papertiant suggests that they are the Châtelet, Conciergerie and Tour de l'Horloge in Paris, and at the centre the Palais de la Cité where Marie's
2 CONC  wedding took place'''.[13]
2 CONT '''A manuscript made for Marie's own use and presented to her in 1406 survives today as BnF, fr. 926. It is a short collection of Christian devotional works, beginning with Bonaventure's Stimulus amoris, translated into French by 
2 CONC Simon de Courcy as the Traitieé de l'esguillon d'amour divine. It includes a miniature of Marie and her daughter''' Bonne (aged about ten at this time) '''kneeling in prayer before the Virgin Mary.[14] The manuscript is now in the
2 CONC  Bibliothèque nationale de France.[15] Marie also selected approximately 40 manuscripts from among her father's collection on his death, as she was still owed the 70,000 franc dowry from her second marriage.[16]
2 CONT '''Duchess of Auvergne and Countess of Montpensier'''
2 CONT All three of Marie's brothers were dead before 1400,[citation needed] which explains the complexity of the negotiations for her third marriage: she and her elder sister Bonne were to be heirs to John of Berry's titles, which requi
2 CONC red royal assent'''.[8][17] John of Berry died on 15 June 1416 ('''by which time Marie's husband was already a prisoner in England). Marie was accordingly appointed Duchess of Auvergne and Countess of Montpensier on 26 April 1418;
2 CONC  these titles were confirmed in 1425. On 17 January 1421, her husband appointed her administrator of his estates as well.[citation needed] He died a prisoner in London in January 1434.[citation needed] Marie died in Lyon on an unk
2 CONC nown date in June of the same year. She was buried at Souvigny Priory'''.[citation needed]
2 CONT '''Family'''
2 CONT '''Louis III de Châtillon'''[2]
2 CONT '''Philip of Artois, Count of Eu''' (1358–1397)[2]
2 CONT *Philippe (died 23 December 1397)
2 CONT *Charles of Artois, Count of Eu (c. 1394–1472)
2 CONT *Bonne (1396–1425), who married firstly Philip Count of Nevers (1389–1415), youngest son of Philip the Bold, Duke of Burgundy; and secondly Philip the Good, Duke of Burgundy (1396–1467).
2 CONT *Catherine (1397–1422?), who married John of Bourbon, Lord of Carency.
2 CONT '''John I, Duke of Bourbon[18]'''
2 CONT *Charles I, Duke of Bourbon (1401–1456)
2 CONT *Louis (1403–1412, Paris), Count of Forez
2 CONT *Louis I, Count of Montpensier (1405–1486)
2 CONT https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Marie,_Duchess_of_Auvergne
