1 OCCU Dauphine of Vienne, Dauphin de Viennois, Comte de Gap et d'Embrun
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} - https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Guigues_VII_of_Viennois
2 CONT
2 CONT Guigues VII (1225–1269), of the House of Burgundy, was the dauphin of Vienne and count of Albon, Grenoble, Oisans, Briançon, Embrun, and Gap from 1237 to his death. He was the son of Andrew Guigues VI and Beatrice of Montferrat. W
2 CONC hen his father died, his mother helped guide the leadership of the new Dauphin.[1]
2 CONT
2 CONT He fought great contests over the counties of Embrun and Gap with Charles, Count of Provence and Forcalquier. Those counties were the dowry of his father's first wife, Beatrice of Forcalquier. Guigues VI had repudiated his first w
2 CONC ife and married a second, giving the counties of his former wife to the son of his second: Guigues VII. Charles, as heir to the county of Forcalquier, claimed the counties. A compromise was eventually reached whereby Guigues retai
2 CONC ned Embrun, but Charles received Gap.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1239, the young Dauphin entered negotiations to marry Cecile of Baux with the help of his ally, Raymond VII of Toulouse. Cecile's father, Barral later claimed that he only negotiated the engagement under threats against his lif
2 CONC e. In 1241, he became engaged to Beatrice of Savoy, daughter of Peter of Savoy and Agnes of Faucigny. Peter's brother, Philip agreed to help the Dauphin fight the count of Valentinois.[2]
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1247, Guigues was summoned by Frederick II, Holy Roman Emperor to join the army he was assembling in Savoy. This army was being gathered to attack the Pope in Lyon, though ultimately Frederick was distracted by other battles in
2 CONC  Italy.[3]
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1253, Guigues married Beatrice (1237–1310). For her dowry, Beatrice brought Guigues Faucigny, a distant territory menacing Savoy, the traditional enemy of the Viennois. Many wars of Guigues' descendants would be aimed at the de
2 CONC fence of this land from the Savoyard counts. Guigues had two sons and a daughter:
2 CONT
2 CONT * John I (1264–1282), his successor
2 CONT * Andrew (1267 – c. 1270)
2 CONT * Anne (1255–1298), later successor, married in 1273 to Humbert de la Tour du Pin
2 CONT '''
2 CONT Naming comment''':
2 CONT
2 CONT The numeral Guigues VII of Viennois is from later historians.  His father was never known as Guigues VI in his lifetime.
2 CONT
2 CONT ----------
2 CONT
2 CONT *http://fmg.ac/Projects/MedLands/burgkvien.htm#GuiguesVIIdied1269
2 CONT
2 CONT 3.      '''   GUIGUES de Viennois ''' (1225-Aug 1269, bur Chartreuse Abbaye de Prémol).  The Aymari Rivalli De Allobrogibus names "Guigonem quartum" as son of "Andreas Delphinus" and his wife "Beatricem, Guillermi marchionis Monti
2 CONC sferrati filiam"[322].  The testament of "Dom. Andreas Dalphinus Viennensis et Albonis comitis" is dated 4 Mar 1236, names as his heir "Guigonem filium suum", and appoints "Beatricem uxorem suam comitissam matris eiusdem Guigonis"
2 CONC  as his tutor[323].  He succeeded his father in 1237 as Comte d’Albon, Dauphin, Comte de Gap et d'Embrun, under the regency of his mother.  "G. dalphinus Viennensis et Albonis comes" confirmed donations to Léoncel by "A. pater nos
2 CONC ter" by charter dated 22 Jul 1251, which names "nobilis vir quondam Gucelinus de Roians Poncio quondam abbati dicti loci"[324].  The testament of "Guigo Dalphinus, Vienn. et Albonis comitis", dated 27 Jun 1267, appointed "Johannem
2 CONC  filium meum" as his heir, made bequests to "Annam et Catharinam filias meas…Beatrix uxor mea", and confirmed donations made by "…matertera Margarita vel Domina Ducissa avia mea…meæ amitæ quondam comitissæ Sabaudiæ"[325].  "Guigo 
2 CONC Dalphinus Viennensis et Albonensis comes ac dominus Fucigniaci" confirmed privileges granted to Grenoble Saint-Robert by “domini Andreæ Dalphini...patris nostri et dominæ Beatricis Burgundiæ ducissæ aviæ nostræ” by charter dated 1
2 CONC 0 May 1269[326].  
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Betrothed''' (contract [Jan/May] 1240) to CECILE des Baux, daughter of BARRAL Seigneur de Baux & his wife Béatrice d'Anduze (-21 May 1275).  An agreement dated 1240 between "Raymond VII Comte de Toulouse" and "Guigues Comte du 
2 CONC Viennois" refers to the marriage of the latter to "Cécile sa nièce, fille de Barral de Baux"[327].  This betrothal is confirmed by the judgment dated 10 Mar 1261 under which the marriage between "Vienna e Beatrice figlia di Pietro
2 CONC  di Savoia" was confirmed as valid despite a prior verbal agreement between "detto Delfino e Cecilia di Beaux figlia del Conte Barallo di Beaux, ed indi Moglie d'Amedeo quarto Conte di Savoia"[328].  
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Betrothed''' (contract before Jun 1240, annulled Jun 1240) to SANCHA de Provence, daughter of RAYMOND BERENGER IV Comte de Provence & his wife Beatrix de Savoie (Aix-en-Provence [1225]-Berkhamstead Castle, Buckinghamshire 5 or 
2 CONC 9 Nov 1261, bur Hayles Abbey, Gloucestershire).  A charter dated Jun 1240 records the episcopal annulment of the betrothal of "Guigonem Dalphinum Viennæ et Albonis" and "filiam comitis Provinciæ" because of his prior commitment to
2 CONC  marry "filia Barralis domini de Baucio"[329].  It is assumed that this betrothal relates to Sancha, because her two older sisters were married at that date and her younger sister probably too young to have been betrothed.  
2 CONT
2 CONT '''m''' (Betrothed 3 Dec 1241, [before 22 Apr 1253]) as her first husband, BEATRIX de Savoie Dame de Faucigny, daughter and heiress of PIERRE Comte de Savoie, Duc de Chablais & his wife Agnès Dame de Faucigny ([1237]-21 Apr 1310, 
2 CONC bur Faucigny, Chartreuse Monastery of Melans).  The marriage contract of "Guigoni Dalphino comiti Viennensi et Albonensi" and "Aymo dominus Fuciniaci…Beatricem filiam Agnetis filiæ suæ ex Petro de Sabaudie" is dated 4 Dec 1241[330
2 CONC ].  The testament of "Petri de Sabaudia", dated 8 Jun 1255 at London, names "Beatrix filia sua…Eleonora Angliæ regina…Philippum electrum Lugdunensem fratrem suum, Agneti…Fuciniacensi uxori suæ", and names Henry III King of England
2 CONC  as his executor[331].  The Aymari Rivalli De Allobrogibus records the marriage of "Guigonem quartum" and "Beatricem, Petri comitis Sabaudiæ filiam"[332].  The contract of marriage between "Guigone Delfino di Vienna e d'Albona" an
2 CONC d "Beatrice figlia di Pietro di Savoia" is dated 4 Dec 1241[333].  The marriage between "Vienna e Beatrice figlia di Pietro di Savoia" was confirmed as valid despite a prior verbal agreement between "detto Delfino e Cecilia di Bea
2 CONC ux figlia del Conte Barallo di Beaux, ed indi Moglie d'Amedeo quarto Conte di Savoia", by judgment dated 10 Mar 1261[334].  The testament of "Agnetis dominæ Fuciniaci" dated 17 Oct 1262 appoints "Petrum de Sabaudia maritum suum…et
2 CONC  Beatricem filiam suam uxorem Guigonis Dalphini Viennensis" as her heirs[335].  A second testament of "Agnetis dominæ Fuciniaci conjugis Petri de Sabaudia" dated 16 Nov 1262 elects her burial "in ecclesia Contaminæ", and appoints 
2 CONC "Beatricem filiam suam uxorem Guigonis Dalphini, Vienn. et Albon. comitis" as her heir in one third of her property and her husband as heir in the other two thirds[336].  The testament of "Conte Pietro di Savoia", dated Sep 1264, 
2 CONC appointed "filiam suam Beatricem uxorem Guigonis Dalphini" as his heir[337].  The testament of "Guigo Dalphinus, Vienn. et Albonis comitis", dated 27 Jun 1267, appointed "Johannem filium meum" as his heir, and made bequests to "An
2 CONC nam et Catharinam filias meas…Beatrix uxor mea"[338].  Regent during the minority of her son in Viennois in 1269.  She married secondly (2 Apr 1273) as his second wife, Gaston VII Vicomte de Béarn.  Her second marriage is proved b
2 CONC y the agreement dated 15 Dec 1284 under which "Gastone Visconte di Bearn Signore di Montricher e Castelvecchio" and "Beatrice figlia del Conte Pietro di Savoia Dama di Faussign sua Consorte" reached agreement with "Umberto Signore
2 CONC  di Thoire ed Anna Delfina sua Consorte" concering Comte Gaston's claim to the county of Vienne[339].  She transferred her possessions to her grandson Jean de La Tour 23 Sep 1282, her lands between Seyssel and Freiburg to her cous
2 CONC in Amédée Comte de Savoie, and the barony of Faucigny 29 Apr 1294 to her son-in-law Humbert de La Tour for the benefit of one of his sons reserving herself the usufruct.  
2 CONT
2 CONT    Guigues & his wife had four children: 
