1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Guigues VIII of Viennois
2 CONT
2 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
2 CONT
2 CONT Jump to: navigation, search
2 CONT
2 CONT Guigues VIII de la Tour-du-Pin (1309 – 28 July 1333) was the Dauphin of Vienne from 1318 to his death. He was the eldest son of the Dauphin John II and Beatrice of Hungary.
2 CONT
2 CONT Only nine years of age when his father died, he succeeded under the regency of his uncle Henri Dauphin, the bishop-elect of Metz, which was exercised until 1323. Knight and combatant par excellence, in 1325, at the age of sixteen,
2 CONC  he took Varey, near Pont d'Ain, in a brilliant battle against the Savoyards. Contemporary chronicles say that "l'ost de Savoye fut bellement desconfit." From that date to his death, Guigues was in constant conflict with his Savoy
2 CONC ard neighbours.
2 CONT
2 CONT French influence was reinforced during his reign, especially by his marriage with Isabelle, daughter of Philip V of France.[1] In 1328, at the Battle of Cassel, Philip VI entrusted the command of the Seventh Corps, with its twelve
2 CONC  banners, to him. In the battle, the Flemish burgers were smashed by the French chivalry. For his courage, Guigues was rewarded with the Maison aux Piliers in Grève, Paris.
2 CONT
2 CONT Guigues was finally killed while besieging the Savoyard castle of Perrière in 1333. He left the Dauphiné to his brother Humbert II. He was buried in Saint-André in Grenoble.
