1 NOTE {geni:about_me} - https://it.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ercole_I_d%27Este
2 CONT
2 CONT Ercole I. d’Este (* 26. Oktober 1431 in Ferrara; † 15. Juni 1505 ebenda) war eines der ehelichen Kinder Niccolòs III. d’Este, des Markgrafen von Ferrara, Modena und Reggio. Er folgte seinem Halbbruder Borso d’Este am 20. August 14
2 CONC 71 als Herzog der genannten Gebiete nach.
2 CONT
2 CONT Ercoles Leistung ist die wirtschaftliche und kulturelle Blüte seiner Herzogtümer. Er ließ Ferrara ab 1490 durch den Architekten Biagio Rossetti (1447–1516) befestigen, aber auch erweitern, wobei erstmals in Europa – bei einem mitt
2 CONC elalterlichen Stadtkern – breite und gerade Straßen gebaut wurden.
2 CONT
2 CONT Ercole I. war seit dem 3. Juli 1473 mit Eleonora von Aragón (1450–1493) verheiratet, der Tochter König Ferdinands I. Mit seiner Frau hatte er sechs Kinder, von denen vier zu den wichtigsten Mitgliedern der Familie gehören, mindest
2 CONC ens ein weiteres war außerehelich.
2 CONT
2 CONT     * Isabella d’Este (1474–1539) ∞ 1490 Gianfrancesco II. Gonzaga (1466–1519), Markgraf von Mantua
2 CONT
2 CONT     * Beatrice d’Este (1475–1497) ∞ 1491 Ludovico Sforza (1451–1508) Herzog von Mailand
2 CONT
2 CONT     * Alfonso I. d’Este (1476–1534), Herzog 1505, ∞ 1) 1491 Anna Sforza (1473–1497), Tochter Galeazzo Maria Sforzas, ∞ 2) 1502 Lucrezia Borgia (1480–1519), ∞ 3) Laura Dianti († 1573)
2 CONT
2 CONT     * Ferrante d’Este (1477–1540)
2 CONT
2 CONT     * Giulio d’Este (1478–1561), unehelich
2 CONT
2 CONT     * Ippolito I. d’Este (1479–1520), Kardinal 1493
2 CONT
2 CONT     * Sigismondo d’Este (1480–1524)
2 CONT
2 CONT Siehe auch [Bearbeiten]
2 CONT
2 CONT     * Palazzo dei Diamanti
2 CONT
2 CONT Vorgänger
2 CONT
2 CONT Borso d’Este
2 CONT
2 CONT 	Herzog von Ferrara, Modena und Reggio
2 CONT
2 CONT 1471–1505 	Nachfolger
2 CONT
2 CONT Alfonso I. d’Este
2 CONT
2 CONT Diese Seite wurde zuletzt am 16. Februar 2010 um 21:28 Uhr geändert.
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT Ercole d'Este I
2 CONT
2 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
2 CONT
2 CONT Ercole I d'Este (26 October 1431 – 15 June 1505) was Duke of Ferrara from 1471 until 1505. He was a member of the house of Este. He was nicknamed North Wind and the Diamond.[1]
2 CONT
2 CONT Biography
2 CONT
2 CONT Ercole was born at Ferrara to Nicolò III and Ricciarda da Saluzzo. His maternal grandparents were Thomas III of Saluzzo and Marguerite of Roussy.
2 CONT
2 CONT He was educated at the Neapolitan court of Alfonso, king of Aragon and Naples, from 1445 to 1460; there he studied military arts, chivalry, and acquired the appreciation for architecture all'antica and the fine arts, which would r
2 CONC esult in his becoming one of the most significant art patrons of the Renaissance.[2]
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1471, with the support of the Republic of Venice, he became Duke on the death of his half-brother Borso, profiting of the absence of the latter's on, Niccolò, who was in Mantua. [3] During an absence of Ercole from Ferrara, Nic
2 CONC colò attempted a coup, which was however crushed; Niccolò and his cousin Azzo were beheaded on September 4, 1476. Ercole married Eleonora d'Aragona, daughter of Ferdinand I of Naples, in 1473. The Este alliance with Naples was to 
2 CONC prove a powerful one.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1482-1484 he fought a war with the Republic of Venice, which was allied with Ercole's nemesis, the Della Rovere Pope Sixtus IV, occasioned by the salt monopoly, the War of Ferrara. Ercole was able to end the war by ceding the P
2 CONC olesine at the Peace of Bagnolo, and Ferrara escaped the fate of destruction or absorption into the papal dominions, but the war was a humiliation for Ercole, who lay sick and immobilized while the besieging army destroyed Este pr
2 CONC operties in the surrounding neighbourhoods.
2 CONT
2 CONT After this, he remained neutral in the Italian War of 1494-1498, and tried for the rest of his rule to improve relations with the Papal states. He reluctantly agreed to the marriage of his son Alfonso to Lucrezia Borgia, daughter 
2 CONC of Pope Alexander VI, a marriage that brought notable territorial donations.
2 CONT
2 CONT His subsequent career as a patron may be seen to some extent as compensation for the early military setback: significantly, Ercole was the only Italian ruler who characterized himself as divus on his coinage, like a Roman emperor 
2 CONC (Manca 1989:525, 529).
2 CONT
2 CONT The scale and consistency of Ercole's patronage of the arts was in part a political and cultural statement. He hosted theatrical representations with elaborate scenery and musical intermezzi, some of the first purely secular theat
2 CONC re in Europe since Antiquity[4] and was successful in setting up a musical establishment which was for a few years the finest in Europe, overshadowing the Vatican chapel itself.[5] For the next century Ferrara was to retain the ch
2 CONC aracter of a center of avant-garde music with a decidedly secular emphasis. In music history Ercole was one of the Italian nobles most responsible for bringing the talented Franco-Flemish musicians from northern Europe into Italy.
2 CONC  The most famous composers of Europe either worked for him, were commissioned by him, or dedicated music to him, including Alexander Agricola, Jacob Obrecht, Heinrich Isaac, Adrian Willaert, and Josquin Desprez, whose Missa Hercul
2 CONC es dux Ferrariae not only is dedicated to him, but is based on a theme drawn from the syllables of the Duke's name.
2 CONT
2 CONT Ercole is equally famous as a patron of the arts, as much an expression of his conscious magnificence as his cultivated aloofness, grave and stern as befitted the new ducal rank of Ferrara (Manca 1989:524ff). He made the poet Boia
2 CONC rdo his minister, and also brought the young Ludovico Ariosto into his household.
2 CONT
2 CONT Under Ercole Ferrara became one of the leading cities of Europe; it underwent substantial growth in the Ercolean Addition, approximately doubling in size, under Ercole's direct guidance, producing the first planned and executed ur
2 CONC banistic project of the Renaissance. To enclose it, he extended the city's walls, hiring architect Biagio Rossetti for the work.[6] Many of Ferrara's most famous buildings date from his reign.
2 CONT
2 CONT Ercole was an admirer of church reformer Girolamo Savonarola, who was also from Ferrara, and sought his advice on both spiritual and political matters. Approximately a dozen letters between the two survive from the 1490s. Ercole a
2 CONC ttempted to have Savonarola freed by the Florentine church authorities, but was unsuccessful; the reformist monk was burned at the stake in 1498.[7]
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1503 or 1504, Ercole asked his newly-hired composer Josquin des Prez to write musical testament for him, structured on Savonarola's prison meditation Infelix ego. The result was the Miserere, probably first performed for Holy W
2 CONC eek in 1504, with the tenor part possibly sung by the Duke himself.[8].
2 CONT
2 CONT Ercole died in 1505, and his son Alfonso became Duke.
2 CONT
2 CONT [edit]Family and issue
2 CONT
2 CONT Ercole and Eleonora had six children:
2 CONT
2 CONT Alfonso (who would later marry the infamous Lucrezia Borgia)
2 CONT
2 CONT Isabella (who would later prove to be one of the most powerful women of the Renaissance)
2 CONT
2 CONT Beatrice (who would marry Ludovico Sforza, the future Duke of Milan)
2 CONT
2 CONT Ferrante (who was thrown in prison by his brother Alfonso in 1506, where he died 34 years later)
2 CONT
2 CONT Ippolito (cardinal, army commander and patron of the arts)
2 CONT
2 CONT Sigismondo
2 CONT
2 CONT Ercole had two illegitimate children:
2 CONT
2 CONT Lucrezia
2 CONT
2 CONT Giulio
2 CONT
2 CONT [edit]References
2 CONT
2 CONT Chiappini, Luciano (2001). Gli Estensi. Mille anni di storia. Ferrara.
2 CONT
2 CONT Gardner, Edmund G. (1904). Dukes and Poets in Ferrara: a Study in the Poetry, Religion and Politics of the Fifteenth and Sixteenth Centuries. London.
2 CONT
2 CONT Gundersheimer, Werner L. (1973). Ferrara, the Style of a Renaissance Despotism. Princeton.
2 CONT
2 CONT Lockwood, Lewis (1984). Music in Renaissance Ferrara, 1400-1505: The Creation of a Musical Center in the Italian Renaissance. Oxford.
2 CONT
2 CONT Macey, Patrick (1998). Bonfire Songs: Savonarola's Musical Legacy. Oxford: Clarendon Press. ISBN 0-19-816669-9.
2 CONT
2 CONT Rosenberg, Charles M. (1997). The Este Monuments and Urban Development in Renaissance Ferrara. Cambridge.
2 CONT
2 CONT Tuohy, Thomas (1996). Herculean Ferrara: Ercole d'Este, 1471-1505, and the Invention of a Ducal Capital. Cambridge.
2 CONT
