2 CONT
2 CONT Saint Elizabeth of Portugal, also known as Elizabeth of Aragon (1271 – 4 July 1336) (Elisabet in Catalan, Isabel in both Portuguese and Spanish) was queen consort of Portugal and is a saint of the Roman Catholic Church. 
2 CONT Biography 
2 CONT Marriage
2 CONT
2 CONT Saint Elizabeth showed an early enthusiasm for religion: she said the full Divine Office daily, fasted and did other penances, and attended twice daily choral masses.
2 CONT
2 CONT Elizabeth was married when she was 12 years old to King Denis of Portugal, a poet known as Rei Lavrador, or the farmer king, because he planted a large pine forest, near Leiria. The wood from these trees would later be used to mak
2 CONC e the ships during the discoveries. Elizabeth quietly pursued the regular religious practices of her maidenhood, and was devoted to the poor and sick. Naturally, such a life was a reproach to many around her, and caused ill will i
2 CONC n some quarters. A popular story is told of how her husband's jealousy was roused by an evil-speaking page; of how he condemned the queen's supposedly guilty accomplice to a cruel death; and was finally convinced of her innocence 
2 CONC by the strange accidental substitution of her accuser for the intended victim.
2 CONT
2 CONT They had two children, a daughter named Constance, who married King Ferdinand IV of Castile, and a son Afonso who later became King Afonso IV of Portugal. The latter so greatly resented the favors shown to the king's illegitimate 
2 CONC sons that he rebelled, and in the year 1323, war was declared between him and his father. Elizabeth, however, reconciled her husband and son, and is known in consequence as the "peacemaker." 
2 CONT
2 CONT Dowager Queen
2 CONT
2 CONT King Denis died in the year 1325, and his son Afonso succeeded him. Elizabeth then retired to the convent of the Poor Clares (now known as the Monastery of Santa Clara-a-Velha) which she had founded in the year 1314 at Coimbra. Sh
2 CONC e took the habit of the Franciscan Order, wishing to devote the rest of her life to the poor and sick in obscurity, but she was called forth to act once more as peacemaker. In 1336, Afonso IV marched his troops against Alfonso XI 
2 CONC of Castile, to whom he had married his daughter Maria, and who had neglected and ill-treated her. In spite of age and weakness, the queen dowager insisted on hurrying to Estremoz, where the two kings' armies were drawn up. She aga
2 CONC in stopped the fighting and caused terms of peace to be arranged, but the exertion brought on her final illness. As soon as her mission was fulfilled, she died of a fever on July 4, 1336, at Estremoz Castle.
2 CONT
2 CONT St. Elizabeth was buried at the Monastery of Santa Clara-a-Velha in Coimbra, in a magnificent Gothic sarcophagus. In the early 17th century, her remains were transferred to a new sarcophagus made of silver and glass. After the mon
2 CONC astery was abandoned due to frequent floods, her tomb was transferred to the new Monastery of Santa Clara-a-Nova, Coimbra, where it can be visited today. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Canonization and Feast Day
2 CONT
2 CONT Miracles were said to have followed upon her death. She was canonized by Pope Urban VIII on the 25th of May, 1625, and her feast was inserted in the Roman Catholic Calendar of Saints for celebration on the 4th of July. In the year
2 CONC  1694, Pope Innocent XII moved her feast to the 8th of July, so it would not conflict with the celebration of the Octave of Saints Peter and Paul, the Apostles. [4] In 1955, Pope Pius XII abolished this octave. The 1962 Roman Miss
2 CONC al changed the rank of the feast from "Double" to "Third-Class Feast.” [6] The 1969 reform of the Calendar classified the celebration as an optional "Memorial" and restored it to the date of July 4th. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Family and Ancestors
2 CONT
2 CONT She was named after her great-aunt Saint Elizabeth of Hungary, but was known in Portuguese and Spanish as "Isabel." She was the younger sister of King Alfonso III of Aragon and King James II of Aragon. She was also the older siste
2 CONC r of King Frederick III of Sicily.
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT Elizabeth of Aragon From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
2 CONT
2 CONT St. Elisabeth of Aragon (1271–4 July 1336) (Elisabet in Catalan, Isabel in Portuguese) was queen consort of Portugal and is, like her great-aunt St. Elisabeth of Hungary who had been canonized in 1235 for her miracles in Thuringia
2 CONC  (Germany), a Saint of the Roman Catholic Church. She is also known as Rainha Santa Isabel in Portuguese (Queen Saint Elisabeth).
2 CONT
2 CONT Marriage
2 CONT
2 CONT She showed an early enthusiasm for religion: she said the full Divine Office daily, fasted and did other penances, and attended twice daily choral masses. Elizabeth was married very early to Denis of Portugal, a poet known as Rei 
2 CONC Lavrador, or the farmer king, because he planted a large pine forest, near Leiria. The wood from these trees would later be used to make the boats during the discoveries. Elizabeth quietly pursued the regular religious practices o
2 CONC f her maidenhood, and was devoted to the poor and sick. Naturally, such a life was a reproach to many around her, and caused ill will in some quarters. A popular story is told of how her husband's jealousy was roused by an evil-sp
2 CONC eaking page; of how he condemned the queen's supposedly guilty accomplice to a cruel death; and was finally convinced of her innocence by the strange accidental substitution of her accuser for the intended victim. They had two chi
2 CONC ldren, a daughter Constance, who married Ferdinand IV of Castile, and a son Afonso (later Afonso IV of Portugal). The latter so greatly resented the favours shown to the king's illegitimate sons that he rebelled, and in 1323, war 
2 CONC was declared between him and his father. Elisabeth, however, reconciled her husband and son, and is known in consequence as the "peacemaker.” 
2 CONT
2 CONT Dowager Queen
2 CONT
2 CONT Denis died in 1325, his son succeeding him. Elisabeth then retired to the convent of the Poor Clares (now known as Monastery of Santa Clara-a-Velha) which she had founded in 1314 at Coimbra. She took the habit of the Franciscan Or
2 CONC der, wishing to devote the rest of her life to the poor and sick in obscurity, but she was called forth to act once more as peacemaker. In 1336, Afonso IV marched his troops against Alfonso XI of Castile, to whom he had married hi
2 CONC s daughter Maria, and who had neglected and ill-treated her. In spite of age and weakness, the queen dowager insisted on hurrying to Estremoz, where the two kings' armies were drawn up. She again stopped the fighting and caused te
2 CONC rms of peace to be arranged, but the exertion brought on her final illness. As soon as her mission was fulfilled, she died of a fever on the 8th of July, 1336, at Estremoz Castle. Elizabeth was buried at the Monastery of Santa Cla
2 CONC ra-a-Velha in Coimbra, in a magnificent Gothic sarcophagus. In the early 17th century, her remains were transferred to a new sarcophagus made of silver and glass. After the monastery was abandoned due to frequent floods, her tomb 
2 CONC was transferred to the new Monastery of Santa Clara-a-Nova, were it can be visited today. Miracles were said to have followed her death. She was canonized by Pope Urban VIII on the 25th of  May, 1625,[1] and her feast is kept on J
2 CONC uly 4th, the day of her death and the date on which her feast was initially celebrated in the Roman Catholic calendar of saints. In 1694, the feast was moved to the 8th of July, outside the Octave of Saints Peter and Paul, [2] but
2 CONC  in 1969 it was restored to its original date. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Family and Ancestors
2 CONT
2 CONT She was named after her great-aunt St. Elisabeth of Hungary, but is known in Portuguese by "Isabel.” She was a younger sister of Alfonso III of Aragon and James II of Aragon. She was also an older sister of Frederick III of Sicily
2 CONC .
