1 NAME Thomas De /de Percy/ Bishop of Norwich
2 GIVN Thomas De
2 SURN de Percy
2 NSFX Bishop of Norwich
2 _MARNM de Percy
1 SEX M
1 BIRT
2 DATE ABT 1328
2 PLAC Alnwick Castle, Northumberland, England 
2 ADDR
1 OCCU unknown
2 DATE 1356
2 PLAC bishop of Norwich
2 ADDR
1 DEAT
2 DATE 8 AUG 1369
2 PLAC Blofield, Norfolk, , England
2 ADDR
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} https://fmg.ac/Projects/MedLands/ENGLISHNOBILITYMEDIEVAL3P-S.htm#WilliamPercydied1245B
2 CONT
2 CONT ==Thomas Percy (bishop of Norwich)==
2 CONT '''Thomas Percy was a medieval Bishop of Norwich. He was the son of''' Henry de Percy, 2nd Baron Percy and Idonia, daughter of Robert de Clifford, 1st Baron de Clifford.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Percy was nominated 4 February 1355 and was consecrated on 3 January 1356. He died on 8 August 1369.[1]
2 CONT
2 CONT From: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Thomas_Percy_(bishop_of_Norwich)
2 CONT
2 CONT ______________________
2 CONT *'''Thomas PERCY (Bishop of Norwich)
2 CONT *'''Notes: Bishop of Norwich (1356-1369).
2 CONT *Father: Henry PERCY (2º B. Percy)
2 CONT *Mother: Idoine CLIFFORD
2 CONT *From: http://www.tudorplace.com.ar/PERCY.htm#Thomas PERCY (Bishop of Norwich) _____________
2 CONT *'''Thomas de Percy1
2 CONT *'''M, #158255, b. 1333, d. 8 August 1369
2 CONT *Last Edited=31 May 2015
2 CONT *Consanguinity Index=0.31%
2 CONT *'''     Thomas de Percy was born in 1333 at Alnwick Castle, Northumberland, England.1,2 He was the son of''' Henry de Percy, 2nd Lord Percy and Idoine de Clifford.1 '''He died on 8 August 1369.2
2 CONT *'''     He held the office of Bishop of Norwich between 1356 and 1369.2
2 CONT *Citations
2 CONT *[S125] Richard Glanville-Brown, online <e-mail address>, Richard Glanville-Brown (RR 2, Milton, Ontario, Canada), downloaded 17 August 2005.
2 CONT *[S37] BP2003 volume 2, page 2939. See link for full details for this source. Hereinafter cited as. [S37]
2 CONT *From: http://www.thepeerage.com/p15826.htm#i158255 _____________________
2 CONT *'''Thomas de Percy
2 CONT *'''Birth: 	1332, England
2 CONT *'''Death: 	Aug. 8, 1369  Blofield, Norfolk, England
2 CONT *'''Younger son''' of Sir Henry de Percy and Idoine Clifford. '''Grandson of''' Sir Henry de Percy and Eleanor de Arundel FitzAlan, Sir Robert Clifford and Maud de Clare.
2 CONT *'''Thomas studied at Oxford University under Master William de Blythe in 1351, obtained his B.A. and M.A. degrees from Oxford, and was then appointed Canon of Chester-le-Street in 1351, Rector of Catton, Yorkshire in 1352, Canon 
2 CONC of Chichester in 1353. He was chosen as Bishop of Norwich by papal provision in 1355, but under excommunication in 1359. Thomas, Bishop of Norwich, died testate at Blofield, Norfolk on 08 August 1369, and was buried in his cathedr
2 CONC al at Norwich. 
2 CONT *Family links: 
2 CONT * Parents:
2 CONT *  Henry de Percy (1301 - 1352)
2 CONT *  Idoine Clifford Percy (1300 - 1365)
2 CONT * Siblings:
2 CONT *  Isabel de Percy de Aton*
2 CONT *  Eleanor de Percy FitzWalter*
2 CONT *  Henry de Percy (1320 - 1368)*
2 CONT *  Thomas de Percy (1332 - 1369)
2 CONT *  Margaret Percy De Ferrers (1333 - 1368)*
2 CONT *  Maud de Percy Neville (1335 - 1379)*
2 CONT *'''Burial:  Norwich Cathedral, Norwich, City of Norwich, Norfolk, England
2 CONT *Find A Grave Memorial# 105581871
2 CONT *From: http://www.findagrave.com/cgi-bin/fg.cgi?page=gr&GRid=105581871 _________________________
2 CONT *'''Dictionary of National Biography, 1885-1900, Volume 44
2 CONT *Percy, Henry (1299?-1352)	by Charles Lethbridge Kingsford
2 CONT *PERCY, HENRY, second Baron Percy of Alnwick (1299?–1352), was elder son of Henry Percy, first baron Percy of Alnwick [q. v.], and is said to have been sixteen years old at his father's death, but was apparently still a minor on 2
2 CONC 8 June 1320 (Cal. Close Rolls, Edw. II, 1318–23, p. 201). He had seisin of his lands on 26 Dec. 1321, though he had not yet made proof of his age (ib. p. 411). He was with Thomas of Lancaster at Pontefract on 21 May 1321, but was 
2 CONC warden of Scarborough Castle for the king on 13 Feb. 1322, and later in the year was employed against the adherents of Thomas of Lancaster in Yorkshire, and afterwards against the Scots. On 26 Sept. he was censured for letting the
2 CONC  Scots escape unharmed. During the reign of Edward II he was summoned to various parliaments, and in 1324–5 for service in Guyenne. After the landing of Queen Isabella in September 1326 he joined her at Gloucester (Murimuth, p. 47
2 CONC ), and was one of the council of government appointed in the parliament of January 1327 (Stubbs, Const. Hist. ii. 385). On 13 Feb. 1328 he was appointed warden of the marches, and shortly afterwards commissioned to treat for peace
2 CONC  with Scotland (Fœdera, ii. 688–9). In the summer he was besieged by Thomas Randolph, earl of Moray [q. v.], at Alnwick (Scalachronica, p. 155). On 5 Sept. he was appointed chief warden of the marches, and on 9 Oct. one of the com
2 CONC missioners to renew the negotiations with Scotland, and assisted in completing the convention at Edinburgh on 17 March 1328, which was ratified by Edward at Northampton on 4 May (Fœdera, ii. 715, 719, 734, 740). On 1 March 1328 he
2 CONC  obtained a grant of Warkworth from the king (Cal. Pat. Rolls, Edw. III, p. 243). He had recovered his Scottish lands under the treaty with Bruce. In May 1329 he went over to France with the king, and was present when Edward did h
2 CONC omage at Amiens on 6 June (Fœdera, ii. 764–5). During 1331 and 1332 he was employed as a justiciar and warden of the Scottish marches (Bain, iii. 1026, 1032, 1056, 1057). He was with Edward at the siege of Berwick in July 1333, an
2 CONC d probably at the battle of Halidon Hill. On 1 Oct. he was appointed to attend Edward Baliol's parliament, and was present at Edinburgh for this purpose in February 1334 (ib. iii. 1094; Fœdera, ii. 876). He had previously been app
2 CONC ointed constable of Berwick, and afterwards held the offices of constable of Berwick and Jedworth as compensation for surrendering his claims on Annandale and Lochmaben. In February 1335 he likewise received all the fees of Patric
2 CONC k, earl of March, in Northumberland.
2 CONT *In January 1335 he defeated the Scots, who were raiding in Redesdale (Chron. Edw. I and Edw. II, ii. 121). In the following July he took part in Edward's invasion of Scotland, advancing from Berwick in company with Baliol (Chron.
2 CONC  Lanercost, p. 281). In July 1336 he was with Edward III at Perth, and apparently was again in Scotland early in 1337 (Bain, iii. 1209, 1230). In October 1337 he was fighting with the Scots in Allendale, and early in 1338 was sent
2 CONC  to besiege Dunbar (ib. iii. 1268; Chron. Lanercost, p. 295). In February 1339 he was a commissioner of array at York, and in October was again directed to help Baliol (Fœdera, ii. 1070, 1093). On 28 April 1340 he was appointed to
2 CONC  treat with the Scots, and in June was one of the councillors of the young Duke of Cornwall during Edward's absence abroad (ib. ii. 1122, 1125). During 1341 he defeated the Scots at Farmley (Chron. de Melsa, iii. 49), and was empl
2 CONC oyed in the abortive attempt to relieve Stirling (Bain, iii. 1378). In 1342 he was present at the siege of Nantes (Froissart, iii. 24), and in 1343 was engaged in keeping order on the Scottish marches (Fœdera, ii. 1225, 1230, 1239
2 CONC ). In 1345 he took part in defeating the invasion of Cumberland by William Douglas (Ypodigma Neustriæ, p. 285). In July 1346 Percy was one of the guardians of the kingdom during Edward's absence; and when in October David Bruce in
2 CONC vaded England, he commanded the first division at the battle of Neville's Cross, where his valour contributed to the English victory (Froissart, iii. 129, iv. 20, 22, ed. Luce; Chron. Lanercost, pp. 348–50). After the battle Percy
2 CONC  fell ill, and so could not share in the advance into Scotland (ib. p. 352). On 26 Jan. 1347 he was ordered to serve under Edward Baliol for a year (Bain, iii. 1479), and during this and the following year was engaged in the Scott
2 CONC ish marches. He was employed in the negotiations with Scotland in 1349 and 1350, and in 1351 was a commissioner of array in Northumberland. He died on 26 Feb. 1352, and was buried at Alnwick; his will, dated 13 Sept. 1349, is prin
2 CONC ted in ‘Testamenta Eboracensia,’ i. 57–61 (Surtees Soc.) Percy had been summoned to parliament from 1322. It was through him and his father that ‘the Percies became the hereditary guardians of the north and the scourge of Scotland
2 CONC ’ (Burton, Hist. Scotland, iii. 4). The Lanercost chronicler (p. 350) describes him as ‘bonus prœliator, parvus miles et providus.’ He married Idonea (in his will she is called Imania), daughter of Robert Clifford, who died in 136
2 CONC 5, and founded a chantry for herself and her husband at Meaux (Chron. de Melsa, iii. 163). By her he had six sons and four daughters.
2 CONT *The eldest son, Henry Percy, third Baron Percy (1322–1368), took part in the campaign of Crécy in 1346 and the expedition to Gascony in 1349. After his father's death he was on several occasions employed as warden of the Scottish
2 CONC  marches, and served in Edward's French expedition in 1355 (Avesbury, p. 427). He died on 17 June 1368, having married (1) Mary (1320–1362), daughter of Henry, earl of Lancaster [q. v.], by whom he had two sons, Henry, first earl 
2 CONC of Northumberland, and Thomas (d. 1403), earl of Worcester, both of whom are separately noticed; and (2) Joan (d. 1369), daughter of John de Orby, by whom he had a daughter Mary (1367–1395), who married John, lord Ros of Hamlake.
2 CONT *'''The fifth son, Thomas (1333–1369), was apparently at Rome when William Bateman [q. v.], bishop of Norwich, died in 1355, and was, at the request of Henry, duke of Lancaster, provided to that see by the pope, though only twenty
2 CONC -two years of age. He was consecrated at Waverley on 3 Jan. 1356. He had some dispute with the monks of his cathedral about the appropriation of certain tithes, and undertook extensive repairs in his church, to the cost of which h
2 CONC e contributed four hundred marks. He was trier of petitions from England, Scotland, Wales, and Ireland in the parliaments of 1363, 1364–5, 1366, and 1369, in which year he died on 8 Aug. His will, dated 25 March 1368 and proved 15
2 CONC  Nov. 1369, is preserved at Lambeth (Stubbs, Reg. Sacr.; Le Neve; Wharton, Anglia Sacra, i. 415; Rymer, iii. i. 341; Rolls of Parl. ii. 275 et seq.; Walsingham, Hist. Angl. i. 309; Leland, Collect. i. 182).
2 CONT *[Chronicles of Edward I and Edward II, Chronicon de Melsa, Murimuth's and Avesbury's Chronicles (all these in Rolls Ser.); Gray's Scalachronica (Maitland Club); Lanercost Chronicle (Bannatyne Club); G. le Baker's Chron. ed. Thomp
2 CONC son; Bain's Calendar of Documents relating to Scotland; Rymer's Fœdera (Record edit.); Palgrave's Parliamentary Writs; Rolls of Parliament; Calendars of Close Rolls, Edward II, and Patent Rolls, Edward III; Dugdale's Baronage, i. 
2 CONC 273–6; Collins's Peerage, ed. Brydges, ii. 241–9; De Fonblanque's Annals of the House of Percy, i. 71–96; Longman's Life and Times of Edward III.]
2 CONT *From: https://en.wikisource.org/wiki/Percy,_Henry_(1299%3F-1352)_(DNB00)
