1 NOTE {geni:about_me} another possible death date is 17 June 1368
2 CONT
2 CONT =================================================================================
2 CONT
2 CONT "Henry Percy, 3rd Baron Percy of Alnwick (c. 1321–1368), was the eldest son of Henry de Percy, 2nd Baron Percy (1301–1352), and his wife, Idonea, daughter of Robert de Clifford, 1st Baron de Clifford.
2 CONT Alnwick Castle- the Percy seat in Northumberland
2 CONT
2 CONT He served in France under the earl of Arundel in March 1344 and in August 1346 was at the Battle of Crécy. He then transferred to service in Gascony under the earl of Lancaster. In July 1352 he was made joint warden of the marches
2 CONC  towards Scotland, and in September 1355 keeper of Roxburgh Castle and sheriff of Northumberland for two years.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1356 he took part in the invasion of Scotland that followed Edward Balliol's surrender of the kingdom and crown of Scotland to Edward III. In July 1356 he was once again joint warden of the marches and then took part in the neg
2 CONC otiations that led to the treaty of Berwick of October 1357.
2 CONT
2 CONT He participated in Edward III's military efforts in France and in September 1355 was marshal of the royal army at Calais. In 1360 he took part in Edward's campaign to capture Rheims.
2 CONT
2 CONT He died on or about 18 May 1368, probably at his castle of Alnwick, and was buried in Alnwick Abbey. He had married twice: firstly Mary of Lancaster (d. 1362), daughter of Henry, 3rd Earl of Lancaster, and secondly Joan (d. 1368),
2 CONC  the daughter and sole heiress of John, Lord Orreby. With his first wife he had two sons, Henry and Thomas. With his second wife he had a son who died in his father's lifetime and a daughter, Mary. He was succeeded by his eldest s
2 CONC on Henry, who became the 1st earl of Northumberland. His younger son Thomas became the 1st Earl of Worcester."
2 CONT
