1 NOTE {geni:about_me} '''Henry Hussey, 2nd Baron Hussey,''' (1292–1349) of Harting and Palborough, co. Sussex, was an English nobleman. He was the son of Henry Hussey,  1st Baron Hussey and wife Isabel Moores. Over the generations the s
2 CONC urname Hussey has had many spellings. While Hussey is the most commonly used today, older variations include Huse, Husee, Hoese, Husy, Huzzey and Hussy.
2 CONT
2 CONT "Sir Henry Huse, knight", was returned as Knight of the Shire for Dorset October 11, 1311, according to Knights of Edward I. He served in the Scotch expeditions, 1333-35. 
2 CONT
2 CONT He was married first, c.1314, to Maud but she appears to have died about 1316. On their wedding day his father gave the bride and groom an estate in Kent. A son, named Mark Hussey, was born to Henry and Maud in about 1316.
2 CONT
2 CONT About 1317 Henry Hussey was remarried to Katherine FitzAlan, daughter of Edmund FitzAlan, Earl of Arundel, and sister of Richard FitzAlan, later Earl of Arundel. Their children were Elizabeth Hussey (circa 1318), Henry Hussey (cir
2 CONC ca 1320), and Richard Hussey (circa 1323). Katherine died in 1375, according to History of Gloucestershire. Her will was proved in 1376.
2 CONT
2 CONT Sir Henry appears in several legal documents both before and after his death:
2 CONT *In "Easter week, 1345" in Risley, Gloucestershire an inquisition determined that Henry Hussey held a moiety of Saperton manor and a moiety of Rusyndene manor in Gloucestershire, from the king by a knight's service. 
2 CONT *In an inquisition held at Saperton, Gloucestershire in 1346 mention was made of reenfoeffing Henry Hussey for life in Saperton manor and Broderindone [sic] manor, remainder in tail successively to his sons, Henry Hussey and Richa
2 CONC rd Hussey, "his heirs by his wife Catherine," and daughter, Elizabeth Hussey.
2 CONT *According to the Patent Records in 1348 Henry Hussey (with others) was commissioned "to determine whether a wall on a river flowing near the border of Kent and Sussex, near Knellesflote, should be dismantled."
2 CONT *In an inquisition held September 26, 1349 at Marlborough, Wiltshire it was determined that he held a moeity of Standen manor and Tudeworth manor.
2 CONT *At Marlborough on September 26, 1349, it was stated that in Wilts. he held a moeity of the Manor of Standen and the Manor of Tudeworth. On the death of Walter de L'Isle in 1352 it was found that he held l/2 of Pulburough manor jo
2 CONC intly with his wife, Joan, remainder to Sir Henry Husee for life and his son, Henry Husee, and the heirs of his body."
2 CONT
2 CONT Sir Henry Hussey died July 21, 1349, according to Complete Peerage. He left property in Gloucestershire, Sussex, Southampton, Surrey, Kent and Wiltshire. Henry Hussey, his grandson and son of Mark Hussey, was named as his next hei
2 CONC r and stated to be aged six. His widow Katherine FitzAlan-Hussey later married Sir Andrew Peverell.
2 CONT
2 CONT Child of Sir Henry Hussey and first wife Maude:
2 CONT *Sir Mark Hussey, born c.1316 in Sussex County, England, and died before 1349
2 CONT
2 CONT Children of Sir Henry Hussey and second wife, Katherine FitzAlan:
2 CONT * Elizabeth HUSSEY b: Abt 1318 in Hussey Manor, Standen, Berkshire, England
2 CONT * Henry HUSSEY b: Abt 1320 in Hussey Manor, Standen, Berkshire, England
2 CONT * Richard (d.s.p.) HUSSEY b: Abt 1323 in Hussey Manor, Standen, Berkshire, England
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Links to additional material:'''
