1 NOTE {geni:about_me} h 
2 CONT
2 CONT '''William Latimer, 4th Baron Latimer, KG'''
2 CONT
2 CONT Born William Latimer 24 March 1330(1330-03-24) Scampston, North Yorkshire, England  Died 28 May 1381(1381-05-28)  Resting place Guisborough, Yorkshire, England  Nationality Englis From Wikipedia
2 CONT
2 CONT http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/William_Latimer,_4th_Baron_Latimer
2 CONT
2 CONT  (24 March 1330 – 28 May 1381) was an English noble, soldier and diplomat. After serving in France and for the household of Edward III, he was impeached during the Good Parliament of 1376, the earliest recorded impeachment in the 
2 CONC Parliament of England.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Early life and service in France'''
2 CONT
2 CONT Born on 24 March 1330 in Scampston, North Yorkshire, England,[1] Latimer was the son of William Latimer, 3rd Baron Latimer, by Elizabeth, daughter of John de Botetourt, 1st Baron Botetourt. He had married Elizabeth, daughter of Ed
2 CONC mund FitzAlan, 9th Earl of Arundel and Alice de Warenne, by 1353 and they had a daughter, Elizabeth (1357–1395). She married firstly John Neville, 3rd Baron Neville de Raby and secondly Robert Willoughby, 4th Baron Willoughby de E
2 CONC resby.
2 CONT
2 CONT He was present at the Battle of Crécy in 1346, and by 1351 he had been knighted and was in royal service in Calais. In January 1356 he was present when Edward Balliol surrendered his claim to the Scottish throne and he served in G
2 CONC ascony in 1359. He was created a Knight of the Garter in 1361 in succession to Sir William FitzWarin and fought on the side of John de Montfort, Duke of Brittany at the Battle of Auray in 1364. In 1368 he was appointed Keeper of B
2 CONC écherel and in 1370 of Saint-Sauveur-le-Vicomte.[2]
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Return to England and impeachment'''
2 CONT
2 CONT After returning to England, he served as Steward of the Household from 1368 to 1370 and Chamberlain of the Household from 1371. His son-in-law John Neville, Lord Neville de Raby was appointed Steward of the Household in the same y
2 CONC ear, and until 1376 they were prominent figures in court[2] and Latimer was high in favour with John of Gaunt, Duke of Lancaster, the king's son. He became Constable of Dover Castle and Warden of the Cinque Ports in 1372. He was a
2 CONC lso involved in negotiations with Portugal in 1373 and France in 1375.
2 CONT
2 CONT When Parliament was called in April 1376, known as the Good Parliament and led by Peter de la Mare, the members wanted to remove corrupt advisers from court. Latimer, Neville, London merchant Richard Lyons and Alice Perrers were a
2 CONC ccused, and the charges against Latimer were that he had been guilty of oppression in Brittany; had sold the castle of Saint-Sauveur to the enemy, and impeded the relief of Bécherel in 1375; that he had taken bribes for the releas
2 CONC e of captured ships, and retained fines paid to the king, notably by Sir Robert Knolles, and the city of Bristol; and finally, that in association with Robert Lyons he had obtained money from the crown by the repayment of fictitio
2 CONC us loans. Seconded by William of Wykeham, de la Mare sought to have Latimer immediately convicted, with the Commons acting on behalf of the king. They were unsuccessful and a trial took place.[3] The charges were proven and he was
2 CONC  removed from his positions in the royal household and on the council, fined and imprisoned. He was pardoned in October 1376 and with Lancaster's recovered influence he returned to favour.
2 CONT
2 CONT Latimer's impeachment is the earliest recorded in Parliament.[4]
2 CONT
2 CONT [edit] Later lifeLatimer was named an executor of the will of Edward III, who died in June 1377. In 1377 he became governor of Calais, and took part against the Spaniards at the battle of Sluys. He also accompanied Thomas of Woods
2 CONC tock, Earl of Buckingham on his expedition through France into Brittany in 1380.
2 CONT
2 CONT Latimer died on 28 May 1381 and was buried at Guisborough, Yorkshire. He was survived by his wife and their daughter.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Footnotes''' 1.^ "Rillington, an East Yorkshire village". Timothy J. Owston. http://freespace.virgin.net/owston.tj/rillingt.htm. Retrieved 8 November 2009.  2.^ a b Tuck, Anthony (1999). Crown and nobility: England 1272–1461 (2
2 CONC nd ed.). Wiley-Blackwell. pp. 140–141. ISBN 9780631214663.  3.^ Plucknett, Theodore Frank Thomas (1983). Studies in English legal history. Continuum International Publishing Group. p. 159. ISBN 9780907628118.  4.^ Gay, Oonagh (30 
2 CONC November 2004). "Standard Note SN/PC/2666: Impeachment" (PDF). House of Commons Library. http://www.parliament.uk/commons/lib/research/notes/snpc-02666.pdf. Retrieved 1 November 2009. 
2 CONT
2 CONT '''References'''
2 CONT
2 CONT Holmes, George (January 2008). "Latimer, William, fourth Baron Latimer (1330–1381)". Oxford Dictionary of National Biography. Oxford University Press. doi:10.1093/ref:odnb/16103. http://www.oxforddnb.com/view/article/16103. Retrie
2 CONC ved 1 November 2009.  subscription or UK public library membership required
2 CONT
