2 CONC liam regis Edwardi filii regis Henrici tertii”, adding that Eneas died after his father and was buried “apud Waldene”[625].  The History of the foundation of Walden abbey records the birth “apud ---” of “Eneas” to “Humfridus de Bo
2 CONC hun” and his wife “Elizabethæ…regis Angliæ Edwardi…filiæ”[626].  He is mentioned in his father's will, but deceased in the 19 Jan 1344 ordination of the chantry of his brother William[627].  The History of the foundation of Walden
2 CONC  abbey records the death “1331 circa festum sancti Michaelis…apud Kymbolton” of “Æneas de Bohun” and his burial at Walden[628]. 
