2 CONT ==notes==
2 CONT
2 CONT From [http://discovery.nationalarchives.gov.uk/details/rd/034e2859-9115-47b2-9193-2b1eb5b482bd The National Archives - The Brotherton Inheritance]
2 CONT
2 CONT >The Bigod lands and title were immediately earmarked for Thomas of Brotherton, a younger son of Edward I born in 1300. In 1310 Thomas's father granted to him the lands and in 1312 the title as well, but Thomas died in 1338 leavin
2 CONC g only two daughters as his heirs, his son Edward having died without issue shortly before. The estate was then divided between his widow Mary (d. 1362), Edward's widow Beatrice (d. 1383), and the two daughters. [Edward had died a
2 CONC nd Beatrice was married to Thomas de Breouse by Sept. 1337: GEC ix. 599.] The younger, Alice, married Edward Montague (d. 1361) and had a son and four daughters of whom only two daughters survived their father: Maud entered Barkin
2 CONC g Abbey and Joan married William de Ufford, who succeeded his father as earl of Suffolk in 1369. Joan and all four of her sons died without surviving issue in 1375, and on William's death in 1382 her portion of the Brotherton inhe
2 CONC ritance passed to her aunt, Thomas of Brotherton's elder daughter Margaret.
2 CONT
