2 CONT In 1338, Margaret inherited the Earldom of Norfolk when her father died, becoming the 2nd Countess of Norfolk. Along with this title came the office of Lord Marshal. To date, she is the only woman to have served in this position (
2 CONC or, as it was called in the future, Earl Marshal).
2 CONT
2 CONT [edit] Second marriage
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1350, Margaret and John de Segrave began seeking a divorce based on the premise that they were contracted in marriage before she was of age, and that she had never consented to marry him. Furthermore, she had begun a relationsh
2 CONC ip with a knight of great renown, Sir Walter Manny, 1st Baron Manny, as she crossed the channel in October 1350 to meet with him in Calais, without the king's permission. The inquisition regarding this incident shows that Margaret
2 CONC  illegally crossed the Channel and met with Manny's servant, who broke his lantern with his foot so she could pass unnoticed.
2 CONT
2 CONT John de Segrave died in 1353, before their divorce could be finalized; the next year Margaret wed Walter Manny shortly before 30 May 1354, and they had two more children:
2 CONT
2 CONT    1. Thomas Manny, drowned in a well as a boy.
2 CONT
2 CONT    2. Anne Manny, 2nd Baroness Manny, wife of John Hastings, 2nd Earl of Pembroke
2 CONT
2 CONT [edit] Later life
2 CONT
2 CONT Sir Walter died in 1371. Margaret never remarried, and as a widow was created Duchess of Norfolk for life in 1397, and her grandson Thomas Mowbray, 1st Duke of Norfolk was created duke the same year. Margaret, Duchess of Norfolk, 
2 CONC died in 1399 and was buried in the choir of the Grey Friars, London.
2 CONT
