2 CONT http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Margaret_Plantagenet,_Duchess_of_Norfolk
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT Margaret, Duchess of Norfolk (also Countess of Norfolk, Lady Manny and Lady de Segrave, later construct Plantagenet; c. 1320 – 24 March 1399) was Countess of Norfolk from 1338 to 1399. 
2 CONT
2 CONT She was the daughter of Thomas of Brotherton, 1st Earl of Norfolk, by his first wife Alice Hayles. She became heiress to the Earldom after the death of her childless brother, Edward, in 1334.
2 CONT
2 CONT first marriage
2 CONT
2 CONT She married firstly in 1337 to Sir John de Segrave, 4th Baron Segrave by whom she had four children:
2 CONT
2 CONT Edmund de Segrave, died in the cradle. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Elizabeth de Segrave (1338-1368), married John de Mowbray, 4th Baron Mowbray 
2 CONT
2 CONT John de Segrave (13 September 1340 - 1349) 
2 CONT
2 CONT Anne de Segrave, Abbess of Barking 
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1338, Margaret inherited the Earldom of Norfolk when her father died, becoming the 2nd Countess of Norfolk. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Along with this title came the office of Lord Marshal.
2 CONT
2 CONT  To date, she is the only woman to have served in this position (or, as it was called in the future, Earl Marshal).
2 CONT
2 CONT ] Second marriage
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1350, Margaret and John de Segrave began seeking a divorce based on the premise that they were contracted in marriage before she was of age, and that she had never consented to marry him. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Furthermore, she had begun a relationship with a knight of great renown, Sir Walter Manny, 1st Baron Manny, as she crossed the channel in October 1350 to meet with him in Calais, without the king's permission.
2 CONT
2 CONT  The inquisition regarding this incident shows that Margaret illegally crossed the Channel and met with Manny's servant, who broke his lantern with his foot so she could pass unnoticed.
2 CONT
2 CONT John de Segrave died in 1353, before their divorce could be finalized; 
2 CONT
2 CONT the next year Margaret wed Walter Manny shortly before 30 May 1354, and they had two more children:
2 CONT
2 CONT Thomas Manny, drowned in a well as a boy. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Anne Manny, 2nd Baroness Manny, wife of John Hastings, 2nd Earl of Pembroke 
2 CONT
2 CONT  Later life
2 CONT
2 CONT Sir Walter died in 1371. Margaret never remarried, and as a widow was created Duchess of Norfolk for life in 1397,
2 CONT
2 CONT  and her grandson Thomas Mowbray, 1st Duke of Norfolk was created duke the same year. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Margaret, Duchess of Norfolk, died in 1399 and was buried in the choir of the Grey Friars, London.
2 CONT
