1 NOTE {geni:about_me} ===Biography 
2 CONT
2 CONT John died young and had no children who survived to adult, despite having already been married.
2 CONT
2 CONT This John is the source of some confusions which have only recently been resolved, because he is not mentioned in Complete Peerage. Complete Peerage perhaps even confuses some information about him with a cousin of the Segrave fam
2 CONC ily who were lords of Folkestone.
2 CONT
2 CONT As explained on the Medieval Genealogy website's Complete Peerage corrections pages, John appears to have been born at Bretby Castle, Derbyshire, 13 September 1340 [1]:
2 CONT
2 CONT He [John's father] had been predeceased by a son John, for whom a dispensation was granted in 1349 to marry Blanche, daughter of John Lord Mowbray [Complete Peerage, vol. 9, p. 384, note e; vol, 10, p. 662, note e]. Brad Verity, i
2 CONC n April 2003, gave further details of Lord Segrave's sons Edmund, who died in infancy, and John, born at Bretby Castle, Derbyshire, 13 September 1340, from C. W. Segrave, The Segrave Family: 1066 to 1935 (1936) [citing the Arundel
2 CONC l Manuscripts].
2 CONT Although John died young, he had already been involved in two marriage negotiations, one of which apparently went ahead. This is something Douglas Richardson has explained:[2]
2 CONT
2 CONT It is somewhat odd that Complete Peerage should have missed the second son of John de Segrave and Margaret of Norfolk named John, as mention of his existence has appeared elsewhere in print. It is known that the younger John de Se
2 CONC grave was contracted on about 4 May 1347 to marry Blanche of Lancaster, 3rd but 2nd surviving daughter and co-heiress of Henry of Lancaster, K.G., Duke of Lancaster, Earl of Derby, Lincoln, and Leicester, Steward of England (desce
2 CONC ndant of King Henry III), by Isabel, daughter of Henry de Beaumont, Knt., 1st Lord Beaumont, Earl of Buchan and Moray [in Scotland], hereditary Constable of Scotland [Reference: Berkeley Castle Muniments, BCM/D/5/101/8]. Blanche o
2 CONC f Lancaster was born about 1340–1343 (aged 18, 19, or 20 in 1361, aged 22 in 1362). This marriage agreement was subsequently voided, and the marriage did not take place. Blanche of Lancaster instead married John of Gaunt, Duke of 
2 CONC Lancaster, about whom much has been written in history books.
2 CONT
2 CONT John de Segrave was next dispensed to marry on 25 March 1349 Blanche Mowbray, daughter of John de Mowbray, Knt., 3rd Lord Mowbray, by his 1st wife, Joan, daughter of Henry of Lancaster, Knt., Earl of Lancaster [Reference: Papal Re
2 CONC gisters: Letters, 3 (1897): 305, available at the following weblink: http://books.google.com/books?id=AiDD_XU7P4kC&pg=PA305&dq=Papal+Registers+Blanche+Segrave].
2 CONT
2 CONT This marriage actually took place, and is duly noted in a pedigree of the Segrave family kept at Chaucombe Abbey:
2 CONT
2 CONT “… Predictus Johannes [de Segrave] nupsit Blanche filie Johannis domini de Mowbraye, & obiit sine herede de se.” [Reference: Nichols, History & Antiqs. of Leicestershire, 3(1) (1800): 240].
2 CONT
2 CONT As John de Segrave was earlier contracted to marry another party, so was Blanche Mowbray. I find that before she married John de Segrave, Blanche Mowbray was contracted in May 1343 to marry Edward de Montagu, son and heir apparent
2 CONC  of Edward de Montagu, Knt., Lord Montagu, by his 1st wife, Alice, daughter and co-heiress of Thomas of Brotherton, Knt., Earl of Norfolk, Marshal of England (younger son of King Edward I). This marriage agreement was subsequently
2 CONC  voided, and the marriage of Blanche and Edward did not take place.
2 CONT
2 CONT As explained by Chris Phillips, some of the confusion may come from Complete Peerage mixing up this John who married Blanche with a cousin from the Folkestone de Segrave family.[3]
2 CONT
2 CONT John de Segrave died BEFORE 1 April 1353 when his father died, meaning that his father's only heir was John's sister Elizabeth.
2 CONT
