1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Elisabeth of Bohemia (1292–1330)
2 CONT
2 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
2 CONT
2 CONT Elisabeth of Bohemia (Czech: Eliška Přemyslovna) (20 January 1292 – 28 September 1330) was Queen of Bohemia and mother of King of Bohemia and Holy Roman Emperor Charles IV.
2 CONT
2 CONT Childhood
2 CONT
2 CONT She was the daughter of Wenceslaus II of Bohemia and Judith of Habsburg. When Elisabeth was five years old, her mother died. Of the ten children only four of them lived to adulthood Wenceslaus, Anne, Elisabeth and Margaret, Elisab
2 CONC eth and her siblings also had a half-sister called Agnes. Six years after the death of her mother, her father Wenceslaus re-married. He chose to marry a Polish princess called Elisabeth Richeza of Poland from the Piast dynasty. El
2 CONC isabeth's father then gained the Crown of Poland.
2 CONT
2 CONT Elisabeth's youth noted a lot of things, a devastating fire at Prague Castle in 1303, the death of her father and the assassination of her brother Wenceslaus.
2 CONT
2 CONT [edit]The fight for the throne
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1306, after the murder of Elisabeth's brother Wenceslaus, Elisabeth's brother-in-law Henry became King of Bohemia. Elisabeth was now the only single princess in the family, she was fourteen years of age so was a good age to mar
2 CONC ry and she became one of the key players on the seizure of power disputes on the Kingdom of Bohemia.
2 CONT
2 CONT From the quarrels of the Bohemian throne between Henry of Bohemia and Rudolph of Habsburg resulted in Rudolph taking Bohemia and marrying Elisabeth Richeza of Poland (Elisabeth's stepmother). Elisabeth went to live in Prague Castl
2 CONC e with her brother's widow, Viola Elisabeth of Cieszyn. But in 1307 the throne returned to her brother-in-law and sister, because of Rudolph's death. They wanted Elisabeth to marry lord of Bergova (Otto of Löbdaburg) for political
2 CONC  reasons. Elisabeth refused to marry Otto and so Elisabeth and Anne fell out with each other.
2 CONT
2 CONT An opposition group was made against Henry and Anne, with Elisabeth as the figure head.
2 CONT
2 CONT [edit]Marriage to John of Luxembourg
2 CONT
2 CONT Elisabeth married the son of Henry VII, Holy Roman Emperor, John of Luxembourg. The wedding took place on September 1310, after John was forced to invade Bohemia. Henry and Anne fled to Carinthia were Anne died in 1313. The corona
2 CONC tion of John and Elisabeth took place on 7 February 1311.
2 CONT
2 CONT After a while Elisabeth grew jealous of John, who had listened to her but had different political opinions than her. Then in 1319 a plot was uncovered which had planned to despose of Elisabeth and John and replace them with their 
2 CONC eldest son Charles. John had the culprits punished but Elisabeth locked herself away at Mělník Castle and took her children with her.
2 CONT
2 CONT John decided to prevent his wife of interferring in the education of their children.
2 CONT
2 CONT Later years
2 CONT
2 CONT Elisabeth was in total isolation and abandoned by all, she left Bohemia and lived in exile in Bavaria. Some of the proceedings considered an act of open hostility towards John, and his noble likes. In exile Elisabeth gave birth to
2 CONC  her last children; twin daughters Anne and Elizabeth. John sent Elisabeth funding during her exile. Elisabeth returned to Bohemia in 1325. When she returned, John found Elisabeth to be ill but she still lived for another five yea
2 CONC rs. She eventually died of Tuberculosis in 1330 aged thirty eight.
2 CONT
2 CONT [edit]Children
2 CONT
2 CONT Elisabeth and John were parents to seven children
2 CONT
2 CONT Margaret (8 July 1313–11 July 1341, Prague), married in Straubing 12 August 1328 to Henry XIV, Duke of Bavaria.
2 CONT
2 CONT Bonne/ Jutta (21 May 1315–11 September 1349, Maubuisson Abbey, Saint-Ouen-l'Aumône), married in Melun 6 August 1332 to King John II of France.
2 CONT
2 CONT Charles IV (14 May 1316–29 November 1378), King of Bohemia and Holy Roman Emperor.
2 CONT
2 CONT Ottokar ("Otto") (22 November 1318–20 April 1320), Prince of Bohemia.
2 CONT
2 CONT John Henry (Jan Jindrich) (12 February 1322, Mělník–12 November 1375), Margrave of Moravia.
2 CONT
2 CONT Anne (1323–3 September 1338), twin of Elisabeth, married 16 February 1335 to Duke Otto of Austria.
2 CONT
2 CONT Elisabeth (1323–1324), twin of Anne
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT Elisabeth of Bohemia (1292-1330)
2 CONT
2 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
2 CONT
2 CONT Elisabeth I of Bohemia (Czech: Eliška Přemyslovna) (born 20 January 1292, died 28 September 1330) was Queen of Bohemia and mother of King of Bohemia and Holy Roman Emperor Charles IV.
2 CONT
2 CONT She was a daughter of King Venceslaus II of Bohemia and Judith of Habsburg. Her maternal grandparents were Rudolph I of Germany and Gertrude of Hohenberg.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1310, Elisabeth married John of Luxembourg who thus became King of Bohemia in her name. They were parents to seven children:
2 CONT
2 CONT Margaret (8 July 1313–11 July 1341, Prague), married in Straubing 12 August 1328 to Henry XIV, Duke of Bavaria.
2 CONT
2 CONT Bonne (21 May 1315–11 September 1349, Maubuisson), married in Melun 6 August 1332 to King John II of France.
2 CONT
2 CONT Charles IV (14 May 1316–29 November 1378), King of Bohemia and Holy Roman Emperor.
2 CONT
2 CONT Ottokar ("Otto") (22 November 1318–20 April 1320), Prince of Bohemia.
2 CONT
2 CONT John Henry (Jan Jindrich) (12 February 1322, Mělník–12 November 1375), Margrave of Moravia.
2 CONT
2 CONT Anna (1323–3 September 1338), twin of Elizabeth, married 16 February 1335 to Duke Otto of Austria.
2 CONT
2 CONT Elizabeth (1323–1324), twin of Anna
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT Élisabeth de Bohême (en tchèque : Eliška Přemyslovna) (20 janvier 1292 - 28 septembre 1330, Prague), reine de Bohême, fille de Venceslas II de Bohème et de Judith de Habsbourg. Elle est la sœur du dernier Přemyslide mâle, Vencesla
2 CONC s III dont la couronne va pendant quelques années à Henri de Görtz, le mari d'Anne de Bohême, la sœur puînée de Venceslas III et aînée d'Élisabeth.
2 CONT
2 CONT Sur l'échiquier politique européen, Élisabeth est, après la mort tragique de son frère, un pion essentiel. Le véritable enjeu de son mariage est le contrôle des riches mines d'argent de Kutná Hora. Sa sœur Anne et son beau-frère H
2 CONC enri songent tout d'abord à la marier au seigneur de Berg, un personnage falot. Avec l'aide de Conrad de Zbraslav, un coup d'État dynastique est organisé : le mariage d'Élisabeth avec le fils de l'Empereur du Saint-Empire et la dé
2 CONC position d'Henri de Görtz du trône de Bohême.
2 CONT
2 CONT Le 1er septembre 1310, âgée de dix-huit ans, elle apporte le royaume de Bohême en dot à Jean de Luxembourg qui en a quatorze. Le couronnement des époux a lieu le 7 février 1311.
2 CONT
2 CONT De cette union sont issus :
2 CONT
2 CONT     * Marguerite (8 juillet 1313 † 11 juillet 1341),
2 CONT
2 CONT     * Bonne (21 mai 1315 † 11 septembre 1349) mariée en 1332 à Jean II, Roi de France (1319-1364),
2 CONT
2 CONT     * Charles IV, Empereur, Roi de Bohême, comte de Luxembourg (1316 † 1378),
2 CONT
2 CONT     * Otakar (22 septembre 1318 † 20 avril 1320),
2 CONT
2 CONT     * Jean-Henri (1322 † 1375),
2 CONT
2 CONT     * Anne (27 mars 1323 † 3 septembre 1338)
2 CONT
2 CONT     * Elisabeth (27 mars 1323 † 1324).
2 CONT
2 CONT Elisabeth, reine de Bohême et comtesse de Luxembourg meurt de la tuberculose le 28 septembre 1330, âgée de trente-neuf ans.
2 CONT
