1 NOTE {geni:about_me} http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Anne_of_Bohemia_%281323%E2%80%931338%29
2 CONT
2 CONT Anne of Bohemia (1323–1338)
2 CONT
2 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
2 CONT
2 CONT Jump to: navigation, search
2 CONT
2 CONT Anne of Bohemia also known as Anna of Luxembourg (1323–3 September 1338) was a daughter of John of Bohemia and his first wife, Elizabeth of Bohemia. Anne was a member of the House of Luxemburg.[1]
2 CONT
2 CONT Contents
2 CONT
2 CONT [show]
2 CONT
2 CONT     * 1 Early Life and Family
2 CONT
2 CONT     * 2 Marriage
2 CONT
2 CONT     * 3 Ancestors
2 CONT
2 CONT     * 4 References
2 CONT
2 CONT [edit] Early Life and Family
2 CONT
2 CONT Anne was born 1323. At the time, her mother was living in exile in Bavaria, away from Bohemia. Anne had a twin sister, Elisabeth who was named after her mother but died aged only one. John sent his wife and new-born twin daughters
2 CONC  money during their exile. Elisabeth returned to Bohemia in 1325. She was only joined by Anne; Elisabeth the younger had died the year before. Her mother was never in great health after they returned, but she lived for another fiv
2 CONC e years. Elisabeth died in 1330, from Tuberculosis.
2 CONT
2 CONT Anne was a younger sister of Charles IV, Holy Roman Emperor, Bonne; first wife of John II of France, Margaret; wife of Henry XIV, Duke of Bavaria and John Henry, Margrave of Moravia.
2 CONT
2 CONT After his Elisabeth's death, John remarried to Beatrice of Bourbon. John and Beatrice had two children, Wenceslaus I, Duke of Luxembourg and Bonne, who died young.
2 CONT
2 CONT [edit] Marriage
2 CONT
2 CONT Anne was betrothed twice, before she married. Her first betrothal was to Ladislaus of Hungary, son of Charles I of Hungary and Elisabeth of Poland. The betrothal ended when Ladislaus died in 1329. If Ladislaus had survived and the
2 CONC  pair were married, Anne could have become Queen of Hungary.
2 CONT
2 CONT She was secondly betrothed to Louis VI the Roman. This betrothal ended because Louis' father, Louis IV, Holy Roman Emperor, could have been excommunicated by the Pope.
2 CONT
2 CONT Anne was eventually married on 25 March 1330 Otto, Duke of Austria. This was a second marriage for Otto, his first wife, Elizabeth of Bavaria had died but had left him two sons.
2 CONT
2 CONT The marriage, was good for John, it gave him a closer alliance with the emperor.
2 CONT
2 CONT The marriage lasted for eight years. During this time, Anne was still a child and was too young to have children. Anne and Otto had no children, and Anna died aged fifteen. Her husband died months later and was succeeded by his so
2 CONC ns from his first marriage to Elisabeth of Bavaria.[2][3]
2 CONT
2 CONT References
2 CONT
2 CONT    1. ^ Anne de Luxembourg
2 CONT
2 CONT    2. ^ Translation from Czech wikipedia, with further references
2 CONT
2 CONT    3. ^ Charles Cawley, Medieval Lands, BOHEMIA
2 CONT
