1 NAME Isabella /Plantagenet/
2 GIVN Isabella
2 _MARNM Plantagenet
1 SEX F
1 BIRT
2 DATE ABT 1330
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Isabella is a Fictional echo of the real https://www.geni.com/people/Isabella-Countess-of-Bedford/6000000003177905048, who married Enguerrand VII de Coucy (Edward III, her father, made him Earl of Bedford).
2 CONT
2 CONT "Having said so much of the [http://www.geni.com/people/Edward-the-Black-Prince/4048607033120128233 Prince of Wales], I cannot take leave of this subject without paying a just tribute to the memory of the illustrious Spaniard, who
2 CONC  was not only his contemporary, but singularly attached to his person, and to the English nation in general. This was '''[http://www.geni.com/people/Sancho-Mart%C3%ADnez-de-Leyva/6000000018908547737 Don Sancho Martinez de Leyva]''
2 CONC ', son of '''[http://www.geni.com/people/Juan-Mart%C3%ADnez-de-Leyva/6000000018928832217 Don John Martinez de Leyva]''', first lord of the bed-chamber to king '''[http://www.geni.com/people/Alfonso-XI-el-Justo-rey-de-Castilla-y-Le
2 CONC %C3%B3n/6000000000111122332 Alfonso XI.]''' and ''Adelantado Mayor'' of Castile. His son Sancho came over to England, where he offered his services to '''[http://www.geni.com/people/Edward-III-of-England/6000000000202115160 Edward
2 CONC  III.]''' and attended on '''[http://www.geni.com/people/John-of-Gaunt/6000000000595940091 John duke of Lancaster]''' in his expedition to Scotland. He afterwards went into France, and served under the Prince of Wales, with whom h
2 CONC e was at the famous battle and victory of Poitiers. After this he commanded the English army in [77] Picardy, and was very instrumental in the advantages gained over the French in those wars; and from his remarkable strength of bo
2 CONC dy was called ''Brazo de fierro'', or "Iron Arm." In recompence for his services, when he returned to England, Edward III. to testify his regard for his person, gave him in marriage his natural daughter '''Elizabeth''', by '''[htt
2 CONC p://www.geni.com/people/Isabella-Suffolk/6000000018908109924 Elizabeth Suffolk]''', countess of Northumberland, granting him further the privilege, in addition to his paternal coat armour, to quarter the armorial bearings of Engla
2 CONC nd." Dillon, 76-77.
2 CONT
2 CONT "There are ascribed to this king [Edward III] two natural children, ''viz. [http://www.geni.com/people/John-Baldac/6000000018908141565 John Baldac]'' 4, and ''Isabella'', married to ''Sancho Martinez de Leiva'' 5, a ''Spanish'' no
2 CONC bleman, who on her account quartered the ''English'' leopards in his ecutcheon." Carte, 539.
2 CONT
2 CONT For a discussion of Sancho and the supposed relationship of his wife to Edward III, see [https://groups.google.com/forum/?fromgroups=#!topic/soc.genealogy.medieval/k3J2T9VAxQA Casa de Leiva and ill dau of King of England] and [htt
2 CONC ps://groups.google.com/forum/?fromgroups=#!topic/soc.genealogy.medieval/nOQ3T3Hf4GU Edward III's illegitimate daughter ?] at soc.genealogy.medieval, visited Jan. 3, 2013.
2 CONT
2 CONT The bottom line in the above links is that there was no Earldom of Northumberland from c. 1191 until 1377 and the beginning of the reign of Richard II (grandson of Edward III).
2 CONT
2 CONT (Neither was there a "Suffolk" family - the Uffords were Earls of Suffolk during the reign of Edward III, but had no daughter Elizabeth or Isabel.)
2 CONT
2 CONT The timeline is seriously screwed up - the battle of Poitiers was in 1356, but John of Gaunt did not become Duke of Lancaster until 1362.
2 CONT
2 CONT Edward III's eldest *legitimate* daughter, Isabella, married Enguerrand VII de Coucy.
2 CONT
