1 NOTE {geni:about_me} '''Isabella of France Queen-Consort of England & Lady of Ireland & Duchess of Aquitaine''' 
2 CONT
2 CONT -------------------- From: http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Isabella_of_France
2 CONT
2 CONT Isabella of France (c. 1295 – 22 August 1358), known as the She-Wolf of France,[1] was the Queen consort of Edward II of England and mother of Edward III. She was the youngest surviving child and only surviving daughter of Philip 
2 CONC IV of France and Joan I of Navarre.
2 CONT
2 CONT Contents:
2 CONT
2 CONT 1 Early life 
2 CONT
2 CONT 2 Marriage 
2 CONT
2 CONT 3 Issue 
2 CONT
2 CONT 4 Affairs 
2 CONT
2 CONT 5 Ambassador to France 
2 CONT
2 CONT 6 Coup d'état 
2 CONT
2 CONT 7 Fall and retirement 
2 CONT
2 CONT 8 Later life 
2 CONT
2 CONT 9 Titles, styles, honours and arms 
2 CONT
2 CONT 9.1 Titles and styles 
2 CONT
2 CONT 10 In fiction 
2 CONT
2 CONT 11 Ancestry 
2 CONT
2 CONT 12 See also 
2 CONT
2 CONT 13 Notes 
2 CONT
2 CONT 14 Sources 
2 CONT
2 CONT 15 External links 
2 CONT
2 CONT Early life:
2 CONT
2 CONT Isabella, third from left, with her father, Philip IV, her future French king brothers, and King Philip's brother Charles of ValoisIsabella was born in Paris on an uncertain date, probably between May and November 1295 [2], to Kin
2 CONC g Philip IV of France and Queen Jeanne of Navarre; she was also (in time) the sister of three French kings. Isabella was not titled a 'princess', as daughters of European monarchs were not given that style until later in history. 
2 CONC Royal women were usually titled 'Lady' or an equivalent in other languages.
2 CONT
2 CONT Marriage:
2 CONT
2 CONT While still an infant, Isabella was promised in marriage by her father to Edward II; the intention was to resolve the conflicts between France and England over the latter's continental possession of Gascony and claims to Anjou, No
2 CONC rmandy and Aquitaine. Pope Boniface VIII had urged the marriage as early as 1298 but was delayed by wrangling over the terms of the marriage contract. The English king, Edward I had also attempted to break the engagement several t
2 CONC imes. Only after he died, in 1307, did the wedding proceed.
2 CONT
2 CONT Isabella's groom, the new King Edward II, looked the part of a Plantagenet king to perfection. He was tall, athletic, and wildly popular at the beginning of his reign. Isabella and Edward were married at Boulogne-sur-Mer on 25 Jan
2 CONC uary 1308. Since he had ascended the throne the previous year, Isabella never held the title Princess of Wales.
2 CONT
2 CONT At the time of her marriage, Isabella was probably about twelve and was described by Geoffrey of Paris as "the beauty of beauties... in the kingdom if not in all Europe." These words may not merely have represented the standard po
2 CONC liteness and flattery of a royal by a chronicler, since Isabella's father and brother are described as very handsome men in the historical literature. Isabella was said to resemble her father, and not her mother Jeanne of Navarre,
2 CONC  a plump woman of high complexion.[3] This would indicate that Isabella was slender and pale-skinned.
2 CONT
2 CONT Issue:
2 CONT
2 CONT Edward and Isabella did manage to produce four children, and she suffered at least one miscarriage. Their itineraries demonstrate that they were together 9 months prior to the births of all four surviving offspring. Their children
2 CONC  were:
2 CONT
2 CONT Edward III of Windsor, born 1312 
2 CONT
2 CONT John of Eltham, Earl of Cornwall, born 1316 
2 CONT
2 CONT Eleanor of Woodstock, born 1318, married Reinoud II of Guelders 
2 CONT
2 CONT Joan of the Tower, born 1321, married David II of Scotland 
2 CONT
2 CONT Affairs:
2 CONT
2 CONT Isabella returns to England with her son, Edward III. Jean Fouquet, 1455x1460.Although Isabella produced four children, the apparently bisexual king was notorious for lavishing sexual attention on a succession of male favourites, 
2 CONC including Piers Gaveston and Hugh le Despenser the younger. Isabella despised the royal favorite, Hugh le Despenser, and in 1321, while pregnant with her youngest child, she dramatically begged Edward to banish Despenser from the 
2 CONC kingdom. Despenser was exiled, but Edward recalled him later that year. This act seems finally to have turned Isabella against her husband altogether. While the nature of her relationship with Roger Mortimer is unknown for this ti
2 CONC me period, she may have helped him escape from the Tower of London in 1323. Later, she openly took Mortimer as her lover. He was married to the wealthy heiress Joan de Geneville, and the father of twelve children.
2 CONT
2 CONT Ambassador to France:
2 CONT
2 CONT When Isabella's brother, King Charles IV of France, seized Edward's French possessions in 1325, she returned to France, initially as a delegate of the King charged with negotiating a peace treaty between the two countries. However
2 CONC , her presence in France became a focal point for the many nobles opposed to Edward's reign. Isabella gathered an army to oppose Edward, in alliance with Roger Mortimer, 1st Earl of March. Enraged by this treachery, Edward demande
2 CONC d that Isabella return to England. Her brother, King Charles, replied, "The queen has come of her own will and may freely return if she wishes. But if she prefers to remain here, she is my sister and I refuse to expel her."
2 CONT
2 CONT Coup d'état:
2 CONT
2 CONT Despite this public show of support by the King of France, Isabella and Mortimer left the French court in summer 1326 and went to William I, Count of Hainaut in Holland, whose wife was Isabella's cousin. William provided them with
2 CONC  eight men of war ships in return for a marriage contract between his daughter Philippa and Isabella's son, Edward. On 21 September 1326, Isabella and Mortimer landed in Suffolk with an army, most of whom were mercenaries. King Ed
2 CONC ward II offered a reward for their deaths and is rumoured to have carried a knife in his hose with which to kill his wife. Isabella responded by offering twice as much money for the head of Hugh the younger Despenser. This reward 
2 CONC was issued from Wallingford Castle.
2 CONT
2 CONT This computer-enhanced image is of Queen Isabella riding triumphantly into Oxford in 1327. It comes from the 'Chronicle of the Counts of Flanders' made in the 15th c. by an artist who worked for Mary of Burgundy.The invasion by Is
2 CONC abella and Mortimer was successful: King Edward's few allies deserted him without a battle; the Despensers were killed, and Edward himself was captured and forced to abdicate in favour of his eldest son, Edward III of England. Sin
2 CONC ce the young king was only fourteen when he was crowned on 1 February 1327, Isabella and Mortimer ruled as regents in his place.
2 CONT
2 CONT According to legend, Isabella and Mortimer famously plotted to murder the deposed king in such a way as not to draw blame on themselves, sending the famous order (in Latin: Eduardum occidere nolite timere bonum est) which, dependi
2 CONC ng on where the comma was inserted, could mean either "Do not be afraid to kill Edward; it is good" or "Do not kill Edward; it is good to fear". In actuality, there is little evidence of just who decided to have Edward assassinate
2 CONC d, and none whatsoever of the note ever having been written. Alison Weir's biography of Isabella puts forward the theory that Edward II in fact escaped death and fled to Europe, where he lived as a hermit for twenty years.
2 CONT
2 CONT Fall and retirement:
2 CONT
2 CONT When Edward III turned 18, he and a few trusted companions staged a coup on 19 October 1330 and had both Isabella and Mortimer taken prisoner. Despite Isabella's cries of "Fair son, have pity on gentle Mortimer", Mortimer was exec
2 CONC uted for treason one month later in November of 1330.
2 CONT
2 CONT Her son spared Isabella's life and she was allowed to retire to Castle Rising in Norfolk. She did not, as legend would have it, go insane; she enjoyed a comfortable retirement and made many visits to her son's court, doting on her
2 CONC  grandchildren.
2 CONT
2 CONT Later life:
2 CONT
2 CONT Isabella took the habit of the Poor Clares before she died on 22 August 1358, and her body was returned to London for burial at the Franciscan church at Newgate. She was buried in her wedding dress. Edward's heart was interred wit
2 CONC h her.
2 CONT
2 CONT Titles, styles, honours and arms:
2 CONT
2 CONT Sophie Marceau as Princess Isabella in Braveheart
2 CONT
2 CONT Titles and styles:
2 CONT
2 CONT Lady Isabella of France 
2 CONT
2 CONT Isabella, by the grace of God, Queen of England, Lady of Ireland and Duchess of Aquitaine 
2 CONT
2 CONT In fiction:
2 CONT
2 CONT Queen Isabella appears as a major character in Christopher Marlowe's play Edward II, and in Derek Jarman's 1991 film based on the play and bearing the same name. She is played by actress Tilda Swinton as a 'femme fatale' whose thw
2 CONC arted love for Edward causes her to turn against him and steal his throne.
2 CONT
2 CONT In the film Braveheart, directed by and starring Mel Gibson, Isabella was played by the French actress Sophie Marceau. In the film, Isabella is depicted as having a romantic affair with the Scottish hero William Wallace, who is po
2 CONC rtrayed as the real father of her son Edward III. This is entirely fictional, as there is no evidence that the two ever met. If they did meet at the time the movie was set, Isabella would have only been three years old.
2 CONT
2 CONT Isabella has also been the subject of a number of historical novels, including Margaret Campbell Barnes' Isabel the Fair, Hilda Lewis' Harlot Queen, Maureen Peters' Isabella, the She-Wolf, Brenda Honeyman's The Queen and Mortimer,
2 CONC  Paul Doherty's The Cup of Ghosts, Jean Plaidy's The Follies of the King, and Edith Felber's Queen of Shadows. She is the title character of The She-Wolf of France by the well-known French novelist Maurice Druon. The series of whi
2 CONC ch the book was part, The Accursed Kings, has been adapted for French television in 1972 and 2005. Most recently, Isabella figures prominently in The Traitor's Wife: A Novel of the Reign of Edward II by Susan Higginbotham. Also, K
2 CONC en Follett's 2007 novel, World Without End uses the alleged murder of Edward II (and the infamous letter) as a plot device. Susan Howatch's Cashelmara and The Wheel of Fortune, based on the lives of the Plantagenet kings, depict h
2 CONC er as a young abused wife and an old widow hidden from her grandchildren in a retirement home run by nuns.
2 CONT
2 CONT Ancestors:
2 CONT
2 CONT Isabella of France's ancestors in three generations Isabella of France:
2 CONT
2 CONT Father: Philip IV of France 
2 CONT
2 CONT Paternal Grandfather: Philip III of France 
2 CONT
2 CONT Paternal Great-grandfather: Louis IX of France 
2 CONT
2 CONT Paternal Great-grandmother: Marguerite of Provence 
2 CONT
2 CONT Paternal Grandmother: Isabella of Aragon 
2 CONT
