1 NOTE {geni:about_me} http://genealogics.org/getperson.php?personID=I00007049&tree=LEO
2 CONT
2 CONT Joan, The Fair Maid of Kent, Countess of Kent, Princess of Wales, Baroness Woodstock and Baroness Wake of Liddell suo iure, Princess of Aquitaine, and Countess of Salisbury. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Joan, Countess of Kent (29 September 1328 – 7 August 1385) is known to history as The Fair Maid of Kent. French chronicler Jean Froissart called her "the most beautiful woman in all the realm of England, and the most loving."
2 CONT
2 CONT Joan is often identified as the countess of Salisbury who, legend says, inspired Edward III's founding of the Order of the Garter.It is just as possible, though, that that countess was her mother-in-law, Catherine Montacute, Count
2 CONC ess of Salisbury.
2 CONT
2 CONT Titles: Princess of Wales, Princess of Aquitaine, Countess of Salisbury, Countess of Kent, Baroness Wake of Liddell.
2 CONT
2 CONT ==Family==
2 CONT * Father:	Edmund of Woodstock, 1st Earl of Kent
2 CONT * Mother: Margaret Wake, 3rd Baroness Wake of Liddell
2 CONT * Spouses: 1) Thomas Holland, 1st Earl of Kent, 2) William Montacute, 2nd Earl of Salisbury, 3) Edward, Prince of Wales
2 CONT
2 CONT Issue
2 CONT # Edmund Holland
2 CONT # Thomas Holland, 2nd Earl of Kent
2 CONT # John Holland, 1st Duke of Exeter
2 CONT # Joan Holland, Duchess of Brittany
2 CONT # Maud Holland, Countess of Ligny
2 CONT # Edward of Angoulême
2 CONT # Richard II of England
2 CONT
2 CONT Links
2 CONT * http://fmg.ac/Projects/MedLands/ENGLISH%20NOBILITY%20MEDIEVAL.htm#Joandied1385
2 CONT * http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Joan,_Countess_of_Kent
2 CONT * http://www.thepeerage.com/p10206.htm#i102052
2 CONT * http://www.findagrave.com/cgi-bin/fg.cgi?page=gr&GRid=46672269
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT http://www.zipworld.com.au/~lnbdds/home/quaker.htm
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT She was called "The Fair Maid of Kent" because of her extraordinary beauty. She was celebrated as one of the most beautiful women of her time and was probably the heroine upon which the "Order of the Garter" was founded. The Frenc
2 CONC h chronicler Froissart called Joan "the most beautiful woman in all the realm of England, and the most loving." She was much loved by the people of England and by her family.
2 CONT
2 CONT Joan of Kent was born in 1328 to Edmund of Woodstock Plantagenet, 1st Earl of Kent, son of King Edward I of England, and Margaret Wake, 3rd Baroness Wake of Liddell. She was the third of four children. Her father, the Earl of Kent
2 CONC , was executed for political reasons when Joan was only a toddler. Her cousin, King Edward III, took responsibility for the family, and brought them to live at the royal court with him.
2 CONT
2 CONT When Joan was twelve years old, she fell in love with a soldier named Thomas Holland. They married in secret, without the approval of Joan's parents. However, that same year, Thomas was sent overseas to fight in the Hundred Years'
2 CONC  War, and that winter, Joan's parents married her to William Montague, son of the 1st Earl of Salisbury. Joan did not disclose her previous marriage to Thomas because she feared that he would be executed for treason.
2 CONT
2 CONT Several years later, Thomas returned to England and discovered that his wife had been married to another man. Now, Thomas confessed his secret marriage to Joan in the hopes that her marriage to Montague would be declared invalid. 
2 CONC When Montague discovered that Joan supported Thomas's case, he became very angry and locked Joan in their home as a prisoner. The marriage between Joan and Montague was eventually annulled in 1349, when Joan was twenty-one. Joan t
2 CONC hen went to live with Thomas, and the happily reunited couple had several children before Thomas's death in 1360.
2 CONT
2 CONT Their children were: Thomas Holland, 2nd Earl of Kent, born 1350; John Holland, 1st Duke of Exeter, born circa 1352; Joan Holland, born 1356, who married John V, Duke of Brittany; and Maud Holland, born 1359, who married Waleran I
2 CONC II of Luxembourg, Count of Ligny. Some sources also list a fifth child, Edmund Holland, born 1354, who died young.
2 CONT
2 CONT Joan inherited the titles of Countess of Kent and Lady Wake of Liddell in 1352 with the death of the last of her siblings.
2 CONT
2 CONT Joan's second marriage in 1651 was to her first cousin once removed, Edward the Black Prince, the eldest son of King Edward III. Though their marriage would have been forbidden because they were closely related, Pope Innocent VI i
2 CONC ntervened and granted a dispensation which allowed the couple to be married. 
2 CONT
2 CONT When Edward was invested Prince of Aquitaine, the couple moved to France, where they had their two children, Edward, born 1365, and Richard, born 1367.
2 CONT
2 CONT She requested in her will she be buried with her first husband, Sir Thomas, at Grefriars Church, which is now the site of a hospital. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Bio by Charlotte, #47579980
2 CONT
2 CONT https://www.jstor.org/stable/10.7722/j.ctt1kgqvnt
2 CONT https://www.wikitree.com/wiki/Plantagenet-125
2 CONT https://www.thoughtco.com/joan-of-kent-3529659
2 CONT https://www.luminarium.org/encyclopedia/joanofkent.htm
2 CONT https://ancestors.familysearch.org/en/KDQ8-TWV/joan-of-kent-1326-1385
2 CONT https://www.englishmonarchs.co.uk/plantagenet_39.html
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT From: http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Joan_of_Kent
2 CONT
2 CONT Princess Joan, LG, suo jure 4th Countess of Kent, 5th Baroness Wake of Liddell (19 September 1328 – 7 August 1385), known to history as The Fair Maid of Kent, was the first post-conquest Princess of Wales as wife to Edward, the Bl
2 CONC ack Prince, son and heir of King Edward III. Although the French chronicler Jean Froissart called her "the most beautiful woman in all the realm of England, and the most loving", the appellation "Fair Maid of Kent" does not appear
2 CONC  to be contemporary.[1] Joan assumed the title of 4th Countess of Kent and 5th Baroness Wake of Liddell after the death of her brother, John, in 1352.
2 CONT
2 CONT Joan was the daughter of Edmund of Woodstock, 1st Earl of Kent, and Margaret Wake, 3rd Baroness Wake of Liddell.[2] Her father Edmund was the son of King Edward I and his second wife, Margaret of France, daughter of Philip III of 
2 CONC France. Edmund's support of his older half-brother, King Edward II of England, placed him in conflict with the queen, Isabella of France, and her lover Roger Mortimer, 1st Earl of March. Edmund was executed after Edward II's depos
2 CONC ition, and Joan's mother, along with her children, was placed under house arrest in Arundel Castle when Joan was only two years old. Early life
2 CONT
2 CONT The Earl's widow, Margaret, was left with four children for whom to care. Joan's first cousin, the new King Edward III, took on the responsibility for the family, and looked after them well. His wife, Queen Philippa, was Joan's se
2 CONC cond cousin. Marriages
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1340, at the age of twelve, Joan entered into a clandestine marriage with Thomas Holland of Upholland, Lancashire without first gaining the royal consent necessary for couples of their rank.[3] The following winter (1340 or 134
2 CONC 1), while Holland was overseas, her family forced her to marry William Montacute, son and heir of the first Earl of Salisbury. Joan later averred that she did not disclose her existing marriage with Thomas Holland because she had 
2 CONC been afraid that disclosing it would lead to Thomas's execution for treason upon his return. She may also have become convinced that the earlier marriage was invalid.[4]
2 CONT
2 CONT Several years later, Thomas Holland returned from the Crusades, having made his fortune, and the full story of his earlier relationship with Joan came out. He appealed to the Pope for the return of his wife and confessed the secre
2 CONC t marriage to the king. When the Earl of Salisbury discovered that Joan supported Holland’s case, he kept her a prisoner in her own home.[5] In 1349, Pope Clement VI annulled Joan’s marriage to the Earl and sent her back to Thomas
2 CONC  Holland, with whom she lived for the next eleven years. They had four known children (though some sources list five), before Holland died in 1360.[citation needed]
2 CONT
2 CONT Their children were:
2 CONT
2 CONT     Thomas Holland, 2nd Earl of Kent
2 CONT     John Holland, 1st Duke of Exeter
2 CONT     Lady Joan Holland (1356–1384), who married John V, Duke of Brittany (1339–1399).
2 CONT     Lady Maud Holland (1359–1391), who married firstly to Hugh Courtenay and secondly to Waleran III of Luxembourg, Count of Ligny (1355–1415).
2 CONT     Edmund Holland (c. 1354), who died young. He was buried in the church of Austin Friars, London.[6]
2 CONT
2 CONT When the last of Joan's siblings died in 1352, she became the 4th Countess of Kent and 5th Lady Wake of Liddell. Descendants of Lady Joan and Thomas Holland include Lady Margaret Beaufort (mother of King Henry VII) and queen conso
2 CONC rts Anne Neville, Elizabeth of York, and Catherine Parr.[citation n
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT From: http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Joan_of_Kent
2 CONT
2 CONT Princess Joan, LG, suo jure 4th Countess of Kent, 5th Baroness Wake of Liddell (19 September 1328 – 7 August 1385), known to history as The Fair Maid of Kent, was the first post-conquest Princess of Wales as wife to Edward, the Bl
2 CONC ack Prince, son and heir of King Edward III. Although the French chronicler Jean Froissart called her "the most beautiful woman in all the realm of England, and the most loving", the appellation "Fair Maid of Kent" does not appear
2 CONC  to be contemporary.[1] Joan assumed the title of 4th Countess of Kent and 5th Baroness Wake of Liddell after the death of her brother, John, in 1352.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1340, at the age of twelve, Joan entered into a clandestine marriage with Thomas Holland of Upholland, Lancashire without first gaining the royal consent necessary for couples of their rank.[3] The following winter (1340 or 134
2 CONC 1), while Holland was overseas, her family forced her to marry William Montacute, son and heir of the first Earl of Salisbury. Joan later averred that she did not disclose her existing marriage with Thomas Holland because she had 
2 CONC been afraid that disclosing it would lead to Thomas's execution for treason upon his return. She may also have become convinced that the earlier marriage was invalid.[4]
2 CONT
2 CONT Several years later, Thomas Holland returned from the Crusades, having made his fortune, and the full story of his earlier relationship with Joan came out. He appealed to the Pope for the return of his wife and confessed the secre
2 CONC t marriage to the king. When the Earl of Salisbury discovered that Joan supported Holland’s case, he kept her a prisoner in her own home.[5] In 1349, Pope Clement VI annulled Joan’s marriage to the Earl and sent her back to Thomas
2 CONC  Holland, with whom she lived for the next eleven years. They had four known children (though some sources list five), before Holland died in 1360.[citation needed]
