1 NOTE {geni:about_me} ====Conjectured Name for Concubine #1
2 CONT
2 CONT * As of 7 Sep 2022, [https://fmg.ac/Projects/MedLands/ENGLAND,%20Kings%201066-1603.htm#Edwarddied1376 ''Medlands''] does not support a name for the mother of Sir Roger of Clarendon. It only prefaces his mother thus: "Edward Prince
2 CONC  of Wales had one illegitimate son by Mistress (1): ROGER de Clarendon..."
2 CONT * Douglas Richardson only cites ''The Complete Peerage'':– “Illegitimate child of Edward of Woodstock, by a mistress, Edith de Willesford. She was living 8 May 1385. ''C.P.R.'' 1361–1385 (1897): 562. — i. ROGER (DE) CLARENDON….” —
2 CONC  '''Note:''' Cokayne relied on the conclusion of George-Frederick Beltz.
2 CONT
2 CONT >'''Books that name the mother of Sir Roger of Clarendon default to one secondary source author's conjecture, or they cite no source at all.''' That author was George-Frederick Beltz. Beltz referred to two primary sources from whi
2 CONC ch he conjectured that Edith de Willesford was the mother of Sir Roger. From his 1841 book titled [https://www.google.com/books/edition/Memorials_of_the_Order_of_the_Garter_fro/vcRcAAAAcAAJ?hl=en&gbpv=1&dq=%22Edith+de+Willesford%2
2 CONC 2&pg=PA17&printsec=frontcover ''Memorials of the Order of the Garter, from Its Foundation to the Present Time, etc.''], Beltz wrote on page 17, footnote. 1:– 
2 CONT >“Register “Sudbury,” ''fo''. 90, in the archiep. regy at Lambeth. It is also printed in Nichols’ Royal Wills, p. 66. It contains, among other bequests, one of a silk bed to SIR ROGER DE CLARENDON. This person was Edward’s natural
2 CONC  son by, '''as it is supposed''', Edith de Willesford, of whom mention is made in ''Rot, Pat.'' 8 ''Ric.'' 2, ''p.'' 2 ''m.'' 13.”
2 CONT
2 CONT ====Sketchy Sources
2 CONT * In Thelma Anna Leese's1996 book titled [https://www.google.com/books/edition/Blood_Royal/OvscAAAAYAAJ?hl=en&gbpv=1&bsq=%22Edith+de+Willesford%22&dq=%22Edith+de+Willesford%22&printsec=frontcover ''Blood Royal: Issue of the Kings 
2 CONC and Queens of Medieval England, 1066-1399: the Normans and Plantagenets''], she mentioned Edward's illegitimate son John Sounder—that he is "believed to be the child of Edith de Willesford." He was "honorably mentioned by [the chr
2 CONC onicler] Froissart. A second illegitimate son was Roger de Clarendon, who was executed by his cousin, Henry IV (Sedgwick, ''The Black Prince'', p. 169)." — ''Note that Leese does not include a name for Roger's mother.''
2 CONT * From [https://www.google.com/books/edition/Britain_s_Royal_Families/7nZ90l1_IzAC?hl=en&gbpv=1&dq=%22Edith+de+Willesford%22&pg=PA95&printsec=frontcover Weir's "Britain's Royal Family"]:– "...By Edith de Willesford (d. after 1385)
2 CONC : 1. Sir Roger de Clarendon..." — '''''Note:''''' Weir's ''Britain's Royal Family'' is not a secondary source. While most lineal relationships and names may be correct, this book seldom refers to primary sources to underpin the in
2 CONC cluded content.
2 CONT * Brian Bevan's 2016 book titled [https://www.google.com/books/edition/Henry_IV/IJMYDAAAQBAJ?hl=en&gbpv=1&dq=%22Edith+de+Willesford%22&pg=PA93&printsec=frontcover ''Henry IV''] merely echoes the same sentiment, '''but he unacademi
2 CONC cally cites no source''': "We know nothing about Roger's mother, except that her name was Edith de Willesford,..."
