1 NOTE     This individual has the following other parents in the Ancestral File:
2 CONT     Enguerrand Vi /COUCY/ (AFN:9RLS-S6) and Katharine, /AUSTRIA/ (AFN:8GD8-N1)
1 NOTE OR "ENGUERRAND"; EARL OF BEDFORD; KG
1 NOTE Enguerrand VII de Coucy, since he had first marched at age fifteen against the English, at 18 hunted down the Jacquerie, the range of de Coucy's experience had extended over an extraordinary variety of combat, diplomacy, governmen
2 CONC t, and social and political relationships. Son-in-law of King Edward III, holding double allegiance to two kings at war, subject of King Jean of France, his position in the annals of English-French history was unique.  He had seen
2 CONC  war as captain of one of the top in comman in eleven campaigns - in Piedmont, Lombardy, Switzerland, Normandy, Languedoc, Tuscanny, northern France, Flanders, Guelders, Tunisia, Genoa, and commanded mercanaries, fought at ally or
2 CONC  antagonist of the Count of Savoy; Gregory XI; Hawkwood; the Visconti of Milan; the Hapsburgs of Austria; the Swiss; Navarrest; Gascons; English; Berbers; the Republic of Florence;and Nobles of Genoa.  He had been diplomat negotia
2 CONC ted with Pope Clement VII; the duke of Brittany; the Count of Flanders; the Queen of Aragon; with the English at peace parlays with France; and the rebels of Paris.  His marriage to Isabella of England, 8 years his senior, was one
2 CONC  of extravagance on her part; his 2nd marriage to Isabella of Lorraine, was one of a man trying to please a wife 30 years his junior.  He had served as adviser to three Kings, Edward III of England, Jean and Charles of France; adv
2 CONC iser to two royal Dukes, Anjou and Orleans; as Lt. General of Picardy and Guienne; Member of the French Royal Council; Grand Bouteiller of France; nominated twice for Constable of France (which he rejected). A truly remarkeable ma
2 CONC n and career of the 14th century.
2 CONT
2 CONT Enguerrand VII de Coucy was born about 1339, son of Enguerrand VI, and became the new Lord of Coucy, powerful man in France, holding lands, revenue, and a galaxy of armorial bearings, and twelve coats of arms of Boisgency; Hainaul
2 CONC t, Dreux, Saxony, Montmirail, Roucy, Baliol, Ponthieu, Chatillon, St. Pol, Gueldres, and Flanders.  Not to mention his mother's side to the Hapsburgs of Austria and Savoy.Enguerrand VII was born while his father was off doing batt
2 CONC le against the English in defense of Tournai on the Flemish border.  Enguerrand was the result of 114 years of Coucy family men, from 1339 to 1453 in five generations.  Actually the 12th generation from when the first Coucy took t
2 CONC he lands from the Bishop of Reims in the 10th Century.
2 CONT
2 CONT Enguerrand VII Sire de Coucy Count of Soissons, died in prison at Brusa, Turkey.    He was age 56.
2 CONT
2 CONT As per his last request, the embalmed heart (some say the body) and bones of Enguerrand VII Sire de Coucy, Count of Soissons, was returned to France was returned by Willay, and an impressive funeral of grandeur was conducted by th
2 CONC e Bishops of Noyon and Laon, the bones (or body) was buried in an imposing tomb at Nogent, and the heart at Ste Trinite, marked by a plaque showing an engraved heart super-imposed over the Coucy arms.  April 1397.
2 CONT
2 CONT After the battle of Nicosa, in which he became captive and prisoner of the Sultan Bejasat, Enguerrand VII de Coucy wrote his last will on 16 February 1397 while being held at Brusa (Bursa) in Turkey.  Probably dictated to the Phys
2 CONC ician Geoffrey Maupoivre, who had accompanied the crusade.  Written in Latin, it dictated his burial at Nogent; orders for a monastary at Ste Trinite; bequests to the Notre Dame de Chartres and others; he ordered his executors to 
2 CONC pay his debts, who included Comte d'Eu; Boucicaut; Guy de Tremoille; Guillaume de Tremoille, Jacques de la Marche, and six other French knights, who witnessed and signed the instrument.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1396 Enguerrand VII de Coucy was asked to lead the Crusade to Constantinople at the request of John V Paleologeous of Constantinople and the Philip Duke of Burgundy, to act as chief council to the young and inexperience Jean de
2 CONC  Nevers - son of Philip.
2 CONT
2 CONT Enguerrand de Coucy VII, declining the honor of Constable of France, was then appointed Captain-General of Picardy, which gave him the town, castle, and seigneury of Mortaigne on the northern French frontier between Tournai and Va
2 CONC lenciennes.  de Coucy took comman of Picardy July 19, 1380.
2 CONT
2 CONT When his father Enguerrand VI died in battle in 1346, the son, Enguerrand VII was only age 7, and inherited Coucy, Marle, LaFere, Boissy-en-Brie, Oisy-en-Cambresis, and their towns and dependencies. He was placed under the Regency
2 CONC  of his father's brother, his uncle Jean De Coucy, Sire de-Havraincourt, named as his guardian, tutor or adviser.  Enguerrand VII mother, Catherine remarried to Conrad de Magedbourg, and then died with a year, leaving the boy an o
2 CONC rphan.
2 CONT
2 CONT Shortly after the birth of his first child, a daughter, and granddaughter of King Edward III of England, the father, Enguerrand Lord de Coucy was named to the vacant earldom of Bedford with a revenue of 300 marks a year, and thus 
2 CONC became known as Ingelram, Earl of Bedford.  And also received the Order of the Garter.
2 CONT
2 CONT Having lost his father in 1346, nine year old Enguerrand de Coucy lost his mother, Catherine, and her new husband during the summer of 1349 to the plaque.
2 CONT
2 CONT A short two months after his father-in-law, King Edward III of England had died, Enguerrand de Coucy renounced his allegiance to England in a letter to the new King, Richard II.  Enguerrand then gave his full allegiance to Charles
2 CONC  King of France.  He then, also, became seperated from his wife, Isabella of England.
