2 CONT
2 CONT Louis of Male
2 CONT
2 CONT Louis II of Flanders (25 October 1330, Male – 30 January 1384, Lille), also Louis III of Artois and Louis I of Palatine Burgundy, known as Louis of Male, was the son of Louis I of Flanders and Margaret I of Burgundy, and Count of 
2 CONC Flanders.
2 CONT
2 CONT On his father's death at the Battle of Crécy in 1346, he inherited the counties of Flanders, Nevers, and Rethel. On the death of his father-in-law in 1355, he took the title of Duke of Brabant, but was unable to wrest the duchy fr
2 CONC om his sister-in-law Joanna, Duchess of Brabant.
2 CONT
2 CONT The latter years of his rule were marked by civil strife. In 1379, he obtained aid from his son-in-law, Philip II of Burgundy, to put down a revolt in Ghent. The Flemings again rose under Philip van Artevelde and expelled him from
2 CONC  Flanders after the Battle of Beverhoutsveld; however, the influence of Philip procured a French army to relieve him, and the Flemings were decisively defeated at the Battle of Roosebeke. However, the citizens of Ghent continued t
2 CONC o resist (with English aid) until after his death in 1384. His mother had died two years previously, leaving him the counties of Artois and the Franche-Comté.
2 CONT
2 CONT [edit] Family
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1347, he married Margaret of Brabant (1323–1368), daughter of John III, Duke of Brabant. They had three children:
2 CONT
2 CONT     * Peter (died young)
2 CONT
2 CONT     * Charles (predeceased his father)
2 CONT
2 CONT     * Margaret III, Countess of Flanders (1348–1405)
2 CONT
2 CONT He also left several illegitimate sons, three of whom were killed at the Battle of Nicopolis.
2 CONT
2 CONT As he left no surviving sons, on his death, his extensive possessions in the Low Countries were inherited by his daughter Margaret.
2 CONT
