1 NOTE {geni:about_me} '''Philippa of Lancaster''' (Portuguese: Filipa [fɨˈlipɐ]; 31 March 1360 – 19 July 1415) was Queen of Portugal from 1387 until 1415 by marriage to King John I. Born into the royal family of England, her marriage se
2 CONC cured the Treaty of Windsor and produced several children who became known as the "Illustrious Generation" in Portugal.
2 CONT
2 CONT Born on 31 March 1360, Philippa was the oldest child of John of Gaunt, 1st Duke of Lancaster and Blanche of Lancaster.
2 CONT
2 CONT ===Children
2 CONT Philippa was apparently a generous and loving queen, the mother of the "Illustrious Generation" (in Portuguese, Ínclita Geração) of infantes (princes) and infantas (princesses). Her children were:[12]
2 CONT * Blanche (13 July 1388 – 6 March 1389), died in infancy.
2 CONT * Alphonse (30 July 1390 – 22 December 1400), heir of the throne, died in childhood at the age of 10, in Braga, and was buried in Braga Cathedral.
2 CONT * Edward (Duarte) (31 October 1391 – 13 September 1438), a writer and an intellectual who succeeded his father as King of Portugal in 1433.
2 CONT * Peter (9 December 1392 – 20 May 1449), Duke of Coimbra, a well-travelled man who served as Regent during the minority of his nephew Afonso V.
2 CONT * Henry (4 March 1394 – 13 November 1460), called "the Navigator", first Duke of Viseu, who guided Portugal to the Age of Discovery.
2 CONT * Isabella (21 February 1397 – 11 December 1471), married Philip III of Burgundy.
2 CONT * Blanche (11 April 1398 – 27 July 1398), died in infancy.
2 CONT * John (13 January 1400 – 18 October 1442), Constable of Portugal, Lord of Reguengos, grandfather of two 16th century Iberian monarchs (Manuel I of Portugal and Isabella I of Castile).
2 CONT * Ferdinand (29 September 1402 – 5 June 1443), called "the Saint Prince", a warrior who was captured during the Disaster of Tangier in 1437 and died a prisoner of the Moors
2 CONT
2 CONT ===References 
