2 CONT 1.Katherine of Lancaster+ b. bt 6 Jun 1372 - 31 Mar 1373, d. 2 Jun 1418
2 CONT
2 CONT 2.John Plantagenet3 b. 1374, d. 1375
2 CONT
2 CONT Constance of Castile (1354 – 24 March 1394) was claimant of the Castilian throne after the death of her father Pedro the Cruel, her mother being a María de Padilla, whom Pedro had secretly married but was then forced to repudiate,
2 CONC  only to keep her as a mistress. She married, at Roquefort, near Bordeaux, Guienne, on 21 September 1371, John of Gaunt, 1st Duke of Lancaster, third son of Edward III of England and Philippa of Hainault, as his second wife. Her y
2 CONC ounger sister, Infanta Isabella, married Edmund of Langley, 1st Duke of York, the fourth son of King Edward III and Queen Philippa.
2 CONT
2 CONT This was the way for Gaunt to obtain a kingdom of his own (he had also pursued Scotland), as his nephew Richard II and the descendants of his brother Lionel of Antwerp, 1st Duke of Clarence stood between him and the Crown of Engla
2 CONC nd. John of Gaunt claimed the title of King of Castile jure uxoris, and insisted that English nobles address him as "my lord of Spain", but was unsuccessful in his attempts to obtain the crown. Their daughter Katherine of Lancaste
2 CONC r was married to the King of the Trastamara line, Henry III of Castile, thus uniting these two rival claims.
2 CONT
2 CONT * Katherine of Lancaster, married Henry III of Castile
2 CONT
2 CONT * John Plantagenet
2 CONT
2 CONT Constance died at Leicester Castle and was buried at Newark Abbey, Leicester.
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT Constance of Castile (1354 Castile – 24 March 1394 Leicester) was claimant of the Castilian throne after the death of her father Pedro the Cruel, her mother being a María de Padilla, whom Pedro had secretly married but was then fo
2 CONC rced to repudiate, only to keep her as a mistress. She married, at Roquefort, near Bordeaux, Guienne, on 21 September 1371, John of Gaunt, 1st Duke of Lancaster, third son of Edward III of England and Philippa of Hainault, as his 
2 CONC second wife. Her younger sister, Infanta Isabella, married Edmund of Langley, 1st Duke of York, the fourth son of King Edward III and Queen Philippa.
2 CONT
2 CONT This was the way for Gaunt to obtain a kingdom of his own (he had also pursued Scotland), as his nephew Richard II and the descendants of his brother Lionel of Antwerp, 1st Duke of Clarence stood between him and the Crown of Engla
2 CONC nd. John of Gaunt claimed the title of King of Castile jure uxoris, and insisted that English nobles address him as "my lord of Spain", but was unsuccessful in his attempts to obtain the crown. Their daughter Katherine of Lancaste
2 CONC r was married to the King of the Trastamara line, Henry III of Castile, thus uniting these two rival claims.
2 CONT
2 CONT Constance died at Leicester Castle and was buried at Newark Abbey, Leicester.
2 CONT
2 CONT [edit] Children
2 CONT
2 CONT Katherine of Lancaster (1372–1418), married King Henry III of Castile (1379–1406) 
2 CONT
2 CONT John Plantagenet (1372–1375) 
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT Constance of Castile (1354-1394)
2 CONT
2 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
2 CONT
2 CONT Constance of Castile (1354 Castile – 24 March 1394 Leicester) was claimant of the Castilian throne after the death of her father Pedro the Cruel. Her mother María de Padilla was of Jewish converso descent. [1] She married, at Roqu
2 CONC efort, near Bordeaux, Guienne, on 21 September 1371, John of Gaunt, 1st Duke of Lancaster, third son of Edward III of England and Philippa of Hainault, as his second wife.
2 CONT
2 CONT This was the way for Gaunt to obtain a kingdom of his own (he had also pursued Scotland), as his nephew Richard II and the descendants of his brother Lionel of Antwerp, 1st Duke of Clarence stood between him and the Crown of Engla
2 CONC nd. John of Gaunt claimed the title of King of Castile jure uxoris, and insisted that English nobles address him as "my lord of Spain", but was unsuccessful in his attempts to obtain the crown. Their daughter Katherine of Lancaste
2 CONC r was married to the King of the Trastamara line, Henry III of Castile, thus uniting these two rival claims.
2 CONT
2 CONT Constance died at Leicester Castle and was buried at Newark Abbey, Leicester.
2 CONT
2 CONT [edit]Children
2 CONT
2 CONT Katherine of Lancaster (1372–1418), married King Henry III of Castile (1379–1406)
2 CONT
2 CONT John Plantagenet (1372–1375)
