2 CONT Infanta Isabella of Castile, Duchess of York (c. 1355 – 23 December 1392) was a daughter of King Peter of Castile and María de Padilla.[1] She was a younger sister of Constance, Duchess of Lancaster.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1372, sometime between the 1 March and 30 April, Isabella married Edmund of Langley, 1st Duke of York, fourth son of Edward III of England and Philippa of Hainault at Wallingford. As a result of her marriage, she became the fir
2 CONC st of a total of eleven women who became (as a courtesy by marriage to their husbands) Duchess of York. They had three children:
2 CONT
2 CONT Edward of Norwich, 2nd Duke of York (1373 - 25 October 1415). 
2 CONT
2 CONT Constance of York (1374 - 29 November 1416). Married Thomas le Despenser and was mother of Isabel le Despenser, Countess of Worcester and Warwick. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Richard of Conisburgh, 3rd Earl of Cambridge (1375 - 5 August 1415). 
2 CONT
2 CONT She was named a Lady Companion of the Order of the Garter in 1378. Isabella died 23 December 1392 and on 14 January 1393 was buried in Kings Langley Manor House in Hertfordshire, England.
2 CONT
2 CONT http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Infanta_Isabella_of_Castile
