1 NAME Edward /of Norwich/ 2nd Duke of York
2 GIVN Edward
2 NSFX 2nd Duke of York
2 _MARNM of Norwich
1 SEX M
1 BURI
2 PLAC St Mary the Virgin and All Saints Churchyard, Fotheringhay, Northamptonshire, England 
2 ADDR
1 BIRT
2 DATE 1373
2 PLAC Conisbrough, Yorkshire, , England
2 ADDR
1 DEAT
2 DATE 25 OCT 1415
2 PLAC Agincourt, Pas-de-Calais, Nord-Pas-de-Calais, France
2 ADDR
1 OCCU 2nd Duke of York
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} '''Sir Edward of Norwich, 1st Duke of Norwich, 1st Duke of Albermale, 2nd Duke of York, 2nd Earl of Cambridge, Duke of Aumale''' Order of the Garter|KG (1373 – 25 October 1415) was a member of the Monarchy of the U
2 CONC nited Kingdom|English royal family who died at the Battle of Agincourt.
2 CONT
2 CONT The son of Edmund of Langley, 1st Duke of York, and his first wife Isabella of Castile, Duchess of York|Isabella of Castile. His paternal grandparents were Edward III of England and Philippa of Hainault. His maternal grandparents 
2 CONC were King Pedro of Castile and Maria de Padilla.
2 CONT
2 CONT Edward is thought to have been born in Norwich. He was close to his cousin Richard II of England|King Richard II, and was created Earl of Rutland by him in 1390, Earl of Cork in about 1396 and then Duke of Aumale in 1397. This ass
2 CONC ociation put him out of favour after the usurpation of Henry IV of England|King Henry IV, and he was deprived of his Dukedom. In 1400 he participated in the Epiphany Rising, an attempted coup against Henry IV to restore the depose
2 CONC d Richard II, but Edward betrayed the conspirators to the king resulting in the death of many of his peers by mob violence. In 1402 he succeeded his father as Duke of York. He married a widow, Philippa de Mohun, but there were no 
2 CONC children from their marriage.
2 CONT
2 CONT Edward wrote “The Master of Game”, a translation of the most famous of the hunting treatises of the Middle Ages, the “Livre de Chasse” of Gaston III of Foix-Béarn|Gaston Phoebus, Count de Foix, adding five chapters of his own.
2 CONT
2 CONT Edward took part in Henry V of England|King Henry V's war on France and died at the Battle of Agincourt, the major English casualty in that battle.
2 CONT
2 CONT On his death, the dukedom did not immediately pass to his nephew, Richard Plantagenet, 3rd Duke of York|Richard Plantagenet, as Richard's father Richard of Conisburgh, 3rd Earl of Cambridge, had been attainted for treason, but the
2 CONC  younger Richard was eventually restored to the Dukedom.
2 CONT
2 CONT As the '''Duke of Aumerle''', he is a major character in William Shakespeare's Richard II (play)|Richard II, and he is also a minor character in Henry V (play)|Henry V. Although his death is depicted by Shakespeare and his adapter
2 CONC s as an act of heroism, it was in fact more of an accident: along with many of the French knights, he was unable to remain upright when unhorsed in the fray and effectively died of suffocation under a pile of other men and horses.
2 CONC  Image:Edward of Norwich Arms.svg|thumb|150px|Coat of arms.
2 CONT
2 CONT ==Titles, styles, honours and arms==
2 CONT ===Arms===
2 CONT As a grandson, in the male line, of the sovereign, Edward bore the arms of the kingdom, differenced by a ''label argent 3-point, per pale Castile and Leon''. Upon his father's death in 1402, Edward inherited his arms, which were t
2 CONC hose of the kingdom, differentiated by a ''label argent of three points, each bearing three torteaux gules''.
2 CONT
2 CONT http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Edward_of_Norwich,_2nd_Duke_of_York
