1 NOTE {geni:about_me} http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Elisabeth_of_Austria_%28d.1353%29
2 CONT
2 CONT Elisabeth of Austria (d.1353)
2 CONT
2 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
2 CONT
2 CONT Jump to: navigation, search
2 CONT
2 CONT Elisabeth of Austria
2 CONT
2 CONT Image of Elisabeth from the 15th century
2 CONT
2 CONT Duchess of Lorraine
2 CONT
2 CONT Tenure 	1307-1329
2 CONT
2 CONT Spouse 	Frederick IV, Duke of Lorraine
2 CONT
2 CONT Among others...
2 CONT
2 CONT Issue
2 CONT
2 CONT Rudolph, Duke of Lorraine
2 CONT
2 CONT Margaret, Countess of Friburg
2 CONT
2 CONT House 	House of Habsburg
2 CONT
2 CONT Father 	Albert I of Germany
2 CONT
2 CONT Mother 	Elisabeth of Tirol
2 CONT
2 CONT Born 	c.1285
2 CONT
2 CONT Vienna Austria
2 CONT
2 CONT Died 	19 May 1353
2 CONT
2 CONT Lorraine
2 CONT
2 CONT Burial 	St George's, Nancy
2 CONT
2 CONT St Paul's, Lavanttal
2 CONT
2 CONT Elisabeth of Austria also known as Isabelle (c.1285-19 May 1353) was a daughter of Albert I of Germany and his wife Elisabeth of Gorizia-Tyrol. She was a member of the House of Habsburg.
2 CONT
2 CONT Contents
2 CONT
2 CONT [show]
2 CONT
2 CONT     * 1 Family
2 CONT
2 CONT     * 2 Life
2 CONT
2 CONT     * 3 Children
2 CONT
2 CONT     * 4 Ancestry
2 CONT
2 CONT     * 5 References
2 CONT
2 CONT [edit] Family
2 CONT
2 CONT Elisabeth was the tenth of twelve children. Her siblings included: Rudolph I of Bohemia, Frederick the Fair, Leopold I, Duke of Austria, Albert II, Duke of Austria, Otto, Duke of Austria and Agnes, Queen of Hungary and Anna, Margr
2 CONC avine of Brandenburg.
2 CONT
2 CONT Elisabeth's maternal grandparents were Meinhard, Duke of Carinthia and Elisabeth of Bavaria, Queen of Germany. Her paternal grandparents were Rudolph I of Germany and his first wife, Gertrude of Hohenburg. Elisabeth of Bavaria was
2 CONC  daughter of Otto II Wittelsbach, Duke of Bavaria and his wife Agnes of the Palatinate.
2 CONT
2 CONT [edit] Life
2 CONT
2 CONT Elisabeth was betrothed at a young age to one of the sons of Philip IV of France, so her father could make stronger connections with France. On May 25, 1300 Elisabeth's eldest brother Rudolph I of Bohemia married Blanche of France
2 CONC , a daughter of Philip IV. After this marriage, Elisabeth's betrothal to one of Philip's sons was scrapped.
2 CONT
2 CONT Elisabeth was instead married to Frederick IV, Duke of Lorraine. The contract was signed on 6 August 1306 and they married in 1307. From this marriage Elisabeth became Duchess of Lorraine and Frederick was able to support Elisabet
2 CONC h's brother Frederick the Fair, to become Holy Roman Emperor. Elisabeth's husband was captured at the Battle of Mühldorf, where he was fighting for Elisabeth's brother and was released by the King of France. Elisabeth became known
2 CONC  as Isabelle[1] in Lorriane.
2 CONT
2 CONT We know that Elisabeth outlived Frederick but his date of death is disputed, he either died on April 21 1329 or August 23 1329, he may have also died during 1328. However, Frederick left Lorriane to their young son Rudolph who was
2 CONC  not yet ten. Elisabeth became regent for her son. Her regency lasted from 1329-1331. Rudolph married aged only ten to Eleanor of Bar and made his father-in-law Edward I of Bar regent in place of Elisabeth.
2 CONT
2 CONT Elisabeth died on 19 May 1353 and was buried at St George's in Lorraine's capital Nancy. She was later moved to St Paul's in Lavanttal.
2 CONT
2 CONT [edit] Children
2 CONT
2 CONT Elisabeth and Frederick were married for twenty two years, in that time the couple had six children[2]:
2 CONT
2 CONT     * Rudolph, Duke of Lorraine (1320 – 26 August 1346), successor of his father
2 CONT
2 CONT     * Frederick, Earl of Luneville
2 CONT
2 CONT     * Margaret (d.9 August 1376), married Jean de Chalon, lord of Auberive (d.1350), then Conrad, count of Friburg, and lastly Ulrich (d.1377), lord of Rappoltstein
2 CONT
2 CONT     * Agnes (died young)
2 CONT
2 CONT     * Theobald (died young)
2 CONT
2 CONT     * Albert (died young)
2 CONT
2 CONT Many of the couple's children died during childhood, only Rudolph and Margaret lived to adulthood.
2 CONT
2 CONT References
2 CONT
2 CONT    1. ^ Austria, Medieval Lands
2 CONT
2 CONT    2. ^ Translation from Italian Wikipedia
2 CONT
