1 NOTE {geni:about_me} https://en.m.wikipedia.org/wiki/Elizabeth_of_Görlitz
2 CONT
2 CONT Elisabeth of Görlitz (November 1390 - 2 August 1451)[1] reigned as Duchess of Luxemburg from 1411 to 1443.
2 CONT
2 CONT Elisabeth
2 CONT
2 CONT Depiction on the tomb of Elisabeth von Görlitz, Duchess of Luxembourg, in Trier (Germany)
2 CONT Duchess of Luxemburg
2 CONT Reign
2 CONT 1415–1419/1425–1443
2 CONT Predecessor
2 CONT Anthony
2 CONT Successor
2 CONT Philip the Good
2 CONT Born
2 CONT November 1390
2 CONT Hořovice
2 CONT Died
2 CONT 2 August 1451 (aged 60)
2 CONT Trier
2 CONT Spouse
2 CONT Anthony, Duke of Brabant
2 CONT John III, Duke of Bavaria-Straubing
2 CONT House
2 CONT House of Luxembourg
2 CONT Father
2 CONT John of Görlitz
2 CONT Mother
2 CONT Richardis Catherine of Mecklenburg
2 CONT Contents
2 CONT Life
2 CONT edit
2 CONT Elisabeth was the only daughter and heiress of John of Görlitz, the third son of Charles IV, Holy Roman Emperor. He was Duke of Lusatia and Görlitz, and also Elector of Brandenburg for a brief period. Her mother, Richardis Catheri
2 CONC ne of Sweden, was the daughter of King Albert of Sweden.
2 CONT
2 CONT Reign
2 CONT edit
2 CONT The Duchy of Luxemburg was mortgaged to Elisabeth by her uncle King Sigismund of Hungary, who later also became King of Bohemia and Holy Roman Emperor. He was unable to repay the loan, and subsequently left Elisabeth in control of
2 CONC  the duchy.
2 CONT
2 CONT Elizabeth's first marriage took place in Brussels on 16 July 1409, to Antoine, Duke of Brabant.[1] He defended her against three uprisings of the Luxemburgian nobility, until his death in 1415.
2 CONT
2 CONT John III, Duke of Bavaria, was Elizabeth's second husband. He died in 1425, and they did not have any children.[a] After his death, she became heavily indebted.
2 CONT
2 CONT Deposition
2 CONT edit
2 CONT In 1441, she made a treaty with Philip III, Duke of Burgundy, allowing him to immediately assume the administrative duties of Luxemburg and inherit the duchy upon her death. He agreed to this, but chose to launch a night attack on
2 CONC  the territory two years later, taking immediate control. Elisabeth was subsequently expelled from Luxemburg by Philip's forces.
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT Further reading
2 CONT edit
