2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Eleanor_of_Portugal,_Holy_Roman_Empress
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT Eleanor of Portugal (18 September 1434 – 3 September 1467) was Empress of the Holy Roman Empire.
2 CONT
2 CONT She was a Portuguese infanta (princess), daughter of King Edward of Portugal and his wife Leonor of Aragon. She was the consort of Holy Roman Emperor, Frederick III, and the mother of Emperor Maximilian I.
2 CONT
2 CONT Eleanor was born in Torres Vedras on 18 September 1434, one of the nine children of King Edward of Portugal and Eleanor of Aragon. She was the third eldest daughter, but her two older sisters died when they were young, leaving Ele
2 CONC anor as the surviving eldest daughter.
2 CONT
2 CONT When her father King Edward died five days before her fourth birthday, Eleanor's brother Afonso V succeeded him as king with her mother as regent. The following March, her mother gave birth to another daughter, Joan, who would bec
2 CONC ome the notorious wife of Henry IV of Castile.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1440, Eleanor's mother was forced to go into exile in Castile after losing the litigation with her brother-in-law Pedro, Duke of Coimbra for the regency of the young King Afonso. She left Eleanor behind because Eleanor was ill 
2 CONC at the time.
2 CONT
2 CONT On 16 March 1452 in Rome, she married the German King Frederick III, and the two were crowned Holy Roman Emperor and Empress three days later, on 19 March 1452 by Pope Nicholas V at St. Peter's Basilica. Her dowry was used by her 
2 CONC husband to alleviate his financial problems and cement his power. Frederick III was the last Holy Roman Emperor to be crowned in Rome.
2 CONT
2 CONT With Frederick III she had five children:
2 CONT
2 CONT     * Christopher, Archduke of Austria (1455-1456)
2 CONT
2 CONT     * Maximilian I, Holy Roman Emperor (1459-1519)
2 CONT
2 CONT     * Helena, Archduchess of Austria (1460-1461)
2 CONT
2 CONT     * Kunigunde, Archduchess of Austria (1465-1520), married Albert IV, Duke of Bavaria
2 CONT
2 CONT     * John, Archduke of Austria (1466-1467)
2 CONT
2 CONT It is from Empress Eleanor that the Portuguese descent of Charles V, Holy Roman Emperor, derives.
2 CONT
2 CONT Eleanor died in Wiener Neustadt on 3 September 1467, and is buried in the same city in the Monastery of the Order of Cister.
2 CONT
2 CONT Her tombstone is in the Neukloster of Wiener Neustadt.
2 CONT
2 CONT Empress:
2 CONT
2 CONT The marriage was likely suggested by Isabella of Portugal, duchess of Burgundy. The arrangements was made by Eleanor's uncle, the king of Naples who, in 1448, sent artists from his court to paint Eleanor. Eleanor was also suggeste
2 CONC d to marry the king of France, but it was said, that she herself preferred to marry Frederick, because this would give her the title empress instead of queen. The practical negotiations was made in Naples and completed in 1451. Du
2 CONC ring the sea travel, the fleet escorting Eleanor to Italy was tormented by pirates and storms, and there were rumours that she had been lost at sea. Eleanor and Frederick met in Sienna : Frederick was to have paled when he saw her
2 CONC , out of excitement but also worry that she would have troubles giving birth because of her frail appearance. The marriage took place in Rome. Upon her coronation, she was also given the name Helena, but she never used this name. 
2 CONC The festivities was hosted by her uncle, the king of Naples.
2 CONT
2 CONT Eleanor and Frederick were disimilar, and her interest for dance, gambling and hunting was not shared by Frederick, and their relationship was affected by their differences. Frederick sent Eleanor's Portuguese entourage home after
2 CONC  the wedding because of the cost, and she suffered from homesickness; he also blamed her for having caused the death of several of their children by letting them eat Portuguese food, and therefore took over the upbringing of the r
2 CONC emaining children entirely for himself. During the period of captivity in Vienna, when people were forced to eat rats, cats and dogs, she cheered people up. History have claimed that Eleanor was taken from a splendid court in Port
2 CONC ugal to a cultural wasteland in Vienna because of her spouse's strict economic sense.
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT Eleanor of Portugal was a Portuguese infanta, daughter of King Edward of Portugal and his wife Leonor of Aragon. Leonor was born in Torres Vedras on September 18, 1434.
2 CONT
2 CONT On 16 March 1452 she married the German King Frederick III and the two were crowned Holy Roman Emperor and Empress three days later, on 19 March 1452. Her dowry was used by her husband to alleviate his financial problems.
2 CONT
2 CONT With Frederick III she had five children:
2 CONT
2 CONT Christopher, Archduke of Austria (1454-1455) 
2 CONT
2 CONT Maximilian I, Holy Roman Emperor (1459-1519) 
2 CONT
2 CONT Helen, Archduchess of Austria (1460-1461) 
2 CONT
2 CONT Kunigunde of Austria (1465-1520), married Albert IV, Duke of Bavaria 
2 CONT
2 CONT John, Archduke of Austria (1466-1467) 
2 CONT
2 CONT It is from Empress Leonor that the Portuguese descent of Charles V, Holy Roman Emperor derives.
2 CONT
2 CONT Leonor died in Wiener Neustadt on September 3, 1467, and is buried in the same city in the Monastery of the Order of Cister.
2 CONT
2 CONT Her tombstone is in the Neukloster of Wiener Neustadt.
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT Eleanor of Portugal (18 September 1434 – 3 September 1467) was Empress of the Holy Roman Empire. A Portuguese infanta (princess), daughter of King Edward of Portugal and his wife Eleanor of Aragon, she was the consort of Holy Roma
2 CONC n Emperor, Frederick III, and the mother of Emperor Maximilian I.
2 CONT
2 CONT Background
2 CONT
2 CONT Eleanor was born in Torres Vedras on 18 September 1434, one of the nine children of King Edward of Portugal and Eleanor of Aragon. She was the third eldest daughter, but her two older sisters died when they were young, leaving Ele
2 CONC anor as the surviving eldest daughter.
2 CONT
2 CONT When her father King Edward died five days before her fourth birthday, Eleanor's brother Afonso V succeeded him as king with her mother as regent. The following March, her mother gave birth to another daughter, Joan, who would bec
2 CONC ome the notorious wife of Henry IV of Castile.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1440, Eleanor's mother was forced to go into exile in Castile after losing the litigation with her brother-in-law, Infante Peter, Duke of Coimbra, for the regency of the young King Afonso. She left Eleanor behind because Eleano
2 CONC r was ill at the time. Empress Frederick III and Eleanor of Portugal.
2 CONT
2 CONT The marriage was likely suggested by Isabella of Portugal, duchess of Burgundy. Arrangements were made by Eleanor's maternal uncle Alfonso V, King of Aragon and Naples who, in 1448, sent artists from his court to paint Eleanor. El
2 CONC eanor was also suggested to marry Louis, Dauphin of France, eldest son of King Charles VII, but it was said, that she herself preferred to marry Frederick, because this would give her the title of Empress instead of Queen. The pra
2 CONC ctical negotiations was made in Naples and completed in 1451. During the sea travel, the fleet escorting Eleanor to Italy was tormented by pirates and storms, and there were rumours that she had been lost at sea. Eleanor and Frede
2 CONC rick met in Sienna : Frederick was to have paled when he saw her, out of excitement but also worry that she would have troubles giving birth because of her frail appearance. The marriage took place in Rome. Upon her coronation, sh
2 CONC e was also given the name Helena, but she never used this name. The festivities was hosted by her uncle, the king of Naples.
2 CONT
2 CONT Eleanor and Frederick were dissimilar, and her interest for dance, gambling and hunting was not shared by Frederick, and their relationship was affected by their differences. Frederick sent Eleanor's Portuguese entourage home afte
2 CONC r the wedding because of the cost, and she suffered from homesickness; he also blamed her for having caused the death of several of their children by letting them eat Portuguese food, and therefore took over the upbringing of the 
2 CONC remaining children entirely for himself. During the period of captivity in Vienna, when people were forced to eat rats, cats and dogs, she cheered people up. History have claimed that Eleanor was taken from a splendid court in Por
2 CONC tugal to a cultural wasteland in Vienna because of her spouse's strict economic sense. Marriage and children Eneias Silvio Piccolomini (the future Pope Pius II) celebrating the marriage between Frederick III and Eleanor.
2 CONT
2 CONT On 16 March 1452 in Rome, she married the German King Frederick III, and the two were crowned Holy Roman Emperor and Empress three days later, on 19 March 1452 by Pope Nicholas V at St. Peter's Basilica. Her dowry was used by her 
2 CONC husband to alleviate his financial problems and cement his power. Frederick III was the last Holy Roman Emperor to be crowned in Rome.
2 CONT
2 CONT With Frederick III she had five children:
2 CONT
2 CONT     Archduke Christopher (16 November 1455 – 21 March 1456)
2 CONT     Maximilian I, Holy Roman Emperor (22 March 1459 – 12 January 1519)
2 CONT     Archduchess Helena (3 November 1460 – 28 February 1461)
2 CONT     Archduchess Kunigunde (16 March 1465 – 6 August 1520), married Albert IV, Duke of Bavaria
2 CONT     Archduke John (9 August 1466 – 10 February 1467)
2 CONT
2 CONT Eleanor died in Wiener Neustadt on 3 September 1467, and is buried in the Neukloster, the Cistercian monastery of that same city, where her tombstone may be seen. Ancestry
