1 NOTE {geni:about_me} http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hugh_IV_of_Cyprus
2 CONT
2 CONT Hugh IV of Cyprus (or Hugh IV of Lusignan) (c. 1295 or 1293-1296 – 10 October 1359) was King of Cyprus from 31 March 1324 to his abdication, on 24 November 1358 and, nominally, King of Jerusalem, as Hugh II, until his death. The s
2 CONC on of Guy of Cyprus, Constable of Cyprus (son of Hugh III of Cyprus and wife Isabella of Ibelin), and Eschiva of Ibelin, Hugh succeeded his father as Constable of Cyprus in 1318, and later succeeded to the throne of Cyprus on the 
2 CONC death of his uncle Henry II, since Henry II had no son. He was a member of the House of Lusignan.
2 CONT
2 CONT History
2 CONT
2 CONT Hugh appears to have been content to rule Cyprus, as he prevented his son, Peter I, from going to Western Europe to recruit support for a new crusade to recover their Kingdom of Jerusalem. In 1344, he joined a league with Venice a
2 CONC nd the Knights Hospitallers which burnt a Turkish fleet in Smyrna and captured the city. In 1345 the allies defeated the Turks at Imbros by land and sea, but Hugh could see little benefit for his kingdom in these endeavors and wit
2 CONC hdrew from the league.
2 CONT
2 CONT He was crowned as King of Cyprus at Santa Sophia, in Nicosia, on 15 April or 25 April 1324. In the same year, on 13 May, he was crowned at Saint Nicholas Cathedral, in Famagusta as Titular King of Jerusalem. As a leader, King Hugh
2 CONC  signed an agreement with Venice, which had to do with the activities of the Venician merchants who were settling in Cyprus. That caused problems with the Republic of Genoese who were rivals of the Venetians; however he negotiated
2 CONC  with them and had agreement in 1329. The Genoese demanded that Hugh pay the debit of his uncle Henry II. He died in Nicosia.
2 CONT
2 CONT During his reign, he was strict about issues relating to justice. When his two sons left wthout his permission for a trip in Europe, he arrested the man who helped them to leave the island, he imprisoned and tortured him, and he c
2 CONC ut off a hand and a foot before he hanged him in April 1349. He managed to bring back his two sons and he imprisoned them.
2 CONT
2 CONT Other sources show that he was well educated and had an interest in art, literature, and philosophy and had much knowledge of Latin literature. He owned a summer villa in Lapithos and organised philosophical meetings. The Italian 
2 CONC writer Boccaccio, wrote Genealogia Deorum Gentilium at the request of Hugh IV.
2 CONT
2 CONT Hugh resigned the crown to his son, Peter I (rather than his grandson Hugh) in 1358, and died on 10 October 1359 in Nicosia.
2 CONT
2 CONT Family
2 CONT
2 CONT Hugh was married twice, both times to ladies of the house of Ibelin, whose fathers were both named "Guy of Ibelin", one being Count of Jaffa and the other Seneschal of Cyprus.
2 CONT
2 CONT Hugh's first marriage was in 1307/1310 to Marie d'Ibelin (1294 – before 30 June 1318), daughter of Guy of Ibelin (1250–1304), Count of Jaffa, and wife and cousin Marie d'Ibelin, Lady of Askalon and Naumachia. They had at least one
2 CONC  son: 
2 CONT
2 CONT Guy of Lusignan (c. 1316 or 1315-1316 – soon before 24 September 1343 and buried in Nicosia), Constable of Cyprus (1336–1338) and Titular Prince of Galilee ca 1320. He married by proxy at the Château de Bourbon on 29 November 1328
2 CONC  and in person at Santa Sophia, Nicosia on 15–30 January 1330 Marie of Bourbon (1315–1387 in Naples and buried there), Princess of Achaia - later remarried on 9 September 1347 in Naples to Prince Robert II of Tarento (1299/1319 – 
2 CONC 10 September 1364 in Naples and buried there), Titular Emperor of Constantinople in 1343, etc, without issue - and they left one son: 
2 CONT
2 CONT Hugh of Lusignan (1335–1385/1386 in Cyprus), who succeeded his father as Titular Prince of Galilee in 1343, Senator of Rome on 12 August 1360 and Lord of Arnecha and Leondaki in January 1365. He married after Autumn 1365 Marie de 
2 CONC Morphou (d. after 1383), dau.of Sir Jean de Morphou, Comte de Roucha, without issue
2 CONT
2 CONT His second marriage took place on 18 June 1318, to Alix of Ibelin (1304/1306 – after 6 August 1386 and buried at Saint Dominic's, Nicosia), daughter of Guy of Ibelin by his wife and cousin Isabelle d'Ibelin. Children with Alice we
2 CONC re: 
2 CONT
2 CONT Eschiva of Lusignan (c. 1323 or 1322-1324 – of the plague, 1363 and buried in Nicosia), married after 5 March 1337/1339, separated since 22 April 1341, Infante Fernando (Ferran) of Majorca (March/April, 1317 – ca 1343/1347), Vicom
2 CONC te d'Omelas or Aumelàs.
2 CONT
2 CONT Peter I of Lusignan (1328–1369), succeeded him as King of Cyprus and Jerusalem.
2 CONT
2 CONT John of Lusignan (c. 1329 or 1329/1330–1375), Regent of Cyprus and Titular Prince of Antioch, murdered, married twice, firstly in 1343 to Constance, daughter of Frederick III of Sicily and Eleanor of Anjou, without issue, and seco
2 CONC ndly in 1350 to Alice d'Ibelin (d. after 1373), by whom he had issue.
2 CONT
2 CONT James I of Lusignan (1334–1398), succeeded his nephew Peter II of Cyprus.
2 CONT
2 CONT Three other children of Hugh whose filiation is uncertain:
2 CONT
2 CONT Thomas of Lusignan (d. 15 November 1340), unmarried and without issue
2 CONT
2 CONT Perrot of Lusignan (d. 29 June 1353), unmarried and without issue
2 CONT
2 CONT Margaret of Lusignan, married in 1347/1349 Gautier de Dampierre(-sur-Salon) (d. after 1373), Seneschal of Cyprus.
