1 OCCU comte d'Armagnac (1418-1450), comte de Fésenzac et de Rodez, vicomte de Lomagne, seigneur de Castelnau de Montmiral
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} John IV of Armagnac
2 CONT
2 CONT http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/John_IV_of_Armagnac
2 CONT
2 CONT John IV (15 October 1396  Rodez- 5 November 1450 Isle-Jordan) was a Count of Armagnac, of Fézensac and Rodez from 1418 to 1450. He was the son of Bernard VII of Armagnac, Count d' Armagnac, of Fézensac, Pardiac and Rodez and the b
2 CONC rother of Bernard of Armagnac, Count de Pardiac, of La Marche and Duke of Nemours.
2 CONT
2 CONT His father had taken by force the county of Comminges, but Jean IV could not prevent the second marriage of Marguerite to Mathieu de Foix in 1419. Subsequently they took the County of Comminges. In 1425, he recognized the King of 
2 CONC Castile as overlord of Armagnac. The French king, occupied fighting the English, could not intervene, but did not overlook the affront. Later, Jean IV negotiated the marriage of his daughter Isabelle with Henri VI, but he backed o
2 CONC ff from this plan after threats from the king of France. In 1440, he took part in a revolt of the barons and the Dauphin of France, but the coalition was overcome by Charles VII, who pardoned the insurrectionists. He asked the Cou
2 CONC nt of Armagnac to give up its kingly formula, but this last was refused.
2 CONT
2 CONT Charles VII then asked the Dauphin to punish the recalcitrant Jean IV. Besieged in the Isle-Jordan, Jean was made prisoner and imprisoned in Carcassonne in 1443. He was pardoned three years latter, but his counties were directed b
2 CONC y royal officers, and he did not have any more capacities until his death.
2 CONT
2 CONT [edit]Marriages and children
2 CONT
2 CONT He married on 16 June 1407 Blanche of Brittany (1395-1419), daughter of John V, Duke of Brittany and Jeanne d' Evreux, and had one child:
2 CONT
2 CONT Bonne (b. 1416; † before 1448)
2 CONT
2 CONT He married secondly on 10 May 1419, shortly after the death of his first wife. His second wife was Isabelle d' Évreux (b. 1395; † 1450), daughter of Charles III, king of Navarre and Eleanor of Castille. They had five children:
2 CONT
2 CONT Marie of Armagnac (b. 1420; † 1473), married in 1437 John II of Alençon (b. 1409; † 1476), Duke of Alençon; maternal great-grandparents of King Henry IV of France.
2 CONT
2 CONT Jean V of Armagnac (b. 1420; † 1473), Viscount of Lomagne, then Count d' Armagnac, of Fézensac and Rodez.
2 CONT
2 CONT Eléonore (b. 1423; † 1456), married in 1446 Louis de Chalon (b. 1389; † 1463), Prince d'Orange, Lord of Arlay and Arguel.
2 CONT
2 CONT Charles Ier (b. 1425; † 1497), Viscount of Fézensaguet, then Count d' Armagnac, of Fézensac and Rodez.
2 CONT
2 CONT Isabelle (b. 1430; † 1476), Lady of the Four-Valleys.
2 CONT
2 CONT [edit]
