1 NAME Jean /Malet de Graville/
2 GIVN Jean
2 SURN Malet de Graville
2 _MARNM Malet de Graville
1 NAME Jean VI Malet, seigneur de Graville
1 SEX M
1 BIRT
2 DATE ABT 1420
1 DEAT
2 DATE ABT 1482
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Jehan or Jean VI Malet de Graville known as the Younger (around 1420-1482), Chamberlain of the Dauphin, future Louis XI, who would marry:
2 CONT around 1437 Marie de Montauban, daughter of Guillaume, lord of Montauban and Bonne de Visconti,
2 CONT in 1440 Marie de Montberon, daughter of François Ier de Montberon, Baron de Montberon and Maulévrier, and Louise de Clermont d'Aunay,
2 CONT Charles Malet, Pastor of Montfort and Beaufou,
2 CONT Louise Malet.
2 CONT
2 CONT Birth of Jean VI Malet de Graville
2 CONT Jehan or Jean VI Malet de Graville dit le Jeune was born around 1420 , son of Jean V Malet de Graville and Jacqueline de Montagu.
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT Marriage of Jean VI Malet de Graville with Marie de Montauban
2 CONT Jean VI Malet de Graville married around 1437 Marie de Montauban, daughter of Guillaume de Montauban and Bonne Visconti.
2 CONT
2 CONT Marriage of Jean VI Malet de Graville with Marie de Montauban
2 CONT Jean VI Malet de Graville is the son of Jean V Malet de Graville (1390-1449), Lord of Graville, Master of the Crossbowmen of France, and Jacqueline de Montagu (1398-1436), Heiress of Marcoussis.
2 CONT
2 CONT Jean VI Malet de Graville marries Marie de Montauban, daughter of Guillaume de Montauban (died in 1432), Lord of Landal and Bona Visconti (1385-1469), Lady of Landal. Their children are:
2 CONT
2 CONT Louis I Malet de Graville (1438-1516), Lord of Graville who will marry Marie de Balzac (1440-1503),
2 CONT Jean Malet de Graville (died 1470).
2 CONT
2 CONT Birth of Louis Malet de Graville
2 CONT
2 CONT Louis Malet de Graville was born in Paris in 1438 , son of Jean VI Malet de Graville .
2 CONT
2 CONT His parents having died young, Louis received further education from his maternal uncles after completing his humanities at the Collège de Montaigu in Paris.
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT Marriage of Jean VI Malet de Graville with Marie de Montberon
2 CONT
2 CONT Jean VI Malet de Graville married on July 6, 1440 Marie de Montberon (died after 1468), Dame de Chef-Boutonne, daughter of François Ier de Montberon, Baron de Maulévrier (1392-around 1470) and Louise de Clermont (born in 1385 ). T
2 CONC heir children are:
2 CONT
2 CONT Marie Malet de Graville (born in 1445 or 1455) who will marry:
2 CONT Louis de Clermont (died 1477),
2 CONT July 8, 1478 Antoine de Beaumont-Bressuire (circa 1455-1511), Lord of Bury,
2 CONT Louise Malet de Graville (circa 1448-1499) who married Guillaume de Rouville (died 1492), Grand veneur de France,
2 CONT Renée Malet de Graville who will marry Jean III Martel (died in 1488), Lord of Bacqueville,
2 CONT Jeanne Malet de Graville who will marry François de Tourzel d'Alègre (died in 1512), Lord of Précy-sur-Vrin.
2 CONT
2 CONT Jean VI Malet de Graville
2 CONT
2 CONT Jean VI Malet de Graville became Chamberlain to Louis XI in 1445 .
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT Death of Gilles de Bretagne
2 CONT
2 CONT Death of Francis I the Fratricide
2 CONT
2 CONT Pierre II de Dreux Duke of Brittany and Count of Guingamp
2 CONT
2 CONT Gilles de Bretagne, brother of François Ier the Fratricide, represents the pro-English party in the duchy.
2 CONT
2 CONT Gilles de Bretagne died on the night of April 24 to 25, 1450 , strangled at the Château de la Hardouinaye by Olivier de Méel with the complicity of Robert Rouxel.
2 CONT
2 CONT Olivier de Méel fled to France and found asylum in the castle of Marcoussis belonging to Jean VI Malet de Graville , Lord of Graville, who married Marie de Montauban.
2 CONT
2 CONT François I the Fratricide is accused of the murder of his brother Gilles de Bretagne.
2 CONT
2 CONT François I the Fratricide died at the manor of Plaisance-en-Saint-Avé on July 17, 1450 .
2 CONT
2 CONT Pierre II de Dreux became Duke of Brittany and Count of Guingamp in August 1450 under the name of Pierre II de Bretagne.
2 CONT
2 CONT After his coronation; Françoise d'Amboise is quickly named the good duchess.
2 CONT
2 CONT Françoise d'Amboise is already much loved and appreciated for her spirit, her cheerfulness and her patience.
2 CONT
2 CONT Françoise d'Amboise takes a discreet but active part in the government of Brittany.
2 CONT
2 CONT She helps the little ones, the poor and the sick.
2 CONT
2 CONT She also deals with justice issues.
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT Death of Jean VI Malet de Graville Lord of Graville
2 CONT
2 CONT Jean VI Malet de Graville died after 1453 .
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT Liberation of Jean VI Malet de Graville
2 CONT
2 CONT Jean VI de Graville was freed in 1478 .
2 CONT
2 CONT Castle of Milly-la-Forêt
2 CONT Death of Jean VI Malet de Graville
2 CONT
2 CONT Jean VI Malet de Graville died at Pont-de-Chamois in 1482 .
2 CONT
2 CONT His body was transported by water to Graville, for 98 pounds 2 sols and 6 deniers.
2 CONT
2 CONT Louis Malet de Graville inherited from his father.
2 CONT
2 CONT At that time, the county of Milly-la-Forêt extended over the châtellenies of Fleury, Achères, Nainville, Boutigny and Cély.
2 CONT
2 CONT Louis Malet de Graville rebuilt and fortified the castle of Milly-la-Forêt.
2 CONT
2 CONT Milly-la-Forêt has one of the most important fairs in the Ile-de-France region.
2 CONT
2 CONT Louis Malet de Graville surrounds Milly-la-Forêt with fortifications, pierced by:
2 CONT
2 CONT the castle gate,
2 CONT St. Peter's Gate,
2 CONT the door of Melun,
2 CONT the gate of Fontainebleau,
2 CONT the door of Lyons,
2 CONT the Saint-Jacques gate,
2 CONT The Frog Gate.
2 CONT [http://www.histoireeurope.fr/RechercheLocution.php?Locutions=Jean+VI+Malet+de+Graville]
2 CONT
2 CONT ------------------------------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT "Jean VI de Graville, Lord of Marcoussis
2 CONT
2 CONT At the end of the Hundred Years War, economic life resumed in the Hurepoix, as did habits and customs. A man named Michel, receiver of the land of Guillerville, once again took over the receipt of the rights, in particular for the
2 CONC  goods sold at a rent or head cens fifty years earlier. The vassals like that of Villiers-sous-Longpont make their "adveux": Charles de Garenne to Jehan de Graville, because of his seigniory of Guillerville for the new hostel of V
2 CONC illiers, of a manor at the crossroads ... This declaration made for formalize his property for the sale that follows.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1450, Olivier de Méel, a Breton nobleman accused of having assassinated Gilles de Bretagne, had taken refuge in France at the castle of Marcoussis with Jean VI Malet de Graville. The husband of Marie de Montauban sheltered a va
2 CONC ssal of his father-in-law. Marie's older brother, Jean de Montauban, Marshal of Brittany was suspected of some complicity in the crime of which his brother Arthur was the main perpetrator. Later, two squires of the Comte de Richem
2 CONC ont arrived at Marcoussis to kidnap the fugitive who was condemned and beheaded at Vannes on June 8, 1451.
2 CONT
2 CONT In an act of 1452, nobleman Jehan Malet, knight, lord of Graville, and lord of Marcoussis , affirms for truth that it happened to him by the death of Mrs. Jacqueline de Montagu, his mother, 60 acres of arable land in two rooms:
2 CONT - 50 arpens sitting in front of the door of the monastery close to the meadows of the chioenere, to the land of the walnut trees of the Red House, - 10 arpens
2 CONT leading to the wall of the gardens of the castle of Marcoussis, to the walls of the park and gardens of the Célestins,
2 CONT - item behind lostel des Célestins, a piece of land enclosed by walls of the said park now being in bush and thorns, leading to the Mesnil fountain and at the other end to the river,
2 CONT - item a room of boys containing 80 arpens from Moquets to the old path of Bruyères, leading to the prior of Bruyères, - item
2 CONT 4 arpens of meadow in fallow land and desert, called the meadow of Chionere, holding at the top.
2 CONT Which lands Jehan Malet confesses to having given the said goods to the religious prior and convent of the Célestins of Marcoussis . This sale made for two hundred gold escus counted, paid in gold escus of 22 Parisian sols by hone
2 CONC st person brother Nicole Wibet, prior. The seller saying to use the sum in necessary repairs to the chastel of Marcoussis, ponds and other belongings, which are now in great ruin, by means of which price the salesman divested hims
2 CONC elf of the inheritances cy above declare. The irrevocable sale even by the wife of Robert de Sarrebruce, Lord of Commercy for which the said Malet bears his guarantee of the property of the said Marcoussis for certain trials pendi
2 CONC ng."
2 CONT [https://vieux-marcoussis.pagesperso-orange.fr/Chroniques/graville5.htm]
