2 CONT
2 CONT Johanna Sophia of Bavaria
2 CONT
2 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
2 CONT
2 CONT Johanna Sophia of Bavaria (c.1373- November 15, 1410) was the youngest daughter of Albert I, Duke of Bavaria and his first wife Margaret of Brieg. She was a member of the House of Wittelsbach.
2 CONT
2 CONT [edit]Marriage
2 CONT
2 CONT On 13 June 1395, Johanna Sophia married Albert IV, Duke of Austria in Vienna. The marriage produced two children; both of the children survived to adulthood. They were:
2 CONT
2 CONT Albert V (16 August 1397–27 October 1439, Neszmély, Hungary).
2 CONT
2 CONT Margarete (26 June 1395, Vienna–24 December 1447), married in Landshut 25 November 1412 to Duke Henry XVI of Bavaria.
2 CONT
2 CONT Albert would often quarrel with members of Johanna Sophia's family, such as their brother in law Wenceslaus, King of the Romans and his half brother Sigismund, Holy Roman Emperor. This ended only when Albert died in 1404.
2 CONT
2 CONT [edit]Family
2 CONT
2 CONT For some reason, Johanna Sophia had the same first as her older sister, Joanna; who was married to Albert's old enermy Wenceslaus. Her other siblings were:
2 CONT
2 CONT Katharina (c. 1361 – 1400, Hattem), married in Geertruidenberg in 1379 William I of Gelders and Jülich
2 CONT
2 CONT Margaret (1363 – January 23, 1423, Dijon), married in Cambrai in 1385 John the Fearless
2 CONT
2 CONT William VI, Count of Holland (1365–1417)
2 CONT
2 CONT Albert II, Duke of Bavaria-Straubing (1369 – January 21, 1397, Kelheim)
2 CONT
2 CONT John, Count of Holland (1374/76 – 1425), Bishop of Liège.
2 CONT
2 CONT Johanna Sophia's son, Albert II of Germany married Elisabeth II of Bohemia and they were the parents of three children, Ladislaus the Posthumous, Anne, Duchess of Luxembourg and Elisabeth of Austria, who became Queen consort of Po
2 CONC land.
2 CONT
2 CONT [edit]Death
2 CONT
2 CONT Johanna Sophia died aged thirty six or thirty seven, in Vienna. Almost three decades after her death, her son Albert became King of Germany.
2 CONT
