1 NOTE Albert II  (1397-1439), Holy Roman emperor (1438-39) and duke of Austria 
2 CONT (1404-39) as Albert V. He inherited the duchy in 1404 and in 1437 
2 CONT succeeded his father-in-law, the Holy Roman emperor Sigismund, as king of 
2 CONT Bohemia and Hungary. In 1438 he became king of Germany. Although 
2 CONT recognized as Holy Roman emperor, he was never crowned. During Albert's 
2 CONT brief reign, the Poles invaded Bohemia, and the Turks invaded Hungary.
1 NOTE King Albert II of Germany died 27 October from Dysentery while fighting the Turks in Hungary.
2 CONT
2 CONT Albrecht V of Bohemia-Hungary, son-in-law of Sigismund of Hungary (Luxembourg), succeeded his father-in-law as Emperor of Germany in 1438.  Bringing the House of Hapsburg to the throne.  He ruled until 1439, and was succeeded by F
2 CONC rederick III.
2 CONT
2 CONT Albert V of the Hapsburgs, heir of his father, Albert IV, inherited the dukedom of his father's half of Austria in 1404.  He reigned as Duke Albert V until 1439.  He was succeeded by his son, Ladislaus.
