1 NOTE {geni:about_me} ===William de Willoughby
2 CONT * 5th Baron Willoughby de Eresby
2 CONT *M, b. circa 1370, d. 4 December 1409
2 CONT *Father     Sir Robert Willoughby, 4th Baron Willoughby de Eresby3,16 b. c 1349, d. 9 Aug 1396
2 CONT *Mother     Margery la Zouche d. 18 Oct 1391
2 CONT
2 CONT '''William Willoughby, 5th Baron Willoughby de Eresby, KG''' (c.1370 – 4 December 1409) was an English baron. 
2 CONT
2 CONT ===Biography 
2 CONT
2 CONT William de Willoughby was born circa 1370 at of Eresby, Lincolnshire, England, the son of Robert Willoughby and his first wife Margery la Zouche. He was his father's main heir.  He was said to between 24 and 30 when his father die
2 CONC d in 1396, pointing to a birth year in the period 1365-1372.
2 CONT
2 CONT William married twice. He and Lucy le Strange obtained a marriage license on 3 January 1383 at Middle, Shropshire, England; They had 2 sons (Sir Robert, 6th Lord Willoughby, Comte de Vendôme & Beaumont-sur-Oise; & Sir Thomas) and 
2 CONC 3 daughters (Elizabeth, wife of Sir Henry, 5th Lord Beaumont; Margery, wife of Sir William, 4th Lord FitzHugh; & Margaret, wife of Sir Thomas Skipwith, & of Sir William Oldhall).
2 CONT
2 CONT Sir William de Willoughby, 5th Lord Willoughby married Joan Holand, daughter of Sir Thomas Holand, 2nd Earl Kent, Lord Holland, 6th Lord Wake, Lord Woodstock, Marshal of England and Alice FitzAlan, between 1 August 1402 and 9 Augu
2 CONC st 1404; No issue. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Family 1      
2 CONT
2 CONT Lucy le Strange (b. c 1364, d. bt 28 Apr 1398 - 9 Aug 1404) was the  daughter of Roger le Strange and Aline FitzAlan. They married after 3 January 1382/3, when a licence for their marriage was dated.[1][2] They were certainly marr
2 CONC ied by 3 April 1383 when William's father gave a recognisance to Lucy's mother.[4] They had five children.
2 CONT
2 CONT The children of Lucy le Strange and William de Willoughby were:
2 CONT # Sir Robert Willoughby, 6th Lord Willoughby b. c 1385, d. 25 Jul 1452.  Married 1) Elizabeth Montagu 2) Maud Stanhope
2 CONT # Elizabeth Willoughby b. c 1388, d. 14 Nov 1427, who married Henry Beaumont.
2 CONT # Margaret Willoughby b. c 1393, d. c 1455, who married Thomas Skipwith and William Oldhall.
2 CONT # Sir Thomas Willoughby b. c 1395, d. b 1 Jul 1439.  Married Jane FitzAlan.
2 CONT # Margery Willoughby b. c 1400, d. b 22 Oct 1452, who married William FitzHugh.
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT Family 2 Joan Holand b. bt 1380 - d. 12 Apr 1434. (No children)
2 CONT
2 CONT Lady Joan Holland (d. 12 April 1434), was the widow of Edmund of Langley, 1st Duke of York when she married William Willoughby.  After his death his widow married, thirdly, Henry Scrope, 3rd Baron Scrope of Masham, who was beheade
2 CONC d on 5 August 1415 after the discovery of the Southampton Plot on the eve of King Henry V's invasion of France. She married, fourthly, Henry Bromflete, Lord Vescy (d. 16 January 1469).
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT Sir William de Willoughby, 5th Lord Willoughby died on 4 December 1409 at Edgefield, England; Buried at Spilsby, Lincolnshire, with his 1st wife.  A chapel in the church at Spilsby still contains the monuments and brasses of sever
2 CONC al early members of the Willoughby family, including the 5th baron and his first wife.
2 CONT
2 CONT Writs for his Inquisitions Post Mortem were issued on 10 December 1409.  It show him holding lands in Cambridgeshire, Lincolnshire, Norfolk, Northamptonshire and Suffolk.
2 CONT
2 CONT In Shakespeare
2 CONT
2 CONT William has a minor appearance in Shakespeare's Richard II (Act II Scene I, where he is critical of Richard II's government).
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT ===Origins
2 CONT
2 CONT https://www.geni.com/people/Lady-Alice-Skipwith-Fictional/6000000006444299265 was not his mother.
2 CONT
2 CONT "Robtus Dns Willoughbie de Eresbie ob: Anno 20: Ri:. 2 = Dau of the Lo Zouch" [Reference: H. St. George, Wiltshire Vis. Peds. 1623 (H.S.P. 105-106) (1954): 216–218 (Willoughbie pedigree)].
2 CONT
2 CONT {{https://www.geni.com/media/proxy?media_id=6000000185051121869&size=large}}
2 CONT
2 CONT Also see Douglas Richardson, Nov 30, 2013, posts at <[https://groups.google.com/g/soc.genealogy.medieval/c/ahPQ9Q93d7g/m/Tnw91AvBCAAJ Soc.Gen.Medieval]>
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT ===Heraldry 
2 CONT
2 CONT Garter stall plate of William Willoughby, 5th Baron Willoughby de Eresby KG, showing on a shield couchée the arms borne by Willoughby: Quarterly 1 & 4: Sable, a cross engrailed or (Ufford); 2 & 3: Gules, a cross moline argent (Bec
2 CONC  of Eresby)
2 CONT
2 CONT {{https://www.geni.com/media/proxy?media_id=6000000185050090915&size=large}}
2 CONT ---
