2 CONT ==House of Neville==
2 CONT *The House of Neville (also the House of Nevill) is a noble house of early medieval origin, which was a leading force in English politics in the later Middle Ages. The family became one of the two major powers in northern England 
2 CONC along with the House of Percy and played a central role in the Wars of the Roses. .... etc.
2 CONT ===Members in the male line===
2 CONT *John Neville, 3rd Baron Neville de Raby
2 CONT ** 1. Ralph de Neville, 1st Earl of Westmorland, 1364 - 1425
2 CONT ***    A. John Neville, Lord Neville (d. 1420) 
2 CONT ****       I. Ralph Neville, 2nd Earl of Westmorland
2 CONT ****       II. John Neville, Baron Neville
2 CONT *****          a. Ralph Neville, 3rd Earl of Westmorland
2 CONT ******              i. Ralph Neville, Lord Neville
2 CONT *******                 1. Ralph Neville, 4th Earl of Westmorland
2 CONT ********                     A. Henry Neville, 5th Earl of Westmorland
2 CONT ********                          I. Charles Neville, 6th Earl of Westmorland
2 CONT ****'''       III. Sir Thomas Neville
2 CONT ***    B. .... etc.
2 CONT *From: http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/House_of_Neville _________________________
2 CONT *Ralph Neville, 2nd Earl of Westmorland (4 April 1406 – 3 November 1484) was an English peer.
2 CONT *Ralph Neville, 2nd Earl of Westmorland, was born 4 April 1406[1] in Cockermouth, Cumberland, the eldest son of Sir John Neville (d.1420), and Elizabeth Holland (c. 1388 – 3 or 4 January 1423), the daughter of Thomas Holland, 2nd 
2 CONC Earl of Kent, and Alice FitzAlan (d.17 March 1416).[2]
2 CONT *He had two brothers, John Neville, Baron Neville (c.1410-1461), who was slain at the Battle of Towton on 29 March 1461, and '''Sir Thomas Neville (died c. 1461) of Brancepeth, Durham''', and one sister, Margaret, who married Sir 
2 CONC William Lucy of Woodcroft, Bedfordshire.[3] .... etc.
2 CONT *From: http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ralph_Neville,_2nd_Earl_of_Westmorland __________________________
2 CONT *John Neville, Baron Neville (c.1410 – 29 March 1461) was an English nobleman and soldier, slain at the Battle of Towton. His son succeeded to the earldom of Westmorland.
2 CONT *John Neville was born about 1410,[1] the second son of Sir John Neville (d.1420) and Elizabeth Holland (c.1388 – 3 or 4 January 1423), the daughter of Thomas Holland, 2nd Earl of Kent, and Alice FitzAlan (d.17 March 1416).[2]
2 CONT *He had two brothers, Ralph Neville, 2nd Earl of Westmorland, and '''Sir Thomas Neville (died c. 1461) of Brancepeth, Durham''', and one sister, Margaret, who married Sir William Lucy of Woodcroft, Bedfordshire.[3]
2 CONT *Sometime before 5 February 1442 Neville married Anne Holland, widow of his nephew, Sir John Neville (d. shortly before 16 March 1450), the son of Ralph Neville, 2nd Earl of Westmorland and his first wife, Lady Elizabeth Percy. An
2 CONC ne Holland was the daughter of John Holland, 2nd Duke of Exeter by his first wife, Anne Stafford, the daughter of Edmund Stafford, 5th Earl of Stafford and the widow of Edmund Mortimer, 5th Earl of March .[4] John Neville and Anne
2 CONC  Holland had one son, Ralph Neville, 3rd Earl of Westmorland.[5] .... etc.
2 CONT *From: http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/John_Neville,_Baron_Neville _________________________
2 CONT *Ralph Neville, 1st Earl of Westmorland, 4th Baron Neville de Raby,[a] Earl Marshal, KG, PC (c. 1364 – 21 October 1425), was an English nobleman of the House of Neville.
2 CONT *Ralph Neville was born about 1364, the son of John Neville, 3rd Baron Neville de Raby, and The Hon Maud Percy (d. before 18 February 1379), daughter of Henry de Percy, 2nd Baron Percy of Alnwick, Northumberland, by Idoine de Clif
2 CONC ford, daughter of Robert de Clifford, 1st Baron de Clifford.[1] Neville had a younger brother, and five sisters:[2] .... etc.
2 CONT *Neville married firstly, Margaret Stafford (d. 9 June 1396), the eldest daughter of Hugh Stafford, 2nd Earl of Stafford, and Philippa Beauchamp, the daughter of Thomas Beauchamp, 11th Earl of Warwick, by Katherine Mortimer, the d
2 CONC aughter of Roger Mortimer, 1st Earl of March.[13] They had two sons and six daughters:
2 CONT ** Sir John Neville (c.1387 – before 20 May 1420), who married Elizabeth Holland, fifth daughter of Thomas Holland, 2nd Earl of Kent, and Alice FitzAlan, and by her had three sons, Ralph Neville, 2nd Earl of Westmorland, John Nevi
2 CONC lle, Baron Neville, and '''Sir Thomas Neville''', and a daughter, Margaret Neville.[14]
2 CONT ** Sir Ralph Neville (d. 25 Feb 1458), .... etc.
2 CONT *From: http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ralph_Neville,_1st_Earl_of_Westmorland ____________________________
