1 OCCU Hertiginna av Lothringen 1473 och Bar 1480
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} '''Yolande (2 November 1428 in Nancy – 23 March 1483 in Nancy) was Duchess of Lorraine (1473) and Bar (1480). She was the daughter of''' Isabella, Duchess of Lorraine, and René of Anjou (King of Naples, Duke of Anj
2 CONC ou, Bar and Lorraine, Count of Provence).[1] '''Though she was nominally in control of major territories, she ceded her power and titles to her husband and her son. In addition, her younger sister''' was Margaret of Anjou, Queen o
2 CONC f England.
2 CONT '''In the 19th century, a romanticised version of her early life was popularised by the play King René's Daughter''' by Henrik Hertz, in which she is portrayed as a beautiful blind princess living in an isolated garden paradise. I
2 CONC t was later adapted to Tchaikovsky's opera Iolanta. '''There is no evidence that she was ever blind.'''
2 CONT Marriage and children
2 CONT '''In 1445, she married her second cousin Frederick II, Count of Vaudémont''' (1420–1470), '''at Nancy.[1] The marriage was a dynastic alliance, arranged to end the dispute which existed between René of Anjou and Frederick's fathe
2 CONC r, Antoine of Vaudémont, regarding the succession to the Duchy of Lorraine.'''
2 CONT '''Children'''
2 CONT 1. René (future Duke of Lorraine) (1451–1508), Duke of Lorraine.[1] On 1 September 1485 he married, secondly, Philippa of Guelders.
2 CONT 2. Nicolas of Lorraine, Lord of Joinville and Bauffremont, died in 1476.
2 CONT 3. Peter of Lorraine, died in 1451.
2 CONT 4. Jeanne of Lorraine (1458 – 25 January 1480), married in 1474 to Charles IV, Duke of Anjou[2]
2 CONT 5. Yolande of Lorraine, who died in 1500, married in 1497 to William II, Landgrave of Hesse, by whom she had issue.
2 CONT 6. Marguerite of Lorraine (1463–1521), married in 1488 to René, Duke of Alençon (1454–1492)
2 CONT Reign
2 CONT '''In 1473, on the death of her nephew''' Nicolas, '''she inherited the Duchy of Lorraine, but passed it immediately to her eldest son''' René II. In 1480, '''after the death of her father, she did the same with the Duchy of Bar. 
2 CONC She died on 23 March 1483, which was the birthday of her sister''' Queen Margaret, who had died the previous summer. '''Yolande was 54 years old.'''
2 CONT Cultural references
2 CONT '''In 1845 the Danish poet Henrik Hertz wrote the poetic drama Kong Renés Datter (King René’s Daughter)''', a romanticised account of her life, in which she is depicted as a beautiful sixteen-year-old princess who lives in a prote
2 CONC cted garden paradise. Blinded in a childhood accident, her attendants must keep from her the knowledge that she is blind, while a Moorish physician conducts a long slow medical procedure to restore her sight. Once it is complete, 
2 CONC she must be told of her blindness to awaken the desire to see. Count Vaudémont arrives for his arranged marriage, which he resents. He accidentally finds her secret garden, and falls in love with her without knowing who she is. He
2 CONC  discovers she is blind, and tells her so, but she cannot understand him. However, the physician is now able to complete the treatment and she is cured.
2 CONT The portrayal of Yolande as a saintly dreaming beauty (regularly placed in an entranced sleep by the physician) was immensely popular. The play was translated into numerous languages. The Russian translation by Fyodor Miller was a
2 CONC dapted by Vladimir Zotov, whose version was used as the basis for the opera Iolanta, written by Tchaikovsky, with libretto by his brother Modest Ilyich Tchaikovsky. It received its premiere on 18 December 1892 in St. Petersburg.[3
2 CONC ]
2 CONT The heroine's name was given as "Iolanthe" in the original Danish version and in the English translation by Theodore Martin, making this version of her name familiar in the 19th century. In 1913 a silent film of Hertz's play was m
2 CONC ade by the Thanhouser Company, starring Maude Fealy as Yolande.[4] It was also adapted in 1990 as the German film Das Licht der Liebe.[5]
2 CONT In the pseudohistorical book The Holy Blood and the Holy Grail Yolande de Bar was alleged to have been the tenth Grand Master of the Priory of Sion, succeeding her father, King René. The evidence for this claim was derived from th
2 CONC e Dossiers Secrets d'Henri Lobineau, '''forged documents created in 1967.'''
2 CONT etc.
2 CONT From: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Yolande,_Duchess_of_Lorraine
2 CONT ____
2 CONT '''Jolanta de Anjou'''1,2
2 CONT Last Edited	29 Nov 2005
2 CONT F, #18455, '''b. 2 November 1428, d. 23 February 1484'''
2 CONT '''Father'''	Renatus, Duke of Anjou, Lorraine, & Bar, King of Naples, Jerusalem, Valence, & Majorca, Comte de Provence, Barcelona, Forcalquier, & Piemont1 b. 16 Jan 1409, d. 10 Jul 1480
2 CONT '''Mother'''	Isabel of Lorraine b. c 1400, d. 28 Feb 1453
2 CONT Charts	
2 CONT 14 Generation Pedigree of George III, King of Great Britain & Ireland
2 CONT 14 Generation Pedigree of Friedrich Wilhelm III, King of Prussia
2 CONT '''     Jolanta de Anjou was born on 2 November 1428 at Bar le Duc, France. She married Frederick VI, Count Vaudemont, Baron Joinville, Seigneur de Lambesc, Suse, Verbenne, Rumigny, & Boves''', son of Anton, Count Vaudemont, Lord 
2 CONC Joinville, Seigneur de Rumigny, Boves, & Florennes and Mary of Harcourt, '''circa February 1445 at Nancy, France.1 Jolanta de Anjou died on 23 February 1484 at Chateau de Bar le Duc, Bar le Duc, France, at age 55; Buried at Joinvi
2 CONC lle.'''1
2 CONT '''Family'''
2 CONT '''Frederick VI, Count Vaudemont, Baron Joinville, Seigneur de Lambesc, Suse, Verbenne, Rumigny, & Boves''' b. 1417, d. 31 Aug 1470
2 CONT '''Children	'''
2 CONT *Jolanthe of Lorraine+1,3 d. 21 May 1500
2 CONT *Nicholas, Seigneur de Joinville & Bauffremont1 d. c 1476
2 CONT *Peter of Lorraine1 d. 1451
2 CONT *Renatus II, Duke of Lorraine & Bar, Count Vaudemont, d'Harcourt, & Guise, Baron de Joinville, King of Sicily & Jerusalem+ b. 2 May 1451, d. 10 Dec 1508
2 CONT *Jeanne of Lorraine1,3 b. 1458, d. 25 Jan 1480
2 CONT *Margareta of Lorraine+1,4 b. 1463, d. 2 Nov 1521
