1 NOTE Margaret of Anjou (1430-82), queen consort of Henry VI of England. Owing to Henry�s imbecility, Margaret�s authority was supreme. She strove to uphold the rights of her son Edward in the wars of the Roses. [World Wide Illustrated 
2 CONC Encyclopedia, 1935]
2 CONT
2 CONT Owing to Henry�s weak intellect, Margaret was virtual sovereign. In 1447, she and the Beaufort party had Gloucester arrested for treason; five days later he was found dead in his bed, but there is no there is no proof that he was 
2 CONC murdered. The war of 1449, in which Normandy was lost, was laid to her charge. Margaret took up leadership of the Lancastrian faction until her capture at the Battle of Tewkesbury. She lay in the Tower for 4 years until ransomed b
2 CONC y Louis XI, retiring to France. {Chamber�s Biographical Dictionary} [GADD.GED]
