1 NOTE {geni:about_me} https://en.m.wikipedia.org/wiki/Louis_of_Cyprus
2 CONT
2 CONT Louis of Cyprus
2 CONT
2 CONT Louis of Savoy (Italian: Ludovico; 1436–37 ; April 1482), Count of Geneva from 1460, became King of Cyprus in 1459 upon his second marriage to Charlotte of Cyprus, reigning together with and in the right of his wife until 1464. He
2 CONC  was the second son and namesake of Louis, Duke of Savoy and his wife, Anne of Lusignan,[1] daughter of King Janus of Cyprus.
2 CONT
2 CONT Louis
2 CONT King of Cyprus (jure uxoris)
2 CONT Reign
2 CONT 1459–1464
2 CONT Predecessor
2 CONT Charlotte (as sole monarch)
2 CONT Successor
2 CONT James II
2 CONT Co-ruler
2 CONT Charlotte
2 CONT Contender
2 CONT James II (from 1460)
2 CONT Count of Geneva
2 CONT Reign
2 CONT 1460–1482
2 CONT Born
2 CONT June 1436
2 CONT Geneva
2 CONT Died
2 CONT April 1482
2 CONT priory of Ripaille
2 CONT Spouses
2 CONT Annabella of Scotland
2 CONT Charlotte of Cyprus
2 CONT House
2 CONT Savoy
2 CONT Father
2 CONT Louis, Duke of Savoy
2 CONT Mother
2 CONT Anne of Lusignan
2 CONT Contents
2 CONT Life
2 CONT edit
2 CONT Louis was born, according to Samuel Guichenon, in June 1431, in Geneva, but the historian specifies in note that he was born in 1436.[2] The birth in June 1436 is therefore that adopted by contemporary authors.[3][4] Guichenon als
2 CONC o specifies that the prince is 8 years old when he married in 1444. Some mention a period between 1436 and 1437, [5] especially for this last year the Swiss historian Édouard Mallet (1805–1856).[6]
2 CONT
2 CONT On 14 December 1444, at Stirling Castle, he was married to Annabella, youngest daughter of King James I of Scotland (d. 1437) and sister of King James II of Scotland.[a] The official wedding never took place and the marriage was a
2 CONC nnulled in 1458.[8]
2 CONT
2 CONT On 7 October 1458, his cousin Charlotte became Queen of Cyprus. He married her on 7 October 1459 and became King of Cyprus[9] as well as the titular King of Jerusalem and of the Armenian Kingdom of Cilicia. Their reign ended when 
2 CONC they were deposed.[10]
2 CONT
2 CONT Louis died in April 1482, at the priory of Ripaille.[7][11]
2 CONT
2 CONT Notes
2 CONT edit
2 CONT  According to George Hill, upon Annabella's arrival in Savoy, Charles VII of France opposed the marriage, and the arrangement was broken on 3 March 1455. This was accepted by James II of Scotland on 7 May 1456.[7]
2 CONT References
2 CONT edit
2 CONT  Ward, Prothero & Leathes 1911, p. table 67.
2 CONT  Samuel Guichenon, Histoire généalogique de la royale maison de Savoie, justifiée par titres, fondations de monastères, manuscrits, anciens monuments, histoires et autres preuves authentiques. Livres 1–2 / ; enrichie de plusieurs 
2 CONC portraits, sceaux, monnaies, sculptures et armoiries, G. Barbier, Lyon, 1660, p.536-546 on line.
2 CONT  Douglas Richardson, Magna Carta Ancestry: A Study in Colonial and Medieval Families, ed. Kimball G. Everingham, vol. I, Salt Lake City, 2011 (on line)
2 CONT  (it) Giuseppe Gullino, Dizionario Biografico degli Italiani Volume 66 (2006) – [Treccani], « Ludovico, di Savoia, re di Cipro » online.
2 CONT  Candida Syndikus, Sabine Rogge, Caterina Cornaro Last Queen of Cyprus and Daughter of Venice Ultima regina di Cipro e figlia di Venezia, Waxmann Verlag, 2013, page 403.
2 CONT  Édouard Mallet, Documents genevois inédits pour la généalogie de la maison souveraine de Savoie, depuis le XIIe jusqu'au XVe siècle, Impr. Royale, 1836, p. 31 (online).
2 CONT  Hill 1948, p. 610.
2 CONT  Eva Pibiri, « A la recherche d'une épouse. Ambassades et voyages autour des fiançailles d'Annabelle d'Ecosse et de Louis de Savoie, comte de Genève (1444–1445) », Cahiers lausannois d'histoire médiévale, vol. L'itinérance des sei
