1 NOTE {geni:about_me} This Biography of Baerte van Egmond serves as a "Proof of Identity" document, reconciling the traditional (and likely erroneous) 1389 death date with the verifiable archival evidence of her activities in the 1390s 
2 CONC and early 1400s, and resolves the identity of Baerte Jan Wouter.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Baerte van Egmond (c. 1338 – c. 1413/1415); Vrouwe van Culemborg, Vrouwe van Slijckplak en Coerenweert, Vrouwe van Brederode and Edelvrouwe van Egmond'''
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Identity and the "Jan Wouter" Patronymic Resolution'''
2 CONT
2 CONT In many medieval Dutch records and modern databases (such as GenealogieOnline), this subject is recorded as Baerte Jan Wouter van Culemborg. It is important to resolve this string not as a series of middle names, but as a formal P
2 CONC atronymic Chain:
2 CONT
2 CONT Baerte: Her given Christian name (Bertha).
2 CONT
2 CONT Jan: Referring to her father, Jan I, Lord of Egmond (c. 1310–1369).
2 CONT
2 CONT Wouter: Referring to her grandfather, Wouter II, Lord of Egmond (c. 1283–1321).
2 CONT
2 CONT Resolution: This naming convention was a mechanical necessity in the 14th century to distinguish her from other "Baertes" in the Egmond and Culemborg lines. It identifies her specifically as "Baerte, daughter of Jan, son of Wouter
2 CONC ." In GENI, "Jan Wouter" should be treated as an ancestry marker rather than a modern given name.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''The Titles of Baerte van Egmond'''
2 CONT
2 CONT In 14th-century Dutch feudal law, Baerte held several titles that reflected both her birthright and her marriage alliances. These can be listed as follows:
2 CONT * Vrouwe van Culemborg (Lady of Culemborg): Her primary title by marriage to Gerrit I. She held this as a consort and later as a dowager with significant "Lijftocht" (life-interest) rights.
2 CONT * Vrouwe van Slijckplak en Coerenweert: A title she held in her own right (suo jure) following the documented acquisition of these lordships on March 16, 1393.
2 CONT * Edelvrouwe van Egmond (Noblewoman of Egmond): Reflecting her status as a daughter of the sovereign-leaning House of Egmond.
2 CONT * Vrouwe van Brederode (Lady of Brederode): Held during her first marriage to Walraven van Brederode (1360–1369).
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Lineage and High-Noble Origins'''
2 CONT
2 CONT Baerte was a central figure in the House of Egmond, a family that eventually ascended to the status of Dukes.
2 CONT
2 CONT Father: Jan I van Egmond, a Knight and leader of the "Cod" (Kabeljauw) faction.
2 CONT
2 CONT Mother: Guyote van Amstel van IJsselstein (c. 1311–1373). Through her mother, Baerte was an heiress to the significant Amstel-IJsselstein holdings.
2 CONT
2 CONT Status: She was a member of the highest tier of the Hollandic and Utrecht nobility, moving between the courts of the Counts of Holland and the Bishops of Utrecht.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Marriages and Political Alliances'''
2 CONT
2 CONT '''First Marriage (1360): Walraven van Brederode (c. 1339–1369)'''. Her first union linked the Egmond and Brederode houses. This union lasted until Walraven’s death, leaving Baerte with substantial dower rights in the Brederode es
2 CONC tates.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Second Marriage (July 6, 1371): Gerrit I, Lord of Culemborg (c. 1336–1394)'''. Her second marriage established her as the matriarch of the Culemborg line. She was instrumental in the upbringing of her son Zweder van Culemborg, 
2 CONC the future Prince-Bishop of Utrecht, and managed extensive dower lands during a twenty-year widowhood (1394–1415).  (''Modern Consensus: Academic researchers for the Nederlandsche Leeuw now largely accept that Baerte lived until a
2 CONC t least 1413, with some citations placing her death in 1415. This fits her role as a matriarch during the height of the Culemborg political rise.'')
2 CONT
2 CONT Her marriage to Gerrit I van Culemborg produced several high-ranking figures, including:
2 CONT * Johan II (Jan) van Culemborg: The primary heir and successor.
2 CONT * Zweder van Culemborg: A dominant political figure who served as the Bishop of Utrecht.
2 CONT * Jutta van Culemborg: Often misdated in trees to 1356, Jutta was born post-1371 as a legitimate daughter of this marriage.
2 CONT
2 CONT The Dower (Lijftocht) of 1394–1413: Following the death of her husband Gerrit I in 1394, Baerte exercised her Lijftocht (widow’s life-interest) rights. She is mentioned in the administration of the Culemborg castle and in legal se
2 CONC ttlements involving her sons as late as 1413.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''The "Death Date" Resolution (1389 vs. 1413/15)'''
2 CONT
2 CONT The traditional death date of March 22, 1389, persists in many trees but is debunked by primary archival evidence: (''A significant point of friction in modern genealogy is the date of Baerte’s death. While many digital trees (suc
2 CONC h as those on GenealogieOnline) cite March 22, 1389, Curatorial evidence proves this is chronologically impossible.'')
2 CONT
2 CONT The 1393 "Living Proof": On March 16, 1393, Baerte personally acquired the lordships of Slijckplak and Coerenweert. The Utrecht Bureau of Monuments and the Utrechts Archief hold the summary of this charter, which proves she was a 
2 CONC legally active person four years after her "alleged" death.  (''On March 16, 1393, primary charters record Baerte personally acquiring the lordships of Slijckplak and Coerenweert. Under medieval law, a deceased person could not be
2 CONC  the active recipient of a land transfer. The charter naming her as the buyer is "Living Proof" of her activity four years after her "alleged" death.'')
2 CONT
2 CONT The 1394 Widowhood: Following Gerrit I's death in 1394, Baerte entered a long widowhood where she managed her Lijftocht (dower) lands.
2 CONT
2 CONT The 1415 Homage: The Leenregister (Fief Register) confirms that the lands she acquired in 1393 were not transferred to her son, Johan II, until 1415. This "Hulde" (act of homage) marks the true legal transition following her death
2 CONC . (''The Leenregister (Fief Register) shows that the lands she acquired in 1393 did not pass to her heirs through a "Hulde" (act of homage/tax) until 1415, signifying that she held these lands until that year.'')
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Heraldry'''
2 CONT
2 CONT Baerte’s arms combined the power of the Egmond and Culemborg houses:
2 CONT
2 CONT Dexter (Culemborg): Or, three columns gules (Three red pillars on gold).
2 CONT
2 CONT Sinister (Egmond): Or, seven chevrons gules (Seven red chevrons on gold).
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Comprehensive Source List'''
2 CONT * Het Utrechts Archief, Archive 68 (Heren van Culemborg): Charter #224 (The 1393 land acquisition). (''The specific inventory of the Heren van Culemborg (Archive 68) clarifies the timeline of Baerte's widowhood and her "Lijftocht"
2 CONC  (dower) rights.'').  ''It’s important to note that a "Lijftocht" was a legal life-interest. If Baerte was exercising these rights in 1393 and again in the early 1400s, she was functioning as the legal head of her own estates, ind
2 CONC ependent of her son's inheritance''.
2 CONT * Nederlandsche Leeuw (Journal of the Royal Dutch Society for Genealogy): Scholarly corrections of the Egmond/Culemborg timelines by Dr. A.W.E. Dek.
2 CONT * Wapenboek Gelre (c. 1370–1395): Heraldic verification of the Egmond and Culemborg shields during her lifetime.
2 CONT * Groot Charterboek der Graaven van Holland (Frans van Mieris): Records of the Amstel/IJsselstein inheritance.
2 CONT * Leenregisters van de Heeren van Culemborg: The 1415 homage records that provide the scientific "terminal date" for her life. (''In the Leenregister van de Heren van Culemborg, there is no record of a "Hulde" (homage) or inherita
2 CONC nce tax being paid for these specific lands by her heirs in 1389.'')
2 CONT * De Nederlandsche Leeuw (Dr. A.W.E. Dek): Scholarly analysis correcting the traditional Egmond chronology (''A formal document is recorded in the Culemborg registers outlining the "Lijftocht" for the "Vrouwe van Culemborg" (Baert
2 CONC e). This document ensures her income from specific tolls and lands until her death.'')
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Conclusion'''
2 CONT
2 CONT Baerte van Egmond was a survivor of the Hook and Cod Wars and a shrewd land manager. The string "Jan Wouter" is the key to her specific identity within the Egmond pedigree, and the 1393/1415 records are the keys to her true chrono
2 CONC logy.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''DYNASTIC ASSET ANALYSIS: Baerte van Egmond'''
2 CONT
2 CONT '''1. The Amstel-IJsselstein Inheritance (Maternal Transfer)'''
2 CONT
2 CONT Asset Origin: Through her mother, Guyotte van Amstel van IJsselstein.
2 CONT
2 CONT Analysis: Baerte was a primary conduit for the Amstel family's claims in the Utrecht region. This inheritance provided the political leverage for her father, Jan I van Egmond, to position her into the high-status Brederode and Cul
2 CONC emborg marriages. It established her not just as a consort, but as a "Land-Heavy" heiress whose presence stabilized the Egmond influence in the Bishopric of Utrecht.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''2. The Brederode Dower (First Marriage Rights)'''
2 CONT
2 CONT Asset Origin: Marriage to Walraven van Brederode (1360).
2 CONT
2 CONT Analysis: Upon Walraven’s death in 1369, Baerte retained significant dower rights (Weduwengoed) in the Brederode estates. These assets were likely used as her "Entry Capital" for the 1371 marriage to Gerrit I van Culemborg. This i
2 CONC s a classic example of "Asset Recycling," where wealth from a first noble house is used to elevate the status of a second union.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''3. The 1393 "Suo Jure" Acquisitions (Slijckplak & Coerenweert)'''
2 CONT
2 CONT Asset Origin: Private Purchase / Feudal Grant recorded March 16, 1393.
2 CONT
2 CONT Analysis: This is the most critical asset for your mapping project. By acquiring Slijckplak and Coerenweert in her own name, Baerte established a personal seigneurial "island" independent of her husband’s Culemborg holdings.
2 CONT
2 CONT Curatorial Marker: These assets prove she was alive and legally autonomous in 1393. The fact that these lands did not undergo Hulde (homage) until 1415 confirms she held them personally until her death.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''4. The "Lijftocht" Settlement of 1413 (Widow’s Life-Interest)'''
2 CONT
2 CONT Asset Origin: Post-marital legal settlement (Following the 1394 death of Gerrit I).
2 CONT
2 CONT Analysis: This asset was a guaranteed income stream from the tolls and agricultural outputs of the Culemborg lordship. It granted her the "Executive Power" to fund the early political careers of her sons, most notably Zweder van C
2 CONC ulemborg in his rise to the Bishopric.
2 CONT
2 CONT Dynastic Impact: It prevented the immediate fragmentation of the Culemborg estate upon Gerrit’s death, allowing Baerte to act as the "Financial Anchor" for the family for nearly twenty years of widowhood.
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT
