0 @N6000000002188017486@ NOTE Manfred II of Saluzzo
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Manfred II (1140 – 1215) was the second margrave of Saluzzo from his father's death in 1175 to his own. He was the son of Manfred I and Eleanor. He placed the capital of the margravate definitively in Saluzzo.
1 CONT
1 CONT He expanded the march and fought against the expansionism of the neighbouring counts of Savoy. After several minor skirmished, the two principalities came to terms in 1213 and peace was established for the final two years of his l
1 CONC ife. However, on his death, his widow, Alaisia of Montferrat, had to pay tribute on behalf of her son and for the next century, Saluzzo was a vassal of Savoy. He was succeeded by his eponymous son, Manfred III.
1 CONT
1 CONT Preceded by:
1 CONT Manfred I Margrave of Saluzzo
1 CONT 1175 – 1215 Succeeded by:
1 CONT Manfred III 
0 @N6000000004303404346@ NOTE WIKIPEDIA:
1 CONT
1 CONT Manfred II of Saluzzo
1 CONT
1 CONT Manfred II (1140 – 1215) was the second margrave of Saluzzo from his father's death in 1175 to his own. He was the son of Manfred I and Eleanor. He placed the capital of the margravate definitively in Saluzzo.
1 CONT He married Azalaïs of Montferrat before 1182, forming an alliance with one of the most powerful dynasties in northern Italy. He had at least five children with her:
1 CONT
1 CONT •	Agnes, married Comita III of Torres
1 CONT •	Boniface (the heir, who predeceased his father), married Maria, daughter of aforementioned Comita
1 CONT •	Margaret, married Geoffrey de Salvaing
1 CONT •	(unnamed daughter), married Marquis William II of Ceva
1 CONT •	Thomas
1 CONT He also fathered an illegitimate son, Bastardino.
1 CONT
1 CONT He expanded the march and fought against the expansionism of the neighbouring counts of Savoy. After several minor skirmishes, the two principalities came to terms in 1213 and peace was established for the final two years of his l
1 CONC ife. Since his eldest son Boniface had predeceased him in 1212, he was succeeded by his grandson, Manfred III, under the regency of Azalaïs. She had to pay tribute on behalf of young Manfred, and for the next century, Saluzzo was 
1 CONC a vassal of Savoy.
1 CONT
1 CONT Preceded by Manfred I
1 CONT Margrave of Saluzzo 1175 – 1215
1 CONT Succeeded by Manfred III
1 CONT --------------------
1 CONT
1 CONT Children
1 CONT 1.	 Bonifacio del Vasto , di Saluzzo b: ABT 1180 in Saluzzo, Turin, Italy
1 CONT 2.	 Daughter del Vasto di Saluzzo b: ABT 1180 in Saluzzo, Turin, Italy
0 @N6000000004303404418@ NOTE AKA  Adilasia.
1 CONT WIKIPEDIA:
1 CONT
1 CONT Azalaïs of Montferrat
1 CONT
1 CONT Azalaïs of Montferrat (also Adelasia or Alasia) (d. 1232), was marchioness and regent of Saluzzo.
1 CONT
1 CONT Azalaïs was one of at least three daughters of William V of Montferrat and his wife Judith of Babenberg. Her brothers included William of Montferrat, Count of Jaffa and Ascalon, Conrad I of Jerusalem, and Boniface of Montferrat.
1 CONT She married Marquis Manfred II of Saluzzo before 1182, in which year she received lands in Saluzzo, Racconigi, Villa, Centallo and Quaranta, in case her marriage (like that of her sister Agnes) should need to be annulled for reaso
1 CONC ns of sterility. However, after this date, she bore at least five children:
1 CONT
1 CONT •	Agnes m. Comita III, giudice of Logudoro, in Sardinia.
1 CONT •	Boniface (the heir, who predeceased his father) m. Maria of Torres (daughter of Agnes's husband).
1 CONT •	Margaret m. Geoffrey de Salvaing.
1 CONT •	(unnamed daughter) m. Marquis William II of Ceva.
1 CONT •	Thomas.
1 CONT Like her brother Boniface, Azalaïs was a patron of troubadours. She is mentioned in Peire Vidal's song, Estat ai gran sazo:
1 CONT Dieus sal l'onrat marques
1 CONT E sa bella seror...
1 CONT (God save the honoured marquis
1 CONT And his beautiful sister)[1]
1 CONT and is the dedicatee of his Bon' aventura don Dieus als Pizas.[2]
1 CONT Around 1192, she had built the church of San Lorenzo, which she granted to the canons of San Lorenzo in Oulx; her eldest son, Boniface, named after her brother, is mentioned for the first time in the donation. However, Boniface di
1 CONC ed in 1212, and with the death of her husband in February 1215, Azalaîs became regent of Saluzzo for her grandson, Manfred III.
1 CONT In 1216, she made a treaty with Thomas I of Savoy for a marriage between his son Amadeus and her grand-daughter Agnes. However, the marriage never took place, possibly on grounds of consanguinity, since Azalaïs was a first cousin 
1 CONC of Thomas's father. Amadeus married Anne of Burgundy, and Agnes became Abbess of the Cistercian convent of Santa Maria della Stella in Rifreddo.[3] Azalaïs also made political and ecclesiastical agreements with Alba and with the B
1 CONC ishop of Asti.
1 CONT When young Manfred reached his majority in 1218, Azalaïs returned to church patronage. In 1224, she endowed the convent of Rifreddo with the income of the church of San Ilario. In 1227, she made further grants to the canons of Oul
1 CONC x. She died in 1232, and was buried in the Cistercian abbey of Santa Maria di Staffarda.
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT Notes
1 CONT 1.	^ Peire Vidal, Estat ai gran sazo
1 CONT 2.	^ Peire Vidal, Bon' aventura don Dieus als Pizas
1 CONT 3.	^ comune.rifreddo.cn.it: Informazioni storiche
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT Sources & Further Reading
1 CONT •	Ernest Hoepffner, Le Troubadour Peire Vidal: sa vie et son œuvre, Paris, 1961
1 CONT •	A M Patrone, "Alasia", Dizionario Biografico degli Italiani, vol. 1, p. 583
1 CONT •	Leopoldo Usseglio, I Marchesi di Monferrato in Italia ed in Oriente durante i secoli XII e XIII, 1926.
