0 @N6000000001877484216@ NOTE Sir
1 CONT 10th Earl of Arundel
1 CONT Source
1 CONT www.thepeerage.com
0 @N6000000001783373315@ NOTE Basic Life Information
1 CONT
1 CONT Richard FitzAlan, "Copped Hat", 10th Earl of Arundel (9th Earl of Arundel per Ancestral Roots) (c. 1306 - 24 January 1376) was an English nobleman and medieval military leader.
1 CONT
1 CONT Lineage
1 CONT
1 CONT FitzAlan was the eldest son of Edmund FitzAlan, 9th Earl of Arundel (8th Earl of Arundel per Ancestral Roots), and Alice de Warenne. His maternal grandparents were William de Warenne, 8th Earl of Surrey and Joan de Vere. William w
1 CONC as the only son of John de Warenne, 7th Earl of Surrey.
1 CONT
1 CONT He was born 1306 in Sussex, England.
1 CONT
1 CONT Alliance with the Despensers
1 CONT
1 CONT Around 1321, FitzAlan's father allied with King Edward II's favorites, Hugh le Despenser, 1st Earl of Winchester and his namesake son, and Richard was married to Isabel le Despenser, daughter of Hugh the Younger. Fortune turned ag
1 CONC ainst the Despenser party, and on 17 November 1326, FitzAlan's father was executed, and he did not succeed to his father's estates or titles.
1 CONT
1 CONT Gradual Restoration
1 CONT
1 CONT However, political conditions had changed by 1330, and over the next few years Richard was gradually able to reacquire the Earldom of Arundel as well as the great estates his father had held in Sussex and in the Welsh Marches.
1 CONT
1 CONT Beyond this, in 1334 he was made Justiciar of North Wales (later his term in this office was made for life), Sheriff for life of Caernarvonshire, and Governor of Caernarfon Castle. He was one of the most trusted supporters of Edwa
1 CONC rd the Black Prince in Wales.
1 CONT
1 CONT Military Service in Scotland
1 CONT
1 CONT Despite his high offices in Wales, in the following decades Arundel spent much of his time fighting in Scotland (during the Second Wars of Scottish Independence) and France (during the Hundred Years' War). In 1337, Arundel was mad
1 CONC e Joint Commander of the English army in the north, and the next year he was made the sole Commander.
1 CONT
1 CONT Notable Victories
1 CONT
1 CONT In 1340 he fought at the Battle of Sluys, and then at the siege of Tournai. After a short term as Warden of the Scottish Marches, he returned to the continent, where he fought in a number of campaigns, and was appointed Joint Lieu
1 CONC tenant of Aquitaine in 1340.
1 CONT
1 CONT Arundel was one of the three principal English commanders at the Battle of Crécy. He spent much of the following years on various military campaigns and diplomatic missions.
1 CONT
1 CONT Great Wealth
1 CONT
1 CONT In 1347 he succeeded to the Earldom of Surrey (or Warenne), which even further increased his great wealth. (He did not however use the additional title until after the death of the Dowager Countess of Surrey in 1361.) He made very
1 CONC  large loans to King Edward III but even so on his death left behind a great sum in hard cash.
1 CONT
1 CONT Marriages and Children
1 CONT
1 CONT Arundel married twice. His first wife (as mentioned above), was Isabel le Despenser. He repudiated her, and had the marriage annulled on the grounds that he had never freely consented to it. Pope Clement VI granted the annulment. 
1 CONC  They had one child:
1 CONT By his first marriage he had one son, Edmund Arundel, who was bastardized by the annulment. This son married Sybil, a daughter of William Montacute, 1st Earl of Salisbury.
1 CONT
1 CONT After the annulment to Isabel le Despenser, he married Eleanor of Lancaster, daughter of Henry, 3rd Earl of Lancaster and Maud Chaworth. By the Eleanor he had 3 sons and 4 surviving daughters:
1 CONT Richard, who succeeded him as 6th Earl of Arundel (10th Earl of Arundel per Ancestral Roots)
1 CONT John Fitzalan,1st Baron Maltravers, who was a Marshall of England, and drowned in 1379
1 CONT Thomas Arundel, who became Archbishop of Canterbury. Joan Fitzalan (1348- 7 April 1419) who married Humphrey de Bohun, 7th Earl of Hereford
1 CONT Alice (1352- 17 March 1416), who married Thomas Holland, 2nd Earl of Kent
1 CONT Mary Fitzalan (died 29 August 1396), who married John Le Strange, 4th Lord Strange of Blackmere
1 CONT Eleanor Fitzalan (1356- before 1366).
1 CONT
1 CONT Death
1 CONT
1 CONT He died 24 January 1376 in Sussex, England.
1 CONT
1 CONT <http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Richard_FitzAlan,_10th_Earl_of_Arundel>
1 CONT
1 CONT Other Source
1 CONT
1 CONT Richard FitzAlan, 10th Earl of Arundel was born circa 1313. He was the son of Sir Edmund Fitzalan, 9th Earl of Arundel and Alice de Warenne. He married, firstly, Isabel le Despenser, daughter of Hugh le Despenser, 3rd Lord le Desp
1 CONC enser and Eleanor de Clare, on 9 February 1320/21. He married, secondly, Lady Eleanor Plantagenet, daughter of Henry Plantagenet, 3rd Earl of Lancaster and Matilda de Chaworth, on 5 February 1345 at Ditton Church, Stoke Puges, Buc
1 CONC kinghamshire, England. He died on 24 January 1376 at Arundel Castle, Arundel, Sussex, England.6 He was buried at Lewes Priory, Lewes, Sussex, England.
1 CONT
1 CONT Richard FitzAlan, 10th Earl of Arundel also went by the nick-name of 'Copped Hat'. He held the office of Justiciar of North Wales in 1334. He held the office of Governor of Carnarvon Castle in 1339.3 He held the office of Admiral 
1 CONC of the West from 1340 to 1341. His marriage to Isabel le Despenser was annulled on 4 December 1344 by Papal mandate, supposedly on the grounds that they were married during their minority, and without their consent. The reality is
1 CONC  that the Earl probably wished to be rid of his wife, who had no value to him after her father's attainder and exectution. He held the office of Sheriff of Shropshire in 1345. He held the office of Admiral of the West between 1345
1 CONC  and 1347. He fought in the Battle of Crécy on 26 August 1346, where he commanded the 2nd division. He fought in the fall of Calais in 1347. He succeeded to the title of 10th Earl of Arundel [E., c. 1138] in 1347. On 30 June 1347 
1 CONC he succeeded to the vast estates of the family of Warenne.He succeeded to the title of Earl of Surrey on 12 April 1361.
1 CONT
1 CONT Child of Richard FitzAlan, 10th Earl of Arundel and Isabel le Despenser
1 CONT Sir Edmund FitzAlan+ b. c 1327, d. a 1377
1 CONT
1 CONT Children of Richard FitzAlan, 10th Earl of Arundel and Lady Eleanor Plantagenet
1 CONT Mary FitzAlan b. bt 1345 - 1372
1 CONT Edmund FitzAlan b. c 1346, d. c 1366
1 CONT Sir Richard FitzAlan, 11th Earl of Arundel+ b. 1346, d. 21 Sep 1397
1 CONT Joan FitzAlan+ b. 1347, d. 7 Apr 1419
1 CONT Alice FitzAlan+ b. c 1350, d. 17 Mar 1416
1 CONT John d'Arundel, 1st Lord Arundel+ b. c 1351, d. 16 Dec 1379
1 CONT Thomas FitzAlan b. c 1353, d. 19 Feb 1413/14
1 CONT Eleanor FitzAlan b. c 1355, d. b 1366
1 CONT
1 CONT <http://thepeerage.com/p10490.htm#i104900>
0 @N6000000001783373326@ NOTE Basic Life Information
1 CONT
1 CONT Richard FitzAlan, "Copped Hat", 10th Earl of Arundel (9th Earl of Arundel per Ancestral Roots) (c. 1306 - 24 January 1376) was an English nobleman and medieval military leader.
1 CONT
1 CONT Lineage
1 CONT
1 CONT FitzAlan was the eldest son of Edmund FitzAlan, 9th Earl of Arundel (8th Earl of Arundel per Ancestral Roots), and Alice de Warenne. His maternal grandparents were William de Warenne, 8th Earl of Surrey and Joan de Vere. William w
1 CONC as the only son of John de Warenne, 7th Earl of Surrey.
1 CONT
1 CONT He was born 1306 in Sussex, England.
1 CONT
1 CONT Alliance with the Despensers
1 CONT
1 CONT Around 1321, FitzAlan's father allied with King Edward II's favorites, Hugh le Despenser, 1st Earl of Winchester and his namesake son, and Richard was married to Isabel le Despenser, daughter of Hugh the Younger. Fortune turned ag
1 CONC ainst the Despenser party, and on 17 November 1326, FitzAlan's father was executed, and he did not succeed to his father's estates or titles.
1 CONT
1 CONT Gradual Restoration
1 CONT
1 CONT However, political conditions had changed by 1330, and over the next few years Richard was gradually able to reacquire the Earldom of Arundel as well as the great estates his father had held in Sussex and in the Welsh Marches.
1 CONT
1 CONT Beyond this, in 1334 he was made Justiciar of North Wales (later his term in this office was made for life), Sheriff for life of Caernarvonshire, and Governor of Caernarfon Castle. He was one of the most trusted supporters of Edwa
1 CONC rd the Black Prince in Wales.
1 CONT
1 CONT Military Service in Scotland
1 CONT
1 CONT Despite his high offices in Wales, in the following decades Arundel spent much of his time fighting in Scotland (during the Second Wars of Scottish Independence) and France (during the Hundred Years' War). In 1337, Arundel was mad
1 CONC e Joint Commander of the English army in the north, and the next year he was made the sole Commander.
1 CONT
1 CONT Notable Victories
1 CONT
1 CONT In 1340 he fought at the Battle of Sluys, and then at the siege of Tournai. After a short term as Warden of the Scottish Marches, he returned to the continent, where he fought in a number of campaigns, and was appointed Joint Lieu
1 CONC tenant of Aquitaine in 1340.
1 CONT
1 CONT Arundel was one of the three principal English commanders at the Battle of Crécy. He spent much of the following years on various military campaigns and diplomatic missions.
1 CONT
1 CONT Great Wealth
1 CONT
1 CONT In 1347 he succeeded to the Earldom of Surrey (or Warenne), which even further increased his great wealth. (He did not however use the additional title until after the death of the Dowager Countess of Surrey in 1361.) He made very
1 CONC  large loans to King Edward III but even so on his death left behind a great sum in hard cash.
1 CONT
1 CONT Marriages and Children
1 CONT
1 CONT Arundel married twice. His first wife (as mentioned above), was Isabel le Despenser. He repudiated her, and had the marriage annulled on the grounds that he had never freely consented to it. Pope Clement VI granted the annulment. 
1 CONC  They had one child:
1 CONT By his first marriage he had one son, Edmund Arundel, who was bastardized by the annulment. This son married Sybil, a daughter of William Montacute, 1st Earl of Salisbury.
1 CONT
1 CONT After the annulment to Isabel le Despenser, he married Eleanor of Lancaster, daughter of Henry, 3rd Earl of Lancaster and Maud Chaworth. By the Eleanor he had 3 sons and 4 surviving daughters:
1 CONT Richard, who succeeded him as 6th Earl of Arundel (10th Earl of Arundel per Ancestral Roots)
1 CONT John Fitzalan,1st Baron Maltravers, who was a Marshall of England, and drowned in 1379
1 CONT Thomas Arundel, who became Archbishop of Canterbury. Joan Fitzalan (1348- 7 April 1419) who married Humphrey de Bohun, 7th Earl of Hereford
1 CONT Alice (1352- 17 March 1416), who married Thomas Holland, 2nd Earl of Kent
1 CONT Mary Fitzalan (died 29 August 1396), who married John Le Strange, 4th Lord Strange of Blackmere
1 CONT Eleanor Fitzalan (1356- before 1366).
1 CONT
1 CONT Death
1 CONT
1 CONT He died 24 January 1376 in Sussex, England.
1 CONT
1 CONT <http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Richard_FitzAlan,_10th_Earl_of_Arundel>
1 CONT
1 CONT Other Source
1 CONT
1 CONT Richard FitzAlan, 10th Earl of Arundel was born circa 1313. He was the son of Sir Edmund Fitzalan, 9th Earl of Arundel and Alice de Warenne. He married, firstly, Isabel le Despenser, daughter of Hugh le Despenser, 3rd Lord le Desp
1 CONC enser and Eleanor de Clare, on 9 February 1320/21. He married, secondly, Lady Eleanor Plantagenet, daughter of Henry Plantagenet, 3rd Earl of Lancaster and Matilda de Chaworth, on 5 February 1345 at Ditton Church, Stoke Puges, Buc
1 CONC kinghamshire, England. He died on 24 January 1376 at Arundel Castle, Arundel, Sussex, England.6 He was buried at Lewes Priory, Lewes, Sussex, England.
1 CONT
1 CONT Richard FitzAlan, 10th Earl of Arundel also went by the nick-name of 'Copped Hat'. He held the office of Justiciar of North Wales in 1334. He held the office of Governor of Carnarvon Castle in 1339.3 He held the office of Admiral 
1 CONC of the West from 1340 to 1341. His marriage to Isabel le Despenser was annulled on 4 December 1344 by Papal mandate, supposedly on the grounds that they were married during their minority, and without their consent. The reality is
1 CONC  that the Earl probably wished to be rid of his wife, who had no value to him after her father's attainder and exectution. He held the office of Sheriff of Shropshire in 1345. He held the office of Admiral of the West between 1345
1 CONC  and 1347. He fought in the Battle of Crécy on 26 August 1346, where he commanded the 2nd division. He fought in the fall of Calais in 1347. He succeeded to the title of 10th Earl of Arundel [E., c. 1138] in 1347. On 30 June 1347 
1 CONC he succeeded to the vast estates of the family of Warenne.He succeeded to the title of Earl of Surrey on 12 April 1361.
1 CONT
1 CONT Child of Richard FitzAlan, 10th Earl of Arundel and Isabel le Despenser
1 CONT Sir Edmund FitzAlan+ b. c 1327, d. a 1377
1 CONT
1 CONT Children of Richard FitzAlan, 10th Earl of Arundel and Lady Eleanor Plantagenet
1 CONT Mary FitzAlan b. bt 1345 - 1372
1 CONT Edmund FitzAlan b. c 1346, d. c 1366
1 CONT Sir Richard FitzAlan, 11th Earl of Arundel+ b. 1346, d. 21 Sep 1397
1 CONT Joan FitzAlan+ b. 1347, d. 7 Apr 1419
1 CONT Alice FitzAlan+ b. c 1350, d. 17 Mar 1416
1 CONT John d'Arundel, 1st Lord Arundel+ b. c 1351, d. 16 Dec 1379
1 CONT Thomas FitzAlan b. c 1353, d. 19 Feb 1413/14
1 CONT Eleanor FitzAlan b. c 1355, d. b 1366
1 CONT
