0 @N6000000006296745695@ NOTE Richard FitzAlan, 11th Earl of Arundel and 10th Earl of Surrey (1346 - 21 September </wiki/September_21> 1397 </wiki/1397>) was an English medieval nobleman and military commander.
1 CONT He was the son of Richard FitzAlan, 10th Earl of Arundel </wiki/Richard_FitzAlan,_10th_Earl_of_Arundel> and Eleanor of Lancaster </wiki/Eleanor_of_Lancaster> and was born in 1346.[1] He succeeded his father on 24 January 1376.
1 CONT His brother was Thomas Arundel </wiki/Thomas_Arundel> who was Bishop of Ely </wiki/Bishop_of_Ely> from 1374 to 1388, then Archbishop of York </wiki/Archbishop_of_York> from 1388 to 1397, then Archbishop of Canterbury </wiki/Archbi
1 CONC shop_of_Canterbury>.[2]
1 CONT At the coronation </wiki/Coronation> of Richard II </wiki/Richard_II_of_England>, Richard FitzAlan carried the crown </wiki/Coronation_crown>.[1]
1 CONT In 1377 he was Admiral </wiki/Admiral> of the West and South.[1] In this capacity, he attacked Harfleur </wiki/Harfleur> at Whitsun </wiki/Whitsun> 1378, but was forced to return to his ships by the defenders. Later, he and John o
1 CONC f Gaunt </wiki/John_of_Gaunt> attempted to seize Saint-Malo </wiki/Saint-Malo> but were unsuccessful.[3]
1 CONT FitzAlan was closely aligned with Thomas, Duke of Gloucester </wiki/Thomas_of_Woodstock,_1st_Duke_of_Gloucester> who was an uncle of King Richard II </wiki/King_Richard_II>. Thomas was opposed to Richard II's desire for peace with
1 CONC  France </wiki/France> in the Hundred Years War </wiki/Hundred_Years_War> and a power struggle between Gloucester and Richard II ensued. In late 1386, Gloucester forced Richard II to name both himself and Richard FitzAlan to Richa
1 CONC rd's Council.[4] This Council was to all intents and purposes a Regency Council for Richard II, however Richard limited the duration of the Council's powers to be one year.[5]
1 CONT In 1386 Richard II named him Admiral </wiki/Admiral> of all England, as well as being knighted a Knight of the Garter </wiki/Knight_of_the_Garter>.[1] As Admiral of All England, he defeated a Franco-Spanish-Flemish fleet off Marga
1 CONC te </wiki/Margate> in March 1387, along with Thomas de Mowbray </wiki/Thomas_de_Mowbray,_1st_Duke_of_Norfolk>, Earl of Nottingham </wiki/Earl_of_Nottingham>.[5].
1 CONT In August, the King dismissed Gloucester and FitzAlan from the Council and replaced them with his own favourites - including the Archbishop of York </wiki/Archbishop_of_York>, Alexander Neville </wiki/Alexander_Neville>, the Duke 
1 CONC of Ireland </wiki/Duke_of_Ireland> Robert de Vere </wiki/Robert_de_Vere,_Duke_of_Ireland>, Michael de la Pole </wiki/Michael_de_la_Pole,_1st_Earl_of_Suffolk> the Earl of Suffolk </wiki/Earl_of_Suffolk>, Sir Robert Tresilian </wiki
1 CONC /Robert_Tresilian> who was the Chief Justice, and the former Mayor of London </wiki/Mayor_of_London> Nicholas Brembre </wiki/Nicholas_Brembre>.[6].
1 CONT The King summoned Gloucester and FitzAlan to meet with the King, but instead of coming, they raised troops and defeated the new Council at Radcot Bridge </wiki/Battle_of_Radcot_Bridge>, taking the favorites prisoner. The Merciless
1 CONC  Parliament </wiki/Merciless_Parliament> the next year condemned the favorites. FitzAlan was one of the Lords Appellant </wiki/Lords_Appellant> who accused and condemned Richard II's favorites.[4]He made himself particularly odiou
1 CONC s to Richard by refusing, along with Gloucester to spare the life of Sir Simon Burley who had been condemned by the Merciless Parliament, even though the queen, Anne of Bohemia went down on her knees before them to beg for mercy. 
1 CONC Richard never forgave this humiliation and planned and waited for his moment of revenge. In 1394 he further antagonized the King by arriving late for the queen's funeral. Richard, in a rage snatched a wand and struck him in the fa
1 CONC ce and drew blood. Shortly after that, Richard feigned a reconciliation but he was only biding his time for the right moment to strike. Arundel was named Governor of Brest </wiki/Brest,_France> in 1388.[1]
1 CONT Peace was concluded with France in 1389, however Richard FitzAlan followed Gloucester's lead and stated that he would never agree with the peace that had been concluded.[4]
1 CONT Arundel married twice.
1 CONT His first wife was Elizabeth de Bohun </wiki/Elizabeth_de_Bohun>, daughter of William de Bohun, 1st Earl of Northampton </wiki/William_de_Bohun,_1st_Earl_of_Northampton> and Elizabeth de Badlesmere </wiki/Elizabeth_de_Badlesmere>.
1 CONC  They married around 28 September 1359 and had seven children:[7][1]
1 CONT Thomas FitzAlan, 12th Earl of Arundel </wiki/Thomas_FitzAlan,_12th_Earl_of_Arundel>[1]
1 CONT Elizabeth FitzAlan </wiki/Elizabeth_FitzAlan>[1][8]
1 CONT Joan FitzAlan </wiki/Joan_FitzAlan> (1375 - 14 November 1435), who married William Beauchamp, 1st Baron Bergavenny </wiki/William_Beauchamp,_1st_Baron_Bergavenny>;[1]
1 CONT Margaret FitzAlan </w/index.php?title=Margaret_FitzAlan&action=edit&redlink=1>, who married Sir Rowland Lenthall </w/index.php?title=Sir_Rowland_Lenthall&action=edit&redlink=1>;[1]
1 CONT Alice FitzAlan </w/index.php?title=Alice_FitzAlan&action=edit&redlink=1>, who married John Cherleton, 4th Baron Cherleton </w/index.php?title=John_Cherleton,_4th_Baron_Cherleton&action=edit&redlink=1>. (not mentioned as an heir of
1 CONC  Thomas in the Complete Peerage)
1 CONT Arundel then married Philippa Mortimer </w/index.php?title=Philippa_Mortimer&action=edit&redlink=1>, daughter of Edmund Mortimer, 3rd Earl of March </wiki/Edmund_Mortimer,_3rd_Earl_of_March>. Her mother was Philippa Plantagenet </
1 CONC wiki/Philippa_Plantagenet>, a daughter of Lionel of Antwerp </wiki/Lionel_of_Antwerp> and thus a granddaughter of Edward III </wiki/Edward_III_of_England>. They had no children.[1]
1 CONT On 12 July 1397 he was arrested for his opposition to Richard II,[1] as well as plotting with Gloucester to imprison the king.[9] He stood trial at Westminster </wiki/Westminster> and was attainted.[10] He was beheaded on 21 Septe
1 CONC mber 1397 and was buried in the church of the Augustin Friars, Bread Street, London </wiki/London>.[1]Tradition holds that his final words were said to the executioner, "Torment me not long, strike off my head in one blow".[11] In
1 CONC  October of 1400, the attainder </wiki/Attainder> was reversed, and Richard's son Thomas succeeded to his father's estates and honors.[1]
