0 @N6000000002322286729@ NOTE The youngest son of John II, he was one of the most powerful men in his day.
1 CONT He acted as regent for his nephew Charles VI, and when Charles went insane,
1 CONT Philip became virtual ruler of France. During the minority of Charles VI,
1 CONT Philip and his brothers shared the regency and government of France, as well
1 CONT as the spoils of power, never hesitating to further their own aims. Even after
1 CONT Charles had rejected his uncles in 1388 to rule for himself, he went insane in
1 CONT 1392 and Philip regained control.
0 @N6000000002187837988@ NOTE Philip II, Duke of Burgundy
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Philip II, Duke of Burgundy, known as the Bold (Philippe II de Bourgogne, le Hardi in French) (January 15, 1342, Pontoise – April 27, 1404, Halle), was the fourth son of King John II of France and his wife, Bonne of Luxembourg.
1 CONT
1 CONT He was created Duke of Touraine in 1360, but as a reward for his valor at the Battle of Poitiers, in 1363 this was exchanged for the Duchy of Burgundy which Philip ruled until his death. Between 1380 and 1388 he ruled France with 
1 CONC his brothers, Louis I of Anjou and John, Duke of Berry, as co-regents, during the minority of their nephew, Charles VI. He and his brother Berry again took up the regency when Charles shown signs of insanity in 1392, holding it un
1 CONC til 1402, when Charles put affairs in the hands of his brother, the Duke of Orleans. Orleans's misrule led to Philip once again being placed in power shortly before his death in 1404.
1 CONT
1 CONT In 1390, he obtained the County of Charolais, which would later become the title of the heirs of his duchy.
1 CONT
1 CONT He was the founder of the second and last ducal House of Burgundy. He married Margaret III, Countess of Flanders (1350–1404/5) on June 19, 1369, thereby uniting the rich dominions of Burgundy and Flanders and had the following iss
1 CONC ue:
1 CONT
1 CONT     * John the Fearless (1371–1419, murdered at Montereau), his eldest son and successor as Duke of Burgundy
1 CONT     * Charles (1372–1373)
1 CONT     * Marguerite of Burgundy (October 1374 – March 8, 1441, Le Quesnoy), Countess of Mortain married William VI, Count of Holland and Duke of Bavaria-Straubing
1 CONT     * Louis (1377–1378)
1 CONT     * Catherine of Burgundy (April 1378, Montbard – January 24, 1425, Grey-sur-Saone), married Leopold IV, Duke of Austria
1 CONT     * Bonne (1379–1399, Arras)
1 CONT     * Antoine, Duke of Brabant (1386–1415, at Agincourt)
1 CONT     * Mary of Burgundy (September 1386, Dijon – October 2, 1422, Thonon-les-Bains), married Amadeus VIII, Duke of Savoy
1 CONT     * Philip II, Count of Nevers and Rethel (1389–1415, at Agincourt)
1 CONT
