1 CONT
1 CONT Philippa Plantagenet, (16 August 1355 – 5 January 1382) was the Countess of Ulster suo jure.
1 CONT
1 CONT Philippa was born in Eltham Palace, Kent, England on 16 August 1355. She was the daughter and only child of Lionel of Antwerp, 1st Duke of Clarence and Elizabeth de Burgh, 4th Countess of Ulster. Her father was the second son of K
1 CONC ing Edward III of England by his Queen consort, Philippa of Hainault.
1 CONT
1 CONT Philippa married Edmund Mortimer, 3rd Earl of March (1351? – 27 December 1381) in about 1368, at Reading Abbey an alliance that would have far-reaching consequences in English history. As a result of her seniority in the line of s
1 CONC uccession to the throne of the Kingdom of England and her marriage into the powerful Mortimer family, her descendants eventually succeeded to the throne as the House of York under Edward IV.
1 CONT
1 CONT She died on 5 January 1382 in Cork, Ireland, and was buried in Wigmore, Herefordshire.
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Marriage and issue
1 CONT
1 CONT She had five children by her marriage to Edmund Mortimer:
1 CONT Name Birth Death Notes
1 CONT Lady Elizabeth de Mortimer 12 February 1370/1371 20 April 1417 She married firstly Henry Percy and secondly Thomas Camoys with whom she had a son, Lord Roger de Camoys, and a daughter, Alice, who was in turn, the mother of William
1 CONC  Hastings, Baron Hastings.
1 CONT Roger Mortimer, 4th Earl of March 11 April 1374 20 July 1398 The House of York's claim to the throne was through his daughter and heir Anne Mortimer.
1 CONT Lady Philippa de Mortimer 21 November 1375 24 September 1401 She married first John Hastings, 3rd Earl of Pembroke c. 1385, secondly Sir Thomas Poynings of Basing and thirdly Richard Fitzalan, 11th Earl of Arundel.
1 CONT Sir Edmund Mortimer 9 November 1376 1409? Married Catrin (Catherine) Glyndwr the daughter of Owain Glyndwr.
1 CONT Sir John de Mortimer c. 1378 1424 
