0 @N6000000002187746199@ NOTE Margaret Plantagenet, Countess of Pembroke
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Margaret Plantagenet (1346-1361) was born 20 July 1346 in Windsor to Edward III of England and his Queen, Philippa of Hainault. She was also known as Margaret of Windsor.
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Marriage
1 CONT
1 CONT Margaret's first marriage prospect was the eldest son of Albert of Austria but this was ended due to politics at the time.
1 CONT
1 CONT A few years later she was affianced to John of Blois son of Charles of Blois and rival of John V of Brittany to the Breton throne, however this was abandoned due to the fact her sister Mary was married to John V.
1 CONT
1 CONT Margaret was brought up with John Hastings, 2nd Earl of Pembroke, son of Laurence Hastings, 1st Earl of Pembroke and his wife Agnes the daughter of Roger Mortimer (the favourite of Isabella of France). As children they had a close
1 CONC  companionship.
1 CONT
1 CONT On May 13, 1359, she became the wife of John Hastings on the same day as her brother Lionel of Antwerp, 1st Duke of Clarence married Elizabeth de Burgh, 4th Countess of Ulster, in Reading.
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Early death
1 CONT
1 CONT Just two years later, Margaret died, in 1361, and was buried in Abingdon Abbey. Her exact date of death is unknown, though she was last mentioned as living on 1 October 1361. She was dead by Christmas of the same year. She and her
1 CONC  husband had no children, and he later remarried, dying in captivity at the age of 27.
0 @N6000000002187746189@ NOTE Mary Plantagenet
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Mary Plantagenet (October 10, 1344 – 1362) Born in Waltham, Hampshire, the daughter of Edward III of England and Philippa of Hainault. She is sometimes styled Mary of Waltham.
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] History
1 CONT
1 CONT Mary was betrothed in childhood to John V of Brittany, who grew up with her.
1 CONT
1 CONT In 1348 Mary met her mother and sister-in-law Joanna. Then in 1360 she went to stay with her sister-in-law Blanche of Lancaster.
1 CONT
1 CONT In 1361 she and John were married at Woodstock Palace[1] and acknowledged as Duke and Duchess of Brittany.
1 CONT
1 CONT Within thirty weeks of marriage Mary became lethargic and died, she was buried in Abingdon Abbey with her sister Margaret Plantagenet, Countess of Pembroke, who died weeks before her.
1 CONT
1 CONT Mary's statue can still be seen on south side of the tomb of Edward III of England in Westminster Abbey. She never did visit Brittany.[2]
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] References
1 CONT
1 CONT    1. ^ Reference from the Peerage
1 CONT    2. ^ Weir, Alison (2002). Britain's Royal Family: A Complete Genealogy. The Bodley Head London, U.K.. ISBN 0-7126-4286-2.  page 112 & 113
