1 CONT Thomas of Woodstock, 1st Duke of Gloucester (January 7, 1355 – September 8 (or 9), 1397) was the thirteenth and youngest child of King Edward III of England and Queen Philippa. He was the fifth of the five sons of Edward III who s
1 CONC urvived to adulthood.
1 CONT
1 CONT Contents [hide]
1 CONT 1 Early life 
1 CONT 2 Dispute with King Richard II 
1 CONT 3 Heirs 
1 CONT 4 References 
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Early life
1 CONT Thomas was born after two short-lived sons, one of whom had also been baptised Thomas. He was born at Woodstock Palace in Oxfordshire. He married Eleanor de Bohun in 1376, and inherited the title Earl of Essex from his father-in-l
1 CONC aw, Humphrey de Bohun, 7th Earl of Hereford. Woodstock's wife's younger sister, Mary de Bohun, was subsequently married to Henry "Bolingbroke," who eventually became Henry IV of England.
1 CONT
1 CONT At the age of 22, in 1377, Woodstock was created Earl of Buckingham. In 1385 he received the title Duke of Aumale, and at about the same time was created Duke of Gloucester.
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Dispute with King Richard II
1 CONT Thomas was the leader of the Lords Appellant, a group of powerful nobles whose ambition to wrest power from King Richard II of England (Thomas' nephew) culminated in a successful rebellion in 1388, which significantly weakened the
1 CONC  king's power. Richard II managed to dispose of the Lords Appellant in 1397, and Thomas was imprisoned in Calais to await trial for treason. He was, however, murdered the same year by Nicholas Colfox, presumably on behalf of Richa
1 CONC rd II, causing an outcry amongst the nobility of England which is considered by many to have added to Richard's unpopularity.
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Heirs
1 CONT Thomas and his wife had one son and four daughters. Following his murder his title was forfeit and did not pass to his son, Humphrey.
1 CONT
1 CONT His eldest daughter, Anne of Gloucester, married into the powerful Stafford family, who were Earls of Stafford and Dukes of Buckingham, and four generations after Thomas, the disposition of the de Bohun estates may have been a mot
1 CONC ivating factor in the involvement of Henry Stafford, 2nd Duke of Buckingham in plots against the crown during the period of Richard III. She later married into the Bourchier family (the Earls of Bath) and established a long Americ
1 CONC an line of descendants.
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] References
