0 @N6000000002188309114@ NOTE Michelle of Valois
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Michelle of Valois
1 CONT
1 CONT Michèle de Valois (January 11, 1395–July 8, 1422) was a daughter of Charles VI of France and Isabella of Bavaria-Ingolstadt. In June, 1409 she married Philip III, Duke of Burgundy. Michelle became melancholic in 1419 following the
1 CONC  involvement of her brother in the murder of her father-in-law, John the Fearless. Michelle died in Ghent in 1422 while her husband was away preparing for the battle of Cone. Michelle fell ill at Ghent and died 1422. All of the in
1 CONC habitants were in grieving as she was much loved by the people. Michelle was interred in the monastery of St Bavon near Ghent.
1 CONT
1 CONT After her death it was believed her death had been brought on by poisoning by a lady attendant from Germany Ourse de la Viefville. This lady had been a close confidante of the Princess, however had been dismissed to Aire just befo
1 CONC re Michelle's death. The lady was never charged.
0 @N6000000002188309125@ NOTE Louis, Dauphin of France (1397-1415)
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Louis, Dauphin of France and Duke of Guyenne (22 January 1397 – 18 December 1415) was a younger son of Charles VI of France and Isabella of Bavaria-Ingolstadt. He was the third child of his parents to hold the title Dauphin of Fra
1 CONC nce (technically Viennois), holding it from the death of his older brother in 1401, when he was likewise made Duke of Aquitaine (Guyenne). He was the Dauphin of Shakespeare's Henry V.
1 CONT
1 CONT In 1412, he married Margaret of Burgundy.
1 CONT
1 CONT
