0 @N6000000002188008385@ NOTE Thomas Holland, 1st Duke of Surrey
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Thomas Holland, 1st Duke of Surrey (1374 - January 7, 1400), also 3rd Earl of Kent.
1 CONT
1 CONT He was the son of Thomas Holland, 2nd Earl of Kent and Alice Fitzalan, daughter of Richard Fitzalan, 10th Earl of Arundel. He was also nephew of Richard II of England. On his father's death in 1397 Holland became 3rd Earl of Kent,
1 CONC  and was elected a Knight of the Garter. At that time Kent's uncle Richard II was removing from power Thomas of Woodstock, Duke of Gloucester, and his associates, and sent Kent to arrest his own uncle — Richard Fitzalan, 11th Earl
1 CONC  of Arundel. In reward he received a share of the forfeited estates, and on 29 September 1397 was created Duke of Surrey. Yet another uncle John Holland was created Duke of Exeter on that day as well.
1 CONT
1 CONT Holland, along with many of Richard's advisors, was arrested after Richard's deposition by Henry IV in 1399. In the end he had to forfeit the honors and estates he had gained after the arrests of Gloucester and Arundel, and thus w
1 CONC ent back to just being Earl of Kent.
1 CONT
1 CONT Early in 1400 Holland, along with his uncle John (now Earl of Huntingdon instead of Duke of Exeter) plotted to kill Henry IV and free Richard II from prison and return him to the throne. This "Epiphany Rising" failed and Holland w
1 CONC as captured and executed.
1 CONT
1 CONT He left no children by his wife, Joan Stafford, daughter of Hugh Stafford, 2nd Earl of Stafford. He was succeeded as earl by his brother Edmund.
1 CONT
1 CONT Preceded by:
1 CONT Thomas Mowbray, 1st Duke of Norfolk Earl Marshal
1 CONT 1398–1399 Succeeded by:
1 CONT Ralph Neville, 1st Earl of Westmorland 
1 CONT Preceded by:
1 CONT New Creation Duke of Surrey
1 CONT 1397–1399 Succeeded by:
1 CONT Forfeit 
1 CONT Preceded by:
1 CONT Thomas Holland Earl of Kent Succeeded by:
1 CONT Edmund Holland 
