0 @N6000000006296745243@ NOTE Lady Eleanor Holland, Countess of Salisbury (1386- after 1413), was an English noblewoman, the daughter of Thomas Holland, 2nd Earl of Kent </wiki/Thomas_Holland,_2nd_Earl_of_Kent>, a half-brother of King Richard II of England </w
1 CONC iki/Richard_II_of_England>. She was the first wife of Thomas Montacute, 4th Earl of Salisbury </wiki/Thomas_Montacute,_4th_Earl_of_Salisbury>. One of her brothers was Edmund Holland, 4th Earl of Kent </wiki/Edmund_Holland,_4th_Ear
1 CONC l_of_Kent>, to whom she was co-heiress. She is not to be confused with her eldest sister Alianore Holland </wiki/Alianore_Holland>, Countess of March </wiki/Earls_of_March> who bore the same name.
1 CONT Lady Eleanor Holland was born in 1386 in Upholland </wiki/Upholland>, Lancashire </wiki/Lancashire>, England, one of the ten children of Thomas Holland, 2nd Earl of Kent and Lady Alice Fitzalan </wiki/Alice_Fitzalan>, sister of Ri
1 CONC chard Fitzalan, 11th Earl of Arundel </wiki/Richard_Fitzalan,_11th_Earl_of_Arundel>. Eleanor's eldest sister, Lady Alianore Holland who married Roger Mortimer, 4th Earl of March </wiki/Roger_Mortimer,_4th_Earl_of_March> shared the
1 CONC  same name. They were named after their maternal grandmother. Eleanor's father was a uterine half-brother of King Richard II. Her eldest brother Thomas Holland, 1st Duke of Surrey </wiki/Thomas_Holland,_1st_Duke_of_Surrey> was beh
1 CONC eaded in 1400 by a mob of angry citizens at Cirencester </wiki/Cirencester> for his role in the Epiphany Rising </wiki/Epiphany_Rising>, which was aimed against the life of King Henry IV of England </wiki/Henry_IV_of_England>, who
1 CONC  had usurped the throne of King Richard. Thomas's heir to the earldom of Kent was her second eldest brother Edmund Holland </wiki/Edmund_Holland,_4th_Earl_of_Kent>, to whom Eleanor became co-heiress.
1 CONT Her paternal grandparents were Thomas Holland, 1st Earl of Kent </wiki/Thomas_Holland,_1st_Earl_of_Kent> and Joan of Kent </wiki/Joan_of_Kent>, who was the mother of King Richard by her second marriage to Edward, the Black Prince 
1 CONC </wiki/Edward,_the_Black_Prince>. Her maternal grandparents were Richard Fitzalan, 10th Earl of Arundel </wiki/Richard_Fitzalan,_10th_Earl_of_Arundel> and Eleanor of Lancaster </wiki/Eleanor_of_Lancaster>.
1 CONT On 23 May 1399, at the age of about thirteen years, Lady Eleanor married as his first wife, Thomas Montacute, 4th Earl of Salisbury </wiki/Thomas_Montacute,_4th_Earl_of_Salisbury>, son of John Montacute, 3rd Earl of Salisbury </wi
1 CONC ki/John_Montacute,_3rd_Earl_of_Salisbury> and Maud Francis. Thomas would later become one of the most important commanders in the Hundred Years War </wiki/Hundred_Years_War>. Eleanor did not assume the title of Countess of Salisbu
1 CONC ry until 14 June 1409, when the title was nominally restored to Thomas.[1] An attainder had been placed on his father's title and estates following his execution for his participation in the Epiphany Rising in 1400 alongside Elean
1 CONC or's brother, Thomas. Eleanor's uncle John Holland, 1st Duke of Exeter </wiki/John_Holland,_1st_Duke_of_Exeter> had also been part of the conspiracy but he had escaped the mob only to be captured in Essex </wiki/Essex> and decapit
1 CONC ated on the orders of her maternal aunt Joan Fitzalan </wiki/Joan_Fitzalan>, mother-in-law of King Henry IV.
1 CONT Thomas and Eleanor made their home at Bisham Manor </wiki/Bisham_Abbey> in Berkshire </wiki/Berkshire>. Together they had one daughter:
1 CONT Alice Montacute, 5th Countess of Salisbury </wiki/Alice_Montacute,_5th_Countess_of_Salisbury> (1407- 3 April/9 December 1462), married in 1420, Richard Neville, 5th Earl of Salisbury </wiki/Richard_Neville,_5th_Earl_of_Salisbury>,
1 CONC  by whom she had ten children.
1 CONT Eleanor died on an unkbown date sometime after 1413. She was buried in Bisham Priory </wiki/Bisham_Abbey>.[2]
1 CONT Thomas married secondly before 1424, Alice Chaucer </w/index.php?title=Alice_Chaucer&action=edit&redlink=1>, daughter of the noted author Geoffrey Chaucer </wiki/Geoffrey_Chaucer>, but their marriage was childless. He was mortally
1 CONC  wounded on 27 October 1428 at the Siege of Orléans </wiki/Siege_of_Orl%C3%A9ans> and died several days later on 3 November. Alice, the daughter of Thomas and Eleanor, succeeded him as 5th Countess of Salisbury suo jure </wiki/Suo
1 CONC _jure>. Through Alice, Eleanor was the great-grandmother of Cecily Bonville </wiki/Cecily_Bonville,_Baroness_Harington_and_Bonville>, one of the wealthiest English heiresses in the 15th century.
