0 @N6000000002188520694@ NOTE Azzo VI of Este
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT • Learn more about citing Wikipedia •
1 CONT
1 CONT Azzo VI (Italian: Arco; 1170 – November 1212), known as Azzolino, was the Marquis of Este (marchio Eystensis) from the death of his father, Azzo V, in 1190 until his death. He was heavily involved in the Guelph politics of Lombard
1 CONC y in the first decade of the 13th century, serving as podestà of Ferrara (1196, 1205, and 1208), Padua (1199), Verona (1206–1207), and Mantua (1207–1208 and 1210–1211). On 20 January 1210 he was created Marquis of Ancona and Count
1 CONC  of Loreto by the Emperor Otto IV. On 10 May 1212 this was reaffirmed by Pope Innocent III.
1 CONT
1 CONT In his capacity as a leading Guelph condottiero. He fought a prolonged war with Salinguerra Torelli. In 1205 he conquered and razed the castle of Frotta, residence of Salinguerra. His opponent responded by allying with Ezzelino II
1 CONC  da Romano and drove Azzo away, but the next year (1206) he had reconquered Frotta and held it until 1209.
1 CONT
1 CONT Azzo's court was a cultural centre in northern Italy, drawing poets and artists from afar. He played host and patron to the troubadours Aimeric de Peguilhan, Peire Raimon de Tolosa, and Rambertino Buvalelli. Rambertino celebrated 
1 CONC Azzo's daughter Beatrice in all of his love songs, an overtly political act in the climate of the times. Azzo's own character is captured in a contemporary vernacular vita of his daughter:
1 CONT
1 CONT     . . .fue bello de forme quasi più de tuti l’altri homini. Hommo prudente et ardito ualente in facti d’arme et de inzegno perspicace: sapiente et mirabile parladore.
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT . . .he was beautiful of form, almost more than all other men. A prudent yet daring man, valiant in feats of arms and of perspicacious learning: a wise and marvellous conversationalist.
1 CONT
1 CONT Azzo's first wife, a daughter of a count Aldobrandino, was dead by 1192, by which time he had remarried to a daughter of Humbert III of Savoy, named either Sophia or Eleanor (as per the epitaph of her daughter Beatrice). She died 
1 CONC on 3 December 1202. On 22 February 1204 Azzo remarried for the third time to Alice, daughter of Reynald de Châtillon, Prince of Antioch. While Alberic de Trois-Fontaines calls Aleydem marchionis Eystensis in Italia as the third of
1 CONC  the three daughters of Raynaldus de Castellione uxor ... relictam principis Raymundi, that is, by Reynald's first wife, the daughter of Raymond of Antioch, it is unlikely that Alice could have been born before 1167, the latest po
1 CONC ssible date for Reynald's first wife's death. She was more likely the daughter of Reynald's last wife, Stephanie de Milly.
1 CONT
1 CONT By his first wife, Azzo had a son, Aldobrandino, who succeeded his father in 1212. By his second wife he his only daughter, the aforementioned Beatrice. By his final wife he left a son, Azzo VII, who eventually succeeded his elder
1 CONC  brother and became head of the family.
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Sources
1 CONT
1 CONT     * Bertoni, Giulio (1915). I Trovatori d'Italia: Biografie, testi, tradizioni, note. Rome: Società Multigrafica Editrice Somu. 
1 CONT     * Cabré, Miriam (1999). "Italian and Catalan troubadours", in Simon B. Gaunt and Sarah Kay: The Troubadours: an Introduction. Cambridge: Cambridge University Press. ISBN 0521574730. 
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] External links
1 CONT
1 CONT     * Foundation for Medieval Genealogy: Medieval Lands Project — Modena/Ferrara, D. Marchesi d'Este
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT Preceded by
1 CONT Folco I Marquis of Este
1 CONT 1209–1212 Succeeded by
1 CONT Azzo VII
1 CONT Lord of Ferrara
1 CONT 1209–1212
