0 @N6000000002188017632@ NOTE William V, Marquess of Montferrat
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT William V of Montferrat (occ./piem. Guilhem, it. Guglielmo) (c. 1115-1191), also known as William the Old to distinguish him from his eldest son, William Longsword, was marquess of Montferrat from c. 1136 to his death in 1191. Wil
1 CONC liam was the only son of marquess Renier I and his wife Gisela, a daughter of William I, Count of Burgundy and widow of Count Humbert II of Savoy. It seems likely, given that he was still fit enough to participate in battle in 118
1 CONC 7, that William was one of his parents' youngest children. He was described by Acerbo Morena as of medium height and compact build, with a round, somewhat ruddy face and hair so fair as to be almost white. He was eloquent, intelli
1 CONC gent and good-humoured, generous but not extravagant. Dynastically, he was extremely well-connected: a nephew of Pope Callixtus II, a half-brother of Amadeus III of Savoy, a brother-in-law of Louis VI of France (through his half-s
1 CONC ister Adelasia of Moriana), and cousin of Alfonso VII of Castile.
1 CONT
1 CONT Contents [hide]
1 CONT 1 Marriage and children 
1 CONT 2 Alliances with the Western and Eastern Empires 
1 CONT 3 William in Outremer 
1 CONT 4 Sources 
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit]
1 CONT Marriage and children
1 CONT William married Judith or Ita von Babenberg, daughter of Leopold III of Austria and Agnes of Germany, sometime before March 28, 1133. Judith was probably about 15 at the time. None of their surviving children seem to have been bor
1 CONC n before 1140 (there may have been older ones who died in infancy), and the youngest son was born in 1162. She died after 1168. They had five sons, four of whom became prominent in the affairs of the Kingdom of Jerusalem and of By
1 CONC zantium:
1 CONT
1 CONT William Longsword, Count of Jaffa and Ascalon, father of Baldwin V of Jerusalem 
1 CONT Conrad, King of Jerusalem 
1 CONT Boniface, his successor to Montferrat and founder of the Kingdom of Thessalonica 
1 CONT Frederick, who entered the Church, and may have been Bishop of Alba (although Usseglio notes there are difficulties in identifying him firmly) 
1 CONT Renier, married into the Byzantine imperial family 
1 CONT and three daughters:
1 CONT
1 CONT Agnes, who married Count Guido Guerra of Ventimiglia. The marriage was annulled on grounds of childlessness before 1180, when Guido remarried, and Agnes entered the convent of Santa Maria di Rocca delle Donne. 
1 CONT Adelasia or Azalaïs (d. 1232), who married Manfredo II, marquess of Saluzzo, c. 1182, and was regent for her grandson Manfredo III. 
1 CONT An unidentified daughter, who married Albert, marquess of Malaspina. 
1 CONT William and Judith's powerful dynastic connections created difficulties in finding suitable wives for his sons, however: too many potential spouses were related within prohibited degrees. In 1167, he unsuccessfully tried to negoti
1 CONC ate marriages for his eldest sons to daughters of Henry II of England - but the girls were very young at the time and were related through Judith's descent from William V of Aquitaine. He then applied for sisters of William I of S
1 CONC cotland, who were not related, but were already married.
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit]
1 CONT Alliances with the Western and Eastern Empires
1 CONT William took part in the Second Crusade, alongside his half-brother Amadeus of Savoy (who died during the campaign), his nephew Louis VII of France, his brother-in-law Count Guido of Biandrate, and his wife's German and Austrian r
1 CONC elatives.
1 CONT
1 CONT As supporters of the imperial party (later known as the Ghibellines), he and his sons fought with the Emperor Frederick Barbarossa (Judith's nephew) in his lengthy struggle against the Lombard League. Following Barbarossa's capitu
1 CONC lation with the Peace of Venice in 1177, William was left to deal with the rebellious towns in the area alone. Meanwhile, the Byzantine emperor Manuel I Komnenos sought support for his own politics in Italy.
1 CONT
1 CONT William broke with Barbarossa and formed an alliance with Manuel. His eldest surviving son, Conrad, was taken prisoner by Barbarossa's Chancellor, Archbishop Christian of Mainz, but then captured the chancellor in battle at Cameri
1 CONC no. In 1179 Manuel suggested a marriage between his daughter Maria, second in line to the throne, and one of William's sons. As Conrad and Boniface were already married, the youngest son, Renier, was married off to the princess, w
1 CONC ho was ten years his senior. Renier and Maria were later killed during the usurpation of Andronikos, and the family rebuilt ties with Barbarossa.
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit]
1 CONT William in Outremer
1 CONT In 1183, with the accession of his grandson Baldwin V, a minor, as co-King of Jerusalem, William, then probably in his late sixties, left the government of Montferrat to Conrad and Boniface, and returned to the east. He was grante
1 CONC d the castle of St. Elias (present-day El Taiyiba). He fought in the Battle of Hattin in 1187, where he was captured by Saladin's forces. In the meantime, his second son, Conrad, had arrived at Tyre from Constantinople. Conrad was
1 CONC  given the command of the defences, and is said to have refused to surrender as much as a stone of its walls to liberate his father, even threatening to shoot him with a crossbow himself when Saladin had him presented as a hostage
1 CONC . Eventually, Saladin withdrew his army from Tyre. In 1188, William was released unharmed at Tortosa, and seems to have ended his days in Tyre, with his son. He probably died in the summer of 1191: Conrad last describes himself as
1 CONC  "marchionis Montisferrati filius" in a charter of May that year.
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit]
1 CONT Sources
1 CONT Haberstumpf, Walter. Dinastie europee nel Mediterraneo orientale. I Monferrato e i Savoia nei secoli XII–XV, 1995 (external link to downloadable text). 
1 CONT Hamilton, Bernard. The Leper King and His Heirs: Baldwin IV and the Crusader Kingdom of Jerusalem, 2000. 
1 CONT Settia, Aldo A. "Guglielmo V di Monferrato, detto il Vecchio", Dizionario Biografico degli Italiani, vol. LX, Roma 2003 (external link) 
1 CONT Usseglio, Leopoldo. I Marchesi di Monferrato in Italia ed in Oriente durante i secoli XII e XIII, 1926. 
1 CONT Preceded by:
1 CONT Renier I Marquess of Montferrat
1 CONT ca. 1136-1191 Succeeded by:
1 CONT Conrad 
0 @N6000000004303404380@ NOTE WIKIPEDIA:
1 CONT
1 CONT William V, Marquess of Montferrat
1 CONT
1 CONT William V of Montferrat (occ./piem. Guilhem, it. Guglielmo) (c. 1115 – 1191), also known as William the Old to distinguish him from his eldest son, William Longsword, was marquess of Montferrat from c. 1136 to his death in 1191. W
1 CONC illiam was the only son of marquess Renier I and his wife Gisela, a daughter of William I, Count of Burgundy and widow of Count Humbert II of Savoy. It seems likely, given that he was still fit enough to participate in battle in 1
1 CONC 187, that William was one of his parents' youngest children. He was described by Acerbo Morena as of medium height and compact build, with a round, somewhat ruddy face and hair so fair as to be almost white. He was eloquent, intel
1 CONC ligent and good-humoured, generous but not extravagant. Dynastically, he was extremely well-connected: a nephew of Pope Callixtus II, a half-brother of Amadeus III of Savoy, a brother-in-law of Louis VI of France (through his half
1 CONC -sister Adelasia of Moriana), and cousin of Alfonso VII of Castile.
1 CONT
1 CONT Marriage and children
1 CONT William married Judith or Ita von Babenberg, daughter of Leopold III of Austria and Agnes of Germany, sometime before March 28, 1133. Judith was probably about 15 at the time. None of their surviving children seem to have been bor
1 CONC n before 1140 (there may have been older ones who died in infancy), and the youngest son was born in 1162. She died after 1168. They had five sons, four of whom became prominent in the affairs of the Kingdom of Jerusalem and of By
1 CONC zantium:
1 CONT •	William Longsword, Count of Jaffa and Ascalon, father of Baldwin V of Jerusalem
1 CONT •	Conrad, King of Jerusalem
1 CONT •	Boniface, his successor to Montferrat and founder of the Kingdom of Thessalonica
1 CONT •	Frederick, who entered the Church and became Bishop of Alba (dates uncertain).
1 CONT •	Renier, married into the Byzantine imperial family
1 CONT and three daughters:
1 CONT •	Agnes, who married Count Guido Guerra III Guidi of Ventimiglia. The marriage was annulled on grounds of childlessness before 1180, when Guido remarried, and Agnes entered the convent of Santa Maria di Rocca delle Donne.
1 CONT •	Adelasia or Azalaïs (d. 1232), who married Manfred II, marquess of Saluzzo, c. 1182, and was regent for her grandson Manfred III.
1 CONT •	An undentified daughter, who married Albert, marquess of Malaspina.
1 CONT The vida of the troubadour Raimbaut de Vaqueiras claims that there was another daughter, Beatrice, who m. Henry I del Carretto, marquess of Savona, and that she is the Bel Cavalher (Fair Knight) of Vaqueiras's songs. However, the 
1 CONC lyrics of Vaqueiras's songs (as opposed to the later vida) describe Beatrice as Boniface's daughter, and thus William's grand-daughter. Otto (Oddone) of Montferrat, (d. 1251), who became Bishop of Porto, and Cardinal in 1227, has 
1 CONC sometimes been identified as a son of William V, and confused with Frederick. However, his dates make it more likely that he was a son of William VI of Montferrat, whether legitimate or not is uncertain.
1 CONT William and Judith's powerful dynastic connections created difficulties in finding suitable wives for his sons, however: too many potential spouses were related within prohibited degrees. In 1167, he unsuccessfully tried to negoti
1 CONC ate marriages for his eldest sons to daughters of Henry II of England - but the girls were very young at the time and were related through Judith's descent from William V of Aquitaine. He then applied for sisters of William I of S
1 CONC cotland, who were not related, but were already married.
1 CONT
1 CONT Alliances with the Western and Eastern Empires
1 CONT William took part in the Second Crusade, alongside his half-brother Amadeus of Savoy (who died during the campaign), his nephew Louis VII of France, his brother-in-law Count Guido of Biandrate, and his wife's German and Austrian r
1 CONC elatives.
1 CONT As supporters of the imperial party (later known as the Ghibellines), he and his sons fought with the Emperor Frederick Barbarossa (Judith's nephew) in his lengthy struggle against the Lombard League. Following Barbarossa's capitu
1 CONC lation with the Peace of Venice in 1177, William was left to deal with the rebellious towns in the area alone. Meanwhile, the Byzantine emperor Manuel I Komnenos sought support for his own politics in Italy.
1 CONT William broke with Barbarossa and formed an alliance with Manuel. His eldest surviving son, Conrad, was taken prisoner by Barbarossa's Chancellor, Archbishop Christian of Mainz, but then captured the chancellor in battle at Cameri
1 CONC no. In 1179 Manuel suggested a marriage between his daughter Maria, second in line to the throne, and one of William's sons. As Conrad and Boniface were already married, the youngest son, Renier, was married off to the princess, w
1 CONC ho was ten years his senior. Renier and Maria were later killed during the usurpation of Andronikos, and the family rebuilt ties with Barbarossa.
1 CONT
1 CONT William in Outremer
1 CONT In 1183, with the accession of his grandson Baldwin V, a minor, as co-King of Jerusalem, William, then probably in his late sixties, left the government of Montferrat to Conrad and Boniface, and returned to the east. He was grante
1 CONC d the castle of St. Elias (present-day El Taiyiba). He fought in the Battle of Hattin in 1187, where he was captured by Saladin's forces. In the meantime, his second son, Conrad, had arrived at Tyre from Constantinople. Conrad was
1 CONC  given the command of the defences. During the siege of Tyre in November that year, he is said to have refused to surrender as much as a stone of its walls to liberate his father, even threatening to shoot him with a crossbow hims
1 CONC elf when Saladin had him presented as a hostage. Eventually, Saladin withdrew his army from Tyre. In 1188, William was released unharmed at Tortosa, and seems to have ended his days in Tyre, with his son. He probably died in the s
1 CONC ummer of 1191: Conrad last describes himself as "marchionis Montisferrati filius" in a charter of May that year.
1 CONT
1 CONT Sources
1 CONT •	Haberstumpf, Walter. Dinastie europee nel Mediterraneo orientale. I Monferrato e i Savoia nei secoli XII–XV, 1995 (external link to downloadable text).
1 CONT •	Hamilton, Bernard. The Leper King and His Heirs: Baldwin IV and the Crusader Kingdom of Jerusalem, 2000.
