0 @N6000000000337139936@ NOTE (Research):James II Encyclop‘dia Britannica Article   born c. 1264 died Nov. 3, 1327, Barcelona, Aragon [Spain]   byname James the Just, Spanish Jaime el Justo king of Aragon from 1295 to 1327 and king of Sicily (as James I) from 
1 CONC 1285 to 1295.   At the death of his father, Peter III, on Nov. 11, 1285, James inherited Sicily, and his elder brother became Alfonso III of Aragon, Catalonia, and Valencia. When his brother died (1291) he inherited Aragon and res
1 CONC igned Sicily (1295), marrying Blanche, daughter of Charles II of Naples, in an endeavour to make peace with the Angevins. Sardinia and Corsica were both assigned to him in compensation for Sicily, but he was able to occupy Sardini
1 CONC a only (1324). He was succeeded by his son Alfonso IV.
1 CONT
1 CONT James II (Jaime II) 1260-1327, king of Aragðn and count of Barcelona (1291-1327), king of Sicily (1285-95).  He succeeded his father, Peter III (Pedro III), in Sicily and his brother Alfonso III, in Aragðn.  James defended Sicily 
1 CONC against the claims of Charles II of Naples until 1295, when he relinquished the island in exchange for the title to Sardinia and Corsica. (Sardinia was annexed in 1323-24, but he did not take Corsica.) James later supported Charle
1 CONC s against the former's own brother, who had been proclaimed king of Sicly as Frederick II.  James was succeeded in Aragðn by his son Alfonso IV.
0 @N6000000002188005667@ NOTE James II of Aragon
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT  James II, King of Aragon (10 August 1267 – 2 November 1327), in Spanish Jaime II, in Catalan Jaume II, also James II of Barcelona, called The Just (Catalan: El Just) was the second son of Peter III of Aragon and Constance of Sici
1 CONC ly.
1 CONT
1 CONT He succeeded his father as King of Sicily in 1285, and was then known as "James I of Sicily." Upon his brother Alfonso III's death in 1291, he succeeded also to the throne of Aragon. By a peace treaty with Charles II of Anjou in 1
1 CONC 296, he agreed to give up Sicily, but the Sicilians instead installed on the throne his brother Frederick. He reigned in Aragon until his death in 1327.
1 CONT
1 CONT He married four times:
1 CONT
1 CONT —His first wife was Elizabeth of Castile, daughter of Sancho IV of Castile. The wedding took place in the city of Soria, in 1 December 1291 (when the bride was only 8 years old); the marriage (who was never consummated) was dissol
1 CONC ved after Sancho's death (1295), when James chose to change his alliances and take advantage of the turmoil inside Castile.
1 CONT
1 CONT —His second wife was Blanca of Anjou, daughter of his family's rival Charles II of Naples. They married in the city of Villabertran, in 29 October 1295 and she was mother of his legitimate children:
1 CONT
1 CONT James (Jaume) (b. 29 September 1296 - d. Tarragona, July 1334), renounced his rights in 1319 to become a monk. He refused to consummate his marriage to Leonor of Castille, who later become the second wife of his brother Alfonso. 
1 CONT Alfonso IV of Aragon. 
1 CONT Maria (b. 1299 - d. as a nun in Sijena, 1316), who married Pedro of Castile, son of Sancho IV of Castile. 
1 CONT Constance (Constança) (b. Valencia, 1 April 1300 - d. Castillo de Garcia Munoz, 19 September 1327), who married Juan Manuel of Castile, nephew of Alfonso X of Castile. 
1 CONT John (Juan) (b. 1304 - d. Pobo, Zaragoza, 19 August 1334), who was first Archbishop of Toledo and Tarragona in 1318, and Patriarch of Alexandria in 1328. 
1 CONT Elizabeth (Isabel) (b. 1305 - d. Styria, 12 July 1330), who married Frederick I of Austria. 
1 CONT Peter (Pere) (b. 1305 - d. Pisa, 4 November 1381), Count of Ribagorça and Prades. Married Jeanne, daughter of Gaston I of Foix. 
1 CONT Blanca (b. 1307 - d. Barcelona, 1348), Prioress of Sixena. 
1 CONT Ramon Berenguer (b. August 1308 - d. a priest at Barcelona, 1366), Count of Empúries. Married firstly with Blanca, daugther of Philip I of Taranto, and secondly with Maria, daughter of Jaime of Aragon. Baron of Ejerica. 
1 CONT Violante (b. Barcelona, October 1310 - d. Pedrola, 19 July 1353), who married firstly with Philip, Despot of Romania, son of Philip I of Taranto and secondly with Lope de Luna, Lord of Segorbe. 
1 CONT —His third wife was Maria of Lusignan, daugther of the King Hugh III of Cyprus. They married by proxy in Santa Sophia, Nicosia, in 15 June 1315, and in person in the city of Girona, in 27 November 1315. This marriage was childless
1 CONC .
1 CONT
1 CONT —His fourth and last wife was Elisenda of Moncada, daugther of Pedro I of Moncada, Lord of Altona and Soses. They married in the coty of Tarragona, in 25 December 1322. This marriage was childless too, and, after the king's death,
1 CONC  she entered to the convent of clarisas in Pedralbes as a nun.
1 CONT
1 CONT In addition to his legitimate offspring, James had three natural children born with Sicilian women:
1 CONT
1 CONT —With Gerolda:
1 CONT
1 CONT Sancho (b. Sicily, 1287 - d. young?). 
1 CONT Napoleón (b. Sicily, 1288 - m. 1338), Lord of Joyosa Guarda (Gioiosaguardia) and Acquafredda (in Sardinia); married a daughter of a Majorcan named Guillermo Robert. 
1 CONT —With Lucrecia:
1 CONT
1 CONT James (Jaume) (b. Mazzara, 1291 - d. 1350), Vicario di Cagliari (1317-1341); married firstly with Jaumetta Guerau, from Majorca, and secondly with Puccia N, a Sardinian woman. 
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT Preceded by:
1 CONT Peter I King of Sicily
1 CONT 1285–1296 Succeeded by:
1 CONT Frederick III 
1 CONT Preceded by:
1 CONT Alfonso III King of Aragon
1 CONT 1291–1327 Succeeded by:
1 CONT Alfonso IV 
1 CONT Count of Barcelona
1 CONT 1291–1327 
1 CONT King of Valencia
1 CONT 1291–1327 
