0 @N6000000003949883297@ NOTE The rule of Hugh III marked the ending of a period of relative peace in the duchy of Burgundy. Hugh was a belligerent man and soon was involved in conflicts against king Louis VII of France over their borders. When Philip Augustus
1 CONC  succeeded Louis in 1180, Hugh seized the opportunity and forced several men to change alliance to Burgundy. Philip II was not happy with the loss of his vassals and invaded the duchy, besieging Chatillon. The town fell and with i
1 CONC t, its garrison, commanded by Eudes, Hugh's heir. A peace was negotiated and Hugh had to pay a high ransom for his son and give up ambitions over French territory.
1 CONT Hugh then turned his energies to the Holy Land, embarking in the Third Crusade in the retinue of Philip II. He was the most trusted ally of Richard, the Lionheart and fought with him against Saladin. When Philip returned to France
1 CONC , he left Hugh in charge of the French troops. Hugh played a major role in the victory of the battle of Arsuf (September 7, 1191) and the conquer of Acre, where he died in the following year.
1 CONT In 1187, Hugh transferred the capital of Burgundy to Dijon, and endeavoured to turn the city into a major commercial centre.
0 @N6000000004303404792@ NOTE WIKIPEDIA:
1 CONT
1 CONT Hugh III, Duke of Burgundy
1 CONT Hugh III of Burgundy (1142 – August 25, 1192, in Acre) was duke of Burgundy between 1162 and 1192. Hugh was the eldest son of duke Eudes II and Marie De Champagne, daughter of Theobald and Mathilda of Carinthia.
1 CONT
1 CONT The rule of Hugh III marked the ending of a period of relative peace in the duchy of Burgundy. Hugh was a belligerent man and soon was involved in conflicts against king Louis VII of France over their borders. When Philip Augustus
1 CONC  succeeded Louis in 1180, Hugh seized the opportunity and forced several men to change alliance to Burgundy. Philip II was not happy with the loss of his vassals and invaded the duchy, besieging Chatillon. The town fell and with i
1 CONC t, its garrison, commanded by Eudes, Hugh's heir. A peace was negotiated and Hugh had to pay a high ransom for his son and give up ambitions over French territory.
1 CONT Hugh then turned his energies to the Holy Land, embarking in the Third Crusade in the retinue of Philip II. He was the most trusted ally of Richard, the Lionheart and fought with him against Saladin. When Philip returned to France
1 CONC , he left Hugh in charge of the French troops. Hugh played a major role in the victory of the battle of Arsuf (September 7, 1191) and the conquest of Acre, where he died in the following year.
1 CONT In 1187, Hugh transferred the capital of Burgundy to Dijon, and endeavoured to turn the city into a major commercial centre.
1 CONT
1 CONT Marriage
1 CONT He was married twice:
1 CONT •	Firstly to Alice of Lorraine (daughter of duke Matthias I), whom he divorced in 1183.
1 CONT •	Secondly to Beatrice De Viennois, daughter of Guigo, Count of Viennois and Beatrice De Montferrat.
1 CONT
1 CONT Issue
1 CONT With Alice:
1 CONT •	Eudes III, his fathers heir
1 CONT With Beatrice:
1 CONT •	Anne of Burgundy, married Amadeus IV of Savoy
1 CONT
1 CONT Reference
1 CONT •	The Royal Ancestry Bible Royal Ancestors of 300 Colonial American Families by Michel L. Call (chart 2718) ISBN 1-933194-22-7
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT See also
1 CONT •	Dukes of Burgundy family tree
1 CONT
1 CONT Preceded by Eudes II
1 CONT Duke of Burgundy 1162–1192
1 CONT Succeeded by Eudes III
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT -------------
1 CONT
1 CONT Marriage 1 Alix (Alice) de Lorraine b: ABT 1150 in Lorraine, France
1 CONT •	Married: 1165 in 1st wife - divorced 1183 4
1 CONT Children
1 CONT 1.	 Eudes III Duke of Burgundy b: 1166 in Dijon, Cote-d'Or, Bourgogne, France
1 CONT 2.	 Alexander de Burgundy , Seigneur de Montagu b: ABT 1170 in Dijon, Cote-d'Or, Bourgogne, France
1 CONT 3.	 Mahaut Countess of Burgundy b: ABT 1190 in Dijon, Cote-d'Or, Bourgogne, France
1 CONT
1 CONT Marriage 2 Beatrice Dauphine de Viennois b: 1161 in Vienne, Loire, Lyonnais/Rhone-Alpes, France
1 CONT •	Married: 1 SEP 1183 in 2nd husband 2nd wife 2
1 CONT Children
1 CONT 1.	 Andre Dauphin de Vienne , Count Albon-Vienois b: 1184 in Bourgogne, France
1 CONT 2.	 Marguerite (Anna) of Geneva b: 1192 in Bourgogne, France
0 @N6000000004533182702@ NOTE From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia.
1 CONT Hugh III of Burgundy (1142–August 25, 1192, in Acre) was duke ofBurgundy between 1162 and 1192. Hugh was the eldest son of duke EudesII and Marie of Blois. He was married twice, first to Beatriced'Albon, then to Alice of Lorraine 
1 CONC (daughter of duke Matthias I), andhad several sons and daughters.
1 CONT
1 CONT The rule of Hugh III marked the ending of a period of relative peacein the duchy of Burgundy. Hugh was a belligerent man and soon wasinvolved in conflicts against king Louis VII of France over theirborders. When Philip Augustus su
1 CONC cceeded Louis in 1180, Hugh seized theopportunity and forced several men to change alliance to Burgundy.Philip II was not happy with the loss of his vassals and invaded theduchy, besieging Chatillon. The town fell and with it, its
1 CONC  garrison,commanded by Eudes, Hugh's heir. A peace was negotiated and Hugh hadto pay a high ransom for his son and give up ambitions over Frenchterritory.
1 CONT
1 CONT Hugh then turned his energies to the Holy Land, embarking in the ThirdCrusade in the retinue of Philip II. He was the most trusted ally ofRichard, the Lionheart and fought with him against Saladin. WhenPhilip returned to France, h
1 CONC e left Hugh in charge of the Frenchtroops. Hugh played a major role in the victory of the battle of Arsuf(September 7, 1191) and the conquer of Acre, where he died in thefollowing year.
1 CONT
1 CONT In 1187, Hugh transferred the capital of Burgundy to Dijon, andendeavoured to turn the city into a major commercial centre.
0 @N6000000009304980084@ NOTE #Générale#Hugues III, Duc de Bourgogne, pair de France, assista le roi Louis le Jeune en la guerre contre Guillaume I de Bourgogne, comte de Chalon, l'an 1166. Fit le voyage en Terre Sainte en 1171, ayant fait l'année précédente d
1 CONC u consentement de sa femme Alix et de son fils Eudes, une donation à l'abbaye de S. Benigne de Dijon. Il fonda à son retour en 1172, la Sainte-Chapelle de Dijon, du consentement de sa femme & de son fils, se trouva au sacre du roi
1 CONC  Philippe Auguste en 1179 & eut guerre contre Hugues seigneur de Vergy en 1180 & 1185.
1 CONT
1 CONT Ayant fait un traité lui & sa femme Béatrix avec Jean évêque de Grenoble, touchant leurs droits communs sur la maison dite de la plaine, et sur le mesurage des grains vendus dans le temps des foires & marchés de la ville de Grenob
1 CONC le, il fut confirmé par le pape Luce III, le 13 Juin 1184. Il accompagna le roy Philippe Auguste au voyage d'outremer, se trouva au siège d'Acre en 1191 et mourut à Tyr le 23 Aout de l'année suivante. Son corps fut apporté à Citea
1 CONC ux, et son tombeau s'y voit sous le portail de la grande Eglise de cette abbaye.
