0 @N6000000001353933236@ NOTE Under Humbert II (1080) occurred the first clash of the House of Savoy with the Piedmontese communes, but he and his successors, Amadeus III (who died on his way home from the crusades) and Thomas I (1189), adopted a policy of con
1 CONC ciliation towards them. Thomas, who reigned until 1222, acquired extensions of territory in the Bugey, Vaud and Romont to the west of the Alps, and Carignano, Pinerolo, Moncalieri and Vigone to the east; he also exercised sway ove
1 CONC r Geneva, Albenga, Savona and Saluzzo. At his death, these territories were divided among his sons, Thomas II obtaining Piedmont, Aimone the Chablais, Peter and Philip other fiefs, and Amadeus IV, the eldest, Savoy and a general o
1 CONC verlordship over his brothers' estates. Thomas II, during the wars in Piedmont, was made prisoner by the citizens of Turin, but was afterwards liberated. [Encyclopaedia Britannica, 1961 ed., Vol. 20, p. 26, SAVOY, HOUSE OF]
