0 @N6000000002188011383@ NOTE Richard FitzAlan, 11th Earl of Arundel
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Richard FitzAlan, 11th Earl of Arundel and 10th Earl of Surrey (1346 – September 21, 1397, beheaded) was an English nobleman and military commander.
1 CONT
1 CONT He was the son of Richard FitzAlan, 10th Earl of Arundel and Eleanor of Lancaster.
1 CONT
1 CONT In 1377 he was Admiral of the West and South, and in 1386 Admiral of all England. In this capacity he defeated a combined Franco-Spanish-Flemish fleet off of Margate in 1387. The following year he was one of the Lords Appellant to
1 CONC  Richard II.
1 CONT
1 CONT In 1397 he was arrested for his opposition to Richard II, and then attainted and beheaded.
1 CONT
1 CONT Arundel married twice. His first wife was Elizabeth de Bohun, daughter of William de Bohun, 1st Earl of Northampton. They married around September 28, 1359 and had four children:
1 CONT
1 CONT Thomas FitzAlan, 12th Earl of Arundel 
1 CONT Elizabeth FitzAlan 
1 CONT Joan FitzAlan (1375 - November 14, 1453), who married William Beauchamp, 1st Lord Bergavanny; 
1 CONT Margaret FitzAlan, who married Sir Rowland Lenthall; 
1 CONT Alice FitzAlan, who married the 4th Baron Charleton. 
1 CONT Arundel then married Philippa Mortimer, daughter of Edmund Mortimer, 3rd Earl of March. Her mother was Philippa Plantagenet, a daughter of Lionel of Antwerp and thus a granddaughter of Edward III. They had no children.
1 CONT
1 CONT Preceded by:
1 CONT Richard FitzAlan Earl of Arundel
1 CONT 1376 - 1397 Succeeded by:
1 CONT Thomas FitzAlan 
