0 @N6000000002188011403@ NOTE Thomas Arundel
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Thomas Arundel (1353-1414) was Archbishop of Canterbury in 1397 and from 1399 until his death, an outspoken opponent of the Lollards.
1 CONT
1 CONT A younger son of Richard Fitzalan, 10th Earl of Arundel, he held the title of Archbishop of York from 1388 before being moved to Canterbury in 1397. However, he was exiled by King Richard II of England, and his tenure was interrup
1 CONC ted by that of Roger Walden. On the accession of Henry IV, Roger Walden, his successor in the primatial see, was declared a usurper, and Arundel restored, 21 October 1399, Walden being translated to London.
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit]
1 CONT Anti-Lollard
1 CONT He is conspicuous as having taken an extremist stand against the Lollards whose new doctrine he, in company with the bishops of the province, petitioned Rome to condemn. Herein lie the roots of the Catholic and Protestant feud; Ri
1 CONC chard and Arundel sought to consolidate the power of the state and church over people's lives, without toleration of dissent. The Lollards, on the other hand, sought to nullify some of the church's more antiquarian perspectives, a
1 CONC nd as such were seen as reformist predecessors of Protestantism. Amongst other plights, they sought an end to the church's insistence on publishing all texts in Latin, which meant that the common man was unable to understand them.
1 CONC  Contemporary literary figures such as Geoffrey Chaucer were behind this campaign, and many speculate that this may have been the cause of Chaucer's premature death. (See Terry Jones book Who Murdered Chaucer? : A Medieval Mystery
1 CONC ). Naturally, the Catholic church was anxious to preserve its position at the forefont of English society, and Arundel spearheaded the bloody backlash against the Lollards. As such, he was selected by the BBC History Magazine as t
1 CONC he 15th century's worst Briton. (BBC), and in 2006, he was selected by the BBC History Magazine as the 9th worst Briton in the last 1000 years.
1 CONT
1 CONT Arundel was also vehement in his sturdy assertion of Transubstantiation and the prerogatives and divine institution of the Papacy. He was less popular with Henry's son and successor, Henry V.
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit]
1 CONT External link
1 CONT Constitutions (1408). Umilta Website. Retrieved on October 11, 2005. Latin transcription; Arundel's prohibition of Bible translation into vernacular languages. 
1 CONT Political Offices 
1 CONT Preceded by:
1 CONT The Earl of Suffolk Lord Chancellor
1 CONT 1386–1389 Succeeded by:
1 CONT William of Wykeham 
1 CONT Preceded by:
1 CONT William of Wykeham Lord Chancellor
1 CONT 1391–1396 Succeeded by:
1 CONT Edmund Stafford 
1 CONT Preceded by:
1 CONT Edmund Stafford Lord Chancellor
1 CONT 1399 Succeeded by:
1 CONT John Scarle 
1 CONT Preceded by:
1 CONT Thomas Langley Lord Chancellor
1 CONT 1407–1410 Succeeded by:
1 CONT Sir Thomas Beaufort 
1 CONT Preceded by:
1 CONT Sir Thomas Beaufort Lord Chancellor
1 CONT 1412–1413 Succeeded by:
1 CONT Henry Beaufort 
1 CONT Religious Posts 
