0 @N6000000002188655029@ NOTE Margaret, Duchess of Norfolk
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Margaret, Duchess of Norfolk (also Countess of Norfolk, Lady Manny and Lady de Segrave, later construct Plantagenet) (c. 1320 – 24 March 1399) was Countess of Norfolk from 1338 to 1399. She was the daughter of Thomas of Brotherton
1 CONC , 1st Earl of Norfolk, by his first wife Alice Hayles. She became heiress to the Earldom after the death of her childless brother, Edward, in 1334.
1 CONT
1 CONT She married firstly in 1337 to Sir John de Segrave, 4th Baron Segrave by whom she had four children:
1 CONT
1 CONT    1. Edmund de Segrave, died in the cradle.
1 CONT    2. Elizabeth de Segrave (1338-1368), married John de Mowbray, 4th Baron Mowbray
1 CONT    3. John de Segrave (13 September 1340-1349)
1 CONT    4. Anne de Segrave, Abbess of Barking
1 CONT
1 CONT In 1338, Margaret inherited the Earldom of Norfolk when her father died, becoming the 2nd Countess of Norfolk. Along with this title came the office of Lord Marshal. To date, she is the only woman to have served in this position (
1 CONC or, as it was called in the future, Earl Marshal).
1 CONT
1 CONT In 1350, Margaret and John de Segrave began seeking a divorce based on the premise that they were contracted in marriage before she was of age, and that she had never consented to marry him. Furthermore, she had begun a relationsh
1 CONC ip with a knight of great renown, Sir Walter Manny, 1st Baron Manny, as she crossed the channel in October 1350 to meet with him in Calais, without the king's permission. The inquisition regarding this incident shows that Margaret
1 CONC  illegally crossed the Channel and met with Manny's servant, who broke his lantern with his foot so she could pass unnoticed.
1 CONT
1 CONT John de Segrave died in 1353, before their divorce could be finalized; the next year Margaret wed Walter Manny shortly before 30 May 1354, and they had two more children:
1 CONT
1 CONT    1. Thomas Manny, drowned in a well as a boy.
1 CONT    2. Anne Manny, 2nd Baroness Manny, wife of John Hastings, 2nd Earl of Pembroke
1 CONT
1 CONT Sir Walter died in 1371. Margaret never remarried, and as a widow was created Duchess of Norfolk for life in 1397, and her grandson Thomas Mowbray, 1st Duke of Norfolk was created duke the same year. Margaret, Duchess of Norfolk, 
1 CONC died in 1399 and was buried in the choir of the Grey Friars, London.
1 CONT Margaret, Duchess of Norfolk
1 CONT House of Plantagenet
1 CONT Born: c.1300 Died: 24 March 1399
1 CONT Political offices
1 CONT Preceded by
1 CONT The Earl of Norfolk Lord Marshal
1 CONT 1338–1377 Succeeded by
1 CONT The Earl of Northumberland
1 CONT Peerage of England
1 CONT Preceded by
1 CONT Thomas Plantagenet Countess of Norfolk
1 CONT suo jure
1 CONT 1338–1399 Succeeded by
1 CONT Thomas de Mowbray
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] See also
1 CONT
1 CONT     * Baron Mowbray
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Sources
1 CONT
1 CONT     * Complete Peerage, Vol.9, sub. Norfolk
1 CONT     * Calendar Inquisitions Miscellaneous, vol. 3, 1937
1 CONT     * Calendar of Entries in the Papal Registers: Letters, 4, 1902
1 CONT     * Segrave, Charles, The Segrave Family: 1066 to 1935
