0 @N6000000002188378003@ NOTE Charles III of Navarre
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Charles III (1361, Nantes – 8 September 1425, Olite), called the Noble, was King of Navarre from 1387 to his death and Count of Évreux from 1387 to 1404, when he exchanged it for the title Duke of Nemours. He spent his reign impro
1 CONC ving the infrastructure of his kingdom, restoring Navarre's pride after the dismal reign of his father, Charles the Bad, and mending strained relations with France.
1 CONT
1 CONT He married the infanta Eleanor, daughter of Henry II of Castile, in 1375, putting an end to the conflict between the Castile and Navarre.
1 CONT
1 CONT As king, his politics were peace with France, Castile, Aragon, and England, support for the Avignon Papacy, and matrimonial alliance. He collaborated with Castile in a war on the Kingdom of Granada. By the Treaty of Paris, he aban
1 CONC doned his claims to Champagne and Brie and made peace with France.
1 CONT
1 CONT In 1413, he created the Cort, a sort of supreme court. He created the title Prince of Viana for the heir the throne, entitling his grandson Charles in 1423. He was a patron of the arts and he finished construction on the great Got
1 CONC hic Cathedral of Pamplona. He built the royal palace at Tafalla and the Palacio Real de Olite, where he died in 1425.
1 CONT
1 CONT He outlived his sons and was succeeded by his daughter, Blanca (1425–1441) and her husband, John II of Aragon (1425–1479).
1 CONT
1 CONT His sister, Joanna of Navarre, married Henry IV of England.
1 CONT Preceded by
1 CONT Charles II 
1 CONT King of Navarre
1 CONT 1387–1425 Succeeded by
1 CONT Blanche I
1 CONT Count of Évreux
1 CONT 1387–1404 Succeeded by
1 CONT to French royal domain
