0 @N6000000002188309773@ NOTE Edmund Mortimer, 3rd Earl of March
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT Jump to: navigation, search
1 CONT
1 CONT Edmund de Mortimer, 3rd Earl of March and jure uxoris Earl of Ulster (1351? – 27 December 1381) was son of Roger Mortimer, 2nd Earl of March, by his wife Philippa, daughter of William Montacute, 1st Earl of Salisbury.
1 CONT Contents
1 CONT [hide]
1 CONT
1 CONT     * 1 Early life
1 CONT     * 2 Political Advancement
1 CONT     * 3 Reign of Richard II
1 CONT     * 4 Children
1 CONT     * 5 Notes
1 CONT     * 6 References
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Early life
1 CONT
1 CONT Being an infant at the death of his father, Edmund, as a ward of the crown, was placed by Edward III of England under the care of William of Wykeham and Richard Fitzalan, 10th Earl of Arundel.
1 CONT
1 CONT The position of the young earl, powerful on account of his possessions and hereditary influence in the Welsh marches, was rendered still more important by his marriage in 1368 at the age of 17 to the 13 year old Philippa, the only
1 CONC  child of Lionel of Antwerp, Duke of Clarence, second son of Edward III.
1 CONT
1 CONT Lionel's wife, Elizabeth, was daughter and heiress of William Donn de Burgh, 3rd Earl of Ulster, and Lionel had himself been created Earl of Ulster before his marriage. Edmund inherited the title Earl of Ulster on Lionel's death.
1 CONT
1 CONT Therefore, the Earl of March not only represented one of the chief Anglo-Norman lordships in Ireland in right of his wife Philippa, but Philippa's line was also the second most senior line of descent in the succession to the crown
1 CONC , after Edward, the Black Prince and his son, King Richard II of England.
1 CONT
1 CONT This marriage had, therefore, far-reaching consequences in English history, ultimately giving rise to the claim of the House of York to the crown of England contested in the Wars of the Roses; Edward IV being descended from the se
1 CONC cond adult son of Edward III as great-great-grandson of Philippa, countess of March, and in the male line from Edmund of Langley, fourth adult son of Edward III.
1 CONT
1 CONT Edmund's son Roger Mortimer, 4th Earl of March would become heir presumptive to the English crown during the reign of Richard II.
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Political Advancement
1 CONT
1 CONT Mortimer, now styled Earl of March and Ulster, became Marshal of England in 1369, and was employed in various diplomatic missions during the next following years. He was a member of the committee appointed by the Peers to confer w
1 CONC ith the Commons in 1373 — the first instance of such a joint conference since the institution of representative parliaments on the question of granting supplies for John of Gaunt's war in France.
1 CONT
1 CONT He participated in the opposition to Edward III and the court party, which grew in strength towards the end of the reign, taking the popular side and being prominent in the Good Parliament of 1376 among the lords who supported the
1 CONC  Prince of Wales and opposed the Court Party and John of Gaunt. The Speaker of the Commons in this parliament was March's steward, Peter de la Mare, who firmly withstood John of Gaunt in stating the grievances of the Commons, in s
1 CONC upporting the impeachment of several high court officials, and in procuring the banishment of the king's mistress, Alice Perrers. March was a member of the administrative council appointed by the same parliament after the death of
1 CONC  Edward, the Black Prince to attend the king and advise him in all public affairs.
1 CONT
1 CONT Following the end of the Good Parliament its acts were reversed by John of Gaunt, March's steward was jailed, and March himself was ordered to inspect Calais and other remote royal castles as part of his duty as Marshall of Englan
1 CONC d. March chose instead to resign the post.[1]
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Reign of Richard II
1 CONT
1 CONT On the accession of Richard II, a minor, in 1377, the Earl became a member of the standing council of government; though as father of the heir-presumptive to the crown he wisely abstained from claiming any actually administrative 
1 CONC office. The most powerful person in the realm was, however, the king's uncle John of Gaunt, whose jealousy of March led to the acceptance by the latter of the Lieutenancy of Ireland in 1379. March succeeded in asserting his author
1 CONC ity in eastern Ulster, but failed to subdue the O'Neills farther west. Proceeding to Munster to put down the turbulency of the chieftains of the south, March died at Cork on 27 December 1381.[1] He was buried in Wigmore Abbey, of 
1 CONC which he had been a benefactor, and where his wife Philippa was also interred.
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Children
1 CONT
1 CONT The earl had two sons and two daughters:[1]
1 CONT
1 CONT     * Elizabeth, married Henry Percy "Hotspur", son of the Earl of Northumberland. She may have later married Thomas de Camoys, 1st Baron Camoys[2]
1 CONT     * His eldest son, Roger, succeeded him as 4th Earl of March and Ulster
1 CONT     * His second son, Edmund played an important part, in conjunction with his brother-in-law Hotspur, in the fortunes of Owain Glyndwr
1 CONT     * Philippa, became the second wife of Richard Fitzalan, 11th Earl of Arundel and then married John of St. John
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Notes
1 CONT
1 CONT    1. ^ a b c Tout
1 CONT    2. ^ Archer, p. 306
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] References
1 CONT
1 CONT     * Archer, Thomas Andrew (1886). "Thomas de Camoys, fifth Baron Camoys". Dictionary of National Biography 8. 305-306. 
1 CONT     * Tout, T. F. (1894). "Edmund de Mortimer, third earl of March". Dictionary of National Biography 39.  
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT Peerage of England
1 CONT Preceded by
1 CONT Roger Mortimer Earl of March
1 CONT 1360–1381 Succeeded by
1 CONT Roger Mortimer
1 CONT Preceded by
1 CONT Lionel of Antwerp Earl of Ulster
1 CONT (jure uxoris
1 CONT by Philippa Plantagenet)
1 CONT 1363–1381
