1 CONT Roger de Mortimer, 4th Earl of March and 6th Earl of Ulster (11 April 1374 – 20 July 1398)[2] was the heir presumptive to Richard II of England between 1385 and 1398.
1 CONT
1 CONT Mortimer was son of the powerful Edmund Mortimer, 3rd Earl of March, and Philippa, Countess of March and Ulster.[1] His mother was the only issue of Lionel of Antwerp, Duke of Clarence, the second surviving son of King Edward III 
1 CONC of England and Philippa of Hainault[3]. Thus, Mortimer was (after his father's death) Richard II's heir if succession is allowed through a female line.
1 CONT
1 CONT Mortimer's mother died quite early and his father on December 27, 1381, so Mortimer succeeded to his title and estates when only seven years old.[1] His hereditary influence and position caused him to be appointed to the lord-lieu
1 CONC tenancy of Ireland on January 24, 1382.[1] His uncle Sir Thomas Mortimer acted as his deputy.[1] This experiment did not work well and Mortimer was replaced by Philip de Courtenay the next year.[1]
1 CONT
1 CONT Being a ward of the Crown, his guardian was Thomas Holland, 2nd Earl of Kent, half-brother to Richard II. The earl of Kent also purchased the rights to choose Mortimer's bride, and 1387 or early 1388 married him to his daughter Al
1 CONC ianor (Eleanor).[4]
1 CONT
1 CONT The importance which he owed to his hereditary influence and possessions, and especially to his descent from Edward III, was immensely increased when Richard II publicly acknowledged him as heir presumptive to the crown in 1385[5]
1 CONC .
1 CONT Contents
1 CONT [hide]
