0 @N6000000003300450893@ NOTE 1328-85, English noblewoman; daughter of Edmund of Woodstock, earl of Ken t, youngest son of Edward I. She early gained wide note for her beauty an d charm, though the appellation Fair Maid of Kent, by which she became kn own, was
1 CONC  probably not contemporary. Her marriage to the earl of Salisbur y was annulled on the grounds of a precontract with Sir Thomas Holland, w hom she then married. Upon the death of her brother in 1352 she became co untess of Kent in
1 CONC  her own right. In 1361, after Holland?s death, she marr ied Edward the Black Prince, by whom she had two sons, Edward (1365-70) a nd Richard (later Richard II). In 1378 she was instrumental in halting pr oceedings against John Wy
1 CONC clif, though there is insufficient evidence to d etermine if she accepted his doctrines. As long as she lived, she was pro bably the principal influence on her son Richard II.
1 CONT Joan of Kent
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia.
1 CONT Joan, Princess of Wales, known to history as "The Fair Maid of Kent", wa s born on September 29, 1328. Her father, Edmund, Earl of Kent, was a so n of King Edward I of England, and a half-brother of Edward II. But the E arl?s roya
1 CONC l blood could not save him from execution at the hands of his s ister-in-law, Queen Isabella, and her lover, Roger Mortimer. His crime wa s to have given support to his brother, the King, when he was deposed fro m the throne.
1 CONT The Earl?s widow, Margaret, was left with four children. Her younger daug hter, Joan, was only two years old. Her cousin, the new King, Edward III , took on the responsibility for the family, and looked after them well . His wife,
1 CONC  Queen Philippa, was well known for her tender-heartedness, an d Joan grew up at court, where she became friendly with her cousins, incl uding Edward, the Black Prince.
1 CONT At the age of twelve, she entered into a clandestine marriage with Thoma s Holland of Broughton. The following year, while Holland was overseas, h er family forced her into a marriage with William de Montacute, Earl of S alisbury.
1 CONC  As Countess of Salisbury, Joan moved in the highest society. So me historians identify her as the mystery woman who appeared at a banque t in Calais and attracted the attention of every man present. Allegedly , while dancing with
1 CONC  the King, the lady lost her blue velvet garter, an d this was the origin of the Order of the Garter. It is more likely tha t Joan's mother-in-law was the woman involved.
1 CONT It was not for several years that Thomas Holland returned from crusade, h aving made his fortune, and the full story of his earlier relationship wi th Joan came out. He appealed to the Pope for the return of his wife. Whe n the Ea
1 CONC rl of Salisbury discovered that Joan supported Holland?s case, h e kept her a virtual prisoner in her own home. That did not help. The Pop e was convinced. He annulled Joan?s marriage to the Earl and sent her bac k to Thomas Holla
1 CONC nd, with whom she lived for the next eleven years. The y had five children, then Holland died.
1 CONT Joan, now widowed but only thirty-two, was a catch by anyone else?s stand ards. She had inherited the earldom of Kent when her brother died in 1353 . She was strikingly beautiful, with perfect features, auburn hair that r eached t
1 CONC o her waist, and dark eyes, and was regarded as one of the most d esirable women in the country. The Black Prince had been in love with he r for years, but his father and mother disapproved. Queen Philippa migh t have made a favou
1 CONC rite of Joan at first, but as her son grew older, sh e had become concerned about the budding romance between the two cousins , and set herself against it.
1 CONT The Archbishop of Canterbury warned the Prince that there could be doubt s cast on the legitimacy of any children Joan might bear him, in view o f the fact that one of her previous husbands, the Earl of Salisbury, wa s still alive
1 CONC , but the marriage went ahead with an assurance of absolutio n from the Pope. They were married in 1361, and almost immediately set sa il for France, since the Black Prince was also the Prince of Aquitaine , a region of France whi
1 CONC ch belonged to the English Crown. Two children we re born in France, both of them sons. The elder son, named Edward after h is father and grandfather, died at the age of six.
1 CONT Around the time of the birth of their younger son, Richard, the prince wa s lured into a war on behalf of Pedro the Cruel, ruler of Castile. The en suing battle was one of the Black Prince?s greatest victories, but King P edro was
1 CONC  killed, and there was no money to pay the troops. In the meantim e, the Princess was forced to raise another army, because the Prince?s en emies were threatening Aquitaine in his absence.
1 CONT By 1371, the Black Prince was no longer able to perform his duties as Pri nce of Aquitaine, and returned to England, where plague was wreaking havo c. It killed Joan?s mother, Margaret, in 1372. Joan inherited her title t o add to
1 CONC  all the others ? Lady Wake of Lidel. In that same year, the Blac k Prince forced himself to attempt one final, abortive campaign in the ho pe of saving his father?s French possessions. His health was now complete ly shattered. La
1 CONC ter the same year, a week before his forty-sixth birthday , he died in his bed at Westminster.
1 CONT Joan?s son, Richard, was now the heir to the throne. Early in his reign , the young King faced the challenge of the Peasant's Revolt. The Lollard s, religious reformers led by John Wyclif, had enjoyed the protection o f Joan of Ke
1 CONC nt, but the violent climax of the popular movement for refor m reduced the feisty Joan to a state of terror, whilst leaving the King w ith an improved reputation. But for Joan, worse was to come. In 1385, Si r John Holland, a grow
1 CONC n-up son of her first marriage, was campaigning wit h the King in Scotland, when a quarrel broke out between him and Lord Sta fford, a favourite of the new Queen. Stafford was killed, and John Hollan d sought sanctuary at the shri
1 CONC ne of St John of Beverley. On the King?s re turn, Holland was condemned to death. Joan pleaded with her son for fou r days to spare his half-brother. On the fifth day, (the exact date in Au gust is not known), she died, at Walling
1 CONC ford Castle. Richard, of course , relented, and pardoned Holland, but the damage was done. Joan was burie d at Stamford in Lincolnshire. Sir John Holland was sent on a pilgrimag e to the Holy Land
0 @N6000000002187746153@ NOTE Joan of England (1335-1348)
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Joan of England (1333 or 1335- 2 September 1348) was the daughter of King Edward III of England and his Queen, Philippa of Hainault. Joan, also known as Joanna, was born perhaps in February of 1333 in the Tower of London.
1 CONT
1 CONT As a child she was put in the care of Marie de St Pol, wife of Aymer de Valence, who was the foundress of Pembroke College. She grew up together with her sister Isabella, her brother Edward and their cousin Joan of Kent.
1 CONT
1 CONT Contents [hide]
1 CONT 1 Life 
1 CONT 2 Travel to Castile 
1 CONT 3 Death 
1 CONT 4 Aftermath 
1 CONT 5 In later Culture 
1 CONT 6 Veil of Mystery 
1 CONT 7 References 
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Life
1 CONT In 1338 Joan was taken on her father's travel to Coblence, where they met Louis IV, Holy Roman Emperor, and were his special guests at the Imperial Diet in the church of Saint Castor. Edward III had formed an alliance with him aga
1 CONC inst Philip VI of France, but in 1341 the emperor deserted him.
1 CONT
1 CONT It is possible that Joan had been betrothed to one of the sons Louis had with his wife Margaret of Holland, Philippa's older sister, and actually stayed in their court to be educated there. However, Edward III withdrew her in 1340
1 CONC .
1 CONT
1 CONT In 1345 she was betrothed to Pedro of Castile, son of Alphonso XI of Castile and Maria of Portugal.
1 CONT
1 CONT In early August of 1348 Joan left England with the blessing of her parents, and thanks to a heavily armed retinue she was, perhaps, the most protected woman of Europe in those days. It is said that her trousseau alone required an 
1 CONC entire ship, and the travel schedule included a visit to a castle of her family in Bordeaux.
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Travel to Castile
1 CONT Edward III spared no expense in the preparation of Joan's journey, equipping her in the most impressive and wonderful way he could. The King loved his daughter, but it's very likely that he also wanted to make a display of power a
1 CONC nd wealth toward his allies in Castile.
1 CONT
1 CONT The fleet that carried the Princess and her retinue consisted of four English ships, which departed from Portsmouth and were received in Bordeaux by the awestruck mayor Raymond de Bisquale. Some say that he immediately warned Joan
1 CONC  and her companions about the danger of the Plague, but they didn't listen and proceeded to settle in the royal castle overlooking the estuary of the Gironde.
1 CONT
1 CONT Joan's entourage included three leading officials: Robert Bouchier, the former royal chancellor; Andrew Ullford, a diplomatic lawyer; and the cathedral priest of Bordeaux, Gerald de Podio, who was to take care of the Princess's sp
1 CONC iritual needs. Joan also had a remarkable Castilian minstrel, Gracias de Gyvill, who had been dispatched to England by Prince Pedro in order to entertain her with music and songs of the land of which she was to be Queen.
1 CONT
1 CONT The Princess was protected by over a hundred formidable English bowmen, some of them veterans of the Battle of Crecy, and she even traveled with a luxurious portable chapel so she could enjoy Catholic services without having to us
1 CONC e the local churches all along the way to Castile.
1 CONT
1 CONT Joan's wedding dress was made with more than 150 meters of rakematiz, a thick imported silk, but she also had a suit of red velvet, five corsets woven with gold patterns of stars, crescents and diamonds and at least two elaborate 
1 CONC dresses with an inbuilt corset.
1 CONT
1 CONT As Princess Joan embarked on her journey to Castile, the Black Death had not taken hold of England yet and it is unlikely that they were aware of the dangers that lay ahead. Joan and her retinue were travelling into the center of 
1 CONC a tragedy the likes of which Europe had never seen, and arrows and walls would not be enough to save her.
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Death
1 CONT Despite the severe outbreak that was taking place in Bordeaux, at first it did not occur to Joan and her advisors to get out of town. Very soon she watched in horror as the members of her entourage began falling sick and dying, an
1 CONC d Robert Bouchier, the main leader of the retinue, died in August 20 of the Plague.
1 CONT
1 CONT Joan feared for her life, and was moved probably to a small village called Loremo where she remained for some time. However, they could not escape the disease and Joan was its first victim in the camp, suffering a violent and quic
1 CONC k attack of the Black Death and dying on September 2nd, 1348, never reaching Castile and leaving her family in sorrow and fear.
1 CONT
1 CONT Some accounts document that Joan was buried in Bayonne Cathedral, and her statue, in Westminster Abbey, is on the South Side of her father's tomb.
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Aftermath
1 CONT Joan's death sent shockwaves back home. Not only was she one of the earliest English victims of the Plague, but her death seemed to prove that even royalty were not going to be spared this deadly affliction.
1 CONT
1 CONT Andrew Ullford, the diplomatic lawyer, was not affected by the Plague and very soon he took off for England, in order to inform the King what had occurred. He did so in October, and the royal family, horrified, realized the true d
1 CONC anger of the disease that had already started to attack their kingdom.
1 CONT
1 CONT On October 15, 1348, Edward III sent a letter to King Alfonso of Castile terminating the marriage arrangements and describing the sorrow that he and his family were suffering after the sudden and tragic death of the Princess. He d
1 CONC escribed Joan as a martyred angel looking down from Heaven to protect the royal family, and concluded with traditional and formal piety:
1 CONT
1 CONT "We have placed our trust in God and our life between his hands, where he held it closely through many dangers"
1 CONT
1 CONT On October 25, Edward III sent an expedition to Bordeaux that was supposed to find the body of Joan and bring it back for burial in London. The leader was a northern ecclesiastical lord, the bishop of Carlisle, who was overpaid by
1 CONC  the King because of the terrible risk involved.
1 CONT
1 CONT It is unknown what happened next. There is no record of Joan's remains being returned to England, nor any account of a funeral of any kind. Joan was taken away by the Plague and turned into a legend, and it has been suggested that
1 CONC  her death, which prevented the dynastic union between England and Castile, altered the course of the Hundred Years' War and changed European history for centuries to come[citation needed].
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] In later Culture
1 CONT Joan appeared in The History Channel's two-hour Special The Plague[1]. In the microsite's gallery, picture #8 represents her. 
1 CONT Her story was part of a book by medievalist Norman Cantor: In the Wake of the Plague: The Black Death & the World it Made, in which the author wonders what would have happened if Joan had survived her journey and married her Princ
1 CONC e. 
1 CONT Joan's life in the weeks leading up to her death are key to the fictional plot of the graphic novel, Destiny: A Chronicle of Deaths Foretold. 
1 CONT Joan is a key character in the early chapters of The Lady Royal by Molly Costain Haycraft, a fictionalized biography of her older sister Isabella. The novel depicts the sisters as close friends and confidantes, and alleges that Is
1 CONC abella was visiting with Joan and her retinue at the time of the younger princess's death. 
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Veil of Mystery
1 CONT Today we know very little about Joan's life and death, which is rather strange given how important and significant her family was. Many pieces of information about this 14th century Princess are lost, including:
1 CONT
1 CONT Year of Birth: Most likely it was 1333, but could be 1334 or 1335. 
1 CONT Day of Birth: February something- Unknown. 
1 CONT Personality: Unknown. 
1 CONT Place of Birth: Tower of London, but others say Woodstock Palace. 
1 CONT Place of Death: Loremo, Bordeaux, or maybe Bayonne. 
1 CONT Grave: Cathedral of Bayonne, but it is unclear whether she was really buried there or not. 
1 CONT Possible loss of her remains: According to noted medievalist Norman Cantor, Joan died in Bordeaux and her body was lost in a rampaging fire that destroyed the royal castle. 
1 CONT We have no portrait of her of any kind. But considering the beauty of Isabella of France (paternal grandmother) and Philippa of Hainault (mother), Joan is likely to have been very beautiful. 
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] References
1 CONT Due to the mysterious circumstances surrounding Joan's story, it's rather difficult to find reliable sources of information about her. The Loremo and Bayonne theories mentioned above are disputed, but most of what is presented her
1 CONC e comes from two respectable sources:
1 CONT
1 CONT The History Channel [2] 
1 CONT In the Wake of the Plague: The Black Death & the World it Made. By Norman Cantor. 
