0 @N6000000002188008899@ NOTE Richard II of England
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Richard II
1 CONT King of England; Lord of Ireland (more...)
1 CONT Portrait at Westminster Abbey, ~1390
1 CONT Portrait at Westminster Abbey, ~1390
1 CONT Reign 22 June 1377 – 29 September 1399
1 CONT Coronation 16 July 1377
1 CONT Predecessor Edward III
1 CONT Regent John, Duke of Lancaster (de facto)
1 CONT Successor Henry IV
1 CONT Consort Anne of Bohemia (1382–1394)
1 CONT Isabella of Valois (1396–)
1 CONT Detail
1 CONT Titles and styles
1 CONT King Richard II
1 CONT The King
1 CONT Prince of Wales
1 CONT Royal house House of Plantagenet
1 CONT Father Edward, the Black Prince
1 CONT Mother Joan of Kent
1 CONT Born 6 January 1367(1367-01-06)
1 CONT Bordeaux, France
1 CONT Died 14 February 1400 (aged 33)
1 CONT Pontefract Castle, West Yorkshire
1 CONT Burial Westminster Abbey, London
1 CONT
1 CONT Richard II (6 January 1367 – 14 February 1400) was King of England from 1377 until he was deposed in 1399. He is famed for his pivotal role in resolving the Peasants' Revolt of 1381, and for his purported misdemeanours as King, wh
1 CONC ich led to both to his forced resignation and civil war.
1 CONT Contents
1 CONT [hide]
1 CONT
1 CONT     * 1 Early life
1 CONT     * 2 First crisis of 1387-88
1 CONT     * 3 A fragile peace
1 CONT     * 4 Second crisis of 1397–99 and Richard's deposition
1 CONT     * 5 Richard as a collector
1 CONT     * 6 Association with Geoffrey Chaucer
1 CONT     * 7 Ancestors
1 CONT     * 8 See also
1 CONT     * 9 Notes
1 CONT     * 10 References
1 CONT     * 11 External links
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Early life
1 CONT Richard II watches Wat Tyler's death and addresses the peasants in the background
1 CONT Richard II watches Wat Tyler's death and addresses the peasants in the background
1 CONT
1 CONT Richard was born in Bordeaux at the feast of Epiphany, with three kings present at his birth. His father was Edward of Woodstock, Prince of Wales, (otherwise known as the Black Prince) and his mother was Joan "The Fair Maid of Ken
1 CONC t". After Richard's elder brother had died in infancy, he became heir to the throne of England (and was created Prince of Wales) in 1376, when the Black Prince died after a wasting illness. The following year his grandfather King 
1 CONC Edward III of England also died, making Richard king at the age of 10.
1 CONT
1 CONT During his minority, three 'continual councils' lasting from June 1377 to January 1380 were responsible for the general governing of the country. In reality John of Gaunt, his uncle, exerted considerable influence on matters of im
1 CONC portance (despite not being a member of any of the three councils) especially with regard to foreign policy. During that time, the Peasants' Revolt of 1381 brought Richard to prominence at the age of 14. It fell to him personally 
1 CONC to negotiate with Wat Tyler, the other rebel leaders, and their massed armed ranks of several thousand. He promised pardon to the leaders of the rebellion, but the promise was not honoured - they were arrested and executed. Althou
1 CONC gh it is now generally accepted that Richard was not sympathetic to the rebels' demands, it remains doubtful whether Richard intended the arrests to occur, or if he was forced to go against his word by militant sections of the Eng
1 CONC lish nobility. Either way, his tactics dispersed the rebel forces from the streets of London back to their homes in the country, thus ending the disorder. But as the young king matured into adulthood he revealed an inability to ma
1 CONC ke the deals and compromises essential to 14th century politics and diplomacy, leading eventually to his deposition in 1399.
1 CONT
1 CONT At St Stephen's Chapel, Westminster on c.January 22, 1382, he married Anne of Bohemia, daughter of Charles IV, Holy Roman Emperor and Elizabeth of Pomerania; but they had no children, and she died on June 7, 1394. On c.October 31,
1 CONC  1396 at St Nicholas' Church, Calais, he married the seven-year-old Princess Isabella of Valois, daughter of Charles VI of France and Isabeau de Bavière; that marriage was also without issue.
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] First crisis of 1387-88
1 CONT English Royalty
1 CONT House of Plantagenet
1 CONT
1 CONT Armorial of Plantagenet
1 CONT Edward III
1 CONT    Edward, Prince of Wales
1 CONT    Lionel, Duke of Clarence
1 CONT    John, Duke of Lancaster
1 CONT    Edmund, Duke of York
1 CONT    Thomas, Duke of Gloucester
1 CONT    Joan of England
1 CONT    Isabella, Countess of Bedford
1 CONT Grandchildren
1 CONT     Richard II
1 CONT     Philippa, Countess of Ulster
1 CONT     Philippa, Queen of Portugal
1 CONT     Elizabeth, Baroness Fanhope and Milbroke
1 CONT     Henry IV
1 CONT     Katherine, Queen of Castile
1 CONT     Edward, Duke of York
1 CONT     Richard, Earl of Cambridge
1 CONT     Constance of York
1 CONT     Anne, Countess of Eu
1 CONT Richard II
1 CONT
1 CONT As Richard began to take over the business of government himself, he sidelined many of the established nobles, such as Thomas de Beauchamp, 12th Earl of Warwick, Richard Fitzalan, 11th Earl of Arundel, and Thomas of Woodstock, 1st
1 CONC  Duke of Gloucester. These individuals, not surprisingly, would help to plot the downfall of Richard II. After having exiled the current council, Richard turned to his inner circle of favourites for his council, men such as Robert
1 CONC  de Vere, 9th Earl of Oxford and Michael de la Pole, whom Richard created Earl of Suffolk and made chancellor of England. It is possible that Richard had a homosexual relationship with de Vere; Thomas Walsingham called it 'obscene
1 CONC '[1] and 'not without a degree of improper intimacy'.[2] The nobles he had snubbed formed the head of a group of the disaffected who called themselves the Lords Appellant. The central tenet of their appeal was continued war with F
1 CONC rance against Richard's policy of peace, an aim that many of them pursued in the interests of personal gain rather than the interests of the nation.
1 CONT
1 CONT In 1386, the English Parliament, under pressure from the Lords Appellant, demanded that Richard remove his unpopular councillors. When he refused, he was told that since he was still a minor, a Council of Government would rule in 
1 CONC his place. Richard had the Earl of Arundel, leader of the Lords Appellant, arrested; but Richard's small army led by de Vere was overpowered by the forces of the Lords Appellant outside Oxford, and Richard was imprisoned in the To
1 CONC wer of London. Subsequently Richard agreed to hold a parliament in order to resolve the Appellants' grievances; the unpopular councillors were forcibly disposed of (eight being executed for treason and the others exiled) in the Me
1 CONC rciless Parliament of 1388. Richard was forced to accept new councillors and was temporarily stripped of almost all his authority.
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] A fragile peace
1 CONT
1 CONT In the years which followed, Richard became more cautious in his dealings with the barons. After having recovered power in 1389, and having made his promise to the Marcolf chamber for better improvements and a better government, R
1 CONC ichard began to improve his relationships with his subjects. In 1390, a tournament was held to celebrate Richard’s coming of age and the apparent new-found harmony since Richard's uncle John of Gaunt's return from Spain. Richard’s
1 CONC  team of knights, The Harts, all wore the identical symbol – a white hart – which Richard had chosen. Richard himself favoured genteel interests like fine food, insisting spoons be used at his court and inventing the handkerchief.
1 CONC  He beautified Westminster Hall with a new ceiling and was a keen and cultured patron of the arts, architecture and literature. His detractors, however, dismissed him as another Edward II, somehow unworthy of his military Plantage
1 CONC net heritage, given his delicate 'unkingly' tastes. Richard also lacked his grandfather's thirst for battle: his Scottish campaign in 1385 was not decisive, and he signed a 28-year truce with France in 1396 which was hugely unpopu
1 CONC lar at home in spite of the dividends that peace brought to the kingdom.
1 CONT
1 CONT Richard's commitment to peace rather than war can also be seen in his first expedition to Ireland in 1394. He put forward a policy based on the understanding that the Irish rebels were motivated largely by the grievances they had 
1 CONC against absentee English landowners and that they were perhaps entitled to some redress in this regard. Those whom he labelled the "wild Irish" - native Irish who had not joined the rebel cause - he treated with kindness and respe
1 CONC ct.[citation needed]
1 CONT
1 CONT Richard seems to have developed a passionate devotion to the old ideal of the Divine Right of Kings, feeling that he should be unquestioned and unfettered in the way he ran the kingdom. He became a stickler for tradition, insistin
1 CONC g on being addressed as ‘majesty’ and ‘highness’ and sitting alone for hours wearing his crown; those addressing him were required to direct their eyes downwards in deference. In the early 1390s, he began to put an emphasis on the
1 CONC  powers of the prerogative and on the subjects' obligation to obey. Richard would react harshly on those who challenged his authority. For example, in 1392, Richard seized the liberties of the city of London when the Londoners ref
1 CONC used to give him a loan. In addition, as king, Richard began to fashion a grander and more exalted style of monarchy. With these changes the royal court became much larger.
1 CONT
1 CONT Richard promised to lower the burden of taxation on his subjects. This promise was not carried through, however, and Richard's subjects continued to be heavily taxed. Under Richard, four and a half subsidies were granted by parlia
1 CONC ment between 1390 and 1398.
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Second crisis of 1397–99 and Richard's deposition
1 CONT
1 CONT In 1397 Richard decided to rid himself of the Lords Appellant who were confining his power, on the pretext of an aristocratic plot. Richard had the Earl of Arundel executed and Warwick exiled, while Gloucester died in captivity. F
1 CONC inally able to exert his autocratic authority over the kingdom, he purged all those he saw as not totally committed to him, fulfilling his own idea of becoming God’s chosen prince.
1 CONT
1 CONT Richard was still childless. The heir to the throne was Roger Mortimer the Earl of March (grandson of Lionel of Antwerp), and after his death in 1398, his seven-year-old son Edmund Mortimer. However, Richard was more concerned wit
1 CONC h Gaunt's son and heir Henry Bolingbroke, whom he banished for ten years on a spurious pretext in 1399. After Gaunt's death, Richard also confiscated Bolingbroke's lands, following the policy of his forebears Henry II and Edward I
1 CONC  in seizing the lands of a powerful noble to centralize power in the crown.
1 CONT Arms of Richard II
1 CONT Arms of Richard II
1 CONT
1 CONT At this point Richard left for a campaign in Ireland, allowing Bolingbroke the opportunity to land in Yorkshire with an army provided by the King of France to reclaim his father's lands. Richard's autocratic ways, deeply unpopular
1 CONC  with many nobles, facilitated Bolingbroke's gaining control quickly of most of southern and eastern England. Bolingbroke had originally just wanted his inheritance and a reimposition of the power of the Lords Appellant, accepting
1 CONC  Richard's right to be king and March's right to succeed him. But by the time Richard finally arrived back on the mainland in Wales, a tide of discontent had swept England. In the King's absence, Bolingbroke, who was generally wel
1 CONC l-liked, was being urged to take the crown himself. It was at this time that he received some Byzantine emissaries who were supposed to be given 3,000 silver marks or £2,000 sterling.
1 CONT
1 CONT Richard was captured at Flint Castle in Wales and taken to London, where crowds pelted him with rubbish. He was held in the Tower of London and eventually forced to abdicate. He was brought, on his request, before parliament, wher
1 CONC e he officially renounced his crown and 33 official charges (including ‘vengeful sentences given against lords’) were made against him. He was not permitted to answer the charges. Parliament then accepted Henry Bolingbroke (Henry 
1 CONC IV) as the new king.
1 CONT
1 CONT Richard was placed in Pontefract Castle, where he died around the second week of February, 1400. He is believed to have been killed by starvation, or otherwise murdered.[3]
1 CONT
1 CONT Richard's body was displayed in the old St Paul's Cathedral, and he was then buried in Kings Langley Church. His coffin was badly designed, however, and it proved easy for disrespectful visitors to place their hands through severa
1 CONC l openings in the coffin and interfere with what was inside. It is said that a schoolboy walked off with Richard's jawbone.[citation needed] Rumours that Richard was still alive persisted well into the reign of Henry V, who decide
1 CONC d to have his body moved to its final resting place in Westminster Abbey with much ceremony in 1413.
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Richard as a collector
1 CONT
1 CONT Richard was a keen collector of precious objects. In 1398/9 they were recorded on a treasure roll, and the treasure roll has survived. It is now held at the British National Archives, Kew, London (reference TNA: PRO, E 101/411/9).
1 CONT
1 CONT The roll lists 1,026 items of treasure, how much each item weighed, and how much it was worth. We learn, for example, that Richard had 11 gold crowns, 157 gold cups, and 320 precious religious objects including bells, chalices and
1 CONC  reliquaries.
1 CONT
1 CONT Each item also has a brief description. The only object listed on the roll that certainly survives is a crown now held in the Schatzkammer der Residenz, Munich. The roll describes the crown as "…set with eleven sapphires, thirty-t
1 CONC hree balas rubies, a hundred and thirty-two pearls, thirty-three diamonds, eight of them imitation gems".
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Association with Geoffrey Chaucer
1 CONT
1 CONT Geoffrey Chaucer served as a diplomat and Clerk of The King's Works for Richard II. Their relationship encompassed all of Richard's reign, and was apparently fruitful. In the decade before Chaucer's death, Richard granted him seve
1 CONC ral gifts and annuities, including: 20 pounds a year for life in 1394, and 252 gallons of wine per year in 1397. Chaucer died on October 25, 1400.
1 CONT
