0 @N6000000003300400883@ NOTE Origins
1 CONT Henry was born at Bolingbroke Castle in Lincolnshire in April of 1366 o r 1367 and was the grandson of Edward III. He is commonly known as Henr y Bolingbroke due to his association with the castle in which he was born . Henry was 
1 CONC the only son of John of Gaunt and Blanche of Lancaster. Bot h of his parents were descended from Henry III and were distant cousins . Blance died from the Black Death when Henry was very young and his upbr inging was left to other
1 CONC  Lancastrian supporters. Henry married Mary Bohun , the daughter of Humphrey de Bohun, Earl of Hereford in 1381 when he wa s fourteen years old. Henry and Mary's first son was born at Monmouth Cas tle in September 1387. His name w
1 CONC as Henry and he would become Henry V.
1 CONT Lords Appellant
1 CONT In 1387 Henry joined the Lord Appellants who opposed King Richard II's co urt favourites and took part in the battle of Radcot Bridge where Rober t de Vere, Richard's favourite was defeated. A year later Henry returne d to the kin
1 CONC g's side possibly because his father, John of Gaunt, had retu rned from Spain. In 1390 Henry took part in an expedition to Lithuania wh ere he joined the Teutonic Knights in a local conflict. He later took a G rand Tour of Europe 
1 CONC on his way to Jerusalem, visiting the important Europ ean states and their leaders.
1 CONT Banished
1 CONT In 1397 several of the Lords Appellants were arrested and convicted of tr eason. Henry was not included, but in 1398 Henry was involved in a disput e with Thomas Mowbray. Mowbray, as one of the Appellants, spoke to Henr y of his c
1 CONC oncerns that the King would convict him of treason as well. Hen ry informed the King but Mowbray denied speaking to Henry. The dispute wa s to be settled by a duel at Coventry in September 1398 but the King deci ded to banish both
1 CONC  men, Henry for ten years and Mowbray for life.
1 CONT King
1 CONT John of Gaunt died in 1399. King Richard then banished Henry for life an d confiscated Gaunt's property and Henry's inheritance. Richard's action s were worrying for many Earls and Barons who relied on their inheritanc e to retain
1 CONC  power and status. Henry decided to take matters into his ow n hands and claim the English throne for himself. Henry saw his chance wh en Richard left England for Ireland to put down a revolt. Henry landed i n Yorkshire in July. H
1 CONC e marched south collecting Lancastrian supporters a s he went. By the end of July Henry had the support of key Barons and whe n Richard returned from Ireland the King was captured. Henry was not th e true heir to the throne. The n
1 CONC ext in line was the Earl of March who wa s only eight years old. Henry convinced Parliament that the country neede d strong leadership and on October 13, 1399, Henry was crowned at Westmin ster Abbey.
1 CONT Murder
1 CONT While Richard was imprisoned his supporters plotted to return the old Kin g to the throne by killing Henry and his family. Henry dealt with the reb ellion and Richard was murdered in Pontefract Castle.
1 CONT Rebellions
1 CONT What started as a land dispute between Owen Glendower, a wealthy land own er in Wales and his rival Lord Grey of Rutin quickly escalated into a rev olt when Henry sided with Rutin and awarded him the land. Glendower's rev olt spre
1 CONC ad and he proclaimed himself the Prince of Wales. He was joined b y the Tudor brothers from Angelsey and began guerrilla warfare against th e English in the North of Wales and the Marcher Lords in the centre. Henr y IV moved into 
1 CONC North Wales to try and stop the rebellion but he was no t able to deal with the Welsh rebels who were more skilled at fighting i n the terrain. The conflict widened when Edmund Mortimer was captured b y Glendower and when Henry re
1 CONC fused to pay a ransom to get him freed, Mort imer sided with the Welsh rebel and even married his daughter. Henry pu t Hotspur (Henry Percy) in charge of subduing the Welsh in North Wales, b ut Hotspur developed several complaints
1 CONC  against the king and joined the r ebellion. At the battle of Shrewsbury on 21st July 1403 Henry managed t o defeat and kill Hotspur before his army could join up with Mortimer. Th e rebellion was joined by others in the north inc
1 CONC luding Archbishop Scrope , but Henry dealt with them all and when Mortimer was killed at the Halec h Castle in 1408 the rebellion was over.
1 CONT Illness
1 CONT Henry suffered from a skin complaint similar to eczema and this became s o severe in 1408 and 1409 that he was immobilised. Henry trusted Arundel , Archbishop of Canterbury, to act as Chancellor and run the country whil e he was u
1 CONC nable to, but pressure was put on him to resign. Prince Henry t ook his place. Arundel returned in 1411 during a serious dispute betwee n the king and his son. It was suggested that the king should abdicate bu t Henry refused. The
1 CONC  king died from the illness on 20 March 1413.
1 CONT
1 CONT Mini Timeline (click here for expanded timeline)
1 CONT 1367 Mar Henry (IV) future king of England in born
1 CONT 1387 Apr Future Henry V born
1 CONT Dec Invasion threat from France
1 CONT 1398 Sep 16 Bolingbroke exiled
1 CONT 1399 Mar Bolingbroke's land confiscated
1 CONT Jul Bolingbroke returns to England
1 CONT Sep Bolingbroke accepted by Parliament
1 CONT Oct 13 Henry IV becomes king
1 CONT Oct 15 A new Prince of Wales
1 CONT 1400 Jan A plot to kill the new King
1 CONT Aug Glendower's revolt starts
1 CONT Aug Henry invades Scotland
1 CONT Oct Henry fights back against Glendower
1 CONT 1401  Hotspur takes control in North Wales
1 CONT Jun Hotspur resigns
1 CONT 1402  Henry IV assembles an army
1 CONT Jun 22 Battle of Pilleth
1 CONT 1403  Revolt of the Percies
1 CONT Jul 21 Battle of Shrewsbury
1 CONT Aug Earl of Northumberland surrenders
1 CONT 1404 May Glendower's Parliament
1 CONT 1405 Apr Scrope's revolt
1 CONT May Scrope executed
1 CONT 1408 Winter Henry IV very ill
1 CONT 1413 Mar 20 Henry IV dies
1 CONT ------------------------------------------------------------------------- -------
1 CONT
1 CONT HENRY IV (r. 1399-1413)
1 CONT Henry IV spent much of the early part of his reign fighting to keep contr ol of his lands. Exiled for life by Richard II in 1399, Henry's successfu l usurpation did not lead to general recognition of his claim (he remaine d unreco
1 CONC gnised as King by Charles VI of France).
1 CONT An outbreak of the plague in 1400 was accompanied by a revolt in Wales le d by Owen Glendower. In 1403, Henry's supporters, the Percys of Northumbe rland, turned against him and conspired with Glendower - the Percys and t he Welsh
1 CONC  were defeated by Henry at the Battle of Shrewsbury. This victor y was followed by the execution of other rebels at York (including the Ar chbishop in 1405).
1 CONT By 1408 Henry had gained control of the country. Henry was dogged by illn ess from 1405 onwards; his son played a greater role in government (eve n opposing the King at times). In 1413, Henry died exhausted, in the Jeru salem Cham
1 CONC ber at Westminster Abbey.
1 CONT Titles: Knight of the Garter
1 CONT Earl of Lancaster (1361 m - 2nd)
1 CONT Earl of Derby (1362 m - 2nd)
1 CONT Earl of Lincoln (1362 m - 2nd)
1 CONT Earl of Leicester (1362 m - 2nd)
1 CONT Earl of Hereford (1397 cr)
1 CONT Duke of Lancaster (1362 cr - 2nd)
1 CONT King of England (1399 - 1413)
1 CONT King Henry was styled as, "Dei Gratia, Rex Angliae et Franciae et Dominu s Hiberniae."
1 CONT
1 CONT Even before he was banished by Richard II, Henry travelled extensively. H e made two crusades to Poland, to fight Lithuanians. During the second tr ip, he decided to visit the Holy Land, stopping in Prague and Venice alon g the wa
1 CONC y.
1 CONT
1 CONT Henry's inheritance from his father John of Gaunt, the Duchy of Lancaster , was by far the largest private fortune of medieval England. His success ors Henry V and Henry VI continued the tradition of keeping the duchy's i ncome se
1 CONC parate from the crown, and to this day the duchy is the extremel y lucrative heritage of the crown.
1 CONT
1 CONT Henry was crowned by Thomas Arundel, Archbishop of Canterbury. The golde n eagle that still holds the holy oil for anointing was made for his coro nation.
1 CONT
1 CONT Henry founded the Order of Knights of the Bath for his coronation, givin g the knights of which the right to conduct the sovereign from bath to co ronation.
1 CONT
1 CONT A character, as Henry Bolingbroke, in Shakespeare's play, Richard II, and , of course, as the title character in 1 and 2 Henry IV.
1 CONT
1 CONT After King Richard banishes Henry, the latter's father, John of Gaunt, gi ves him some advice: GAUNT. Call it a travel that thou tak'st for pleasur e. BOLINGBROKE. My heart will sigh when I miscall it so, Which finds it a n enforc
1 CONC ed pilgrimage.... GAUNT. All places that the eye of heaven visit s Are to a wise man ports and happy havens. Teach thy necessity to reaso n thus: There is no virtue like necessity. Richard II Act 1, scene 3
1 CONT
1 CONT As a character in three Shakespeare plays (Richard II, as well as both pa rts of Henry IV) one of Henry's most famous speeches concerns sleep: "Ho w many thousands of my poorest subjects Are at this hour asleep! O sleep , O gentle
1 CONC  sleep, Nature's soft nurse, how have I frightened thee, That t hou no more will weigh my eyelids down, And steep my senses in forgetfuln ess?... O thou dull god, why liest thou with the vile In loathsome beds , and leav'st the ki
1 CONC ngly couch A watch-case or a common 'larum-bell?... C anst thou, O partial sleep, give thy repose To the wet sea-boy in an hou r so rude; And in the calmest and most stillest night, With all appliance s and means to boot, Deny it 
1 CONC to a king? Then, happy low, lie down! Uneas y lies the head that wears a crown." Henry IV, Part 2 Act 3, scene 1
1 CONT
1 CONT Near the end of Henry IV, Part 2, King Henry observes that fortune alway s has two parts: "Will Fortune never come with both hands full, But writ e her fair words still in foulest letters? She either gives a stomach an d no food- 
1 CONC Such are the poor, in health- or else a feast, And takes awa y the stomach- such are the rich That have abundance and enjoy it not." H enry IV, Part 2 Act 4, scene 4
1 CONT
1 CONT Bolingbroke Castle
1 CONT graphic
1 CONT Bolingbroke Castle is a prime example of 13th century castle design compl ete with a large gatehouse, round towers and a moat. Today the castle i s a ruin with only the ground floors of the towers remaining and the lowe r parts of
1 CONC  the walls.
1 CONT This castle built by Randulph de Blundeville, the Earl of Lincoln, becam e the home of the powerful John of Gaunt in the 14th century and was th e birthplace of his son, Henry Bolingbroke, who later became King Henry I V.
1 CONT The castle came to prominence again in the Civil War serving as a royalis t garrison laid siege by a Parliamentarian force who in October 1643 defe ated a relieving force of Royalists at the nearby Battle of Winceby.
1 CONT After the Civil War the castle was dismantled and gradually deteriorate d until in 1815 the last remaining structure fell. The site was a bumpy f ield until archaeologists excavated the site in the 1960s and 1970s.
1 CONT In 1995 Heritage Lincolnshire took over the management of the site from E nglish Heritage.
1 CONT graphic
