1 CONC any that God was punishing the king for executing an archbishop.
1 CONT
1 CONT Crushing the myriad of rebellions was costly, which involved calling Parliament to fund such activities. The House of Commons used the opportunity to expand its powers in 1401, securing recognition of freedom of debate and freedom
1 CONC  from arrest for dissenting opinions. Lollardy, the Protestant movement founded by John Wycliffe during the reign of Edward III , gained momentum and frightened both secular and clerical landowners, inspiring the first anti-heresy
1 CONC  statute, De Heritico Comburendo, to become law in 1401.
1 CONT
1 CONT Henry, ailing from leprosy and epilepsy, watched as Prince Henry controlled the government for the last two years of his reign. In 1413, Henry died in the Jerusalem Chamber of Westminster Abbey. Rafael Holinshed explained his unpo
1 CONC pularity in Chronicles of England : "... by punishing such as moved with disdain to see him usurp the crown, did at sundry times rebel against him, he won(himself more hatred, than in all his life time ... had been possible for hi
1 CONC m to have weeded out and removed." Unlikely as it may seem (due to the amount of rebellion in his reign), Henry left his eldest son an undisputed succession.
0 @N6000000001876732088@ NOTE <!DOCTYPE html PUBLIC "-//W3C//DTD XHTML 1.0 Transitional//EN"
1 CONT           "http://www.w3.org/TR/xhtml1/DTD/xhtml1-transitional.dtd">
1 CONT <html  xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml" xml:lang="en" lang="en"><head>
1 CONT <title></title>
1 CONT <meta http-equiv="content-type" content="text/html;charset=utf-8"/>
1 CONT <meta http-equiv="Content-Style-Type" content="text/css"/>
1 CONT </head>
1 CONT <body>
1 CONT <div align="left"><span style="font-size:12pt"><b>Marriage and issue</b></span></div>
1 CONT <div align="left"><br />
1 CONT </div>
1 CONT <div align="left"><span style="font-size:12pt">On 27 July 1380 at Arundel Castle, 19 years before his accession, Henry married 
1 CONT Mary de Bohun and had seven children by her:</span></div>
1 CONT <div align="left"><br />
1 CONT </div>
1 CONT <div align="left"><span style="font-size:12pt">&#160;&#160;&#160; * Edward (b&amp;d. April 1382); buried Monmouth Castle, Monmouth</span></div>
1 CONT <div align="left"><span style="font-size:12pt">&#160;&#160;&#160; * Henry V of England</span></div>
1 CONT <div align="left"><span style="font-size:12pt">&#160;&#160;&#160; * Thomas, Duke of Clarence</span></div>
1 CONT <div align="left"><span style="font-size:12pt">&#160;&#160;&#160; * John, Duke of Bedford</span></div>
1 CONT <div align="left"><span style="font-size:12pt">&#160;&#160;&#160; * Humphrey, Duke of Gloucester</span></div>
1 CONT <div align="left"><span style="font-size:12pt">&#160;&#160;&#160; * Blanche of England (1392-1409) married in 1402 Louis III, Elector Palatine</span></div>
1 CONT <div align="left"><span style="font-size:12pt">&#160;&#160;&#160; * Philippa of England (1394-1430) married in 1406 Eric of Pomerania, king of 
1 CONT Denmark, Norway and Sweden.</span></div>
1 CONT <div align="left"><br />
1 CONT </div>
1 CONT <div align="left"><span style="font-size:12pt">Mary died in 1394, and on 7 February 1403 </span></div>
1 CONT <div align="left"><span style="font-size:12pt">Henry married Joanna of Navarre, </span></div>
1 CONT <div align="left"><span style="font-size:12pt">the daughter of Charles d'Evreux, King of Navarre, at Winchester. </span></div>
1 CONT <div align="left"><span style="font-size:12pt">She was the widow of John V of Brittany , </span></div>
1 CONT <div align="left"><span style="font-size:12pt">with whom she had four daughters and four sons, </span></div>
1 CONT <div align="left"><span style="font-size:12pt">but she and Henry had no children. </span></div>
1 CONT <div align="left"><span style="font-size:12pt">The fact that in 1399 Henry had four sons from his first marriage was undoubtedly a 
1 CONT clinching factor in his acceptance onto the throne. </span></div>
1 CONT <div align="left"><span style="font-size:12pt">By contrast, Richard II had no children, and Richard's heir-presumptive Edmund 
1 CONT Mortimer was only seven years old.</span></div>
1 CONT </body>
1 CONT </html>
