1 CONT <html  xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml" xml:lang="en" lang="en"><head>
1 CONT <title></title>
1 CONT <meta http-equiv="content-type" content="text/html;charset=utf-8"/>
1 CONT <meta http-equiv="Content-Style-Type" content="text/css"/>
1 CONT </head>
1 CONT <body>
1 CONT <div align="left"><span style="font-size:12pt">7</span></div>
1 CONT <div align="left"><br />
1 CONT </div>
1 CONT <div align="left"><span style="font-size:12pt">Henry IV (3 April 1366[1] &#150; 20 March 1413) was King of England and Lord of Ireland 
1 CONT (1399&#150;1413). </span></div>
1 CONT <div align="left"><span style="font-size:12pt">Like other kings of England, he also claimed the title of King of France. He was born 
1 CONT at Bolingbroke Castle in Lincolnshire, hence the other name by which he was 
1 CONT known, Henry Bolingbroke. His father, John of Gaunt, was the third son of Edward III, 
1 CONT and enjoyed a position of considerable influence during much of the reign of Richard 
1 CONT II. Henry's mother was Blanche, heiress to the considerable Lancaster estates.</span></div>
1 CONT <div align="left"><br />
1 CONT </div>
1 CONT <div align="left"><br />
1 CONT </div>
1 CONT <div align="left"><span style="font-size:12pt"><b>Siblings</b></span></div>
1 CONT <div align="left"><br />
1 CONT </div>
1 CONT <div align="left"><span style="font-size:12pt">One of his elder sisters, Philippa, married John I of Portugal, and his younger sister 
1 CONT Elizabeth was the mother of John Holland, 2nd Duke of Exeter. His younger half-
1 CONT sister Catherine, the daughter of his father's second wife, Constance of Castile, 
1 CONT ruled as co-consort of Castile, by marrying Henry III. He also had four half-siblings by 
1 CONT Katherine Swynford, his sisters' governess and his father's longtime mistress and 
1 CONT eventual third wife. These four children were surnamed Beaufort.</span></div>
1 CONT <div align="left"><br />
1 CONT </div>
1 CONT <div align="left"><span style="font-size:12pt">Henry's relationship with Katherine Swynford was always a positive one (she was 
1 CONT governess to him and his sisters in youth). His relationship with the Beauforts varied 
1 CONT considerably. In youth he seems to have been close to them all, but rivalries with 
1 CONT Henry and Thomas Beaufort after 1406 proved problematic. His brother-in-law, 
1 CONT Ralph Neville, remained one of his strongest supporters. So did his eldest half-
1 CONT brother, John Beaufort, even though Henry revoked Richard II's grant to John of a 
1 CONT marquessate. Thomas Swynford, a son from Katherine's first marriage to Sir Hugh 
1 CONT Swynford was another loyal companion and Constable of Pontefract Castle, where 
1 CONT King Richard II is said to have died. Eventually, a direct descendant of John of 
1 CONT Gaunt and Katherine Swynford through the Beaufort line would take the throne as 
1 CONT Henry VII.</span></div>
1 CONT <div align="left"><br />
1 CONT </div>
1 CONT <div align="left"><span style="font-size:12pt"><b>Relationship with Richard II</b></span></div>
1 CONT <div align="left"><br />
1 CONT </div>
1 CONT <div align="left"><span style="font-size:12pt">Henry experienced a rather more inconsistent relationship with King Richard II than 
1 CONT his father had. They were first cousins and childhood playmates, they were admitted 
1 CONT together to the Order of the Garter in 1377, but Henry participated in the Lords 
1 CONT Appellant 's rebellion against the King in 1387. After regaining power, Richard did 
1 CONT not punish Henry (many of the other rebellious Barons were executed or exiled). In 
1 CONT fact, Richard elevated Henry from Earl of Derby to Duke of Hereford.</span></div>
1 CONT <div align="left"><br />
1 CONT </div>
1 CONT <div align="left"><span style="font-size:12pt">Henry spent a full year of 1390 supporting the unsuccessful siege of Vilnius (capital 
1 CONT of the Grand Duchy of Lithuania) by Teutonic Knights with his 300 fellow knights. 
1 CONT During this campaign Henry Bolingbroke also bought captured Lithuanian princes 
1 CONT and then apparently took them back to England. Henry's second expedition to 
1 CONT Lithuania in 1392 illustrates the financial benefits to the Order of these guest 
1 CONT crusaders. His small army consisted of over 100 men, including longbow archers 
1 CONT and six minstrels, at a total cost to the Lancastrian purse of &#163;4,360. Much of this 
1 CONT sum benefited the local economy through the purchase of silverware and the hiring 
1 CONT of boats and equipment. Despite the efforts of Bolingbroke and his English 
1 CONT crusaders, two years of attacks on Vilnius proved fruitless. In 1392&#150;1393 Henry 
1 CONT undertook a journey to Jerusalem where he gained a reputation of a seasoned 
1 CONT warrior and courtier.</span></div>
1 CONT <div align="left"><br />
1 CONT </div>
1 CONT <div align="left"><span style="font-size:12pt">However, the relationship between Henry Bolingbroke and the King encountered a 
1 CONT second crisis in 1398, when Richard banished Henry from the kingdom for ten years 
1 CONT after a duel of honour was called by Richard II at Gosford Green near Coventry . 
1 CONT Before the duel could take place, Richard II banished Henry from the kingdom (with 
1 CONT the approval of Henry's father, John of Gaunt) to avoid further bloodshed between 
1 CONT Henry and Thomas de Mowbray, 1st Duke of Norfolk, who was exiled for life.</span></div>
1 CONT <div align="left"><br />
1 CONT </div>
1 CONT <div align="left"><span style="font-size:12pt">John of Gaunt died in 1399, and without explanation, Richard cancelled the legal 
1 CONT documents that would have allowed Henry to inherit Gaunt's land automatically; 
1 CONT instead, Henry would be required to ask for the lands from Richard. After some 
1 CONT hesitation, Henry met with the exiled Thomas Arundel, former (and future) 
1 CONT Archbishop of Canterbury , who had lost his position because of his involvement 
1 CONT with the Lords Appellant. Henry and Arundel returned to England while Richard was 
1 CONT on a military campaign in Ireland. With Arundel as his advisor, Henry Bolingbroke 
1 CONT began a military campaign, confiscating land from those who opposed him and 
1 CONT ordering his soldiers to destroy much of Cheshire. Henry quickly gained enough 
1 CONT power and support to have himself declared King Henry IV, to imprison King 
1 CONT Richard, who died in prison under mysterious circumstances, and to bypass 
1 CONT Richard's seven-year-old heir-presumptive, Edmund de Mortimer. Henry's 
1 CONT coronation, on 13 October 1399, is notable as the first time following the Norman 
1 CONT Conquest that the monarch made an address in English.</span></div>
1 CONT <div align="left"><br />
1 CONT </div>
1 CONT <div align="left"><span style="font-size:12pt">Henry consulted with Parliament frequently, but was sometimes at odds with the 
1 CONT members, especially over ecclesiastical matters. On Arundel's advice, Henry 
1 CONT passed the De heretico comburendo and was thus the first English king to allow the 
1 CONT burning of heretics, mainly to suppress the Lollard movement.</span></div>
1 CONT <div align="left"><br />
1 CONT </div>
1 CONT <div align="left"><span style="font-size:12pt"><b><u>Reign</u></b></span></div>
1 CONT <div align="left"><br />
1 CONT </div>
1 CONT <div align="left"><span style="font-size:12pt"><b>The previous ruler</b></span></div>
1 CONT <div align="left"><br />
1 CONT </div>
1 CONT <div align="left"><span style="font-size:12pt">Henry's first problem was what to do with the deposed Richard, and after an early 
1 CONT assassination plot (The Epiphany Rising ) was foiled in January 1400, he ordered 
1 CONT his death (very probably by starvation). The evidence for this lies in the circulation of 
1 CONT letters in France demonstrating prior knowledge of the death.[2] Richard died on 14 
1 CONT February 1400, and his body was put on public display in the old St Paul's 
1 CONT Cathedral to prove to his supporters that he was dead. He was 33 years old.</span></div>
1 CONT <div align="left"><br />
1 CONT </div>
1 CONT <div align="left"><span style="font-size:12pt"><b>Rebellions</b></span></div>
1 CONT <div align="left"><br />
1 CONT </div>
1 CONT <div align="left"><span style="font-size:12pt">Henry spent much of his reign defending himself against plots, rebellions, and 
1 CONT assassination attempts.</span></div>
1 CONT <div align="left"><br />
1 CONT </div>
1 CONT <div align="left"><span style="font-size:12pt">Rebellions continued throughout the first ten years of Henry's reign, including the 
1 CONT revolt of Owain Glynd?r, who declared himself Prince of Wales in 1400, and the 
1 CONT rebellion of Henry Percy, 1st Earl of Northumberland. The king's success in putting 
1 CONT down these rebellions was due partly to the military ability of his eldest son, Henry of 
1 CONT Monmouth, who would later become king, though the son (who had maintained a 
1 CONT close relationship with Richard II)[citation needed] managed to seize much effective 
1 CONT power from his father in 1410.</span></div>
1 CONT <div align="left"><br />
1 CONT </div>
1 CONT <div align="left"><span style="font-size:12pt">In the last year of Henry's reign, the rebellions picked up speed. &quot;The old fable of a 
1 CONT living Richard was revived&quot;, notes one account, &quot;and emissaries from Scotland 
1 CONT traversed the villages of England, in the last year of Bolingbroke's reign, declaring 
1 CONT that Richard was residing at the Scottish Court, awaiting only a signal from his 
1 CONT friends to repair to London and recover his throne.&quot;</span></div>
1 CONT <div align="left"><br />
1 CONT </div>
1 CONT <div align="left"><span style="font-size:12pt">A suitable-looking impostor was found, and King Richard's old groom circulated 
1 CONT word in the city that his old master was alive in Scotland. &quot;Southwark was incited to 
1 CONT insurrection&quot; by Sir Elias Lyvet (Levett ) and his associate Thomas Clark, who 
1 CONT promised Scottish aid to carry out the insurrection. Ultimately, of course, the 
1 CONT rebellion came to naught. The knight Lyvet was released; his follower thrown into the 
1 CONT Tower.[3]</span></div>
1 CONT <div align="left"><br />
1 CONT </div>
1 CONT <div align="left"><span style="font-size:12pt"><b>Foreign relations</b></span></div>
1 CONT <div align="left"><br />
1 CONT </div>
1 CONT <div align="left"><span style="font-size:12pt">Early in his reign, Henry hosted the visit of Manuel II Palaiologos, the only Byzantine 
1 CONT emperor ever to visit England, from December 1400 to January 1401 at Eltham 
1 CONT Palace, with a joust being given in his honour. He also sent monetary support with 
1 CONT him upon his departure to aid him against the Ottoman Empire.</span></div>
1 CONT <div align="left"><br />
1 CONT </div>
1 CONT <div align="left"><span style="font-size:12pt">In 1406, English pirates captured the future James I of Scotland off the coast of 
1 CONT Flamborough Head as he was going to France.[citation needed] James remained a 
1 CONT prisoner of Henry for the rest of Henry's reign.</span></div>
1 CONT <div align="left"><br />
1 CONT </div>
1 CONT <div align="left"><span style="font-size:12pt"><b>Final illness and death</b></span></div>
1 CONT <div align="left"><br />
1 CONT </div>
1 CONT <div align="left"><span style="font-size:12pt">The later years of Henry's reign were marked by serious health problems. He had a 
1 CONT disfiguring skin disease, and more seriously suffered acute attacks of some grave 
1 CONT illness in June 1405, April 1406, June 1408, during the winter of 1408&#150;09, 
1 CONT December 1412, and then finally a fatal bout in March 1413. Medical historians 
1 CONT have long debated the nature of this affliction or afflictions. The skin disease might 
1 CONT have been leprosy (which did not necessarily mean precisely the same thing in the 
1 CONT 15th century as it does to modern medicine); perhaps psoriasis; perhaps a 
1 CONT symptom of syphilis; or some other disease. The acute attacks have been given a 
1 CONT wide range of explanations, from epilepsy to some form of cardiovascular 
1 CONT disease.[4]</span></div>
1 CONT <div align="left"><br />
1 CONT </div>
1 CONT <div align="left"><span style="font-size:12pt">According to Holinshed, it was predicted that Henry would die in Jerusalem; 
1 CONT Shakespeare's play repeats this. Henry took this to mean that he would die on 
1 CONT crusade. In reality, he died at the house of the Abbot of Westminster, in the 
1 CONT Jerusalem chamber. His executor, Thomas Langley, was at his side.</span></div>
1 CONT <div align="left"><br />
1 CONT </div>
1 CONT <div align="left"><span style="font-size:12pt"><b>Burial</b></span></div>
1 CONT <div align="left"><br />
1 CONT </div>
1 CONT <div align="left"><span style="font-size:12pt">Unusually for a King of England, he was buried not at Westminster Abbey but at 
1 CONT Canterbury Cathedral, on the north side of what is now the Trinity Chapel, as near to 
1 CONT the shrine of Thomas Becket as possible. (No other kings are buried in the 
1 CONT Cathedral, although his uncle Edward, the Black Prince , is buried on the opposite, 
1 CONT south side of the chapel, also as near the shrine as possible.) At the time, Becket's 
1 CONT cult was at its height, as evidenced in the Canterbury Tales written by the court poet 
1 CONT Geoffrey Chaucer , and Henry was particularly devoted to it. (He was anointed at his 
1 CONT coronation with oil supposedly given to Becket by the Virgin Mary and that had then 
1 CONT passed to Henry's father).[5]</span></div>
1 CONT <div align="left"><br />
1 CONT </div>
1 CONT <div align="left"><span style="font-size:12pt">Henry was given an alabaster effigy, alabaster being a valuable English export in 
1 CONT the 15th century. His body was well-embalmed, as an exhumation in 1832 
1 CONT established.[6]</span></div>
1 CONT <div align="left"><br />
1 CONT </div>
1 CONT <div align="left"><span style="font-size:12pt"><b>Titles</b></span></div>
1 CONT <div align="left"><br />
1 CONT </div>
1 CONT <div align="left"><span style="font-size:12pt">&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160; * Henry, surnamed Bolingbroke</span></div>
1 CONT <div align="left"><br />
1 CONT </div>
1 CONT <div align="left"><span style="font-size:12pt">&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160; * Earl of Derby and Northampton</span></div>
1 CONT <div align="left"><span style="font-size:12pt">&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160; * Duke of Hereford - after the punishment of the Lords Appellant</span></div>
1 CONT <div align="left"><span style="font-size:12pt">&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160; * 2nd Duke of Lancaster - Upon his father's death</span></div>
1 CONT <div align="left"><span style="font-size:12pt">&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160; * King of England, Henry IV by deposition of his cousin Richard II</span></div>
1 CONT <div align="left"><br />
1 CONT </div>
1 CONT <div align="left"><span style="font-size:12pt"><b>Arms</b></span></div>
1 CONT <div align="left"><br />
1 CONT </div>
1 CONT <div align="left"><span style="font-size:12pt">Before his father's death in 1399, Henry bore the arms of the kingdom, differenced 
1 CONT by a label of five points ermine. After his father's death, the differenced changed to a 
1 CONT label of five points per pale ermine and France.[7] Upon his accession as king, 
1 CONT Henry updated the arms of the kingdom to match an update in those of royal France 
1 CONT &#151; from a field of fleur-de-lys to just three.</span></div>
1 CONT <div align="left"><br />
1 CONT </div>
1 CONT <div align="left"><span style="font-size:12pt"><b>Seniority in line from Edward III</b></span></div>
1 CONT <div align="left"><br />
1 CONT </div>
1 CONT <div align="left"><span style="font-size:12pt">When Richard II resigned the throne in 1399, there was no question of who was 
1 CONT highest in the order of succession. The country had rallied behind Henry and 
1 CONT supported his claim in parliament. However, the question of the succession never 
1 CONT went away. The problem lay in the fact that Henry was only the most prominent male 
1 CONT heir. This made him heir to the throne according to Edward III's entail to the crown of 
1 CONT 1376[8] but, as Dr Ian Mortimer has recently pointed out in his biography of Henry 
1 CONT IV, this had probably been supplanted by an entail[citation needed] of Richard II 
1 CONT made in 1399. Henry thus had to remove Richard II's settlement of the throne[citation 
1 CONT needed] on their uncle York (Edmund of Langley ) and Langley's Yorkist 
1 CONT descendants and overcome the superior claim of the Mortimers in order to maintain 
1 CONT his inheritance. This fact would later come back to haunt his grandson, Henry VI of 
1 CONT England, who was deposed by Edward IV, son of Richard Plantagenet, Duke of 
1 CONT York, during the Wars of the Roses.</span></div>
1 CONT <div align="left"><br />
1 CONT </div>
1 CONT <div align="left"><span style="font-size:12pt">The following are the senior descendants of Edward III. Note: it is incorrect to 
1 CONT presume that female inheritance of the throne was possible at this time: the only 
1 CONT precedent (the succession in 1199)[citation needed] suggested to lawyers that it 
1 CONT was not. </span></div>
1 CONT <div align="left"><span style="font-size:12pt">The descendants that were alive at the death of Richard II are in bold.</span></div>
1 CONT <div align="left"><br />
1 CONT </div>
1 CONT <div align="left"><span style="font-size:12pt">&#160;&#160;&#160; * Edward III of England (1312-1377)</span></div>
1 CONT <div align="left"><span style="font-size:12pt">&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160; * Edward, the Black Prince (1330-1376)</span></div>
1 CONT <div align="left"><span style="font-size:12pt">&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160; * Edward (1365-1372)</span></div>
1 CONT <div align="left"><span style="font-size:12pt">&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160; * Richard II of England (1367-1400)</span></div>
1 CONT <div align="left"><span style="font-size:12pt">&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160; * Lionel of Antwerp, 1st Duke of Clarence (1338&#150;1368)</span></div>
1 CONT <div align="left"><span style="font-size:12pt">&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160; * Philippa Plantagenet, 5th Countess of Ulster (1355&#150;1382)</span></div>
1 CONT <div align="left"><span style="font-size:12pt">&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160; * Roger Mortimer, 4th Earl of March (1374&#150;1398)</span></div>
1 CONT <div align="left"><span style="font-size:12pt">&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160; * <b>Edmund Mortimer, 5th Earl of March (1391&#150;1425)</b></span><
1 CONC /div>
1 CONT <div align="left"><span style="font-size:12pt">&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160; * Roger Mortimer (died young c. 1411)</span></div>
1 CONT <div align="left"><span style="font-size:12pt">&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160; * <b>Anne de Mortimer (1390&#150;1411)</b></span></div>
1 CONT <div align="left"><span style="font-size:12pt">&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160; * Eleanor (d. 1418)</span></div>
1 CONT <div align="left"><span style="font-size:12pt">&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160; * <b>Edmund Mortimer (1376-1409?)</b></span></div>
1 CONT <div align="left"><span style="font-size:12pt">&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160; * Lady Elizabeth de Mortimer (1370/1371-1417)</span></div>
1 CONT <div align="left"><span style="font-size:12pt">&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160; * Lady Philippa de Mortimer (1375-1401)</span></div>
1 CONT <div align="left"><span style="font-size:12pt">&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160; * John of Gaunt, 1st Duke of Lancaster (1340&#150;1399)</span></div>
1 CONT <div align="left"><span style="font-size:12pt">&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160; * Henry IV of England (1367-1413)</span></div>
1 CONT <div align="left"><span style="font-size:12pt">&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160; * Edmund of Langley, 1st Duke of York</span></div>
1 CONT <div align="left"><span style="font-size:12pt">&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160; * Edward, Duke of Aumerle, later declined to Earl of Rutland</span></div>
1 CONT <div align="left"><span style="font-size:12pt">&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160; * Thomas of Woodstock, 1st Duke of Gloucester</span></div>
1 CONT <div align="left"><br />
1 CONT </div>
1 CONT <div align="left"><br />
1 CONT </div>
1 CONT <div align="left"><span style="font-size:12pt"><b>Ancestors of Henry IV of England</b></span></div>
1 CONT <div align="left"><span style="font-size:12pt">Edward I of England</span></div>
1 CONT <div align="left"><span style="font-size:12pt">Edward II of England</span></div>
1 CONT <div align="left"><span style="font-size:12pt">Eleanor of Castile</span></div>
1 CONT <div align="left"><span style="font-size:12pt">Edward III of England</span></div>
1 CONT <div align="left"><span style="font-size:12pt">Philip IV of France</span></div>
1 CONT <div align="left"><span style="font-size:12pt">Isabella of France</span></div>
1 CONT <div align="left"><span style="font-size:12pt">Joan I of Navarre</span></div>
1 CONT <div align="left"><span style="font-size:12pt">John of Gaunt, 1st Duke of Lancaster</span></div>
1 CONT <div align="left"><span style="font-size:12pt">John II, Count of Hainaut</span></div>
1 CONT <div align="left"><span style="font-size:12pt"> William I, Count of Hainaut</span></div>
1 CONT <div align="left"><span style="font-size:12pt">Philippa of Luxembourg</span></div>
1 CONT <div align="left"><span style="font-size:12pt">Philippa of Hainault</span></div>
1 CONT <div align="left"><span style="font-size:12pt">Charles of Valois</span></div>
1 CONT <div align="left"><span style="font-size:12pt">Jeanne of Valois</span></div>
1 CONT <div align="left"><span style="font-size:12pt">Marguerite of Anjou and Maine</span></div>
1 CONT <div align="left"><span style="font-size:12pt">Henry IV of England</span></div>
1 CONT <div align="left"><span style="font-size:12pt">Edmund Crouchback, 1st Earl of Lancaster</span></div>
1 CONT <div align="left"><span style="font-size:12pt">Henry, 3rd Earl of Lancaster</span></div>
1 CONT <div align="left"><span style="font-size:12pt">Blanche of Artois</span></div>
1 CONT <div align="left"><span style="font-size:12pt">Henry of Grosmont, 1st Duke of Lancaster</span></div>
1 CONT <div align="left"><span style="font-size:12pt">Patrick de Chaworth</span></div>
1 CONT <div align="left"><span style="font-size:12pt">Maud Chaworth</span></div>
1 CONT <div align="left"><span style="font-size:12pt">Isabella de Beauchamp</span></div>
1 CONT <div align="left"><span style="font-size:12pt">Blanche of Lancaster</span></div>
1 CONT <div align="left"><span style="font-size:12pt">Louis de Brienne</span></div>
1 CONT <div align="left"><span style="font-size:12pt">Henry de Beaumont, 4th Earl of Buchan</span></div>
1 CONT <div align="left"><span style="font-size:12pt">Agn&#233;s de Beaumont</span></div>
1 CONT <div align="left"><span style="font-size:12pt">Isabel de Beaumont</span></div>
1 CONT <div align="left"><span style="font-size:12pt">Sir Alexander Comyn</span></div>
1 CONT <div align="left"><span style="font-size:12pt">Alice Comyn</span></div>
1 CONT <div align="left"><span style="font-size:12pt">Joan le Latimer</span></div>
1 CONT <div align="left"><span style="font-size:12pt"> </span></div>
1 CONT <div align="left"><span style="font-size:12pt"><b> Marriage and issue</b></span></div>
1 CONT <div align="left"><br />
1 CONT </div>
1 CONT <div align="left"><span style="font-size:12pt">On 27 July 1380 at Arundel Castle, 19 years before his accession, Henry married 
1 CONT Mary de Bohun and had seven children by her:</span></div>
1 CONT <div align="left"><br />
1 CONT </div>
1 CONT <div align="left"><span style="font-size:12pt">&#160;&#160;&#160; * Edward (b&amp;d. April 1382); buried Monmouth Castle, Monmouth</span></div>
1 CONT <div align="left"><span style="font-size:12pt">&#160;&#160;&#160; * Henry V of England</span></div>
1 CONT <div align="left"><span style="font-size:12pt">&#160;&#160;&#160; * Thomas, Duke of Clarence</span></div>
1 CONT <div align="left"><span style="font-size:12pt">&#160;&#160;&#160; * John, Duke of Bedford</span></div>
1 CONT <div align="left"><span style="font-size:12pt">&#160;&#160;&#160; * Humphrey, Duke of Gloucester</span></div>
1 CONT <div align="left"><span style="font-size:12pt">&#160;&#160;&#160; * Blanche of England (1392-1409) married in 1402 Louis III, Elector Palatine</span></div>
1 CONT <div align="left"><span style="font-size:12pt">&#160;&#160;&#160; * Philippa of England (1394-1430) married in 1406 Eric of Pomerania, king of 
1 CONT Denmark, Norway and Sweden.</span></div>
1 CONT <div align="left"><br />
1 CONT </div>
1 CONT <div align="left"><span style="font-size:12pt">Mary died in 1394, and on 7 February 1403 Henry married Joanna of Navarre, the 
1 CONT daughter of Charles d'Evreux, King of Navarre, at Winchester. She was the widow of 
1 CONT John V of Brittany , with whom she had four daughters and four sons, but she and 
1 CONT Henry had no children. The fact that in 1399 Henry had four sons from his first 
1 CONT marriage was undoubtedly a clinching factor in his acceptance onto the throne. By 
1 CONT contrast, Richard II had no children, and Richard's heir-presumptive Edmund 
1 CONT Mortimer was only seven years old.</span></div>
