0 @N6000000002188309692@ NOTE Richard of Conisburgh, 3rd Earl of Cambridge
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT English Royalty
1 CONT House of Plantagenet
1 CONT
1 CONT Armorial of Plantagenet
1 CONT Edward III
1 CONT    Edward, Prince of Wales
1 CONT    Lionel, Duke of Clarence
1 CONT    John, Duke of Lancaster
1 CONT    Edmund, Duke of York
1 CONT    Thomas, Duke of Gloucester
1 CONT    Joan of England
1 CONT    Isabella, Countess of Bedford
1 CONT Grandchildren
1 CONT     Richard II
1 CONT     Philippa, Countess of Ulster
1 CONT     Philippa, Queen of Portugal
1 CONT     Elizabeth, Baroness Fanhope and Milbroke
1 CONT     Henry IV
1 CONT     Katherine, Queen of Castile
1 CONT     Edward, Duke of York
1 CONT     Richard, Earl of Cambridge
1 CONT     Constance of York
1 CONT     Anne, Countess of Eu
1 CONT
1 CONT Richard of Conisburgh, 3rd Earl of Cambridge (c. 1375 – 5 August 1415) was the younger son of Edmund of Langley, 1st Duke of York and Isabella of Castile.
1 CONT
1 CONT His paternal grandparents were Edward III of England and Philippa of Hainault. His maternal grandparents were Peter of Castile and María de Padilla who was of Jewish descent.[1]
1 CONT
1 CONT He was born at Conisburgh Castle in Yorkshire, and was confirmed in the Earldom of Cambridge, which had been resigned by his brother, in 1414. In about 1406, he married his cousin, Anne Mortimer, also a descendant of Edward III (h
1 CONC is great great granddaughter), through his son Lionel of Antwerp. A papal dispensation was dated for 28 May 1406, making it most likely that the marriage took place in May or June. It was through her that the Yorkist faction in th
1 CONC e Wars of the Roses claimed the throne. Their marriage produced a daughter, Isabel Plantagenet, and a son, Richard Plantagenet, 3rd Duke of York. The latter eventually laid claim to the throne, beginning the Wars of the Roses.
1 CONT
1 CONT It is believed that Anne died giving birth to Richard. Following Anne's death, Cambridge married Matilda Clifford.
1 CONT
1 CONT He was discovered to be one of the fomentors of the Southampton Plot against King Henry V immediately prior to departure on the French campaign. (His elder brother, Edward of Norwich, 2nd Duke of York, would die at the Battle of A
1 CONC gincourt, less than three months later.) He was stripped of all his titles and estates and was executed on 5 August 1415 at Southampton Green, Hampshire, England; before the fleet set sail on 11 August 1415.
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Footnotes
1 CONT
1 CONT    1. ^ Peggy K. Liss, "Isabel the Queen," New York: Oxford University Press, 1992, p. 165; James Reston, Jr. "Dogs of God," New York: Doubleday, p. 18.
1 CONT
