0 @N6000000002188655136@ NOTE John Hastings, 2nd Earl of Pembroke
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT John Hastings, 2nd Earl of Pembroke KG (Sutton Valence, August 29, 1347 – April 16, 1375), was a medieval English nobleman and soldier, was the posthumous son of Laurence Hastings, 1st Earl of Pembroke and Agnes Mortimer. He also 
1 CONC held the title Baron Abergavenny.
1 CONT Contents
1 CONT [hide]
1 CONT
1 CONT     * 1 Marriage
1 CONT     * 2 Military career
1 CONT     * 3 Succession
1 CONT     * 4 References
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Marriage
1 CONT
1 CONT He married on May 19, 1359 in Reading, Margaret (d. 1361), daughter of Edward III of England, but the couple had no children.
1 CONT
1 CONT He afterwards married, in July 1368, Anne, daughter of Sir Walter Manny, who survived him.
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Military career
1 CONT
1 CONT Pembroke fought in the Castilian campaign of his former brother-in-law, Edward the Black Prince, in 1367.
1 CONT
1 CONT He was created a Knight of the Garter in 1369. He was almost captured on a raid into Poitou that year, having refused to share command with Sir John Chandos, but Chandos heard of his plight and rescued him.
1 CONT
1 CONT In 1370, he fought with distinction at the sack of Limoges by the Black Prince.
1 CONT
1 CONT He was surprised by a Castilian fleet and sharply defeated at the Battle of La Rochelle (June 22, 1372), his fleet being sunk or captured. He was taken prisoner and carried to Santander, and died in captivity in Picardy.
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Succession
1 CONT
1 CONT He was succeeded by his son John Hastings, 3rd Earl of Pembroke, born to Anne a few months after his capture.
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] References
1 CONT
1 CONT     * Leigh Rayment's Peerage Page
1 CONT     * The Chronicles of Froissart
1 CONT
1 CONT Peerage of England
1 CONT Preceded by
1 CONT Lawrence Hastings Earl of Pembroke
1 CONT 1348–1375 Succeeded by
1 CONT John Hastings
