0 @N6000000002187834619@ NOTE Otto of Austria
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Otto the Merry (born July 23, 1301, Vienna; died February 17, 1339, Neuberg an der Mürz) was a Duke of Austria and the youngest son of Emperor Albert I..
1 CONT
1 CONT He had two brothers, namely Frederick the Handsome and Albert II. From 1330 onwards, he ruled jointly with Albert.
1 CONT
1 CONT After the death of Henry of Carinthia, Emperor Louis the Bavarian gave Carinthia and the southern part of the Tyrol as an imperial fief on May 2, 1335 in Linz. Otto was inthronized as duke in accordance with the old Carinthian rit
1 CONC e on the Zollfeld, and, from that time onwards, took care rather of Carinthia than of the Duchy of Austria. He founded the monastary of Neuberg an der Mürz in Styria and the Chapel of Saint George in the Augustine Church in Vienna
1 CONC . In February of 1335, he married Anna, the sister of Emperor Charles IV in Znojmo. In 1337 he founded the knightly order Societas Templois for the crusade against the pagan Prussians and Lithuanians. His surname the Merry refers 
1 CONC to the festive life at court.
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT Preceded by:
1 CONT Frederick I Duke of Austria and Duke of Styria: co-ruler to Albert II 
1 CONT Preceded by:
1 CONT Henry V Duke of Carinthia jointly with Albert II 
0 @N6000000002187700624@ NOTE Leopold I of Austria (Habsburg)
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Leopold I (born August 4, 1290 in Vienna, died February 28, 1326 in Strassburg) was a Duke of Austria and Styria from the Habsburg family. He was the third son of King Albert I and brother of Duke Frederick I the Handsome (III as 
1 CONC King of the Romans). After the death of his parents, he became the head of the House of Habsburg. As the administrator of Further Austria, he incurred a decisive loss against the Swiss in the Battle of Morgarten in 1315. He also s
1 CONC truggled to have his brother Frederick elected as King. After Frederick's capture by King Louis the Bavarian after the Battle of Mühldorf he struggled for his release.
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT Albert I Duke of Austria
1 CONT Co-ruler to
1 CONT Frederick I ---- 
0 @N6000000002322312649@ NOTE During the ongoing war between two kings of Germany, his brother Frederick
1 CONT being one of the rivals, Frederick was finally defeated by Louis IV on
1 CONT September, 28, 1322, at Muhldorf in Bararia and was imprisoned in the castle
1 CONT of Trausnitz (Upper Palatinate). In March 1325 he was freed after taking an
1 CONT oath to recognize Louis as king and to see to it that his brother Leopold did
1 CONT so too. When he proved unable to do so he returned voluntarily to prison.
1 CONT After Leopold's death in 1326, Frederick's power was limited to Austria.
