1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Children
1 CONT
1 CONT In Leipzig on 19 November 1460 Ernst married with Elisabeth of Bavaria-Munich. They had seven children:
1 CONT
1 CONT    1. Christine (b. Torgau, 25 December 1461 - d. Odense, 8 December 1521), married on 6 September 1478 to King Johann I of Denmark.
1 CONT    2. Frederick III, Elector of Saxony (b. Torgau, 17 January 1463 - d. Lockau, 5 May 1525).
1 CONT    3. Ernst (b. Meissen, 26 June 1464 - d. Halle, 3 August 1513), Archbishop of Magdeburg (1476-1480), Bishop of Halberstadt (1480-1513).
1 CONT    4. Albrecht (b. Meissen, 8 May 1467 - d. Aschaffenburg, 1 May 1484), Administrator of Mainz.
1 CONT    5. Johann, Elector of Saxony (b. Meissen, 30 June 1468 - d. Schweinitz, 16 August 1532).
1 CONT    6. Margarete (b. Meissen, 4 August 1469 - d. Weimar, 7 December 1528), married on 27 February 1487 to Duke Heinrich of Brünswick-Lüneburg.
1 CONT    7. Wolfgang (b. Meissen, ca. 1473 - d. Torgau, ca. 1478).
1 CONT
1 CONT Preceded by
1 CONT Frederick II Elector of Saxony
1 CONT 1464 – 1486 Succeeded by
1 CONT Frederick III
0 @N6000000002188378237@ NOTE Albert, Duke of Saxony
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Albert (German: Albrecht; 27 January 1443 – 12 September 1500) was a Duke of Saxony. He was nicknamed Albert the Bold or Albert the Courageous and founded the Albertine line of the House of Wettin.
1 CONT
1 CONT Albert was born in Grimma as the third and youngest son (but fifth child in order of birth) of Frederick II the Gentle, Elector of Saxony, and Margarete of Austria, sister of Frederick III, Holy Roman Emperor. Later, he was a memb
1 CONC er of the Order of the Golden Fleece.
1 CONT
1 CONT After escaping from the hands of Kunz von Kaufungen, who had abducted him together with his brother Ernest, he passed some time at the court of the emperor Frederick III in Vienna.
1 CONT
1 CONT In Eger (Cheb) on 11 November 1464 Albert married with Zedena (Sidonie), daughter of Georg Podiebrad, King of Bohemia; but failed to obtain the Bohemian Crown on the death of George in 1471.
1 CONT
1 CONT After the death of his father in 1464, Albert and Ernest ruled their lands together, but in 1485 a division was made by the Treaty of Leipzig, and Albert received Meissen, together with some adjoining districts, and founded the Al
1 CONC bertine branch of the House of Wettin.
1 CONT
1 CONT Regarded as a capable soldier by the emperor, Albert (in 1475) took a prominent part in the campaign against Charles the Bold, duke of Burgundy, and in 1487 led an expedition against Matthias Corvinus, King of Hungary, which faile
1 CONC d owing to lack of support on the part of the emperor.
1 CONT
1 CONT In 1488 he was appointed Stattholder of the Netherlands (until 1493) and marched with the imperial forces to free the Roman king Maximilian from his imprisonment at Bruges, and when, in 1489, the King returned to Germany, Albert w
1 CONC as left as his representative to prosecute the war against the rebels. He was successful in restoring the authority of Maximilian in Holland, Flanders and Brabant, but failed to obtain any repayment of the large sums of money whic
1 CONC h he had spent in these campaigns.
1 CONT
1 CONT His services were rewarded in 1498 when Maximilian bestowed upon him the title of Hereditary governor (potestat) of Friesland, but he had to make good his claim by force of arms. He had to a great extent succeeded, and was paying 
1 CONC a visit to Saxony, when he was recalled by news of a fresh rising. Groningen was captured, but soon afterwards the duke died at Emden. He was buried at Meissen.
1 CONT
1 CONT Albert, who was a man of great strength and considerable skill in feats of arms, delighted in tournaments and knightly exercises. His loyalty to the emperor Frederick, and the expenses incurred in this connection, aroused some irr
1 CONC itation among his subjects, but his rule was a period of prosperity in Saxony.
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Ancestry
1 CONT Albert's ancestors in three generations Albert, Duke of Saxony Father:
1 CONT Frederick II, Elector of Saxony Paternal Grandfather:
1 CONT Frederick I, Elector of Saxony Paternal Great-grandfather:
1 CONT Frederick III, Landgrave of Thuringia
1 CONT Paternal Great-grandmother:
1 CONT Katherina von Henneberg
1 CONT Paternal Grandmother:
1 CONT Catherine of Brunswick Paternal Great-grandfather:
1 CONT Henry the Mild, Duke of Brunswick-Lüneburg
1 CONT Paternal Great-grandmother:
1 CONT Sophie of Pomerania
1 CONT Mother:
1 CONT Margarete of Austria Maternal Grandfather:
1 CONT Ernest, Duke of Austria Maternal Great-grandfather:
1 CONT Leopold III, Duke of Austria
1 CONT Maternal Great-grandmother:
1 CONT Viridis Visconti
1 CONT Maternal Grandmother:
1 CONT Cymburgis of Masovia Maternal Great-grandfather:
1 CONT Siemowit IV, Duke of Masovia
1 CONT Maternal Great-grandmother:
1 CONT Alexandra of Lithuania
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Family and children
1 CONT
1 CONT With his wife Sidonie, Albrecht had nine children:
1 CONT
1 CONT    1. Katharina (b. Meissen, 24 July 1468 - d. Göttingen, 10 February 1524), married firstly on 24 February 1484 in Innsbruck to Duke Sigismund of Austria, and secondly on 1497 to Duke Erich I of Braunschweig-Kalenberg.
1 CONT    2. Georg "der Bärtige" (b. Meissen, 27 August 1471 - d. Dresden, 17 April 1539).
1 CONT    3. Heinrich V "der Fromme" (b. Dresden, 16 March 1473 - d. Dresden, 18 August 1541).
1 CONT    4. Frederick (b. Torgau, 26 October 1474 - d. Rochlitz, 14 December 1510), Grand Master of the Teutonic Knights.
1 CONT    5. Anna (b. Dresden, 3 August 1478 - d. Dresden, 1479).
1 CONT    6. Stillborn child (1479).
1 CONT    7. Ludwig (b. Torgau, 28 September 1481 - d. Torgau?, some days later / d. Torgau?, young after 1498) [?].
1 CONT    8. Johann (b. and d. Torgau, 24 June 1484).
1 CONT    9. Johann (b. Torgau, 2 December 1498 - d. Torgau?, some days later / d. Torgau?, young in September of the same year as his brother Ludwig) [?]. [1]
0 @N6000000002188559267@ NOTE Rupert of Germany
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Emperor
1 CONT Holy Roman Empire
1 CONT
1 CONT Armorial of the Holy Roman Empire
1 CONT
1 CONT Rupert of Germany (German: Ruprecht III "Klem", Pfalzgraf bei Rhein) of the house of Wittelsbach (5 May 1352 – 18 May 1410), he was the son of Rupert II, Elector Palatine of the Rhine and Beatrix of Sicily. Rupert was Elector Pala
1 CONC tine from 1398 and German King from 1400 until his death.
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Life
1 CONT
1 CONT He was born at Amberg, and from his early years took part in the government of the Palatinate to which he succeeded on his father's death in 1398. He was one of the four electors who met at Oberlahnstein in August 1400 and declare
1 CONC d King Wenceslaus deposed. This was followed by the election of Rupert as German king at Rhens on 21 August, and by his coronation at Cologne on 6 January 1401.
1 CONT
1 CONT Winning some recognition in Southern Germany, he made an expedition to the Italian peninsula, where he hoped to receive the imperial crown, and to crush Gian Galeazzo Visconti, Duke of Milan. In the autumn of 1401 he crossed the A
1 CONC lps, but his troops, checked before Brescia, melted away, and in 1402 Rupert, too poor to continue the campaign, returned to Germany.
1 CONT
1 CONT The news of this failure increased the disorder in Germany, but the king met with some success in his efforts to restore peace, and in October 1403 he was recognized by Pope Boniface IX. It was only the indolence of Wenceslaus tha
1 CONC t prevented his overthrow, and in 1406 he was compelled to make certain concessions. The quarrel was complicated by the papal schism, but the king was just beginning to make some headway when he died at his castle of Landskron nea
1 CONC r Oppenheim on 18 May 1410 and was buried at Heidelberg.
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Family and children
1 CONT Rupert with his wife, Elisabeth of Nurnberg
1 CONT Rupert with his wife, Elisabeth of Nurnberg
1 CONT
1 CONT He was married in Amberg on 27 June 1374 with Elisabeth of Nuremberg, daughter of Burgrave Frederick V of Nuremberg and Elisabeth of Meissen. They had the following children:
1 CONT
1 CONT    1. Ruprecht Pipan (20 February 1375, Amberg – 25 January 1397, Amberg).
1 CONT    2. Friedrich (ca. 1377, Amberg – 7 March 1401, Amberg).
1 CONT    3. Louis III, Elector Palatine (23 January 1378 – 30 December 1436, Heidelberg).
1 CONT    4. Pfalzgraf John of Pfalz-Neumarkt (1383, Neunburg vorm Wald – 13/14 March 1443).
1 CONT    5. Pfalzgraf Stefan of Pfalz-Simmern-Zweibrücken (23 June 1385 – 14 February 1459, Simmern).
1 CONT    6. Pfalzgraf Otto I of Pfalz-Mosbach (24 August 1390, Mosbach – 5 July 1461).
1 CONT    7. Margarete (1376 – 27 August 1434, Nancy), married in 1394 to Charles II, Duke of Lorraine.
1 CONT    8. Agnes (1379 – 1401, Heidelberg), married in Heidelberg shortly before March 1400 to Duke Adolf of Kleve.
1 CONT    9. Elisabeth (27 October 1381 – 31 December 1408, Innsbruck), married in Innsbruck 24 December 1407 to Duke Frederick IV of Austria.
1 CONT
1 CONT Rupert commissioned the Ruprecht building in Heidelberg castle. Today there is a Ruprecht-Karls-University in Heidelberg. He was succeeded as Count Palatine by his son Louis III.
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT Preceded by
1 CONT Rupert II Elector Palatine
1 CONT 1398-1410 Succeeded by
1 CONT Louis III
1 CONT Count palatine of Zweibrücken
1 CONT 1398-1410 Succeeded by
1 CONT Stefan
1 CONT Preceded by
1 CONT Wenceslaus German King
1 CONT (formally King of the Romans)
1 CONT 1400-1410 Succeeded by
1 CONT Sigismund
1 CONT & Jobst of Moravia
0 @N6000000002176661847@ NOTE Rupert III as elector Palatine of the Rhine from 1398, a member of the
1 CONT Wittlesbach dynasty, he succeeded Wenceslas, who had been deposed the
1 CONT previous day of Rupert's election on August 22, 1400. Never crowned as
1 CONT emperor, Rupert devoted most of his energies to combating the adherents of
1 CONT Wenceslas in Germany.
