0 @N6000000002188309187@ NOTE Odette de Champdivers
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Odette de Champdivers, La Petite Reine (b. about 1390) was the mistress of Charles VI of France (the Mad) and previously his brother, the Duke of Orléans.
1 CONT Contents
1 CONT [hide]
1 CONT
1 CONT     * 1 Mistress to royalty
1 CONT     * 2 Notes
1 CONT     * 3 References
1 CONT     * 4 See also
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Mistress to royalty
1 CONT
1 CONT Called la petite reine, "the little queen" by her contemporaries, Odette de Champdivers (sometimes Odinette or Oudine) was sent by Queen Isabella to attend upon the sick king, Charles VI; i.e., to replace the queen in the king's b
1 CONC ed. She became royal mistress ca. 1407.
1 CONT
1 CONT Charles VI had begun to display ambivalent characteristics of schizophrenia. His often violent fits led Isabella to fear him. Finally she abandoned Charles entirely, leaving him without necessaries and proper food.
1 CONT
1 CONT Isabella found a lookalike, the fair, young Burgundian Odette de Champdivers, who was then lover to Charles VI's brother, allegedly. She is described as a lively, beautiful young girl with a gentle disposition. She was daughter to
1 CONC  a dealer in horses. Isabella arranged for Odette to take her place as caregiver and lover of the unapproachable king.
1 CONT
1 CONT Odette cared for her unhappy sovereign with the utmost patience and devotion. She is credited for introducing playing cards into France, "for the amusement of [Charles IV] during his paroxysms of insanity."[1]
1 CONT
1 CONT Meanwhile, the voluptuous Isabella found consolation in the arms of her debonair brother-in-law, the Duke of Orléans, Odette's former lover. Together, Odette and Charles VI had a daughter, Margaret. On his deathbed, the last words
1 CONC  of Charles VI were her name: "Odette. Odette." She later passed into the service as mistress of Charles VII of France, the son of Charles VI and Isabella.
1 CONT
1 CONT A breed of French rose has been named for her.
1 CONT
1 CONT Also, the nineteenth-century French novelist Balzac wrote a historical novel inspired by her life titled Odette de Champdivers.
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Notes
1 CONT
1 CONT    1. ^ Pardoe (1849), p. 177 - 178.
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] References
1 CONT
1 CONT     * Pardoe, Julia. (1849). The Court and Reign of Francis the First, King of France. Lea and Blanchard Publishing. pp. 177 - 178. Google Book Search. Retrieved on February 18, 2008.
0 @N6000000002188309176@ NOTE Marguerite bâtarde de France
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Marguerite de Valois, la demoiselle de Belleville, also known as Marguerite bâtarde de France (1407 – January 1458) was the illegitimate daughter of King Charles the Mad and his beloved mistress Odette de Champdivers. She was thei
1 CONC r only child.
1 CONT
1 CONT Marguerite was legitimated in January 1428 by Charles VII of France, her half-brother.[1] He gave her a very ample dowry and married her to the Seigneur of Belleville and Montaigu. The wedding was held on May 3 of that same year i
1 CONC n Poitou. Her husband the Seigneur was Jean III de Harpedenne (or Harpedanne, Harpedane, Harpedène, etc.), son of Jean II de Harpedenne and his wife Jeanne de Mussidan. Some sources claim that she was married under the name of Mar
1 CONC guerite de Falots.
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Descendants
1 CONT
1 CONT Claude de Belleville, the last of their descendants, was killed in the Battle of Coutras on October 20, 1587 under the reign of Henry the Third. The poet Jacques Bereau wrote his epitaph. His father was Charles Harpedenne, knight,
1 CONC  lord of Belleville, Chantonnay, Sigournais, Puybelliard, Beaulieu, Saint-Flayve, La Roche-sur-Yon, etc., knight of the order of the King, and captain of 50 men in arms.
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Notes
1 CONT
1 CONT    1. ^ Cawley, C: "Foundation for Medieval Genealogy." http://fmg.ac/Projects/MedLands/CAPET.htm
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] References
0 @N6000000002188376932@ NOTE John VI, Duke of Brittany
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT John VI the Wise (in Breton, Yann V ar Fur, in french this would be Jean VI but French accounting, influenced by the French view of the Breton War of Succession, makes him Jean V le Sage) (December 24, 1389 – August 29, 1442), was
1 CONC  duke of Brittany, count of Montfort, and titular earl of Richmond, from 1399 to his death. He was son of Duke John V (Jean IV) and Joanna of Navarre. John VI married Joan of France, daughter of King Charles VI "the Mad" and his w
1 CONC ife Isabella of Bavaria-Ingolstadt. By her he had seven children:
1 CONT
1 CONT     * Anne (1409 – aft. 1415)
1 CONT     * Isabelle (1411 – 1442, Auray), married at Redon in 1430 Guy XIV, Count of Laval (d. 1486)
1 CONT     * Margaret (1412–1421)
1 CONT     * Francis I, Duke of Brittany (1414–1450)
1 CONT     * Catherin (1416 – aft. 1421)
1 CONT     * Peter II, Duke of Brittany (1418–1457)
1 CONT     * Gilles of Brittany (1420–1450), Lord of Prince
1 CONT
1 CONT Commemorative plaque of his death in Nantes
1 CONT
1 CONT Unlike his father, John VI was a peaceful man, or so the end of the Breton War of Succession permitted him to be. He strove to reinforce ducal authority, by levying a permanent army and constructing a coherent method of taxation i
1 CONC n Brittany. John VI was also a patron of the Arts and the Church and funded the construction of several cathedrals.
1 CONT [edit] See also
1 CONT
1 CONT     * Dukes of Brittany family tree
1 CONT
1 CONT Preceded by
1 CONT John V Count of Montfort
1 CONT 1389–1442 Succeeded by
1 CONT Franc
0 @N6000000002187825420@ NOTE Philip III, Duke of Burgundy
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Philip the Good or Philippe le Bon (July 31, 1396 – June 15, 1467) was Duke of Burgundy from 1419 until his death. He was a member of a cadet line of the Valois dynasty (the then Royal family of France).
1 CONT
1 CONT  Philip the Good, painted c. 1450 by Roger van der WeydenContents [hide]
1 CONT 1 Family and early life 
1 CONT 2 Early Rule and Alliance with England 
1 CONT 3 Geographic Expansion 
1 CONT 4 Patron of the Arts 
1 CONT 5 See also 
1 CONT 6 External links 
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit]
1 CONT Family and early life
1 CONT Born in Dijon, he was the son of John the Fearless and Margaret of Bavaria-Straubing. On the 28th of January 1405, he was named Count of Charolais in apanage of his father and probably on the same day he was engaged to Michele of 
1 CONC Valois(1395–1422), daughter of Charles VI of France and Isabeau of Bavaria. They were married in June of 1409. Philip subsequently married Bonne of Artois (1393–1425), daughter of Philip of Artois, Count of Eu, and also the widow 
1 CONC of his uncle, Philip II, Count of Nevers, in Moulins-les-Engelbert on November 30, 1424.
1 CONT
1 CONT His third marriage, in Bruges on January 7, 1430 with Isabella of Portugal (1397 - December 17, 1471), daughter of John I of Portugal and Philippa of Lancaster, produced three sons:
1 CONT
1 CONT Antoine (September 30, 1430, Brussels – February 5, 1432, Brussels), Count of Charolais 
1 CONT Joseph (April 24, 1432 – aft. May 6, 1432), Count of Charolais 
1 CONT Charles (1433–1477), Count of Charolais and Philip's successor as Duke 
1 CONT [edit]
1 CONT Early Rule and Alliance with England
1 CONT Philip became duke of Burgundy, count of Flanders, Artois and Franche Comté when his father was assassinated in 1419. Philip accused Charles, the Dauphin of France (his brother-in-law) of planning the murder of his father which ha
1 CONC d taken place during a meeting between the two at Montereau. In 1420 Philip allied himself with Henry V of England under the Treaty of Troyes. In 1423 the alliance was strengthened by the marriage of his sister Anne to John, Duke 
1 CONC of Bedford, regent for Henry VI of England. It is noteworthy that the Treaty of Troyes bastardized the Dauphin, made Henry V of England the successor to Charles the Mad, and made Phillip the Good the Valois who would be King if th
1 CONC e English left France.
1 CONT
1 CONT In 1430 Philip's troops captured Joan of Arc at Compiègne and later handed her over to the English who orchestrated a heresy trial against her. The alliance with England was broken in 1435 when Philip attacked Calais and, under th
1 CONC e terms of the Treaty of Arras, recognized Charles VII as king of France. This alliance was broken in 1439, and in 1440 he supported the revolt of the French nobles (an event known as the Praguerie) and sheltered the Dauphin Louis
1 CONC .
1 CONT
1 CONT Phillip signed the Treaty of Arras which completely revoked the Treaty of Troyes because of the extreme disfavored he suffered as a result of the Joan of Arc incident.
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit]
1 CONT Geographic Expansion
1 CONT Philip preferred to expand his own territory rather than become directly involved in the Hundred Years' War. He incorporated Namur into Burgundian territory in 1429 (March 1, by purchase from John III, Marquis of Namur), Hainault 
1 CONC and Holland, Frisia and Zealand in 1432 (with the defeat of Countess Jacqueline in the last episode of the Hook and Cod wars); inherited the duchy of Brabant and Limburg and the margrave of Antwerp in 1430 (on the death of his cou
1 CONC sin Philip of Saint-Pol); and purchased Luxembourg in 1443 from Elisabeth of Bohemia, Duchess of Luxembourg. Philip also managed to ensure his illegitimate son, David, was elected Bishop of Utrecht in 1456. It is not surprising th
1 CONC at in 1435, Philip began to style himself "Grand Duke of the West".
1 CONT
1 CONT  Illumination from one of the manuscripts commissioned by Philippe.In 1463 Philip returned some of his territory to Louis XI. That year he also created an Estates-General based on the French model. The first meeting of the Estates
1 CONC -General was to obtain a loan for a war against France and to ensure support for the succession of his son, Charles I, to his dominions. Philip died in Bruges in 1467.
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit]
1 CONT Patron of the Arts
1 CONT Philip was considered an extravagant ruler who embodied the qualities of chivalry. He declined membership in the English Order of the Garter in 1422, but created his own order in 1430, the Order of the Golden Fleece, supposedly ba
1 CONC sed on the Knights of the Round Table. He had no fixed capital and set up court in various places, usually Brussels, Bruges, or Lille. He held grand feasts to show off his power to his subjects, and the knights of his Order freque
1 CONC ntly travelled throughout his territory participating in tournaments. In 1454 Philip planned a crusade against the Ottoman Empire, launching it at the Feast of the Pheasant, but this plan never materialized.
1 CONT
1 CONT Philip was also a patron of the arts, commissioning many tapestries and other works of art. It was during his reign that the Burgundian chapel became the musical center of Europe, with the activity of the Burgundian School of comp
1 CONC osers and singers. Gilles Binchois, Robert Morton, and later Guillaume Dufay, the most famous composer of the 15th century, were all part of the court chapel of Philip III.
1 CONT
1 CONT In 1428 Jan van Eyck travelled to Portugal to paint King John I's daughter Isabella before Philip married her. With help from more experienced Portuguese shipbuilders Philip established a shipyard in Bruges. Roger van der Weyden p
1 CONC ainted his portrait (illustration, above left) wearing the collar of the Order of the Golden Fleece.
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit]
1 CONT See also
1 CONT Dukes of Burgundy family tree 
1 CONT [edit]
