0 @N6000000001563244981@ NOTE Henry V, ruled from 1413-1422.
1 CONT
1 CONT Henry V, the eldest son of Henry IV and Mary Bohun, was born in 1387. As per arrangement by the Treaty of Troyes, he married Catherine, daughter of the French King Charles VI, in June 1420. His only child, the future Henry VI, was
1 CONC  born in 1421. 
1 CONT Henry was an accomplished soldier: at age fourteen he fought the Welsh forces of Owen ap Glendower; at age sixteen he commanded his father's forces at the battle of Shrewsbury; and shortly after his accession he put down a major L
1 CONC ollard uprising and an assassination plot by nobles still loyal to Richard II . He proposed to marry Catherine in 1415, demanding the old Plantagenet lands of Normandy and Anjou as his dowry. Charles VI refused and Henry declared 
1 CONC war, opening yet another chapter in the Hundred Years' War. The French war served two purposes - to gain lands lost in previous battles and to focus attention away from any of his cousins' royal ambitions. Henry, possessed a maste
1 CONC rful military mind and defeated the French at the Battle of Agincourt in October 1415, and by 1419 had captured Normandy, Picardy and much of the Capetian stronghold of the Ile-de-France. 
1 CONT
1 CONT By the Treaty of Troyes in 1420, Charles VI not only accepted Henry as his son-in-law, but passed over his own son to name Henry as heir to the French crown. Had Henry lived a mere two months longer, he would have been king of bot
1 CONC h England and France. 
1 CONT Henry had prematurely aged due to living the hard life of a soldier. He became seriously ill and died after returning from yet another French campaign; Catherine had bore his only son while he was away and Henry died having never 
1 CONC seen the child. The historian Rafael Holinshed, in Chronicles of England , summed up Henry's reign as such: "This Henry was a king, of life without spot, a prince whom all men loved, and of none disdained, e captain against whom f
1 CONC ortune never frowned, nor mischance once spurned, whose people him so severe a justicer both loved and obeyed (and so humane withal) that he left no offence unpunished, nor friendship unrewarded; a terror to rebels, and suppressor
1 CONC  of sedition, his virtues notable, his qualities most praiseworthy."
0 @N6000000001578886637@ NOTE Henry V, ruled from 1413-1422.
1 CONT
1 CONT Henry V, the eldest son of Henry IV and Mary Bohun, was born in 1387. As per arrangement by the Treaty of Troyes, he married Catherine, daughter of the French King Charles VI, in June 1420. His only child, the future Henry VI, was
1 CONC  born in 1421.
1 CONT Henry was an accomplished soldier: at age fourteen he fought the Welsh forces of Owen ap Glendower; at age sixteen he commanded his father's forces at the battle of Shrewsbury; and shortly after his accession he put down a major L
1 CONC ollard uprising and an assassination plot by nobles still loyal to Richard II . He proposed to marry Catherine in 1415, demanding the old Plantagenet lands of Normandy and Anjou as his dowry. Charles VI refused and Henry declared 
1 CONC war, opening yet another chapter in the Hundred Years' War. The French war served two purposes - to gain lands lost in previous battles and to focus attention away from any of his cousins' royal ambitions. Henry, possessed a maste
1 CONC rful military mind and defeated the French at the Battle of Agincourt in October 1415, and by 1419 had captured Normandy, Picardy and much of the Capetian stronghold of the Ile-de-France.
1 CONT
1 CONT By the Treaty of Troyes in 1420, Charles VI not only accepted Henry as his son-in-law, but passed over his own son to name Henry as heir to the French crown. Had Henry lived a mere two months longer, he would have been king of bot
1 CONC h England and France.
1 CONT Henry had prematurely aged due to living the hard life of a soldier. He became seriously ill and died after returning from yet another French campaign; Catherine had bore his only son while he was away and Henry died having never 
1 CONC seen the child. The historian Rafael Holinshed, in Chronicles of England , summed up Henry's reign as such: "This Henry was a king, of life without spot, a prince whom all men loved, and of none disdained, e captain against whom f
1 CONC ortune never frowned, nor mischance once spurned, whose people him so severe a justicer both loved and obeyed (and so humane withal) that he left no offence unpunished, nor friendship unrewarded; a terror to rebels, and suppressor
1 CONC  of sedition, his virtues notable, his qualities most praiseworthy."
0 @N6000000001876732098@ NOTE <!DOCTYPE html PUBLIC "-//W3C//DTD XHTML 1.0 Transitional//EN"
1 CONT           "http://www.w3.org/TR/xhtml1/DTD/xhtml1-transitional.dtd">
1 CONT <html  xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml" xml:lang="en" lang="en"><head>
1 CONT <title></title>
1 CONT <meta http-equiv="content-type" content="text/html;charset=utf-8"/>
1 CONT <meta http-equiv="Content-Style-Type" content="text/css"/>
1 CONT </head>
1 CONT <body>
1 CONT <div align="left"><span style="font-size:12pt"><b>Henry V</b></span></div>
1 CONT <div align="left"><span style="font-size:12pt">(16 September 1386 &#150; 31 August 1422) was one of the most significant English 
1 CONT warrior kings of the 15th century. </span></div>
1 CONT <div align="left"><span style="font-size:12pt">He was born at Monmouth, Wales, in the tower above the gatehouse of Monmouth 
1 CONT Castle, and reigned as King of England from 1413 to 1422.</span></div>
1 CONT <div align="left"><br />
1 CONT </div>
1 CONT <div align="left"><span style="font-size:12pt">Henry was the son of Henry of Bolingbroke, later Henry IV, and sixteen-year-old 
1 CONT Mary de Bohun, who was to die in childbirth at 26, before Bolingbroke became king.</span></div>
1 CONT <div align="left"><br />
1 CONT </div>
1 CONT <div align="left"><span style="font-size:12pt">At the time of his birth during the reign of Richard II, Henry was fairly far removed 
1 CONT from the throne, preceded by the king and another collateral line of heirs . The 
1 CONT precise date and even year of his birth are therefore not definitely recorded; sources 
1 CONT offer as the most likely either 9 August or 16 September, in 1386 or 1387.[1] By the 
1 CONT time Henry died, he had not only consolidated power as the King of England but had 
1 CONT also effectively accomplished what generations of his ancestors had failed to 
1 CONT achieve through decades of war: unification of the crowns of England and France in 
1 CONT a single person. In 2002, he was ranked 72nd in the 100 Greatest Britons poll. He 
1 CONT invented the passport as a means of helping his subjects prove who they were in 
1 CONT foreign lands.[2]</span></div>
1 CONT <div align="left"><br />
1 CONT </div>
1 CONT <div align="left"><span style="font-size:12pt"><b>Early accomplishments</b></span></div>
1 CONT <div align="left"><br />
1 CONT </div>
1 CONT <div align="left"><span style="font-size:12pt">Upon the exile of Henry's father in 1398, when Henry was twelve, Richard II took the 
1 CONT boy into his own charge and treated him kindly. The young Henry accompanied King 
1 CONT Richard to Ireland, and while in the royal service there he visited the castle at Trim in 
1 CONT Meath, the ancient meeting place of the Irish Parliament. In 1399, the Lancastrian 
1 CONT usurpation brought Henry's father to the throne and Henry was recalled from Ireland 
1 CONT into prominence as heir to the kingdom of England. He was created Prince of 
1 CONT Wales on the day of his father's coronation. He was created Duke of Lancaster on 
1 CONT 10 November 1399, the third person to hold the title that year. His other titles were 
1 CONT Duke of Cornwall, Earl of Chester, and Duke of Aquitaine. A contemporary record 
1 CONT notes that during that year Henry spent time at The Queen's College, Oxford, under 
1 CONT the care of his uncle Henry Beaufort, the Chancellor of the university.[3]</span></div>
1 CONT <div align="left"><br />
1 CONT </div>
1 CONT <div align="left"><span style="font-size:12pt">From October 1400, the administration was conducted in his name.[citation 
1 CONT needed] Less than three years later, Henry was in actual command of part of the 
1 CONT English forces &#151; he led his own army into Wales against Owain Glynd?r and 
1 CONT returned to join forces with his father to fight Harry Hotspur at Shrewsbury in 1403.[4] 
1 CONT It was there that the sixteen-year-old prince was almost killed by an arrow which 
1 CONT became lodged in his face. An ordinary soldier would have been left to die from 
1 CONT such a wound, but Henry had the benefit of the best possible care, and, over a 
1 CONT period of several days after the incident, the royal physician crafted a special tool to 
1 CONT extract the tip of the arrow without doing further damage. The operation was 
1 CONT successful, though it left the prince with permanent scars which would serve as a 
1 CONT testimony to his experience in battle.[5]</span></div>
1 CONT <div align="left"><br />
1 CONT </div>
1 CONT <div align="left"><span style="font-size:12pt">Energetic and dynamic, Henry is perhaps best remembered for his victory at 
1 CONT Agincourt, a chapter in his life immortalised in Shakespeare's play. His marriage to 
1 CONT Catherine of Valois , daughter of the King of France, was designed to bring peace 
1 CONT to two nations that had been at war for more than 80 years; the couple's firstborn 
1 CONT son was named as heir to the throne of France. However, Henry V died while his 
1 CONT son, Henry VI, was an infant, and the power struggle over control of the French 
1 CONT throne led to renewed hostilities in the Hundred Years' War.</span></div>
1 CONT <div align="left"><br />
1 CONT </div>
1 CONT <div align="left"><span style="font-size:12pt"><b><i>Picture not shown</i></b></span></div>
1 CONT <div align="left"><span style="font-size:12pt"><i>A statue to Henry V below the clock face of the Shire Hall in Monmouth. Henry V 
1 CONT was born in Monmouth Castle in 1387 and the statue was placed on the Shire Hall 
1 CONT in 1792</i></span></div>
1 CONT <div align="left"><br />
1 CONT </div>
1 CONT <div align="left"><span style="font-size:12pt"><b>Role in government and conflict with Henry IV</b></span></div>
1 CONT <div align="left"><br />
1 CONT </div>
1 CONT <div align="left"><span style="font-size:12pt">The Welsh revolt of Owain Glynd?r absorbed Henry's energies until 1408. Then, as 
1 CONT a result of the king's ill-health, Henry began to take a wider share in politics. From 
1 CONT January 1410, helped by his uncles Henry and Thomas Beaufort &#151; legitimated sons 
1 CONT of John of Gaunt &#151; he had practical control of the government.</span></div>
1 CONT <div align="left"><br />
1 CONT </div>
1 CONT <div align="left"><span style="font-size:12pt">Both in foreign and domestic policy he differed from the king, who in November 
1 CONT 1411 discharged the prince from the council. The quarrel of father and son was 
1 CONT political only, though it is probable that the Beauforts had discussed the abdication 
1 CONT of Henry IV, and their opponents certainly endeavoured to defame the prince. It may 
1 CONT be to that political enmity that the tradition of Henry's riotous youth, immortalised by 
1 CONT Shakespeare , is partly due. Henry's record of involvement in war and politics, even 
1 CONT in his youth, disproves this tradition. The most famous incident, his quarrel with the 
1 CONT chief justice, has no contemporary authority and was first related by Sir Thomas 
1 CONT Elyot in 1531.</span></div>
1 CONT <div align="left"><br />
1 CONT </div>
1 CONT <div align="left"><span style="font-size:12pt">The story of Falstaff originated partly in Henry's early friendship with Sir John 
1 CONT Oldcastle. That friendship, and the prince's political opposition to Thomas Arundel, 
1 CONT Archbishop of Canterbury, perhaps encouraged Lollard hopes. If so, their 
1 CONT disappointment may account for the statements of ecclesiastical writers, like 
1 CONT Thomas Walsingham, that Henry on becoming king was changed suddenly into a 
1 CONT new man.</span></div>
1 CONT <div align="left"><br />
1 CONT </div>
1 CONT <div align="left"><span style="font-size:12pt"><b>Accession to the throne</b></span></div>
1 CONT <div align="left"><br />
1 CONT </div>
1 CONT <div align="left"><span style="font-size:12pt">After Henry IV died on 20 March 1413, Henry V succeeded him the next day and 
1 CONT was crowned on 9 April 1413.</span></div>
1 CONT <div align="left"><br />
1 CONT </div>
1 CONT <div align="left"><span style="font-size:12pt"><b>Domestic policy</b></span></div>
1 CONT <div align="left"><br />
1 CONT </div>
1 CONT <div align="left"><span style="font-size:12pt">Henry tackled all of the domestic policies together, and gradually built on them a 
1 CONT wider policy. From the first, he made it clear that he would rule England as the head 
1 CONT of a united nation, and that past differences were to be forgotten. The late King 
1 CONT Richard II of England was honourably reinterred; the young Mortimer was taken into 
1 CONT favour; the heirs of those who had suffered in the last reign were restored gradually 
1 CONT to their titles and estates. Henry used his personal influence in vain, and the gravest 
1 CONT domestic danger was Lollard discontent. But the king's firmness nipped the 
1 CONT movement in the bud (January 1414), and made his own position as ruler secure.</span></div>
1 CONT <div align="left"><br />
1 CONT </div>
1 CONT <div align="left"><span style="font-size:12pt">With the exception of the Southampton Plot in favour of Mortimer, involving Henry 
1 CONT Scrope, 3rd Baron Scrope of Masham and Richard, Earl of Cambridge (grandfather 
1 CONT of the future King Edward IV of England) in July 1415, the rest of his reign was free 
1 CONT from serious trouble at home. Henry V promoted the use of the English language in 
1 CONT government, and his reign marks the appearance of Chancery Standard English as 
1 CONT well as the adoption of English as the language of record within Government. He 
1 CONT was the first king to use English in his personal correspondence since the Norman 
1 CONT conquest 350 years before.[6][7]</span></div>
1 CONT <div align="left"><br />
1 CONT </div>
1 CONT <div align="left"><span style="font-size:12pt"><b>Foreign affairs</b></span></div>
1 CONT <div align="left"><br />
1 CONT </div>
1 CONT <div align="left"><span style="font-size:12pt">Henry could now turn his attention to foreign affairs. A writer of the next generation 
1 CONT was the first to allege that Henry was encouraged by ecclesiastical statesmen to 
1 CONT enter into the French war as a means of diverting attention from home troubles. This 
1 CONT story seems to have no foundation. Old commercial disputes and the support which 
1 CONT the French had lent to Owain Glynd?r were used as an excuse for war, whilst the 
1 CONT disordered state of France afforded no security for peace. The French king, Charles 
1 CONT VI, was prone to mental illness, and his eldest son was an unpromising prospect.</span></div>
1 CONT <div align="left"><br />
1 CONT </div>
1 CONT <div align="left"><span style="font-size:12pt">Following Agincourt, Hungarian King (later Holy Roman Emperor 1433-1437) 
1 CONT Sigismund made a visit to Henry in hopes of making peace between England and 
1 CONT France. His goal was to persuade Henry to modify his demands against the French. 
1 CONT Henry lavishly entertained the emperor and even had him enrolled in the Order of the 
1 CONT Garter. Sigismund in turn inducted Henry into the Order of the Dragon.[8] Henry had 
1 CONT intended to crusade for the order after uniting the English and French thrones, but he 
1 CONT died before fulfilling his plans.[9][10][11] Sigismund left England several months 
1 CONT later, having signed the Treaty of Canterbury, acknowledging English claims to 
1 CONT France.</span></div>
1 CONT <div align="left"><br />
1 CONT </div>
1 CONT <div align="left"><span style="font-size:12pt"><b>Campaigns in France</b></span></div>
1 CONT <div align="left"><span style="font-size:12pt">Henry V of England depicted in Cassell's History of England (1902)</span></div>
1 CONT <div align="left"><br />
1 CONT </div>
1 CONT <div align="left"><span style="font-size:12pt">Henry may have regarded the assertion of his own claims as part of his royal duty, 
1 CONT but in any case, a permanent settlement of the national debate was essential to the 
1 CONT success of his world policy.</span></div>
1 CONT <div align="left"><br />
1 CONT </div>
1 CONT <div align="left"><span style="font-size:12pt"><b>1415 campaign</b></span></div>
1 CONT <div align="left"><br />
1 CONT </div>
1 CONT <div align="left"><span style="font-size:12pt">On 11 August 1415 Henry sailed for France, where his forces besieged the fortress 
1 CONT at Harfleur, capturing it on 22 September. Afterwards, Henry was obliged to march 
1 CONT with his army across the French countryside towards Calais. On the 25 October 
1 CONT 1415, on the plains near the village of Agincourt , he turned to give battle to a 
1 CONT pursuing French army. Despite his men-at-arms being exhausted and outnumbered, 
1 CONT Henry led his men into battle, decisively defeating the French. However this brilliant 
1 CONT conclusion was only the first step in the campaign.</span></div>
1 CONT <div align="left"><br />
1 CONT </div>
1 CONT <div align="left"><span style="font-size:12pt"><b>Diplomacy and command of the sea</b></span></div>
1 CONT <div align="left"><br />
1 CONT </div>
1 CONT <div align="left"><span style="font-size:12pt">Command of the sea was secured by driving the Genoese allies of the French out of 
1 CONT the English Channel . While Henry was occupied with peace negotiations in 1416, a 
1 CONT French and Genoese fleet surrounded the harbour at the English-garrisoned 
1 CONT Harfleur. A French land force also besieged the town. To relieve Harfleur, Henry sent 
1 CONT his brother, the Duke of Bedford, who raised a fleet and set sail from Beachy Head 
1 CONT on 14 August. The Franco-Genoese fleet was defeated the following day after a 
1 CONT gruelling seven hour battle, and Harfleur was relieved. Diplomacy successfully 
1 CONT detached Emperor Sigismund from France, and the Treaty of Canterbury (1416) 
1 CONT paved the way to end the schism in the Church.</span></div>
1 CONT <div align="left"><br />
1 CONT </div>
1 CONT <div align="left"><span style="font-size:12pt"><b>1417 campaign</b></span></div>
1 CONT <div align="left"><br />
1 CONT </div>
1 CONT <div align="left"><span style="font-size:12pt">So, with those two potential enemies gone, and after two years of patient 
1 CONT preparation following Agincourt, in 1417, Henry renewed the war on a larger scale in 
1 CONT 1417. Lower Normandy was quickly conquered, and Rouen cut off from Paris and 
1 CONT besieged. The French were paralysed by the disputes between Burgundians and 
1 CONT Armagnacs . Henry skilfully played them off one against the other, without relaxing 
1 CONT his warlike approach. In January 1419, Rouen fell. Those Norman French who had 
1 CONT resisted were severely punished: Alan Blanchard, who had hung English prisoners 
1 CONT from the walls, was summarily executed; Robert de Livet, Canon of Rouen, who had 
1 CONT excommunicated the English king, was packed off to England and imprisoned for 
1 CONT five years.[12]</span></div>
1 CONT <div align="left"><br />
1 CONT </div>
1 CONT <div align="left"><span style="font-size:12pt">By August, the English were outside the walls of Paris. The intrigues of the French 
1 CONT parties culminated in the assassination of John the Fearless by the Dauphin's 
1 CONT partisans at Montereau (10 September 1419). Philip the Good, the new duke, and 
1 CONT the French court threw themselves into Henry's arms. After six months of 
1 CONT negotiation, the Treaty of Troyes recognised Henry as the heir and regent of France 
1 CONT (see English Kings of France), and on 2 June 1420, he married Catherine of Valois 
1 CONT , the French king's daughter. From June to July, Henry's army besieged and took the 
1 CONT castle at Montereau. He besieged and captured Melun in November, returning to 
1 CONT England shortly thereafter.</span></div>
1 CONT <div align="left"><br />
1 CONT </div>
1 CONT <div align="left"><span style="font-size:12pt"><b>1421 campaign</b></span></div>
1 CONT <div align="left"><br />
1 CONT </div>
1 CONT <div align="left"><span style="font-size:12pt">On 10 June 1421, Henry sailed back to France for what would be his last military 
1 CONT campaign. From July to August, Henry's forces besieged and captured Dreux, thus 
1 CONT relieving allied forces at Chartres. That October, his forces lay siege to Meaux, 
1 CONT capturing it on 2 May 1422. Henry V died suddenly on 31 August 1422 at Bois de 
1 CONT Vincennes near Paris, apparently from dysentery which he had contracted during 
1 CONT the siege of Meaux. He was 34 years old. Before his death, Henry V named his 
1 CONT brother John of Lancaster, 1st Duke of Bedford regent of France in the name of his 
1 CONT son Henry VI, then only a few months old. Henry V did not live to be crowned King of 
1 CONT France himself, as he might confidently have expected after the Treaty of Troyes, as 
1 CONT ironically the sickly Charles VI, to whom he had been named heir, survived him by 
1 CONT two months. Catherine took Henry's body to London and he was buried in 
1 CONT Westminster Abbey on 7 November 1422.</span></div>
1 CONT <div align="left"><br />
1 CONT </div>
1 CONT <div align="left"><span style="font-size:12pt">Following his death, Catherine had a long relationship with a Welsh courtier, Owen 
1 CONT Tudor, whom she may secretly have married. They were the grandparents of King 
1 CONT Henry VII of England.</span></div>
1 CONT <div align="left"><br />
1 CONT </div>
1 CONT <div align="left"><span style="font-size:12pt"><b>Arms</b></span></div>
1 CONT <div align="left"><br />
1 CONT </div>
1 CONT <div align="left"><span style="font-size:12pt">As Prince of Wales, Henry's arms were those of the kingdom, differenced by a label 
1 CONT argent of three points.[13] Upon his accession, he inherited use of the arms of the 
1 CONT kingdom undifferenced.</span></div>
1 CONT <div align="left"><br />
1 CONT </div>
1 CONT <div align="left"><br />
1 CONT </div>
