1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Henry V
1 CONT King of England; Lord of ireland (more...)
1 CONT Reign 21 March 1413 – 31 August 1422
1 CONT Coronation 1413
1 CONT Predecessor Henry IV
1 CONT Successor Henry VI
1 CONT Consort Catherine of Valois
1 CONT Issue
1 CONT Henry VI
1 CONT Detail
1 CONT Titles and styles
1 CONT The King
1 CONT The Prince of Wales
1 CONT The Duke of Cornwall
1 CONT The Duke of Lancaster
1 CONT Royal house House of Lancaster
1 CONT Father Henry IV
1 CONT Mother Mary de Bohun
1 CONT Born 16 September 1387(1387-09-16)
1 CONT Monmouth, Wales
1 CONT Died 31 August 1422 (aged 34)
1 CONT Bois de Vincennes, France
1 CONT Burial Westminster Abbey, London
1 CONT
1 CONT Henry V (16 September 1387 – 31 August 1422) was one of the most significant English warrior kings of the Middle Ages. He was born at Monmouth, Wales, in the tower above the gatehouse of Monmouth Castle, and reigned as King of Eng
1 CONC land from 1413 to 1422.
1 CONT
1 CONT Henry was son of Henry of Bolingbroke, later Henry IV, and sixteen-year-old Mary de Bohun, who was to die in childbirth at 26, before Bolingbroke became king.
1 CONT
1 CONT At the time of his birth during the reign of Richard II, Henry was fairly far removed from the throne, preceded by the King and another preceding collateral line of heirs. The precise date and even year of his birth are therefore 
1 CONC not definitely recorded: sources offer as the most likely either 9 August or 16 September, in 1386 or 1387.[1] By the time Henry died, he had not only consolidated power as the King of England but had also effectively accomplished
1 CONC  what generations of his ancestors had failed to achieve through decades of war: unification of the crowns of England and France in a single person. In 2002 he was ranked 72nd in the 100 Greatest Britons poll.
1 CONT Contents
1 CONT [hide]
1 CONT
1 CONT     * 1 Early accomplishments
1 CONT     * 2 Role in government and conflict with Henry IV
1 CONT     * 3 Accession to the throne
1 CONT           o 3.1 Domestic policy
1 CONT           o 3.2 Foreign affairs
