0 @N6000000002188008916@ NOTE John Holland, 1st Duke of Exeter
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT John Holland, 1st Duke of Exeter (c. 1352 – January 16, 1400), also Earl of Huntingdon, was an English nobleman, primarily remembered for helping cause the downfall of Thomas of Woodstock, 1st Duke of Gloucester and then for consp
1 CONC iring against Henry IV.
1 CONT
1 CONT He was the third son of Thomas Holland, 1st Earl of Kent and Joan "the fair maid of Kent", daughter of Edmund of Woodstock, a son of Edward I. His mother later married Edward, the Black Prince. Holland was thus half-brother to Ric
1 CONC hard II, to whom he remained loyal the rest of his life.
1 CONT
1 CONT Early in Richard's reign, Holland was made a Knight of the Garter (1381). He was also part of the escort that accompanied the queen-to-be, Anne of Bohemia, on her trip to England.
1 CONT
1 CONT Holland had a violent temper, which got him in trouble several times. The most famous incident occurred during Richard II's 1385 expedition to the Kingdom of Scotland. An archer in the service of Ralph Stafford, eldest son of the 
1 CONC Earl of Stafford, killed one of Holland's esquires. Stafford went to find Holland to apologize, but Holland killed him as soon as he identified himself. The king had Holland's lands seized. Their mother died during this time, it i
1 CONC s said of grief at these events.
1 CONT
1 CONT Early the next year Holland reconciled with the Staffords, and had his property restored. Later in 1386 he married Elizabeth Plantagenet, daughter of John of Gaunt, 1st Duke of Lancaster and Blanche of Lancaster. He and Elizabeth 
1 CONC then went on Gaunt's expedition to Spain, where Holland was constable of the English army. After his return to the Kingdom of England, Holland was created Earl of Huntingdon, on June 2, 1387. In 1389 he was appointed Lord Great Ch
1 CONC amberlain for life, admiral of the fleet in the western seas, and constable of Tintagel Castle. During this time he also received large grants of land from the king.
1 CONT
1 CONT Over the next several years he held a number of additional offices: constable of Conway Castle (1394), governor of Carlisle (1395), and then governor and then constable-general of the west marches towards Scotland. His military se
1 CONC rvies were interrupted by a pilgrimage to the Holy Land in 1394 (which may be connected with his earlier troubles with the Staffords).
1 CONT
1 CONT Holland helped the king take down Thomas of Woodstock, 1st Duke of Gloucester and Richard Fitzalan, 11th Earl of Arundel in 1397. He was rewarded by being created Duke of Exeter on September 29.
1 CONT
1 CONT He then went with Richard on the king's 1399 Ireland expedition. When they returned the king sent him to try to negotiate with Holland's brother-in-law Henry Bolingbroke. After Henry deposed Richard and took the throne (as Henry I
1 CONC V), he called to account those who had been involved in the downfall of Thomas of Woodstock, and in the end took away all rewards Richard had give them after Thomas' arrest. Thus Holland became again merely Earl of Huntingdon.
1 CONT
1 CONT Early the next year Holland entered into a conspiracy, called the Epiphany Rising, with his nephew Thomas Holland, Earl of Kent, Thomas le Despencer, 1st Earl of Gloucester, and others. Their aim was to assassinate king Henry and 
1 CONC return Richard (who was in prison) to the throne. Their plot failed, Holland fled, but was caught and executed. Among those who witnessed the execution was Thomas Fitzalan, 12th Earl of Arundel, son of the Earl of Arundel who Holl
1 CONC and had arrested some years before.
1 CONT
1 CONT Holland's lands and titles were forfeited, but eventually they were restored for his second son John Holland, 2nd Duke of Exeter.
1 CONT
1 CONT He had the following children:
1 CONT
1 CONT Richard Holland (d. September 3, 1400) 
1 CONT Constance Holland (1387–1437), married first Thomas Mowbray, 4th Earl of Norfolk, married second Sir John Grey, K.G. 
1 CONT Alice Holland (c. 1392 – c. 1406), married Richard de Vere, 11th Earl of Oxford 
1 CONT John Holland, 2nd Duke of Exeter (1395–1447) 
1 CONT Sir Edward Holland (c. 1399 – aft. 1413) 
1 CONT Political Offices 
