0 @N6000000006296760689@ NOTE Thomas Montacute, 4th Earl of Salisbury, (13 June </wiki/June_13> 1388 </wiki/1388> - 3 November </wiki/November_3> 1428 </wiki/1428>), was an English </wiki/England> nobleman. He was one of the most important English commanders d
1 CONC uring the Hundred Years' War </wiki/Hundred_Years%27_War>.
1 CONT He was the eldest son of John Montacute, 3rd Earl of Salisbury </wiki/John_Montacute,_3rd_Earl_of_Salisbury>, who was killed while plotting against the King </wiki/Henry_IV_of_England> in 1400, and his lands forfeited. Thomas did 
1 CONC get back some of his father's lost lands, and helped his financial position further by marrying Eleanor Holland </wiki/Eleanor_Holland>, a sister and eventual co-heiress of Edmund Holland, 4th Earl of Kent </wiki/Edmund_Holland,_4
1 CONC th_Earl_of_Kent>, and daughter of Thomas Holland, 2nd Earl of Kent </wiki/Thomas_Holland,_2nd_Earl_of_Kent>.
1 CONT Thomas was summoned to Parliament </wiki/English_parliament> as Earl of Salisbury </wiki/Earl_of_Salisbury> in 1409, although he was not formally invested as earl until 1421. In 1414, he was made a Knight of the Garter </wiki/Knig
1 CONC ht_of_the_Garter>. In July 1415, he was one of the seven peers who tried Richard, Earl of Cambridge </wiki/Richard,_Earl_of_Cambridge> on charges of conspiring against the King </wiki/Henry_V_of_England>. Montacute then joined Hen
1 CONC ry V in France </wiki/France>, where he fought at the Siege of Harfleur </wiki/Harfleur> and at the Battle of Agincourt </wiki/Battle_of_Agincourt>. Montacute fought in various other campaigns in France in the following years. In 
1 CONC 1419, he was appointed lieutenant-general of Normandy, and then created Count of Perche, part of Henry V's policy of creating Norman titles for his noblemen. He spent most of the rest of his life as a soldier in France, leading tr
1 CONC oops in the various skirmishes and sieges that were central to that part of the Hundred Years' War </wiki/Hundred_Years%27_War>. On 27 October </wiki/October_27> 1428 </wiki/1428> he was wounded during the Siege of Orléans </wiki/
1 CONC Siege_of_Orl%C3%A9ans>, when a cannonball broke a window near to where he stood, and he died a few days later.
1 CONT He married twice, first (as mentioned above) to Eleanor Holland, and second to Alice Chaucer, daughter of Thomas Chaucer </wiki/Thomas_Chaucer> and granddaughter of Geoffrey Chaucer </wiki/Geoffrey_Chaucer>. They lived at Bisham M
1 CONC anor </wiki/Bisham_Abbey> in Berkshire </wiki/Berkshire>. His only legitimate child was a daughter from the first marriage, Alice </wiki/Alice_Neville,_5th_Countess_of_Salisbury>, who married Richard Neville </wiki/Richard_Neville
1 CONC ,_5th_Earl_of_Salisbury>. Neville succeeded his father-in-law as earl.
0 @N6000000002188655766@ NOTE Thomas Montacute, 4th Earl of Salisbury
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Thomas Montacute, 4th Earl of Salisbury, (13 June 1388 – November 3, 1428), was an English nobleman. He was one of the most important English commanders during the Hundred Years' War.
1 CONT
1 CONT He was the eldest son of John Montacute, 3rd Earl of Salisbury, who was killed while plotting against the King in 1400, and his lands forfeited. Thomas did get back some of his father's lost lands, and helped his financial positio
1 CONC n further by marrying Eleanor Holland, a sister and eventual co-heiress of Edmund Holland, 4th Earl of Kent, and daughter of Thomas Holland, 2nd Earl of Kent.
1 CONT
1 CONT Thomas was summoned to Parliament as Earl of Salisbury in 1409, although he was not formally invested as earl until 1421. In 1414, he was made a Knight of the Garter. In July 1415, he was one of the seven peers who tried Richard, 
1 CONC Earl of Cambridge on charges of conspiring against the King. Montacute then joined Henry V in France, where he fought at the Siege of Harfleur and at the Battle of Agincourt. Montacute fought in various other campaigns in France i
1 CONC n the following years. In 1419, he was appointed lieutenant-general of Normandy, and then created Count of Perche, part of Henry V's policy of creating Norman titles for his noblemen. He spent most of the rest of his life as a sol
1 CONC dier in France, leading troops in the various skirmishes and sieges that were central to that part of the Hundred Years' War. On 27 October 1428 he was wounded during the Siege of Orléans, when a cannonball broke a window near to 
1 CONC where he stood, and he died a few days later.
1 CONT
1 CONT He married twice, first (as mentioned above) to Eleanor Holland, and second to Alice Chaucer, daughter of Thomas Chaucer and granddaughter of Geoffrey Chaucer. They lived at Bisham Manor in Berkshire. His only legitimate child was
1 CONC  a daughter from the first marriage, Alice, who married Richard Neville. Neville succeeded his father-in-law as earl.
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] References
1 CONT
1 CONT     * Hunt, William (1894). "Thomas de Montacute or Montagu, fourth Earl of Salisbury". Dictionary of National Biography 38: 208–211. 
1 CONT     * the Peerage.com on Thomas Montagu, 4th Earl of Salisbury
1 CONT     * FMG on Thomas de Montagu
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT Preceded by
1 CONT John Montacute
1 CONT (Forfeited 1400) Earl of Salisbury
1 CONT 1421–1428 Succeeded by
1 CONT Richard Neville
