0 @N6000000003300308649@ NOTE 1453 Oct Prince Edward is born
1 CONT King Henry's only son was born.
1 CONT 1471 Apr 14 Queen Margaret lands at Weymouth
1 CONT Queen Margaret (wife of Henry VI) and her son Edward the prince of Wale s landed at Waymouth on the very same day as the battle of Barnet. Thei r intention was to assist the Earl of Warwick, but she arrived too late . Determined t
1 CONC o avenge the Barnet defeat, the Queen and her army marche d north towards Wales and men she could count on to join her army. Edwar d had thought of this and to cut the Queen off from Wales, had taken cont rol of the bridges across
1 CONC  the River Severn.
1 CONT May 4 The Battle of Tewkesbury
1 CONT The Yorkists lead by Edward and Richard met the Lancastrians at Tewkesb ury. Richard was able to outflank the Lancastrians lead by Edmund Beaufor t, Duke of Somerset. Once Somerset's men had been dealt with, Richard att acked the 
1 CONC rear of the Lancastrian line which broke apart and fled. Many o f the Lancastrian leaders were caught and killed including Edward the Pri nce of Wales.
1 CONT Margaret displayed her military skill by having her forces outflank the Y orkists, under the Earl of Warwick, and by defeating them at the second b attle of St. Albans. After the battle, Henry VI was found abandoned in th e town, 
1 CONC in similar circumstances to his capture there by the Yorkists nea rly six years earlier.
1 CONT
1 CONT Died of tuberculosis.
1 CONT
1 CONT A character in Shakespeare's plays, 1, 2, and 3 Henry VI, and Richard III
1 CONT
1 CONT Early in Henry VI, Part 2, the Duke of Gloucester (the uncle of Margaret' s husband, King Henry) sums up the chilly greeting Margaret is receivin g from the English court: "She should have stay'd in France, and starv' d in France"
1 CONC  Henry VI, Part 2 Act 1, scene 1
1 CONT
1 CONT After Gloucester dies, in Henry VI, Part 2, Queen Margaret says that sh e will be accused of plotting his death: "It may be judg'd I made the Duk e away; So shall my name with slander's tongue be wounded, And princes' c ourts be f
1 CONC ill'd with my reproach. This get I by his death. Ay me, unhappy ! To be a queen and crown'd with infamy!" Henry VI, Part 2 Act 3, scene 2
